window.WORKBOOK_EPISODES = [{"code":"CUP01","file":"CUP01.srt","srt":"1\n00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,740\nHello everyone, and welcome to Two Cups English.\n\n2\n00:00:03,840 --> 00:00:06,720\nI'm Mia, and Ben is here with me.\n\n3\n00:00:06,780 --> 00:00:09,580\nThis podcast is for people who are learning English.\n\n4\n00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:11,080\nWe speak slowly.\n\n5\n00:00:11,620 --> 00:00:12,980\nWe use simple words.\n\n6\n00:00:13,560 --> 00:00:16,400\nAnd we repeat the important words many times.\n\n7\n00:00:17,320 --> 00:00:21,640\nToday, our topic is small steps every morning.\n\n8\n00:00:21,720 --> 00:00:25,030\nA small step is one small thing you do to start the day.\n\n9\n00:00:26,120 --> 00:00:28,700\nBen, how was your morning today?\n\n10\n00:00:28,780 --> 00:00:29,440\nHi Mia.\n\n11\n00:00:29,960 --> 00:00:30,560\nHi everyone.\n\n12\n00:00:31,440 --> 00:00:34,600\nMy morning today was slow, but it started.\n\n13\n00:00:35,540 --> 00:00:38,820\nI woke up, and I did not want to get up.\n\n14\n00:00:39,660 --> 00:00:40,840\nMy room was dark.\n\n15\n00:00:41,640 --> 00:00:43,600\nMy list for the day was long.\n\n16\n00:00:44,500 --> 00:00:46,320\nSo I did one small thing.\n\n17\n00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:48,449\nI drank a glass of water.\n\n18\n00:00:49,660 --> 00:00:51,920\nAnd then the day started.\n\n19\n00:00:51,930 --> 00:00:53,620\nNot a perfect morning.\n\n20\n00:00:53,700 --> 00:00:54,780\nJust a morning.\n\n21\n00:00:55,840 --> 00:00:57,200\nWhat about you, Mia?\n\n22\n00:00:57,260 --> 00:00:58,240\nDid you want to get up?\n\n23\n00:00:59,520 --> 00:01:01,860\nNo, Ben, I did not want to get up.\n\n24\n00:01:02,560 --> 00:01:03,600\nI looked at the ceiling.\n\n25\n00:01:04,180 --> 00:01:06,000\nMy phone was full of messages.\n\n26\n00:01:06,660 --> 00:01:11,860\nAnd I thought, \"Today is going to be hard.\" But then I made my bed.\n\n27\n00:01:12,460 --> 00:01:13,760\nIt took two minutes.\n\n28\n00:01:14,240 --> 00:01:16,259\nAnd my morning started, like yours.\n\n29\n00:01:17,220 --> 00:01:18,740\nI think many people feel this.\n\n30\n00:01:19,190 --> 00:01:21,540\nThe morning is the hardest part of the day.\n\n31\n00:01:22,440 --> 00:01:24,100\nThe hardest part of the day.\n\n32\n00:01:24,800 --> 00:01:25,380\nYes, Mia.\n\n33\n00:01:26,240 --> 00:01:29,260\nFor me, the problem was always the big plan.\n\n34\n00:01:30,240 --> 00:01:36,200\nEvery morning I said, \"Today I will change my life.\" Exercise.\n\n35\n00:01:36,980 --> 00:01:37,330\nReading.\n\n36\n00:01:38,260 --> 00:01:38,820\nGood food.\n\n37\n00:01:39,800 --> 00:01:40,880\nOne hour of study.\n\n38\n00:01:41,880 --> 00:01:44,180\nFour big things before eight o'clock.\n\n39\n00:01:45,360 --> 00:01:48,620\nAnd every morning, I did none of them.\n\n40\n00:01:49,600 --> 00:01:50,520\nDo you know that feeling?\n\n41\n00:01:51,600 --> 00:01:53,700\nI know it very well, Ben.\n\n42\n00:01:53,740 --> 00:01:57,080\nWhen I look at the whole day, I feel tired before I start.\n\n43\n00:01:57,880 --> 00:01:59,000\nWork feels big.\n\n44\n00:01:59,760 --> 00:02:01,020\nStudy feels big.\n\n45\n00:02:01,800 --> 00:02:03,940\nEven a small message feels big.\n\n46\n00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:06,880\nSo what changed for you?\n\n47\n00:02:06,980 --> 00:02:08,780\nHow did you stop the big plan?\n\n48\n00:02:09,759 --> 00:02:11,300\nSomething very simple, Mia.\n\n49\n00:02:12,400 --> 00:02:15,540\nI learned that a morning does not need a big plan.\n\n50\n00:02:16,480 --> 00:02:19,020\nA morning needs one small step.\n\n51\n00:02:19,800 --> 00:02:20,560\nNot four.\n\n52\n00:02:21,620 --> 00:02:23,140\nAnd that is our topic today.\n\n53\n00:02:24,240 --> 00:02:25,420\nSo here is our plan.\n\n54\n00:02:26,380 --> 00:02:29,160\nIn part one, we talk about our own mornings.\n\n55\n00:02:30,040 --> 00:02:32,680\nOur small steps, one by one.\n\n56\n00:02:33,700 --> 00:02:36,980\nIn part two, we ask why small steps work.\n\n57\n00:02:37,980 --> 00:02:39,920\nAnd I have a true story for that.\n\n58\n00:02:41,040 --> 00:02:44,580\nIn part three, we share simple ideas for tomorrow morning.\n\n59\n00:02:45,540 --> 00:02:46,359\nDoes that sound good?\n\n60\n00:02:47,360 --> 00:02:48,360\nThat sounds good, Ben.\n\n61\n00:02:49,040 --> 00:02:53,040\nAnd to everyone listening, please do not worry if you miss something.\n\n62\n00:02:53,580 --> 00:02:54,500\nYou will hear it again.\n\n63\n00:02:55,140 --> 00:02:59,380\nYou can listen while you walk, while you cook, or while you drink your tea.\n\n64\n00:03:00,320 --> 00:03:00,900\nOkay, Ben.\n\n65\n00:03:01,200 --> 00:03:01,700\nPart one.\n\n66\n00:03:02,300 --> 00:03:04,420\nTell me about your first small step.\n\n67\n00:03:05,160 --> 00:03:07,120\nWhat is the very first thing you do?\n\n68\n00:03:08,120 --> 00:03:09,120\nOkay, part one.\n\n69\n00:03:10,080 --> 00:03:13,000\nMy first small step is a glass of water.\n\n70\n00:03:14,020 --> 00:03:14,600\nThat is all.\n\n71\n00:03:15,620 --> 00:03:19,150\nBefore coffee, before my phone, before anything.\n\n72\n00:03:20,380 --> 00:03:22,180\nI put the glass next to my bed at night.\n\n73\n00:03:23,020 --> 00:03:25,310\nSo in the morning, the water is right there.\n\n74\n00:03:26,440 --> 00:03:27,660\nI do not need to think.\n\n75\n00:03:28,500 --> 00:03:29,740\nI do not need to choose.\n\n76\n00:03:30,460 --> 00:03:31,300\nI just drink.\n\n77\n00:03:32,300 --> 00:03:33,660\nIt takes twenty seconds.\n\n78\n00:03:34,800 --> 00:03:35,920\nWhat is your first step, Mia?\n\n79\n00:03:36,780 --> 00:03:39,360\nMy first step is smaller than water, Ben.\n\n80\n00:03:40,140 --> 00:03:42,860\nWhen I open my eyes, I do not look at my phone.\n\n81\n00:03:43,280 --> 00:03:44,320\nThat is the first thing.\n\n82\n00:03:44,960 --> 00:03:47,580\nThe phone stays on the table.\n\n83\n00:03:47,640 --> 00:03:48,380\nThen I sit up.\n\n84\n00:03:49,100 --> 00:03:51,200\nI put my feet on the floor.\n\n85\n00:03:51,440 --> 00:03:53,060\nI feel the floor.\n\n86\n00:03:53,100 --> 00:03:55,940\nIt is cold, but it is real.\n\n87\n00:03:56,000 --> 00:03:58,880\nI stay there for ten seconds, and I breathe.\n\n88\n00:03:59,780 --> 00:04:00,980\nThat is my first step.\n\n89\n00:04:02,640 --> 00:04:03,980\nFeet on the floor, Mia.\n\n90\n00:04:04,120 --> 00:04:04,650\nI love that.\n\n91\n00:04:05,420 --> 00:04:06,610\nIt is so small.\n\n92\n00:04:07,120 --> 00:04:12,140\nBut it tells your body, \"The day starts now.\" Do you feel different after those ten seconds?\n\n93\n00:04:13,320 --> 00:04:14,040\nA little, Ben.\n\n94\n00:04:14,600 --> 00:04:15,300\nOnly a little.\n\n95\n00:04:15,740 --> 00:04:16,400\nNot a lot.\n\n96\n00:04:16,940 --> 00:04:18,450\nBut a little is enough to start.\n\n97\n00:04:19,760 --> 00:04:22,200\nI think we wait for a big feeling in the morning.\n\n98\n00:04:22,700 --> 00:04:24,640\nWe wait to feel ready.\n\n99\n00:04:24,680 --> 00:04:26,920\nWe wait to feel strong.\n\n100\n00:04:26,940 --> 00:04:28,860\nBut the big feeling does not come.\n\n101\n00:04:29,700 --> 00:04:32,400\nIn the morning, we have only small feelings.\n\n102\n00:04:32,940 --> 00:04:34,340\nA little sleepy.\n\n103\n00:04:34,420 --> 00:04:35,920\nA little slow.\n\n104\n00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:36,860\nA little worried.\n\n105\n00:04:38,280 --> 00:04:39,740\nOnly small feelings, Mia.\n\n106\n00:04:39,980 --> 00:04:40,740\nThat is true.\n\n107\n00:04:41,479 --> 00:04:43,980\nSo we need small steps, not big ones.\n\n108\n00:04:44,740 --> 00:04:47,540\nA small step is something we can do when we feel small.\n\n109\n00:04:48,340 --> 00:04:48,860\nSitting up.\n\n110\n00:04:49,700 --> 00:04:50,400\nDrinking water.\n\n111\n00:04:51,320 --> 00:04:52,120\nOpening the window.\n\n112\n00:04:53,260 --> 00:04:54,260\nWhat is your second step?\n\n113\n00:04:54,800 --> 00:04:55,380\nAfter the floor?\n\n114\n00:04:56,800 --> 00:04:59,720\nAfter the floor, my second step is my bed, Ben.\n\n115\n00:05:00,420 --> 00:05:01,820\nI make my bed.\n\n116\n00:05:01,900 --> 00:05:03,180\nI pull the blanket flat.\n\n117\n00:05:03,700 --> 00:05:05,800\nI put the pillow in the corner.\n\n118\n00:05:05,820 --> 00:05:07,940\nIt takes two minutes, maybe less.\n\n119\n00:05:08,580 --> 00:05:09,600\nAnd then I look at it.\n\n120\n00:05:10,180 --> 00:05:10,860\nIt is neat.\n\n121\n00:05:11,420 --> 00:05:12,080\nIt is done.\n\n122\n00:05:12,820 --> 00:05:14,880\nThis is the first finished thing of my day.\n\n123\n00:05:15,520 --> 00:05:16,460\nNobody sees it.\n\n124\n00:05:16,840 --> 00:05:19,640\nNobody says \"good job.\" But I see it.\n\n125\n00:05:21,100 --> 00:05:26,010\n\"Nobody says good job, but I see it.\" That is a beautiful sentence, Mia.\n\n126\n00:05:26,730 --> 00:05:28,900\nAnd that small feeling stays with you, right?\n\n127\n00:05:29,140 --> 00:05:30,220\nIt comes with you to work?\n\n128\n00:05:31,520 --> 00:05:32,080\nYes, Ben.\n\n129\n00:05:32,520 --> 00:05:33,300\nIt comes with me.\n\n130\n00:05:34,060 --> 00:05:36,120\nI feel like a person who finishes things.\n\n131\n00:05:36,980 --> 00:05:38,260\nAnd what is your second step?\n\n132\n00:05:38,720 --> 00:05:39,440\nAfter the water?\n\n133\n00:05:41,100 --> 00:05:42,800\nMy second step is the window, Mia.\n\n134\n00:05:43,600 --> 00:05:46,440\nI open the window, and I look outside for one minute.\n\n135\n00:05:47,120 --> 00:05:47,980\nI look at the sky.\n\n136\n00:05:48,520 --> 00:05:49,480\nI look at the trees.\n\n137\n00:05:50,300 --> 00:05:51,400\nSometimes it is raining.\n\n138\n00:05:52,200 --> 00:05:53,660\nSometimes a bird is on the roof.\n\n139\n00:05:54,540 --> 00:05:55,800\nI do not do anything.\n\n140\n00:05:56,280 --> 00:05:56,840\nI just look.\n\n141\n00:05:57,720 --> 00:05:59,760\nThis one minute changes my morning.\n\n142\n00:06:00,680 --> 00:06:02,520\nMy mind stops thinking about work.\n\n143\n00:06:03,240 --> 00:06:04,380\nIt thinks about the sky.\n\n144\n00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:08,080\nA mind that thinks about the sky, Ben.\n\n145\n00:06:08,690 --> 00:06:10,100\nThat is a calm mind.\n\n146\n00:06:11,080 --> 00:06:11,840\nAnd then?\n\n147\n00:06:11,900 --> 00:06:13,560\nDo you close the window and start the day?\n\n148\n00:06:15,020 --> 00:06:17,160\nYes, Mia, but I have one more step.\n\n149\n00:06:17,680 --> 00:06:18,420\nA third step.\n\n150\n00:06:19,209 --> 00:06:19,820\nMy notebook.\n\n151\n00:06:20,770 --> 00:06:22,900\nI keep a small notebook on the kitchen table.\n\n152\n00:06:23,880 --> 00:06:26,160\nEvery morning, I write one line.\n\n153\n00:06:26,220 --> 00:06:27,660\nOnly one.\n\n154\n00:06:27,700 --> 00:06:28,920\nToday's one thing.\n\n155\n00:06:29,880 --> 00:06:37,840\nFor example, \"Call the doctor.\" Or, \"Study English for ten minutes.\" One line, not a list.\n\n156\n00:06:38,560 --> 00:06:39,970\nA list makes me feel tired.\n\n157\n00:06:40,960 --> 00:06:43,440\nOne line makes me feel clear.\n\n158\n00:06:44,260 --> 00:06:45,840\nOne line, not a list.\n\n159\n00:06:46,260 --> 00:06:48,020\nBen, I want to try that.\n\n160\n00:06:48,080 --> 00:06:49,860\nBecause my problem is the list.\n\n161\n00:06:50,470 --> 00:06:51,960\nMy list has ten things.\n\n162\n00:06:52,500 --> 00:06:55,500\nI finish three, and I feel like I failed.\n\n163\n00:06:55,520 --> 00:06:59,300\nBut if my list has one thing, and I finish it, I win.\n\n164\n00:07:00,240 --> 00:07:01,140\nYou win, Mia.\n\n165\n00:07:01,220 --> 00:07:02,360\nThat is exactly it.\n\n166\n00:07:03,040 --> 00:07:04,500\nSo what is your third step?\n\n167\n00:07:04,580 --> 00:07:05,700\nAfter the bed?\n\n168\n00:07:05,800 --> 00:07:07,840\nMy third step is tea, Ben.\n\n169\n00:07:08,520 --> 00:07:10,360\nI put water in the kettle, and I wait.\n\n170\n00:07:10,980 --> 00:07:12,600\nThis waiting is important.\n\n171\n00:07:13,180 --> 00:07:15,000\nI do not look at my phone while I wait.\n\n172\n00:07:15,600 --> 00:07:17,900\nI stand in the kitchen and listen to the water.\n\n173\n00:07:18,640 --> 00:07:21,320\nIt is a small sound, and it grows.\n\n174\n00:07:22,140 --> 00:07:24,800\nWhen the tea is ready, I hold the cup with two hands.\n\n175\n00:07:25,300 --> 00:07:26,020\nIt is warm.\n\n176\n00:07:26,780 --> 00:07:28,400\nI drink slowly.\n\n177\n00:07:28,460 --> 00:07:29,560\nFive minutes.\n\n178\n00:07:30,240 --> 00:07:31,440\nNobody talks to me.\n\n179\n00:07:32,400 --> 00:07:34,460\nAnd then I feel ready for people.\n\n180\n00:07:35,290 --> 00:07:38,560\n\"Then I feel ready for people.\" I understand that, Mia.\n\n181\n00:07:39,100 --> 00:07:41,040\nFive quiet minutes before the world.\n\n182\n00:07:41,920 --> 00:07:45,660\nSo your morning is feet, bed, tea.\n\n183\n00:07:45,720 --> 00:07:46,820\nThree small steps.\n\n184\n00:07:47,580 --> 00:07:51,120\nAnd mine is water, window, one line.\n\n185\n00:07:51,740 --> 00:07:52,340\nAlso three.\n\n186\n00:07:53,220 --> 00:07:54,660\nHow long do your three steps take?\n\n187\n00:07:54,820 --> 00:07:55,280\nAll together?\n\n188\n00:07:55,940 --> 00:07:57,140\nMaybe ten minutes, Ben.\n\n189\n00:07:57,720 --> 00:07:59,620\nFeet on the floor, ten seconds.\n\n190\n00:08:00,060 --> 00:08:01,380\nBed, two minutes.\n\n191\n00:08:01,800 --> 00:08:03,240\nTea, five minutes.\n\n192\n00:08:03,760 --> 00:08:05,300\nTen minutes for the whole morning.\n\n193\n00:08:05,780 --> 00:08:07,540\nAnd then I feel like the day has started.\n\n194\n00:08:08,700 --> 00:08:10,100\nWhat about your worst morning?\n\n195\n00:08:10,620 --> 00:08:12,880\nDo you have small steps on a bad day too?\n\n196\n00:08:13,700 --> 00:08:15,560\nMy worst morning, Mia.\n\n197\n00:08:15,900 --> 00:08:17,300\nLet me tell you.\n\n198\n00:08:17,380 --> 00:08:19,560\nLast winter, I had a big project at work.\n\n199\n00:08:20,340 --> 00:08:22,140\nEvery morning, I woke up with fear.\n\n200\n00:08:22,900 --> 00:08:25,340\nI lay in bed and thought about the whole project.\n\n201\n00:08:25,920 --> 00:08:26,540\nEvery part.\n\n202\n00:08:27,140 --> 00:08:27,960\nEvery problem.\n\n203\n00:08:28,740 --> 00:08:29,720\nMy heart went fast.\n\n204\n00:08:30,360 --> 00:08:31,140\nI did not get up.\n\n205\n00:08:31,760 --> 00:08:34,000\nI stayed in bed for one hour, thinking.\n\n206\n00:08:34,660 --> 00:08:37,380\nAnd after that hour, I felt worse, not better.\n\n207\n00:08:38,060 --> 00:08:39,500\nWorse, not better.\n\n208\n00:08:40,180 --> 00:08:41,660\nBen, I know that hour.\n\n209\n00:08:42,280 --> 00:08:44,510\nThinking about the whole thing does not help.\n\n210\n00:08:44,920 --> 00:08:46,860\nIt makes the thing bigger.\n\n211\n00:08:46,900 --> 00:08:47,600\nWhat did you do?\n\n212\n00:08:48,720 --> 00:08:51,460\nOne morning, I gave up on the big plan, Mia.\n\n213\n00:08:51,520 --> 00:08:52,560\nI said, \"Okay.\n\n214\n00:08:52,680 --> 00:08:53,820\nI cannot do the project.\n\n215\n00:08:54,310 --> 00:08:57,220\nBut I can open my laptop.\" So I got up.\n\n216\n00:08:57,720 --> 00:08:58,620\nI drank my water.\n\n217\n00:08:59,060 --> 00:08:59,960\nI opened the window.\n\n218\n00:09:00,440 --> 00:09:01,500\nI opened my laptop.\n\n219\n00:09:02,160 --> 00:09:03,260\nThat was my only goal.\n\n220\n00:09:03,800 --> 00:09:04,900\nOpen the laptop.\n\n221\n00:09:05,720 --> 00:09:10,220\nAnd then I was sitting there, and the laptop was open, so I wrote one sentence.\n\n222\n00:09:10,940 --> 00:09:11,460\nThen another.\n\n223\n00:09:12,380 --> 00:09:14,380\nAfter one hour, I had one page.\n\n224\n00:09:15,320 --> 00:09:16,540\nOne page, Ben.\n\n225\n00:09:16,980 --> 00:09:18,340\nNot a good page, maybe.\n\n226\n00:09:18,680 --> 00:09:19,660\nBut a page.\n\n227\n00:09:20,300 --> 00:09:22,440\nAnd the project was a little smaller after that?\n\n228\n00:09:23,220 --> 00:09:24,220\nA little smaller, Mia.\n\n229\n00:09:24,480 --> 00:09:25,460\nYes.\n\n230\n00:09:25,540 --> 00:09:27,109\nEvery morning, a little smaller.\n\n231\n00:09:27,940 --> 00:09:30,980\nAfter ten mornings, it was small enough to hold.\n\n232\n00:09:31,080 --> 00:09:32,870\nAfter twenty mornings, it was finished.\n\n233\n00:09:33,660 --> 00:09:36,820\nAnd I did not feel brave on any of those mornings.\n\n234\n00:09:36,980 --> 00:09:40,080\nI just did one small step each morning.\n\n235\n00:09:40,090 --> 00:09:41,600\n\"Brave is not needed.\n\n236\n00:09:42,080 --> 00:09:45,840\nSmall is needed.\" Ben, I want to put that on my wall.\n\n237\n00:09:46,980 --> 00:09:48,440\nOkay, so that was part one.\n\n238\n00:09:48,940 --> 00:09:49,640\nOur mornings.\n\n239\n00:09:50,040 --> 00:09:56,540\nFeet on the floor, water, a bed, a window, tea, one line in a notebook.\n\n240\n00:09:57,080 --> 00:09:58,460\nSix small steps.\n\n241\n00:09:59,120 --> 00:10:00,100\nNow, part two.\n\n242\n00:10:00,820 --> 00:10:02,260\nWhy do small steps work?\n\n243\n00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:04,660\nBen, you said you have a true story.\n\n244\n00:10:05,400 --> 00:10:06,060\nI do, Mia.\n\n245\n00:10:07,340 --> 00:10:08,180\nOkay, part two.\n\n246\n00:10:08,760 --> 00:10:10,000\nThis is a true story.\n\n247\n00:10:10,900 --> 00:10:15,020\nIn 2014, a man named William McRaven gave a speech.\n\n248\n00:10:15,640 --> 00:10:17,830\nHe was an admiral in the United States Navy.\n\n249\n00:10:18,640 --> 00:10:20,980\nHe spoke to students at the University of Texas.\n\n250\n00:10:21,580 --> 00:10:24,180\nIt was their last day at university.\n\n251\n00:10:24,220 --> 00:10:26,390\nMany people thought he would talk about big things.\n\n252\n00:10:26,980 --> 00:10:27,280\nWar.\n\n253\n00:10:27,760 --> 00:10:28,270\nLeadership.\n\n254\n00:10:28,620 --> 00:10:29,110\nThe future.\n\n255\n00:10:29,900 --> 00:10:31,880\nBut he started with something very small.\n\n256\n00:10:32,580 --> 00:10:34,360\nSomething very small?\n\n257\n00:10:34,400 --> 00:10:35,220\nWhat did he say, Ben?\n\n258\n00:10:35,880 --> 00:10:41,700\nHe said, \"If you want to change the world, start by making your bed.\" That was his first idea, Mia.\n\n259\n00:10:42,120 --> 00:10:43,360\nTo thousands of students.\n\n260\n00:10:44,080 --> 00:10:44,860\nMake your bed.\n\n261\n00:10:45,520 --> 00:10:46,380\nMake your bed.\n\n262\n00:10:46,920 --> 00:10:50,739\nBen, that is such a small thing to say to thousands of students.\n\n263\n00:10:50,780 --> 00:10:52,180\nWhy did he say that?\n\n264\n00:10:52,220 --> 00:10:53,700\nHe explained why, Mia.\n\n265\n00:10:53,780 --> 00:10:56,930\nIn the Navy, every morning, the instructors checked the beds.\n\n266\n00:10:57,430 --> 00:10:59,059\nThe blanket had to be flat.\n\n267\n00:10:59,100 --> 00:11:01,380\nThe pillow had to be in the right place.\n\n268\n00:11:01,440 --> 00:11:02,920\nIt was a simple task.\n\n269\n00:11:03,020 --> 00:11:04,900\nSome people thought it was silly.\n\n270\n00:11:04,920 --> 00:11:10,120\nBut McRaven said, when you make your bed, you finish the first task of the day.\n\n271\n00:11:10,180 --> 00:11:12,340\nIt gives a small feeling of pride.\n\n272\n00:11:12,360 --> 00:11:14,630\nAnd that small feeling helps with the next task.\n\n273\n00:11:15,030 --> 00:11:15,600\nAnd the next.\n\n274\n00:11:16,120 --> 00:11:16,760\nAnd the next.\n\n275\n00:11:17,540 --> 00:11:19,840\nOne small task becomes many tasks.\n\n276\n00:11:20,260 --> 00:11:21,340\nI love that idea, Ben.\n\n277\n00:11:21,900 --> 00:11:23,300\nAnd I think he said one more thing.\n\n278\n00:11:23,980 --> 00:11:26,940\nIf we have a bad day, we come home to a bed that is made.\n\n279\n00:11:27,480 --> 00:11:28,580\nA bed that we made.\n\n280\n00:11:29,180 --> 00:11:32,980\nAnd that small thing says, \"Tomorrow will be better.\" Is that right?\n\n281\n00:11:33,640 --> 00:11:34,600\nThat is right, Mia.\n\n282\n00:11:35,150 --> 00:11:36,800\nThat speech became a small book.\n\n283\n00:11:37,500 --> 00:11:38,780\nMillions of people read it.\n\n284\n00:11:39,600 --> 00:11:42,340\nWhy do you think so many people read a book about making a bed?\n\n285\n00:11:43,040 --> 00:11:44,920\nBecause it is not a big idea, Ben.\n\n286\n00:11:45,380 --> 00:11:46,540\nIt is a small idea.\n\n287\n00:11:47,140 --> 00:11:48,940\nEveryone can make a bed.\n\n288\n00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:51,560\nNobody can change the world before breakfast.\n\n289\n00:11:51,600 --> 00:11:53,600\nBut everyone can make a bed.\n\n290\n00:11:53,760 --> 00:11:55,280\nSmall steps are for everyone.\n\n291\n00:11:55,720 --> 00:11:56,800\nFor the tired person.\n\n292\n00:11:57,160 --> 00:11:58,140\nFor the busy person.\n\n293\n00:11:58,660 --> 00:12:00,540\nFor the person who feels sad in the morning.\n\n294\n00:12:01,180 --> 00:12:03,760\n\"Small steps are for everyone.\" Yes, Mia.\n\n295\n00:12:04,420 --> 00:12:06,960\nNobody needs energy for a small step.\n\n296\n00:12:07,020 --> 00:12:08,620\nNobody needs a plan.\n\n297\n00:12:08,680 --> 00:12:11,780\nWe need two minutes and one small action.\n\n298\n00:12:11,820 --> 00:12:14,000\nAnd there is one more reason small steps work.\n\n299\n00:12:14,690 --> 00:12:17,500\nThe first step decides the shape of the morning.\n\n300\n00:12:17,529 --> 00:12:19,020\nThe shape of the morning?\n\n301\n00:12:19,060 --> 00:12:19,800\nWhat do you mean, Ben?\n\n302\n00:12:20,520 --> 00:12:23,800\nMcRaven said the first task matters because it is the first, Mia.\n\n303\n00:12:24,280 --> 00:12:25,480\nNot because it is important.\n\n304\n00:12:26,140 --> 00:12:27,560\nMaking a bed is not important.\n\n305\n00:12:28,100 --> 00:12:30,520\nNobody's life changes because of a flat blanket.\n\n306\n00:12:31,100 --> 00:12:34,820\nBut if the first task is finished, the second task is easier to start.\n\n307\n00:12:35,680 --> 00:12:40,140\nIf the first task is a phone and thirty minutes of scrolling, the second task is harder.\n\n308\n00:12:40,929 --> 00:12:42,560\nThe first step sets the direction.\n\n309\n00:12:43,340 --> 00:12:45,580\nThe first step sets the direction, Ben.\n\n310\n00:12:46,280 --> 00:12:55,560\nSo the question is not, \"How do I have a great day?\" The question is, \"What is my first step?\" And the answer should be very small.\n\n311\n00:12:56,120 --> 00:12:57,190\nSmaller than we think.\n\n312\n00:12:57,880 --> 00:12:58,840\nNot exercise.\n\n313\n00:12:59,259 --> 00:13:00,700\nNot one hour of study.\n\n314\n00:13:01,640 --> 00:13:02,540\nFeet on the floor.\n\n315\n00:13:03,400 --> 00:13:03,780\nWater.\n\n316\n00:13:04,660 --> 00:13:05,130\nWindow.\n\n317\n00:13:06,220 --> 00:13:06,500\nBed.\n\n318\n00:13:07,620 --> 00:13:08,270\nJust one, Mia.\n\n319\n00:13:08,650 --> 00:13:13,320\nAnd the rest of the morning follows that first step, like water follows the first drop.\n\n320\n00:13:14,480 --> 00:13:15,329\nCan I ask you something?\n\n321\n00:13:15,969 --> 00:13:18,630\nWhat do you do when the small step does not work?\n\n322\n00:13:19,220 --> 00:13:21,700\nWhen you make your bed, and you still feel bad?\n\n323\n00:13:23,080 --> 00:13:24,160\nThat happens, Ben.\n\n324\n00:13:24,820 --> 00:13:27,760\nSome mornings, I do my steps, and I still feel bad.\n\n325\n00:13:28,300 --> 00:13:32,350\nMy bed is made, my tea is warm, and my heart is still heavy.\n\n326\n00:13:33,210 --> 00:13:36,200\nAnd on those mornings, I try to remember one thing.\n\n327\n00:13:36,930 --> 00:13:39,160\nThe step is not for the feeling.\n\n328\n00:13:39,200 --> 00:13:41,580\nThe step is for the day.\n\n329\n00:13:41,590 --> 00:13:47,400\n\"The step is for the day, not for the feeling.\" Mia, that is a beautiful answer.\n\n330\n00:13:47,520 --> 00:13:50,170\nSo even if you feel bad, the day has started?\n\n331\n00:13:51,100 --> 00:13:51,740\nYes, Ben.\n\n332\n00:13:52,500 --> 00:13:54,730\nEven if I feel bad, my bed is made.\n\n333\n00:13:55,240 --> 00:13:56,740\nMy body has water.\n\n334\n00:13:56,780 --> 00:13:58,780\nMy day has started.\n\n335\n00:13:58,790 --> 00:14:01,700\nThe good feeling comes later, or it does not come.\n\n336\n00:14:02,320 --> 00:14:03,680\nBut the day starts anyway.\n\n337\n00:14:04,260 --> 00:14:05,160\nThat is enough.\n\n338\n00:14:06,200 --> 00:14:07,100\nThat is enough.\n\n339\n00:14:07,760 --> 00:14:10,700\nI think many people stop because they wait for the feeling, Mia.\n\n340\n00:14:11,370 --> 00:14:20,220\nThey do the small step one time, they do not feel great, and they say, \"It does not work.\" But the small step is not magic.\n\n341\n00:14:20,280 --> 00:14:21,620\nIt is a start.\n\n342\n00:14:21,680 --> 00:14:23,280\nThe morning does not need to be good.\n\n343\n00:14:23,960 --> 00:14:25,840\nIt needs to begin.\n\n344\n00:14:25,870 --> 00:14:29,580\n\"It needs to begin.\" Ben, I want to stay with that word.\n\n345\n00:14:30,130 --> 00:14:30,600\nStart.\n\n346\n00:14:31,290 --> 00:14:33,319\nWe think a start must be big.\n\n347\n00:14:33,400 --> 00:14:34,480\nA new year.\n\n348\n00:14:34,490 --> 00:14:35,520\nA new job.\n\n349\n00:14:35,580 --> 00:14:36,150\nA Monday.\n\n350\n00:14:36,980 --> 00:14:39,190\nBut a start is just the first small step.\n\n351\n00:14:39,880 --> 00:14:40,980\nI can start at six.\n\n352\n00:14:41,360 --> 00:14:42,280\nI can start at ten.\n\n353\n00:14:42,920 --> 00:14:44,540\nI can start again after lunch.\n\n354\n00:14:45,360 --> 00:14:45,880\nDo you agree?\n\n355\n00:14:46,400 --> 00:14:47,680\nCan a morning start late?\n\n356\n00:14:49,080 --> 00:14:49,800\nIt can, Mia.\n\n357\n00:14:50,460 --> 00:14:51,980\nLast week, I woke up late.\n\n358\n00:14:52,560 --> 00:14:53,560\nNo time for water.\n\n359\n00:14:54,060 --> 00:14:54,620\nNo window.\n\n360\n00:14:55,240 --> 00:14:56,220\nI ran out of the door.\n\n361\n00:14:57,100 --> 00:14:59,920\nIn the past, a morning like that broke my whole day.\n\n362\n00:15:00,720 --> 00:15:04,140\nBut at ten o'clock, at my desk, I drank a glass of water.\n\n363\n00:15:04,880 --> 00:15:06,100\nThat was my small step.\n\n364\n00:15:06,700 --> 00:15:07,760\nLate, but real.\n\n365\n00:15:08,360 --> 00:15:09,420\nThe morning was gone.\n\n366\n00:15:10,010 --> 00:15:11,220\nThe day was not.\n\n367\n00:15:12,320 --> 00:15:16,800\n\"The morning was gone, but the day was not.\" That is important, Ben.\n\n368\n00:15:17,480 --> 00:15:19,240\nSmall steps do not have a deadline.\n\n369\n00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:22,940\nIf the first step is late, it is still the first step.\n\n370\n00:15:23,860 --> 00:15:25,340\nOkay, so that was part two.\n\n371\n00:15:25,900 --> 00:15:27,600\nWhy small steps work.\n\n372\n00:15:28,300 --> 00:15:29,980\nWe talked about McRaven and the bed.\n\n373\n00:15:30,620 --> 00:15:32,650\nWe said the first step sets the direction.\n\n374\n00:15:33,400 --> 00:15:34,880\nNow, part three.\n\n375\n00:15:34,960 --> 00:15:37,260\nSimple ideas for tomorrow morning.\n\n376\n00:15:37,280 --> 00:15:38,290\nBen, you go first.\n\n377\n00:15:39,240 --> 00:15:40,740\nOkay, part three.\n\n378\n00:15:40,780 --> 00:15:42,900\nMy first idea is about the evening, Mia.\n\n379\n00:15:43,570 --> 00:15:46,160\nA good morning starts the night before.\n\n380\n00:15:46,200 --> 00:15:48,210\nBefore bed, I fill my glass of water.\n\n381\n00:15:48,920 --> 00:15:50,840\nI put my clothes for tomorrow on the chair.\n\n382\n00:15:51,580 --> 00:15:53,440\nI open my notebook to a clean page.\n\n383\n00:15:54,260 --> 00:15:56,839\nI put my phone on the table, not next to my pillow.\n\n384\n00:15:57,580 --> 00:15:58,520\nFour small things.\n\n385\n00:15:59,000 --> 00:15:59,660\nFive minutes.\n\n386\n00:16:00,540 --> 00:16:04,200\nAnd in the morning, my first steps are easy, because I prepared them.\n\n387\n00:16:05,820 --> 00:16:08,339\nThe phone on the table, not next to the pillow.\n\n388\n00:16:08,990 --> 00:16:11,440\nBen, that one is hard for me.\n\n389\n00:16:11,480 --> 00:16:13,020\nBut I do the same with my cup.\n\n390\n00:16:13,440 --> 00:16:14,740\nI wash it at night.\n\n391\n00:16:14,800 --> 00:16:17,529\nIn the morning, it is there, clean, waiting.\n\n392\n00:16:18,720 --> 00:16:20,480\nMy first idea is about the key.\n\n393\n00:16:21,200 --> 00:16:24,740\nMy whole morning can break because of a lost key.\n\n394\n00:16:24,800 --> 00:16:27,640\nSo now my key lives in one place.\n\n395\n00:16:27,700 --> 00:16:29,500\nA small bowl next to the door.\n\n396\n00:16:30,360 --> 00:16:32,669\nEvery evening, the key goes in the bowl.\n\n397\n00:16:34,040 --> 00:16:36,820\nThe answer to a lost key is a bowl.\n\n398\n00:16:36,960 --> 00:16:38,320\nI love how simple that is, Mia.\n\n399\n00:16:39,140 --> 00:16:41,520\nWe look for big answers to small problems.\n\n400\n00:16:42,100 --> 00:16:43,520\nBut the answer is a bowl.\n\n401\n00:16:44,620 --> 00:16:46,660\nMy second idea is the one line.\n\n402\n00:16:47,380 --> 00:16:49,980\nTomorrow morning, write one line in a notebook.\n\n403\n00:16:50,760 --> 00:16:51,840\nToday's one thing.\n\n404\n00:16:52,600 --> 00:16:53,160\nNot a list.\n\n405\n00:16:53,860 --> 00:16:54,680\nOne line.\n\n406\n00:16:55,460 --> 00:16:59,120\nWhen you finish that one thing, your day is a good day.\n\n407\n00:16:59,160 --> 00:17:00,420\nEverything else is extra.\n\n408\n00:17:01,160 --> 00:17:02,780\nEverything else is extra, Ben.\n\n409\n00:17:03,280 --> 00:17:03,880\nI like that.\n\n410\n00:17:04,960 --> 00:17:07,160\nMy second idea is for the afternoon.\n\n411\n00:17:07,800 --> 00:17:10,180\nBecause small steps are not only for the morning.\n\n412\n00:17:11,000 --> 00:17:13,280\nAt two o'clock, my eyes are heavy.\n\n413\n00:17:13,940 --> 00:17:17,260\nI want to open my phone and look at nothing for thirty minutes.\n\n414\n00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:19,160\nSo I stand up.\n\n415\n00:17:19,920 --> 00:17:21,120\nI walk to the window.\n\n416\n00:17:21,840 --> 00:17:23,580\nI look outside for one minute.\n\n417\n00:17:24,140 --> 00:17:27,320\nThen I come back, and I can work again for one hour.\n\n418\n00:17:28,120 --> 00:17:29,720\nOne minute at the window, Mia.\n\n419\n00:17:30,200 --> 00:17:31,260\nThat works for me too.\n\n420\n00:17:32,100 --> 00:17:34,800\nAnd in the afternoon, my step is water again.\n\n421\n00:17:35,840 --> 00:17:37,600\nI know, I talk about water a lot.\n\n422\n00:17:38,320 --> 00:17:40,840\nBut when I feel slow, I am often just thirsty.\n\n423\n00:17:41,700 --> 00:17:43,640\nSmall problems have small answers.\n\n424\n00:17:44,420 --> 00:17:46,540\nWe do not need a new life.\n\n425\n00:17:46,580 --> 00:17:49,240\nWe need a glass of water and one minute at the window.\n\n426\n00:17:50,129 --> 00:17:52,540\nSmall problems have small answers.\n\n427\n00:17:53,300 --> 00:17:54,560\nDo you have a third idea, Ben?\n\n428\n00:17:55,100 --> 00:17:57,240\nSomething for our listeners who are learning English?\n\n429\n00:17:58,120 --> 00:17:59,040\nYes, Mia.\n\n430\n00:17:59,080 --> 00:18:00,940\nMy third idea is ten minutes.\n\n431\n00:18:01,820 --> 00:18:03,820\nLearning a language is a very big thing.\n\n432\n00:18:04,380 --> 00:18:05,240\nIt takes years.\n\n433\n00:18:06,080 --> 00:18:08,140\nNobody can learn English in one morning.\n\n434\n00:18:09,020 --> 00:18:10,740\nAnd that is why many people stop.\n\n435\n00:18:11,470 --> 00:18:13,879\nThey look at the whole mountain, and they feel small.\n\n436\n00:18:14,820 --> 00:18:17,100\nBut English is also made of small steps.\n\n437\n00:18:18,020 --> 00:18:18,540\nOne word.\n\n438\n00:18:19,160 --> 00:18:19,940\nOne sentence.\n\n439\n00:18:20,720 --> 00:18:22,900\nTen minutes in the morning with a cup of coffee.\n\n440\n00:18:23,760 --> 00:18:25,539\nThat is how I learned Spanish, actually.\n\n441\n00:18:26,240 --> 00:18:27,560\nTen minutes with coffee?\n\n442\n00:18:28,220 --> 00:18:29,030\nNot a class, Ben?\n\n443\n00:18:29,960 --> 00:18:30,840\nNot a class, Mia.\n\n444\n00:18:31,160 --> 00:18:32,320\nTen minutes with my coffee.\n\n445\n00:18:32,900 --> 00:18:34,340\nI listened to one short thing.\n\n446\n00:18:35,060 --> 00:18:38,020\nI said the sentences out loud, quietly, in my kitchen.\n\n447\n00:18:38,880 --> 00:18:40,700\nMy neighbor probably thought I was strange.\n\n448\n00:18:41,680 --> 00:18:43,740\nBut after one year, I could talk to people.\n\n449\n00:18:44,180 --> 00:18:44,899\nNot perfectly.\n\n450\n00:18:45,200 --> 00:18:45,880\nBut I could talk.\n\n451\n00:18:46,680 --> 00:18:48,960\nAnd it started with ten minutes and a cup of coffee.\n\n452\n00:18:49,660 --> 00:18:51,380\nA small step, every morning.\n\n453\n00:18:52,380 --> 00:18:55,179\nA small step, every morning.\n\n454\n00:18:55,300 --> 00:18:58,980\nSo for everyone listening now, this podcast can be your small step.\n\n455\n00:18:59,780 --> 00:19:01,140\nTen minutes, or five.\n\n456\n00:19:01,860 --> 00:19:02,640\nListen a little.\n\n457\n00:19:03,240 --> 00:19:04,820\nSay one sentence out loud.\n\n458\n00:19:05,400 --> 00:19:07,240\nThen close the video and go to your day.\n\n459\n00:19:07,900 --> 00:19:09,300\nTomorrow, listen again.\n\n460\n00:19:10,150 --> 00:19:12,060\nWe are not asking for one hour.\n\n461\n00:19:12,700 --> 00:19:14,529\nWe are asking for a small step.\n\n462\n00:19:15,520 --> 00:19:16,480\nA small step, Mia.\n\n463\n00:19:17,490 --> 00:19:18,780\nAnd I have one last idea.\n\n464\n00:19:19,600 --> 00:19:22,840\nIt is for the people who feel bad about their mornings.\n\n465\n00:19:22,920 --> 00:19:24,160\nBecause I was that person.\n\n466\n00:19:24,960 --> 00:19:26,620\nI thought I was lazy.\n\n467\n00:19:26,780 --> 00:19:30,040\nI thought other people had energy, and I did not.\n\n468\n00:19:30,110 --> 00:19:34,940\nI watched videos of people who wake up at five and run and cook and read.\n\n469\n00:19:34,960 --> 00:19:36,920\nAnd I felt worse.\n\n470\n00:19:36,980 --> 00:19:38,430\nAnd now, Ben?\n\n471\n00:19:38,440 --> 00:19:39,220\nWhat do you think now?\n\n472\n00:19:40,280 --> 00:19:42,680\nNow I think those videos were not about me, Mia.\n\n473\n00:19:43,360 --> 00:19:44,400\nMy morning is different.\n\n474\n00:19:45,100 --> 00:19:46,440\nMy small steps are different.\n\n475\n00:19:46,740 --> 00:19:47,480\nAnd that is fine.\n\n476\n00:19:48,460 --> 00:19:50,700\nThe goal is not a perfect morning.\n\n477\n00:19:50,760 --> 00:19:52,380\nThe goal is a morning that starts.\n\n478\n00:19:53,280 --> 00:19:55,640\nAnd I think that is our message today.\n\n479\n00:19:55,680 --> 00:19:57,820\nWe do not need a perfect morning.\n\n480\n00:19:57,840 --> 00:19:59,200\nWe need a morning that starts.\n\n481\n00:19:59,940 --> 00:20:02,020\nAnd a morning starts with one small step.\n\n482\n00:20:03,180 --> 00:20:04,360\nA morning that starts.\n\n483\n00:20:05,280 --> 00:20:10,380\nBen, let's repeat today's important words, slowly, because our listeners want to remember them.\n\n484\n00:20:11,640 --> 00:20:12,040\nSmall.\n\n485\n00:20:12,660 --> 00:20:13,360\nNot big.\n\n486\n00:20:14,320 --> 00:20:15,400\nA small step.\n\n487\n00:20:16,380 --> 00:20:17,480\nA small feeling.\n\n488\n00:20:18,460 --> 00:20:19,540\nA small problem.\n\n489\n00:20:20,700 --> 00:20:21,120\nStep.\n\n490\n00:20:21,820 --> 00:20:22,600\nOne movement.\n\n491\n00:20:23,120 --> 00:20:23,860\nOne action.\n\n492\n00:20:24,980 --> 00:20:26,000\nStep by step.\n\n493\n00:20:27,160 --> 00:20:27,560\nMorning.\n\n494\n00:20:27,980 --> 00:20:29,100\nThe start of the day.\n\n495\n00:20:29,920 --> 00:20:30,700\nEvery morning.\n\n496\n00:20:31,640 --> 00:20:32,280\nThis morning.\n\n497\n00:20:33,640 --> 00:20:34,500\nStart.\n\n498\n00:20:34,510 --> 00:20:35,160\nTo begin.\n\n499\n00:20:36,060 --> 00:20:36,960\nThe day starts.\n\n500\n00:20:37,760 --> 00:20:38,690\nWe start small.\n\n501\n00:20:39,940 --> 00:20:40,209\nFeel.\n\n502\n00:20:40,920 --> 00:20:42,280\nTo have a feeling inside.\n\n503\n00:20:43,200 --> 00:20:44,100\nI feel tired.\n\n504\n00:20:44,880 --> 00:20:45,580\nI feel ready.\n\n505\n00:20:46,420 --> 00:20:47,360\nI feel a little better.\n\n506\n00:20:48,840 --> 00:20:49,160\nSmall.\n\n507\n00:20:50,220 --> 00:20:50,520\nStep.\n\n508\n00:20:51,640 --> 00:20:52,110\nMorning.\n\n509\n00:20:53,240 --> 00:20:53,580\nStart.\n\n510\n00:20:55,020 --> 00:20:55,340\nFeel.\n\n511\n00:20:56,660 --> 00:20:57,720\nFive small words.\n\n512\n00:20:58,280 --> 00:20:59,580\nAnd one small idea.\n\n513\n00:21:00,500 --> 00:21:02,580\nA morning starts with one small step.\n\n514\n00:21:03,600 --> 00:21:06,040\nNow, Mia, do you have a question for our listeners?\n\n515\n00:21:07,220 --> 00:21:07,780\nYes, Ben.\n\n516\n00:21:08,360 --> 00:21:10,320\nEveryone listening, here is my question.\n\n517\n00:21:10,960 --> 00:21:13,060\nWhat is your first small step tomorrow morning?\n\n518\n00:21:13,720 --> 00:21:14,920\nNot a plan.\n\n519\n00:21:15,000 --> 00:21:15,640\nNot a list.\n\n520\n00:21:16,140 --> 00:21:17,100\nJust the first step.\n\n521\n00:21:17,860 --> 00:21:19,180\nFeet on the floor?\n\n522\n00:21:19,260 --> 00:21:20,070\nA glass of water?\n\n523\n00:21:20,820 --> 00:21:21,300\nThe window?\n\n524\n00:21:22,020 --> 00:21:22,400\nThe bed?\n\n525\n00:21:23,180 --> 00:21:25,580\nWrite it in the comments in one short sentence.\n\n526\n00:21:26,100 --> 00:21:32,720\nFor example, \"Tomorrow, I will drink a glass of water.\" One sentence is a small step in English.\n\n527\n00:21:34,060 --> 00:21:37,500\nAnd if this episode helped you, please subscribe to Two Cups English.\n\n528\n00:21:38,240 --> 00:21:40,300\nWe make a new episode every few days.\n\n529\n00:21:41,040 --> 00:21:45,940\nSlow English, simple words, and one true story each time.\n\n530\n00:21:45,950 --> 00:21:48,309\nThe full text of this episode is in the description.\n\n531\n00:21:49,200 --> 00:21:50,820\nReading while listening helps a lot.\n\n532\n00:21:51,420 --> 00:21:52,380\nIt is free.\n\n533\n00:21:52,480 --> 00:21:52,590\nTake it.\n\n534\n00:21:53,540 --> 00:21:55,339\nThank you for spending this time with us.\n\n535\n00:21:55,999 --> 00:21:57,419\nMaybe you listened while cooking.\n\n536\n00:21:58,139 --> 00:21:59,139\nMaybe while walking.\n\n537\n00:21:59,919 --> 00:22:02,780\nMaybe in bed, in the dark, before sleep.\n\n538\n00:22:03,820 --> 00:22:06,439\nWherever you are, we are happy you are here.\n\n539\n00:22:07,079 --> 00:22:09,539\nAnd remember, nobody needs a big morning.\n\n540\n00:22:10,039 --> 00:22:11,400\nWe need a small step.\n\n541\n00:22:12,279 --> 00:22:12,899\nThis is Ben.\n\n542\n00:22:13,559 --> 00:22:14,279\nAnd this is Mia.\n\n543\n00:22:14,920 --> 00:22:16,419\nThis was Two Cups English.\n\n544\n00:22:17,039 --> 00:22:18,080\nStart small tomorrow.\n\n545\n00:22:18,679 --> 00:22:19,820\nWe will see you next time."},{"code":"CUP02","file":"CUP02.srt","srt":"1\n00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,280\nHello everyone, and welcome to Two Cups English.\n\n2\n00:00:03,840 --> 00:00:06,720\nI'm Mia, and Ben is here with me.\n\n3\n00:00:06,780 --> 00:00:09,560\nThis podcast is for people who are learning English.\n\n4\n00:00:10,220 --> 00:00:11,060\nWe speak slowly.\n\n5\n00:00:11,620 --> 00:00:12,980\nWe use simple words.\n\n6\n00:00:13,560 --> 00:00:16,400\nAnd we repeat the important words many times.\n\n7\n00:00:17,360 --> 00:00:19,920\nToday, our topic is slow days.\n\n8\n00:00:20,840 --> 00:00:23,580\nA slow day is a day with no plan.\n\n9\n00:00:23,660 --> 00:00:24,830\nA day with nothing.\n\n10\n00:00:25,900 --> 00:00:27,250\nBen, how was your Sunday?\n\n11\n00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:28,820\nHi Mia.\n\n12\n00:00:28,840 --> 00:00:29,340\nHi everyone.\n\n13\n00:00:30,280 --> 00:00:32,200\nMy Sunday was very slow.\n\n14\n00:00:33,100 --> 00:00:34,680\nI stayed in bed until ten.\n\n15\n00:00:35,440 --> 00:00:36,320\nI did not cook.\n\n16\n00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:37,880\nI did not clean.\n\n17\n00:00:38,780 --> 00:00:41,160\nI just looked at the window and drank my coffee.\n\n18\n00:00:42,260 --> 00:00:44,540\nFor the first hour, I felt great.\n\n19\n00:00:45,600 --> 00:00:52,680\nBut then a small voice said, \"Ben, you are wasting the day.\" Do you know that voice, Mia?\n\n20\n00:00:53,660 --> 00:00:56,020\nOh, I know that voice very well, Ben.\n\n21\n00:00:56,520 --> 00:00:58,640\nIt talks to me every weekend.\n\n22\n00:00:58,660 --> 00:01:00,320\nIt says, \"Do something.\n\n23\n00:01:00,580 --> 00:01:01,320\nBe useful.\n\n24\n00:01:01,840 --> 00:01:04,860\nOther people are busy.\" And I feel bad.\n\n25\n00:01:05,400 --> 00:01:07,039\nOn a free day, I feel bad.\n\n26\n00:01:07,720 --> 00:01:08,700\nThat is strange, right?\n\n27\n00:01:09,360 --> 00:01:11,720\nWe work all week, and then we cannot rest.\n\n28\n00:01:12,580 --> 00:01:14,520\nYes, that is very strange, Mia.\n\n29\n00:01:15,680 --> 00:01:17,760\nSo what do you do when the voice comes?\n\n30\n00:01:18,580 --> 00:01:19,540\nDo you get up and work?\n\n31\n00:01:20,140 --> 00:01:21,240\nOr do you stay in bed?\n\n32\n00:01:22,180 --> 00:01:23,680\nSometimes I get up.\n\n33\n00:01:23,760 --> 00:01:26,440\nI do many small things, and I feel tired at night.\n\n34\n00:01:27,080 --> 00:01:28,240\nBut sometimes I stay.\n\n35\n00:01:28,910 --> 00:01:31,180\nAnd that is the day I want to talk about today.\n\n36\n00:01:31,800 --> 00:01:32,540\nA slow day.\n\n37\n00:01:32,960 --> 00:01:33,940\nA day with nothing.\n\n38\n00:01:34,460 --> 00:01:35,980\nAnd why it is okay.\n\n39\n00:01:36,940 --> 00:01:37,620\nI like that, Mia.\n\n40\n00:01:38,210 --> 00:01:42,680\n\"It is okay.\" I think many people need to hear those words.\n\n41\n00:01:43,680 --> 00:01:45,180\nSo here is our plan for today.\n\n42\n00:01:46,140 --> 00:01:49,280\nIn part one, we talk about our own slow days.\n\n43\n00:01:50,060 --> 00:01:51,900\nWhat we do, and what we feel.\n\n44\n00:01:53,080 --> 00:01:56,400\nIn part two, we ask why a slow day is okay.\n\n45\n00:01:57,340 --> 00:01:58,920\nAnd I have a true story for that.\n\n46\n00:02:00,220 --> 00:02:03,720\nIn part three, we share simple ideas for a slow day.\n\n47\n00:02:04,440 --> 00:02:05,240\nDoes that sound good?\n\n48\n00:02:06,120 --> 00:02:07,080\nThat sounds good, Ben.\n\n49\n00:02:07,600 --> 00:02:11,070\nAnd to everyone listening, please do not worry if you miss something.\n\n50\n00:02:11,620 --> 00:02:13,040\nYou will hear it again.\n\n51\n00:02:13,100 --> 00:02:17,800\nYou can listen while you cook, while you walk, or while you rest.\n\n52\n00:02:17,820 --> 00:02:19,400\nYes, you can rest and listen.\n\n53\n00:02:19,780 --> 00:02:20,500\nThat is allowed.\n\n54\n00:02:21,380 --> 00:02:21,920\nOkay, Ben.\n\n55\n00:02:22,160 --> 00:02:22,640\nPart one.\n\n56\n00:02:23,200 --> 00:02:24,400\nTell me about your slow day.\n\n57\n00:02:24,840 --> 00:02:25,740\nStart from the morning.\n\n58\n00:02:26,760 --> 00:02:27,880\nOkay, part one.\n\n59\n00:02:28,820 --> 00:02:30,170\nMy slow day starts late.\n\n60\n00:02:31,180 --> 00:02:32,340\nI do not set an alarm.\n\n61\n00:02:33,220 --> 00:02:34,900\nI wake up when my body wakes up.\n\n62\n00:02:35,880 --> 00:02:37,680\nLast Sunday, that was nine thirty.\n\n63\n00:02:38,660 --> 00:02:39,840\nI stayed in bed for a while.\n\n64\n00:02:40,540 --> 00:02:41,780\nI did not look at my phone.\n\n65\n00:02:42,640 --> 00:02:45,020\nI looked at the light on the wall.\n\n66\n00:02:45,140 --> 00:02:47,060\nThen I got up, and I made coffee.\n\n67\n00:02:48,180 --> 00:02:48,620\nSlowly.\n\n68\n00:02:49,489 --> 00:02:50,329\nI did not hurry.\n\n69\n00:02:51,240 --> 00:02:53,000\nAnd I sat by the window with my cup.\n\n70\n00:02:54,080 --> 00:02:55,040\nI love that, Ben.\n\n71\n00:02:55,710 --> 00:02:59,780\n\"I wake up when my body wakes up.\" That is a beautiful sentence.\n\n72\n00:03:00,600 --> 00:03:02,680\nMy slow day is a little different.\n\n73\n00:03:02,780 --> 00:03:05,400\nI wake up early, even on a slow day.\n\n74\n00:03:05,440 --> 00:03:07,160\nMy body is used to it.\n\n75\n00:03:07,180 --> 00:03:08,280\nBut I do not get up.\n\n76\n00:03:08,780 --> 00:03:09,900\nI stay under the blanket.\n\n77\n00:03:10,440 --> 00:03:11,940\nI listen to the sounds outside.\n\n78\n00:03:12,620 --> 00:03:13,180\nBirds.\n\n79\n00:03:13,460 --> 00:03:14,020\nA car.\n\n80\n00:03:14,640 --> 00:03:15,840\nRain, if I am lucky.\n\n81\n00:03:16,420 --> 00:03:17,900\nI love rain on a slow day.\n\n82\n00:03:19,500 --> 00:03:21,440\nRain is perfect for a slow day, Mia.\n\n83\n00:03:21,900 --> 00:03:24,459\nNobody says, \"Why are you inside?\" when it rains.\n\n84\n00:03:25,080 --> 00:03:26,020\nSo you stay in bed.\n\n85\n00:03:26,600 --> 00:03:27,390\nWhat comes next?\n\n86\n00:03:27,860 --> 00:03:28,720\nDo you eat breakfast?\n\n87\n00:03:29,320 --> 00:03:31,740\nYes, Ben, but a slow breakfast.\n\n88\n00:03:32,200 --> 00:03:33,899\nNot toast standing in the kitchen.\n\n89\n00:03:34,700 --> 00:03:35,400\nI sit down.\n\n90\n00:03:35,860 --> 00:03:36,680\nI make tea.\n\n91\n00:03:37,240 --> 00:03:40,000\nI put butter on the bread, and I eat slowly.\n\n92\n00:03:40,820 --> 00:03:41,920\nSometimes I read a little.\n\n93\n00:03:42,640 --> 00:03:43,800\nSometimes I read nothing.\n\n94\n00:03:44,380 --> 00:03:45,520\nI just look at my cup.\n\n95\n00:03:46,440 --> 00:03:47,920\nTwenty minutes for breakfast.\n\n96\n00:03:48,660 --> 00:03:50,719\nOn a work day, it is three minutes.\n\n97\n00:03:51,300 --> 00:03:53,000\nThree minutes on a work day.\n\n98\n00:03:53,080 --> 00:03:54,480\nTwenty minutes on a slow day.\n\n99\n00:03:55,060 --> 00:03:56,460\nThat is a big difference, Mia.\n\n100\n00:03:57,090 --> 00:03:58,940\nAnd I think the difference is not the food.\n\n101\n00:03:59,480 --> 00:04:00,820\nThe food is the same.\n\n102\n00:04:00,860 --> 00:04:02,520\nBread and tea.\n\n103\n00:04:02,530 --> 00:04:04,640\nThe difference is the time.\n\n104\n00:04:04,720 --> 00:04:07,580\nOn a slow day, we give time to small things.\n\n105\n00:04:08,220 --> 00:04:09,160\nExactly, Ben.\n\n106\n00:04:09,740 --> 00:04:11,470\nWe give time to small things.\n\n107\n00:04:12,700 --> 00:04:16,740\nSo after your coffee by the window, what do you do?\n\n108\n00:04:16,820 --> 00:04:17,960\nOr do you do nothing?\n\n109\n00:04:19,060 --> 00:04:20,160\nMostly nothing, Mia.\n\n110\n00:04:20,570 --> 00:04:21,800\nAnd that is hard for me.\n\n111\n00:04:22,520 --> 00:04:24,600\nAfter coffee, I feel the voice coming.\n\n112\n00:04:25,220 --> 00:04:29,100\n\"Ben, do something.\" So I do one small thing.\n\n113\n00:04:29,720 --> 00:04:30,700\nI water the plants.\n\n114\n00:04:31,260 --> 00:04:32,420\nIt takes five minutes.\n\n115\n00:04:33,020 --> 00:04:34,080\nThen I sit again.\n\n116\n00:04:34,740 --> 00:04:35,940\nMaybe I play some music.\n\n117\n00:04:36,420 --> 00:04:37,040\nOld songs.\n\n118\n00:04:37,560 --> 00:04:39,020\nAnd I listen to the whole song.\n\n119\n00:04:39,590 --> 00:04:40,500\nNot while I work.\n\n120\n00:04:41,020 --> 00:04:41,600\nJust listen.\n\n121\n00:04:42,220 --> 00:04:43,780\nListening to a whole song.\n\n122\n00:04:44,420 --> 00:04:46,320\nBen, that is so rare now.\n\n123\n00:04:47,080 --> 00:04:49,180\nI always play music while I do something else.\n\n124\n00:04:49,780 --> 00:04:50,340\nCooking.\n\n125\n00:04:50,660 --> 00:04:51,220\nCleaning.\n\n126\n00:04:51,640 --> 00:04:53,180\nWriting messages.\n\n127\n00:04:53,260 --> 00:04:54,620\nI never just listen.\n\n128\n00:04:55,500 --> 00:04:58,280\nOn my next slow day, I want to try that.\n\n129\n00:04:58,420 --> 00:05:00,290\nOne song, and nothing else.\n\n130\n00:05:00,840 --> 00:05:01,960\nTry it, Mia.\n\n131\n00:05:02,020 --> 00:05:03,260\nIt feels strange at first.\n\n132\n00:05:03,720 --> 00:05:05,030\nYour hands want to do something.\n\n133\n00:05:05,560 --> 00:05:08,200\nBut after one minute, you relax.\n\n134\n00:05:08,320 --> 00:05:09,880\nNow, what about the afternoon?\n\n135\n00:05:10,440 --> 00:05:13,400\nFor me, the afternoon is the hard part of a slow day.\n\n136\n00:05:13,920 --> 00:05:14,460\nWhat about you?\n\n137\n00:05:15,600 --> 00:05:17,660\nThe afternoon is hard for me too, Ben.\n\n138\n00:05:18,480 --> 00:05:20,680\nIn the morning, a slow day feels good.\n\n139\n00:05:21,360 --> 00:05:24,060\nBut at two o'clock, the voice gets loud.\n\n140\n00:05:24,790 --> 00:05:25,960\n\"Half the day is gone.\n\n141\n00:05:26,260 --> 00:05:29,130\nYou did nothing.\" So I do this.\n\n142\n00:05:29,740 --> 00:05:30,630\nI go for a walk.\n\n143\n00:05:31,420 --> 00:05:32,440\nNot a fast walk.\n\n144\n00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:34,060\nA slow walk.\n\n145\n00:05:34,840 --> 00:05:36,440\nI do not go anywhere.\n\n146\n00:05:36,560 --> 00:05:39,220\nI just walk around my street and come back.\n\n147\n00:05:39,320 --> 00:05:40,980\nA walk with no place to go.\n\n148\n00:05:42,080 --> 00:05:43,460\nI like that, Mia.\n\n149\n00:05:43,540 --> 00:05:44,740\nMost walks have a goal.\n\n150\n00:05:45,340 --> 00:05:45,820\nThe shop.\n\n151\n00:05:46,260 --> 00:05:46,700\nThe bus.\n\n152\n00:05:47,320 --> 00:05:47,580\nWork.\n\n153\n00:05:48,260 --> 00:05:49,940\nBut a slow walk has no goal.\n\n154\n00:05:50,480 --> 00:05:51,600\nThe walk is the goal.\n\n155\n00:05:52,420 --> 00:05:53,360\nHow long do you walk?\n\n156\n00:05:53,980 --> 00:05:55,280\nMaybe thirty minutes, Ben.\n\n157\n00:05:56,060 --> 00:05:57,400\nSometimes I sit on a bench.\n\n158\n00:05:58,040 --> 00:05:58,800\nI watch people.\n\n159\n00:05:59,520 --> 00:06:00,760\nA child with a dog.\n\n160\n00:06:01,140 --> 00:06:02,460\nAn old man with bread.\n\n161\n00:06:03,220 --> 00:06:04,140\nNobody knows me.\n\n162\n00:06:04,780 --> 00:06:05,720\nNobody needs me.\n\n163\n00:06:06,440 --> 00:06:07,459\nAnd that feels good.\n\n164\n00:06:08,280 --> 00:06:10,940\nThen I go home, and I feel a little lighter.\n\n165\n00:06:11,490 --> 00:06:15,780\n\"Nobody needs me, and that feels good.\" That is honest, Mia.\n\n166\n00:06:16,300 --> 00:06:17,640\nI think many people feel that.\n\n167\n00:06:18,320 --> 00:06:20,260\nAll week, everyone needs us.\n\n168\n00:06:20,820 --> 00:06:21,360\nOur boss.\n\n169\n00:06:21,940 --> 00:06:23,120\nOur family.\n\n170\n00:06:23,200 --> 00:06:24,640\nOur phone.\n\n171\n00:06:24,680 --> 00:06:27,640\nOn a slow day, nobody needs us for a few hours.\n\n172\n00:06:28,220 --> 00:06:29,440\nAnd that is not lonely.\n\n173\n00:06:30,040 --> 00:06:31,040\nThat is rest.\n\n174\n00:06:31,680 --> 00:06:32,300\nYes, Ben.\n\n175\n00:06:32,760 --> 00:06:33,860\nThat is rest.\n\n176\n00:06:34,800 --> 00:06:36,420\nAnd what is your afternoon like?\n\n177\n00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:37,910\nDo you also go out?\n\n178\n00:06:38,720 --> 00:06:39,700\nNot always, Mia.\n\n179\n00:06:39,940 --> 00:06:41,060\nSometimes I take a nap.\n\n180\n00:06:41,900 --> 00:06:42,820\nA real nap.\n\n181\n00:06:42,900 --> 00:06:44,820\nOn the sofa, with a blanket.\n\n182\n00:06:44,860 --> 00:06:46,840\nTwenty minutes, maybe thirty.\n\n183\n00:06:46,900 --> 00:06:50,220\nWhen I was younger, I thought naps were for children and old people.\n\n184\n00:06:50,800 --> 00:06:52,620\nNow I think naps are for tired people.\n\n185\n00:06:53,260 --> 00:06:55,220\nAnd I am a tired person on Sunday.\n\n186\n00:06:56,660 --> 00:06:58,820\nWe are all tired people on Sunday, Ben.\n\n187\n00:06:59,540 --> 00:07:02,420\nI think a nap is a very good slow day thing.\n\n188\n00:07:03,060 --> 00:07:04,600\nDo you feel bad after the nap?\n\n189\n00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:05,480\nOr good?\n\n190\n00:07:06,180 --> 00:07:07,600\nFor one minute, I feel bad.\n\n191\n00:07:08,040 --> 00:07:14,620\nI wake up and think, \"Oh no, I slept.\" But then I stand up, I drink water, and I feel new.\n\n192\n00:07:14,660 --> 00:07:16,729\nLike a small new morning in the afternoon.\n\n193\n00:07:17,400 --> 00:07:18,660\nSo the nap is good.\n\n194\n00:07:18,700 --> 00:07:19,840\nThe voice is wrong.\n\n195\n00:07:20,460 --> 00:07:22,040\nBen, can I ask something?\n\n196\n00:07:22,720 --> 00:07:23,580\nDo you live alone?\n\n197\n00:07:24,120 --> 00:07:26,020\nBecause a slow day alone is easy.\n\n198\n00:07:26,300 --> 00:07:28,620\nBut a slow day with other people is different.\n\n199\n00:07:29,120 --> 00:07:30,420\nGood question, Mia.\n\n200\n00:07:30,540 --> 00:07:31,460\nI live with my brother.\n\n201\n00:07:31,760 --> 00:07:33,240\nAnd yes, it is different.\n\n202\n00:07:33,920 --> 00:07:35,960\nOn my slow day, he wants to talk.\n\n203\n00:07:36,380 --> 00:07:37,300\nHe wants to go out.\n\n204\n00:07:37,820 --> 00:07:39,160\nHe wants to fix the bike.\n\n205\n00:07:40,080 --> 00:07:41,520\nAnd I say, \"Not today.\n\n206\n00:07:41,980 --> 00:07:45,400\nToday I rest.\" At first, he did not understand.\n\n207\n00:07:45,880 --> 00:07:46,540\nNow he knows.\n\n208\n00:07:47,100 --> 00:07:48,520\nSunday is my slow day.\n\n209\n00:07:48,790 --> 00:07:50,600\nAnd sometimes, he rests too.\n\n210\n00:07:51,390 --> 00:07:53,700\nWe sit in the same room and do nothing together.\n\n211\n00:07:54,420 --> 00:07:56,040\nDoing nothing together.\n\n212\n00:07:56,160 --> 00:07:57,220\nI like that a lot, Ben.\n\n213\n00:07:57,860 --> 00:07:59,660\nMy sister has two small children.\n\n214\n00:08:00,140 --> 00:08:02,480\nShe says a slow day is impossible for her.\n\n215\n00:08:02,870 --> 00:08:04,160\nThe children never stop.\n\n216\n00:08:04,840 --> 00:08:06,540\nBut last month, she tried something.\n\n217\n00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:09,840\nShe told the children, \"Today is a slow day.\n\n218\n00:08:09,880 --> 00:08:10,920\nWe stay home.\n\n219\n00:08:10,960 --> 00:08:14,080\nWe do nothing.\" And the children loved it.\n\n220\n00:08:14,140 --> 00:08:15,680\nThey built a house with blankets.\n\n221\n00:08:16,080 --> 00:08:18,480\nThey rested in the house all afternoon.\n\n222\n00:08:19,380 --> 00:08:20,160\nA blanket house.\n\n223\n00:08:20,560 --> 00:08:22,280\nThat is the perfect slow day, Mia.\n\n224\n00:08:22,960 --> 00:08:24,240\nChildren know how to rest.\n\n225\n00:08:24,630 --> 00:08:25,800\nThey do not feel guilty.\n\n226\n00:08:26,290 --> 00:08:27,560\nThey do not hear the voice.\n\n227\n00:08:27,960 --> 00:08:29,590\nThey just stop when they are tired.\n\n228\n00:08:30,390 --> 00:08:31,340\nWe can learn from them.\n\n229\n00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:33,460\nNothing is okay for a child.\n\n230\n00:08:33,980 --> 00:08:35,640\nSo nothing is okay for us.\n\n231\n00:08:36,320 --> 00:08:37,220\nThe voice is wrong.\n\n232\n00:08:37,960 --> 00:08:39,240\nI want to remember that, Ben.\n\n233\n00:08:39,980 --> 00:08:40,660\nAnd the evening?\n\n234\n00:08:41,220 --> 00:08:42,580\nHow does your slow day end?\n\n235\n00:08:43,160 --> 00:08:44,940\nMy slow day ends early, Mia.\n\n236\n00:08:45,600 --> 00:08:46,660\nI cook something simple.\n\n237\n00:08:47,260 --> 00:08:47,600\nEggs.\n\n238\n00:08:48,119 --> 00:08:48,460\nRice.\n\n239\n00:08:49,119 --> 00:08:49,420\nSoup.\n\n240\n00:08:50,220 --> 00:08:52,440\nI eat at the table, not in front of the computer.\n\n241\n00:08:53,140 --> 00:08:54,700\nThen I wash the dishes slowly.\n\n242\n00:08:55,240 --> 00:08:56,460\nThen I read a few pages.\n\n243\n00:08:56,960 --> 00:08:58,120\nAnd I go to bed at ten.\n\n244\n00:08:58,760 --> 00:08:59,640\nNo phone in bed.\n\n245\n00:09:00,280 --> 00:09:01,960\nAnd I sleep very well.\n\n246\n00:09:02,020 --> 00:09:03,280\nBetter than any other night.\n\n247\n00:09:03,840 --> 00:09:05,140\nThat is a good evening, Ben.\n\n248\n00:09:05,720 --> 00:09:06,760\nMy evening is similar.\n\n249\n00:09:07,460 --> 00:09:08,620\nBut I have one more thing.\n\n250\n00:09:09,240 --> 00:09:12,960\nBefore bed, I say to myself, \"Today I did nothing.\n\n251\n00:09:12,980 --> 00:09:15,980\nAnd that is okay.\" I say it out loud.\n\n252\n00:09:16,020 --> 00:09:17,240\nIt sounds silly.\n\n253\n00:09:17,260 --> 00:09:18,440\nBut it helps.\n\n254\n00:09:18,480 --> 00:09:19,840\nThe voice is quiet after that.\n\n255\n00:09:20,800 --> 00:09:22,100\nThat is not silly, Mia.\n\n256\n00:09:22,380 --> 00:09:23,100\nThat is smart.\n\n257\n00:09:23,540 --> 00:09:24,540\nYou answer the voice.\n\n258\n00:09:25,560 --> 00:09:27,020\nOkay, so that was part one.\n\n259\n00:09:27,380 --> 00:09:28,140\nOur slow days.\n\n260\n00:09:28,660 --> 00:09:33,260\nLate mornings, slow breakfast, a walk with no goal, a nap, a simple dinner.\n\n261\n00:09:33,730 --> 00:09:35,180\nAnd a small sentence before bed.\n\n262\n00:09:36,040 --> 00:09:37,039\nNow, part two.\n\n263\n00:09:37,860 --> 00:09:39,080\nWhy is a slow day okay?\n\n264\n00:09:39,820 --> 00:09:42,060\nMia, do you want to start, or should I tell my story?\n\n265\n00:09:42,820 --> 00:09:43,900\nTell your story, Ben.\n\n266\n00:09:44,320 --> 00:09:45,620\nYou said it is a true story.\n\n267\n00:09:46,160 --> 00:09:46,760\nI want to hear it.\n\n268\n00:09:47,620 --> 00:09:49,060\nOkay, part two.\n\n269\n00:09:49,120 --> 00:09:50,140\nThis is a true story.\n\n270\n00:09:50,940 --> 00:09:55,170\nIn 2019, a writer named Olga Mecking wrote an article for The New York Times.\n\n271\n00:09:55,800 --> 00:09:56,860\nShe lives in the Netherlands.\n\n272\n00:09:57,460 --> 00:09:59,180\nIn the Netherlands, people have a word.\n\n273\n00:09:59,700 --> 00:10:03,240\nThe word is \"niksen.\" Niksen means doing nothing.\n\n274\n00:10:03,800 --> 00:10:04,440\nNot reading.\n\n275\n00:10:04,900 --> 00:10:05,620\nNot watching.\n\n276\n00:10:06,120 --> 00:10:07,290\nNot thinking about work.\n\n277\n00:10:07,840 --> 00:10:08,760\nJust doing nothing.\n\n278\n00:10:09,380 --> 00:10:09,700\nSitting.\n\n279\n00:10:10,260 --> 00:10:11,220\nLooking out of the window.\n\n280\n00:10:12,080 --> 00:10:13,600\nA word for doing nothing?\n\n281\n00:10:14,040 --> 00:10:15,330\nBen, that is wonderful.\n\n282\n00:10:15,900 --> 00:10:17,680\nIn English, we do not have that word.\n\n283\n00:10:18,140 --> 00:10:21,340\nWe only have \"lazy.\" And lazy is a bad word.\n\n284\n00:10:22,160 --> 00:10:23,640\nYes, Mia, and that was her point.\n\n285\n00:10:24,360 --> 00:10:28,020\nOlga wrote that in many countries, doing nothing feels bad.\n\n286\n00:10:28,060 --> 00:10:29,460\nWe call it lazy.\n\n287\n00:10:29,500 --> 00:10:30,740\nWe call it wasting time.\n\n288\n00:10:31,450 --> 00:10:33,500\nBut in the Netherlands, niksen is normal.\n\n289\n00:10:34,080 --> 00:10:38,400\nPeople say, \"I am going to niksen for a while.\" And nobody feels bad.\n\n290\n00:10:39,180 --> 00:10:41,140\nThe article became very popular.\n\n291\n00:10:41,210 --> 00:10:42,110\nMany people read it.\n\n292\n00:10:42,900 --> 00:10:46,140\nLater, in 2021, she wrote a book with the same name.\n\n293\n00:10:46,800 --> 00:10:48,120\nNiksen.\n\n294\n00:10:48,280 --> 00:10:49,820\nMany people read it.\n\n295\n00:10:49,880 --> 00:10:56,700\nSo many people wanted to hear, \"It is okay to do nothing.\" Ben, why do you think the article was so popular?\n\n296\n00:10:57,840 --> 00:11:00,160\nI think because everyone is tired, Mia.\n\n297\n00:11:00,220 --> 00:11:00,960\nWe work.\n\n298\n00:11:00,980 --> 00:11:01,520\nWe study.\n\n299\n00:11:01,980 --> 00:11:03,000\nWe look at our phones.\n\n300\n00:11:03,620 --> 00:11:04,940\nEven our free time is busy.\n\n301\n00:11:05,600 --> 00:11:08,320\nWe watch a show, and we check messages at the same time.\n\n302\n00:11:09,040 --> 00:11:13,670\nSo when someone says, \"You can do nothing, and it is good for you,\" people feel relief.\n\n303\n00:11:14,240 --> 00:11:16,230\nLike a heavy bag coming off their shoulders.\n\n304\n00:11:17,220 --> 00:11:19,380\nA heavy bag coming off.\n\n305\n00:11:19,420 --> 00:11:20,320\nYes, Ben.\n\n306\n00:11:20,520 --> 00:11:21,860\nI felt that when you said the word.\n\n307\n00:11:22,420 --> 00:11:22,980\nNiksen.\n\n308\n00:11:23,800 --> 00:11:25,420\nBut let me ask a hard question.\n\n309\n00:11:26,080 --> 00:11:27,420\nIs it really good for us?\n\n310\n00:11:27,940 --> 00:11:29,480\nOr does it just feel good?\n\n311\n00:11:30,359 --> 00:11:31,160\nGood question, Mia.\n\n312\n00:11:31,800 --> 00:11:33,040\nOlga wrote about that too.\n\n313\n00:11:33,780 --> 00:11:36,100\nWhen we do nothing, our mind is not empty.\n\n314\n00:11:36,740 --> 00:11:37,880\nIt is working quietly.\n\n315\n00:11:38,580 --> 00:11:39,340\nIdeas come.\n\n316\n00:11:39,900 --> 00:11:41,000\nProblems get smaller.\n\n317\n00:11:41,720 --> 00:11:45,890\nMany people say, \"I had my best idea in the shower.\" Why?\n\n318\n00:11:46,580 --> 00:11:48,280\nBecause in the shower, we do nothing.\n\n319\n00:11:48,920 --> 00:11:51,060\nSo a slow day is not empty.\n\n320\n00:11:51,100 --> 00:11:53,250\nIt is a quiet room where ideas can come in.\n\n321\n00:11:54,149 --> 00:11:56,340\nA quiet room where ideas can come in.\n\n322\n00:11:57,000 --> 00:11:58,880\nBen, I think that is true.\n\n323\n00:11:58,920 --> 00:12:01,160\nMy best ideas never come at my desk.\n\n324\n00:12:01,630 --> 00:12:03,100\nThey come on my slow walk.\n\n325\n00:12:03,600 --> 00:12:04,480\nOr in the bath.\n\n326\n00:12:05,140 --> 00:12:06,740\nOr in bed, before sleep.\n\n327\n00:12:07,500 --> 00:12:09,380\nSo the slow day is doing something.\n\n328\n00:12:09,960 --> 00:12:11,640\nIt is just doing it quietly.\n\n329\n00:12:12,440 --> 00:12:13,240\nExactly, Mia.\n\n330\n00:12:13,810 --> 00:12:15,020\nAnd there is one more thing.\n\n331\n00:12:15,640 --> 00:12:17,420\nRest is not a reward.\n\n332\n00:12:18,160 --> 00:12:20,420\nMany people think, \"First I work hard.\n\n333\n00:12:20,960 --> 00:12:23,720\nThen I can rest.\" But rest is not a prize.\n\n334\n00:12:24,320 --> 00:12:27,300\nRest is a part of life, like food and water.\n\n335\n00:12:27,960 --> 00:12:32,980\nWe do not say, \"I worked hard, so now I can drink water.\" We just drink.\n\n336\n00:12:33,620 --> 00:12:34,800\nRest should be the same.\n\n337\n00:12:36,040 --> 00:12:40,020\n\"Rest is not a reward.\" Ben, I want to stop here for a moment.\n\n338\n00:12:40,500 --> 00:12:42,840\nBecause I think this is the big idea of today.\n\n339\n00:12:43,640 --> 00:12:45,420\nI always thought rest was a prize.\n\n340\n00:12:46,040 --> 00:12:47,680\nFinish the list, then rest.\n\n341\n00:12:48,460 --> 00:12:49,880\nBut the list never finishes.\n\n342\n00:12:50,200 --> 00:12:51,200\nSo I never rest.\n\n343\n00:12:51,949 --> 00:12:54,820\nAnd then Sunday comes, and I feel guilty.\n\n344\n00:12:54,920 --> 00:12:55,720\nYes, Mia.\n\n345\n00:12:55,760 --> 00:12:57,620\nThe list never finishes.\n\n346\n00:12:57,740 --> 00:12:58,640\nThat is the trap.\n\n347\n00:12:59,360 --> 00:13:02,350\nIf rest comes after the list, rest never comes.\n\n348\n00:13:03,040 --> 00:13:05,500\nSo we need to put rest inside the day.\n\n349\n00:13:06,080 --> 00:13:07,120\nNot after.\n\n350\n00:13:07,180 --> 00:13:08,300\nInside.\n\n351\n00:13:08,360 --> 00:13:10,420\nA slow day is a whole day of rest.\n\n352\n00:13:10,960 --> 00:13:13,800\nBut even a small slow hour is good.\n\n353\n00:13:13,810 --> 00:13:16,480\nDo you have small slow times in your week?\n\n354\n00:13:16,520 --> 00:13:17,730\nNot a whole day.\n\n355\n00:13:17,800 --> 00:13:19,770\nJust an hour?\n\n356\n00:13:19,780 --> 00:13:22,010\nBen, I want to say one more thing about permission.\n\n357\n00:13:22,780 --> 00:13:24,560\nSome slow days are not nice.\n\n358\n00:13:25,380 --> 00:13:27,040\nI rest, and I still feel bad.\n\n359\n00:13:27,840 --> 00:13:29,140\nThe voice does not stop.\n\n360\n00:13:29,940 --> 00:13:32,940\nI look at the ceiling, and I feel empty, not calm.\n\n361\n00:13:33,720 --> 00:13:35,140\nIs that slow day still okay?\n\n362\n00:13:36,220 --> 00:13:37,240\nYes, Mia.\n\n363\n00:13:37,320 --> 00:13:38,780\nThat slow day is still okay.\n\n364\n00:13:39,480 --> 00:13:41,180\nRest is not always a good feeling.\n\n365\n00:13:41,840 --> 00:13:43,640\nSometimes rest is just stopping.\n\n366\n00:13:44,300 --> 00:13:45,660\nYour body stops.\n\n367\n00:13:45,720 --> 00:13:47,100\nYour hands stop.\n\n368\n00:13:47,160 --> 00:13:49,880\nEven if your mind is loud, your body is resting.\n\n369\n00:13:50,590 --> 00:13:52,940\nAnd the next day, your body says thank you.\n\n370\n00:13:53,580 --> 00:13:54,700\nThe feeling comes later.\n\n371\n00:13:55,300 --> 00:13:56,240\nOr it does not come.\n\n372\n00:13:56,800 --> 00:13:57,980\nBut the rest is real.\n\n373\n00:13:59,100 --> 00:14:02,700\nThe rest is real, even when the feeling is not.\n\n374\n00:14:02,710 --> 00:14:03,600\nThat helps me, Ben.\n\n375\n00:14:04,140 --> 00:14:06,540\nBecause I always wait for the calm feeling.\n\n376\n00:14:06,580 --> 00:14:12,080\nAnd when it does not come, I think, \"I did it wrong.\" But there is no wrong way to rest.\n\n377\n00:14:12,680 --> 00:14:15,380\nA slow day is okay even when it is not perfect.\n\n378\n00:14:16,420 --> 00:14:17,840\nExactly, Mia.\n\n379\n00:14:17,880 --> 00:14:19,320\nThere is no wrong way to rest.\n\n380\n00:14:19,900 --> 00:14:21,900\nA perfect slow day is not the goal.\n\n381\n00:14:22,579 --> 00:14:23,840\nA slow day is the goal.\n\n382\n00:14:24,620 --> 00:14:25,720\nJust a day with less.\n\n383\n00:14:26,380 --> 00:14:27,080\nLess noise.\n\n384\n00:14:27,620 --> 00:14:28,200\nLess work.\n\n385\n00:14:28,740 --> 00:14:29,960\nLess list.\n\n386\n00:14:30,030 --> 00:14:30,720\nMore nothing.\n\n387\n00:14:31,290 --> 00:14:32,340\nAnd nothing is okay.\n\n388\n00:14:33,620 --> 00:14:34,300\nI think so, Ben.\n\n389\n00:14:35,300 --> 00:14:36,700\nWednesday evening.\n\n390\n00:14:36,860 --> 00:14:39,020\nI do not plan anything on Wednesday evening.\n\n391\n00:14:39,640 --> 00:14:40,260\nI come home.\n\n392\n00:14:40,780 --> 00:14:41,380\nI make tea.\n\n393\n00:14:42,040 --> 00:14:42,480\nI sit.\n\n394\n00:14:43,280 --> 00:14:44,460\nSometimes I call my mother.\n\n395\n00:14:45,260 --> 00:14:46,400\nSometimes I do nothing.\n\n396\n00:14:47,240 --> 00:14:49,560\nIt is a small slow day in the middle of the week.\n\n397\n00:14:50,280 --> 00:14:52,380\nAnd Thursday is always better because of it.\n\n398\n00:14:53,580 --> 00:14:55,240\nA small slow day on Wednesday.\n\n399\n00:14:55,940 --> 00:14:57,020\nI love that.\n\n400\n00:14:57,060 --> 00:14:58,620\nAnd it shows something.\n\n401\n00:14:58,680 --> 00:15:00,440\nA slow day is not about time.\n\n402\n00:15:01,040 --> 00:15:02,640\nIt is about permission.\n\n403\n00:15:02,680 --> 00:15:05,120\nYou give yourself permission to do nothing.\n\n404\n00:15:05,200 --> 00:15:06,760\nEven for one hour.\n\n405\n00:15:06,780 --> 00:15:08,410\nAnd that permission is the hard part.\n\n406\n00:15:09,180 --> 00:15:10,220\nPermission.\n\n407\n00:15:10,320 --> 00:15:10,980\nYes, Ben.\n\n408\n00:15:11,370 --> 00:15:13,360\nNobody gives us permission.\n\n409\n00:15:13,420 --> 00:15:21,860\nOur boss does not say, \"Please rest.\" Our phone does not say, \"Please stop.\" So we must say it to ourselves.\n\n410\n00:15:23,230 --> 00:15:24,140\n\"It is okay.\n\n411\n00:15:24,720 --> 00:15:29,490\nYou can have a slow day.\" Everyone listening, you can say it too.\n\n412\n00:15:30,400 --> 00:15:31,340\nIt is okay.\n\n413\n00:15:33,040 --> 00:15:34,140\nIt is okay.\n\n414\n00:15:35,400 --> 00:15:37,060\nOkay, so that was part two.\n\n415\n00:15:37,120 --> 00:15:39,440\nWhy a slow day is okay.\n\n416\n00:15:39,480 --> 00:15:42,570\nWe talked about niksen, the Dutch word for doing nothing.\n\n417\n00:15:43,380 --> 00:15:46,320\nWe said the mind works quietly when we rest.\n\n418\n00:15:46,380 --> 00:15:48,740\nAnd we said rest is not a reward.\n\n419\n00:15:48,780 --> 00:15:49,800\nIt is a part of life.\n\n420\n00:15:50,940 --> 00:15:52,360\nNow, part three.\n\n421\n00:15:52,440 --> 00:15:54,180\nSimple ideas for a slow day.\n\n422\n00:15:55,080 --> 00:15:56,700\nMia, you go first this time.\n\n423\n00:15:58,020 --> 00:15:59,040\nOkay, part three.\n\n424\n00:15:59,740 --> 00:16:02,180\nMy first idea is very simple.\n\n425\n00:16:02,240 --> 00:16:04,600\nPut your phone in another room.\n\n426\n00:16:04,680 --> 00:16:05,600\nNot off.\n\n427\n00:16:05,660 --> 00:16:06,960\nJust in another room.\n\n428\n00:16:07,800 --> 00:16:10,430\nOn a slow day, the phone is the biggest enemy.\n\n429\n00:16:11,020 --> 00:16:13,099\nIt brings the whole world into your bed.\n\n430\n00:16:13,740 --> 00:16:14,099\nWork.\n\n431\n00:16:14,560 --> 00:16:15,060\nNews.\n\n432\n00:16:15,380 --> 00:16:16,940\nOther people's busy lives.\n\n433\n00:16:17,640 --> 00:16:19,140\nSo the phone goes to the kitchen.\n\n434\n00:16:19,660 --> 00:16:20,860\nAnd I stay in the bedroom.\n\n435\n00:16:22,330 --> 00:16:23,810\nThe phone goes to the kitchen.\n\n436\n00:16:24,340 --> 00:16:25,460\nThat is a good rule, Mia.\n\n437\n00:16:26,620 --> 00:16:28,350\nMy first idea is about the morning.\n\n438\n00:16:29,240 --> 00:16:30,600\nDo not set an alarm.\n\n439\n00:16:31,380 --> 00:16:33,540\nLet your body decide.\n\n440\n00:16:33,580 --> 00:16:36,400\nOn a work day, the alarm is the boss.\n\n441\n00:16:36,480 --> 00:16:38,680\nOn a slow day, your body is the boss.\n\n442\n00:16:39,540 --> 00:16:42,440\nIf it wants ten hours of sleep, give it ten hours.\n\n443\n00:16:43,740 --> 00:16:44,820\nTen hours.\n\n444\n00:16:45,800 --> 00:16:47,340\nMy body would love that, Ben.\n\n445\n00:16:48,120 --> 00:16:49,860\nMy second idea is about food.\n\n446\n00:16:50,600 --> 00:16:51,680\nCook something slow.\n\n447\n00:16:52,220 --> 00:16:53,100\nNot fast food.\n\n448\n00:16:53,460 --> 00:16:54,500\nNot a quick sandwich.\n\n449\n00:16:55,020 --> 00:16:56,280\nSomething that takes time.\n\n450\n00:16:56,960 --> 00:16:57,300\nSoup.\n\n451\n00:16:57,820 --> 00:16:58,980\nRice with vegetables.\n\n452\n00:16:59,560 --> 00:17:01,100\nA cake, if you like baking.\n\n453\n00:17:01,820 --> 00:17:03,180\nThe cooking is the rest.\n\n454\n00:17:03,720 --> 00:17:07,120\nYou stir, you wait, you smell.\n\n455\n00:17:07,160 --> 00:17:09,700\nYour hands are busy, but your mind is quiet.\n\n456\n00:17:10,990 --> 00:17:16,940\n\"Your hands are busy, but your mind is quiet.\" Mia, that is exactly niksen.\n\n457\n00:17:18,020 --> 00:17:19,420\nMy second idea is smaller.\n\n458\n00:17:20,320 --> 00:17:22,040\nSit by a window for ten minutes.\n\n459\n00:17:22,840 --> 00:17:23,540\nJust sit.\n\n460\n00:17:24,220 --> 00:17:24,900\nDo not read.\n\n461\n00:17:25,320 --> 00:17:26,120\nDo not scroll.\n\n462\n00:17:26,820 --> 00:17:27,540\nLook outside.\n\n463\n00:17:28,340 --> 00:17:31,240\nWatch the clouds, or the street, or the rain.\n\n464\n00:17:32,360 --> 00:17:32,960\nTen minutes.\n\n465\n00:17:33,760 --> 00:17:34,900\nIt feels long at first.\n\n466\n00:17:35,580 --> 00:17:36,720\nThen it feels short.\n\n467\n00:17:38,260 --> 00:17:39,600\nI want to try that today, Ben.\n\n468\n00:17:40,040 --> 00:17:41,189\nTen minutes at the window.\n\n469\n00:17:42,080 --> 00:17:43,100\nDo you have a third idea?\n\n470\n00:17:44,520 --> 00:17:45,200\nYes, Mia.\n\n471\n00:17:45,260 --> 00:17:46,960\nMy third idea is about words.\n\n472\n00:17:47,820 --> 00:17:49,180\nChange the words you use.\n\n473\n00:17:50,100 --> 00:17:56,120\nDo not say, \"I did nothing today.\" Say, \"I rested today.\" Same day.\n\n474\n00:17:56,480 --> 00:17:57,200\nDifferent words.\n\n475\n00:17:58,080 --> 00:17:59,400\n\"Nothing\" sounds empty.\n\n476\n00:17:59,720 --> 00:18:01,700\n\"Rest\" sounds full.\n\n477\n00:18:02,620 --> 00:18:05,060\nAnd the voice inside us listens to our words.\n\n478\n00:18:06,520 --> 00:18:08,200\nSame day, different words.\n\n479\n00:18:08,640 --> 00:18:10,260\nThat is very true, Ben.\n\n480\n00:18:10,330 --> 00:18:11,740\nWords change how we feel.\n\n481\n00:18:12,720 --> 00:18:15,120\nMy third idea is about other people.\n\n482\n00:18:15,200 --> 00:18:18,990\nTell someone, \"Today is my slow day.\" Your family.\n\n483\n00:18:19,420 --> 00:18:20,040\nYour friend.\n\n484\n00:18:20,330 --> 00:18:21,000\nYour roommate.\n\n485\n00:18:21,730 --> 00:18:24,560\nThen they do not ask, \"What are you doing?\" They know.\n\n486\n00:18:25,160 --> 00:18:26,820\nAnd you do not need to explain.\n\n487\n00:18:27,780 --> 00:18:29,450\nAnd maybe they want a slow day too.\n\n488\n00:18:30,160 --> 00:18:31,520\nAnd I have a fourth idea, Mia.\n\n489\n00:18:32,180 --> 00:18:33,620\nDo not plan the slow day.\n\n490\n00:18:34,260 --> 00:18:35,020\nThis is important.\n\n491\n00:18:35,580 --> 00:18:37,180\nMany people make a plan for rest.\n\n492\n00:18:37,640 --> 00:18:38,780\n\"At ten, I read.\n\n493\n00:18:39,220 --> 00:18:40,400\nAt eleven, I walk.\n\n494\n00:18:40,880 --> 00:18:45,660\nAt twelve, I nap.\" But then the slow day becomes a work day with different tasks.\n\n495\n00:18:46,240 --> 00:18:46,940\nSo no plan.\n\n496\n00:18:47,440 --> 00:18:50,870\nWake up, and ask, \"What do I want now?\" And do that.\n\n497\n00:18:51,560 --> 00:18:52,370\nMaybe it is nothing.\n\n498\n00:18:52,960 --> 00:18:54,090\nThen nothing is the answer.\n\n499\n00:18:55,050 --> 00:18:58,900\n\"Then nothing is the answer.\" Ben, I do that every time.\n\n500\n00:18:59,460 --> 00:19:00,760\nI write a rest plan.\n\n501\n00:19:00,820 --> 00:19:01,600\nBath at ten.\n\n502\n00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:02,860\nBook at eleven.\n\n503\n00:19:03,480 --> 00:19:06,100\nAnd then I feel bad because I am behind on my rest.\n\n504\n00:19:06,970 --> 00:19:08,280\nThat is silly.\n\n505\n00:19:08,340 --> 00:19:09,060\nOkay.\n\n506\n00:19:09,120 --> 00:19:09,720\nNo plan.\n\n507\n00:19:10,120 --> 00:19:10,840\nJust the day.\n\n508\n00:19:11,640 --> 00:19:12,960\nMaybe they want a slow day too.\n\n509\n00:19:13,640 --> 00:19:14,880\nThen you can do nothing together.\n\n510\n00:19:15,540 --> 00:19:16,920\nThat is the best kind of nothing.\n\n511\n00:19:17,720 --> 00:19:19,320\nAnd I have one last idea, Mia.\n\n512\n00:19:19,980 --> 00:19:22,180\nIt is for people who cannot have a whole slow day.\n\n513\n00:19:22,940 --> 00:19:23,340\nParents.\n\n514\n00:19:23,900 --> 00:19:24,980\nPeople with two jobs.\n\n515\n00:19:25,440 --> 00:19:26,720\nStudents before an exam.\n\n516\n00:19:27,440 --> 00:19:29,820\nFor them, a slow day is not possible.\n\n517\n00:19:30,420 --> 00:19:32,200\nBut a slow ten minutes is possible.\n\n518\n00:19:32,820 --> 00:19:33,360\nYes, Ben.\n\n519\n00:19:33,780 --> 00:19:34,640\nThat is important.\n\n520\n00:19:35,280 --> 00:19:37,080\nNot everyone can have a slow Sunday.\n\n521\n00:19:37,700 --> 00:19:39,620\nBut everyone can have a slow ten minutes.\n\n522\n00:19:40,160 --> 00:19:41,660\nA cup of tea with no phone.\n\n523\n00:19:42,120 --> 00:19:42,820\nA short walk.\n\n524\n00:19:43,540 --> 00:19:45,860\nSitting in the car for a moment before going inside.\n\n525\n00:19:46,380 --> 00:19:48,600\nTen slow minutes inside a fast day.\n\n526\n00:19:49,170 --> 00:19:50,460\nThat is enough.\n\n527\n00:19:50,560 --> 00:19:51,180\nThat is enough.\n\n528\n00:19:51,870 --> 00:19:53,660\nAnd I think that is our message today, Mia.\n\n529\n00:19:54,300 --> 00:19:55,400\nA slow day is okay.\n\n530\n00:19:56,040 --> 00:19:57,160\nA slow hour is okay.\n\n531\n00:19:57,920 --> 00:19:59,320\nA slow ten minutes is okay.\n\n532\n00:20:00,180 --> 00:20:04,480\nThe voice will say, \"You are wasting time.\" And we can answer, \"No.\n\n533\n00:20:04,980 --> 00:20:05,600\nI am resting.\n\n534\n00:20:06,150 --> 00:20:07,080\nAnd rest is okay.\"\n\n535\n00:20:07,700 --> 00:20:08,560\nRest is okay.\n\n536\n00:20:09,320 --> 00:20:14,610\nBen, let's repeat today's important words, slowly, because our listeners want to remember them.\n\n537\n00:20:15,480 --> 00:20:16,360\nSlow.\n\n538\n00:20:16,420 --> 00:20:17,160\nNot fast.\n\n539\n00:20:17,900 --> 00:20:19,040\nA slow day.\n\n540\n00:20:19,140 --> 00:20:19,840\nA slow walk.\n\n541\n00:20:20,500 --> 00:20:21,600\nA slow breakfast.\n\n542\n00:20:22,460 --> 00:20:22,900\nRest.\n\n543\n00:20:23,500 --> 00:20:25,140\nTo stop and do nothing for a while.\n\n544\n00:20:25,820 --> 00:20:26,560\nI need rest.\n\n545\n00:20:27,200 --> 00:20:28,420\nRest is not a reward.\n\n546\n00:20:29,040 --> 00:20:29,320\nOkay.\n\n547\n00:20:29,540 --> 00:20:29,860\nFine.\n\n548\n00:20:30,060 --> 00:20:30,540\nGood enough.\n\n549\n00:20:31,080 --> 00:20:31,760\nIt is okay.\n\n550\n00:20:32,240 --> 00:20:33,000\nYou are okay.\n\n551\n00:20:33,740 --> 00:20:34,180\nNothing.\n\n552\n00:20:34,620 --> 00:20:35,460\nNot one thing.\n\n553\n00:20:36,200 --> 00:20:37,840\nI did nothing today.\n\n554\n00:20:37,900 --> 00:20:38,800\nA day with nothing.\n\n555\n00:20:39,580 --> 00:20:39,880\nDay.\n\n556\n00:20:40,270 --> 00:20:41,220\nTwenty-four hours.\n\n557\n00:20:41,760 --> 00:20:42,400\nA slow day.\n\n558\n00:20:42,820 --> 00:20:43,780\nA free day.\n\n559\n00:20:43,860 --> 00:20:44,430\nA good day.\n\n560\n00:20:45,340 --> 00:20:46,340\nSlow.\n\n561\n00:20:46,460 --> 00:20:46,820\nRest.\n\n562\n00:20:47,600 --> 00:20:47,900\nOkay.\n\n563\n00:20:48,560 --> 00:20:48,930\nNothing.\n\n564\n00:20:49,720 --> 00:20:49,980\nDay.\n\n565\n00:20:50,920 --> 00:20:52,520\nFive small words.\n\n566\n00:20:52,550 --> 00:20:53,660\nAnd one big idea.\n\n567\n00:20:54,540 --> 00:20:55,920\nIt is okay to have a slow day.\n\n568\n00:20:56,740 --> 00:20:59,160\nNow, Mia, do you have a question for our listeners?\n\n569\n00:20:59,780 --> 00:21:00,360\nYes, Ben.\n\n570\n00:21:00,980 --> 00:21:02,900\nEveryone listening, here is my question.\n\n571\n00:21:03,600 --> 00:21:04,840\nWhat is your slow day thing?\n\n572\n00:21:05,400 --> 00:21:05,880\nA nap?\n\n573\n00:21:06,220 --> 00:21:06,730\nA walk?\n\n574\n00:21:07,300 --> 00:21:08,620\nTea by the window?\n\n575\n00:21:08,660 --> 00:21:10,069\nMusic, and nothing else?\n\n576\n00:21:10,870 --> 00:21:13,160\nWrite it in the comments in one short sentence.\n\n577\n00:21:13,620 --> 00:21:18,790\nFor example: \"On my slow day, I take a nap.\" One sentence is enough.\n\n578\n00:21:19,280 --> 00:21:21,620\nReading your sentences is our slow day thing.\n\n579\n00:21:22,160 --> 00:21:26,460\nAnd if this episode helped you, please subscribe to Two Cups English.\n\n580\n00:21:26,520 --> 00:21:28,939\nWe make a new episode every few days.\n\n581\n00:21:29,020 --> 00:21:33,520\nSlow English, simple words, and one true story each time.\n\n582\n00:21:33,580 --> 00:21:36,450\nThe full text of this episode is in the description.\n\n583\n00:21:36,500 --> 00:21:38,520\nReading while listening helps a lot.\n\n584\n00:21:38,560 --> 00:21:39,580\nIt is free.\n\n585\n00:21:39,620 --> 00:21:39,880\nTake it.\n\n586\n00:21:40,660 --> 00:21:43,020\nThank you for spending this slow time with us.\n\n587\n00:21:43,539 --> 00:21:44,759\nMaybe you listened in bed.\n\n588\n00:21:45,219 --> 00:21:46,119\nMaybe on a walk.\n\n589\n00:21:46,719 --> 00:21:47,999\nMaybe with a cup of tea.\n\n590\n00:21:48,820 --> 00:21:51,279\nWherever you are, we are happy you are here.\n\n591\n00:21:51,599 --> 00:21:54,939\nAnd remember, it is okay to have a slow day.\n\n592\n00:21:55,499 --> 00:21:56,059\nThis is Ben.\n\n593\n00:21:56,480 --> 00:21:57,280\nAnd this is Mia.\n\n594\n00:21:57,780 --> 00:21:59,359\nThis was Two Cups English.\n\n595\n00:21:59,940 --> 00:22:00,479\nRest well.\n\n596\n00:22:00,940 --> 00:22:02,179\nWe will see you next time."},{"code":"CUP03","file":"CUP03.srt","srt":"1\n00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,240\nHello everyone, and welcome to Two Cups English.\n\n2\n00:00:03,840 --> 00:00:06,699\nI'm Mia, and Ben is here with me.\n\n3\n00:00:06,780 --> 00:00:09,580\nThis podcast is for people who are learning English.\n\n4\n00:00:10,220 --> 00:00:11,080\nWe speak slowly.\n\n5\n00:00:11,620 --> 00:00:12,980\nWe use simple words.\n\n6\n00:00:13,560 --> 00:00:16,419\nAnd we repeat the important words many times.\n\n7\n00:00:17,300 --> 00:00:20,260\nToday, our topic is the perfect moment.\n\n8\n00:00:20,900 --> 00:00:22,940\nThe moment when everything is ready.\n\n9\n00:00:23,920 --> 00:00:26,020\nBen, are you waiting for something right now?\n\n10\n00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:27,480\nHi Mia.\n\n11\n00:00:27,920 --> 00:00:28,440\nHi everyone.\n\n12\n00:00:29,230 --> 00:00:31,740\nYes, I am always waiting for something.\n\n13\n00:00:32,580 --> 00:00:34,740\nRight now, I am waiting to fix my bike.\n\n14\n00:00:36,080 --> 00:00:41,580\nI said, \"I will fix it when I have a free Saturday.\" That was in March.\n\n15\n00:00:42,400 --> 00:00:44,000\nIt is now September.\n\n16\n00:00:45,460 --> 00:00:47,200\nThe bike is still broken.\n\n17\n00:00:48,320 --> 00:00:49,060\nWhat about you, Mia?\n\n18\n00:00:49,670 --> 00:00:50,720\nAre you waiting for something?\n\n19\n00:00:51,960 --> 00:00:52,760\nI am, Ben.\n\n20\n00:00:53,340 --> 00:00:55,760\nFor two years, I wanted to learn to swim.\n\n21\n00:00:56,340 --> 00:00:57,890\nI was thirty-one.\n\n22\n00:00:57,940 --> 00:01:07,920\nI said, \"I will start when I have time.\" Then, \"I will start in summer.\" Then, \"when work is calm.\" Two years passed.\n\n23\n00:01:08,320 --> 00:01:09,260\nI did not swim.\n\n24\n00:01:09,940 --> 00:01:10,480\nI waited.\n\n25\n00:01:11,260 --> 00:01:13,380\nAnd the perfect moment never came.\n\n26\n00:01:14,120 --> 00:01:16,520\nNot one perfect day in two years.\n\n27\n00:01:17,480 --> 00:01:18,890\nI think many people know this.\n\n28\n00:01:19,470 --> 00:01:21,040\nWe wait for the perfect moment.\n\n29\n00:01:21,660 --> 00:01:24,800\nAnd the perfect moment is not coming.\n\n30\n00:01:24,910 --> 00:01:29,620\n\"The perfect moment is not coming.\" That is our topic today, Mia.\n\n31\n00:01:30,400 --> 00:01:32,700\nAnd I think it is good news, actually.\n\n32\n00:01:33,660 --> 00:01:34,880\nSo here is our plan.\n\n33\n00:01:35,720 --> 00:01:38,970\nIn part one, we tell our own waiting stories.\n\n34\n00:01:39,900 --> 00:01:42,160\nMia's swimming, and my podcast.\n\n35\n00:01:43,240 --> 00:01:47,520\nIn part two, we ask why the perfect moment never comes.\n\n36\n00:01:48,400 --> 00:01:50,610\nAnd I have a true story for that.\n\n37\n00:01:51,660 --> 00:01:55,440\nIn part three, we share simple ideas for starting now.\n\n38\n00:01:56,540 --> 00:01:57,260\nDoes that sound good?\n\n39\n00:01:58,160 --> 00:01:59,200\nThat sounds good, Ben.\n\n40\n00:01:59,540 --> 00:02:03,760\nAnd to everyone listening, please do not worry if you miss something.\n\n41\n00:02:04,260 --> 00:02:05,200\nYou will hear it again.\n\n42\n00:02:05,940 --> 00:02:09,959\nYou can listen while you walk, while you cook, or while you wait for the bus.\n\n43\n00:02:10,960 --> 00:02:11,560\nOkay, Ben.\n\n44\n00:02:11,800 --> 00:02:12,360\nPart one.\n\n45\n00:02:12,700 --> 00:02:13,280\nMy swimming.\n\n46\n00:02:13,960 --> 00:02:15,280\nDo you want to know how it ended?\n\n47\n00:02:16,280 --> 00:02:17,180\nOf course, Mia.\n\n48\n00:02:17,820 --> 00:02:20,140\nI have wanted to ask you this for weeks.\n\n49\n00:02:20,980 --> 00:02:21,980\nDid you swim in the end?\n\n50\n00:02:22,640 --> 00:02:24,720\nOr is the pool still waiting for you?\n\n51\n00:02:25,700 --> 00:02:26,480\nI did, Ben.\n\n52\n00:02:26,700 --> 00:02:28,780\nAnd it was not a perfect moment.\n\n53\n00:02:29,360 --> 00:02:30,200\nIt was a Tuesday.\n\n54\n00:02:30,700 --> 00:02:31,400\nIt was raining.\n\n55\n00:02:31,880 --> 00:02:32,580\nI was tired.\n\n56\n00:02:33,200 --> 00:02:34,340\nI had a bad day at work.\n\n57\n00:02:34,840 --> 00:02:37,020\nAnd on the way home, I passed the pool.\n\n58\n00:02:37,920 --> 00:02:40,460\nAnd I thought, \"This is a bad moment.\n\n59\n00:02:41,020 --> 00:02:41,740\nI am tired.\n\n60\n00:02:42,040 --> 00:02:42,740\nI am sad.\n\n61\n00:02:43,180 --> 00:02:43,960\nIt is raining.\"\n\n62\n00:02:45,140 --> 00:02:46,460\nA bad moment, Mia.\n\n63\n00:02:47,260 --> 00:02:49,460\nTired, sad, and raining.\n\n64\n00:02:50,360 --> 00:02:52,300\nThat is three reasons to go home.\n\n65\n00:02:53,320 --> 00:02:54,360\nSo what happened?\n\n66\n00:02:55,160 --> 00:02:55,810\nDid you go home?\n\n67\n00:02:56,920 --> 00:03:02,320\nThen I thought, \"Every moment was a bad moment for two years.\" So I went in.\n\n68\n00:03:03,020 --> 00:03:03,760\nI bought a ticket.\n\n69\n00:03:04,400 --> 00:03:07,180\nI swam for ten minutes, badly.\n\n70\n00:03:07,300 --> 00:03:08,220\nAnd I started.\n\n71\n00:03:08,880 --> 00:03:09,960\nOn a bad Tuesday.\n\n72\n00:03:10,580 --> 00:03:11,520\nNot a perfect day.\n\n73\n00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:12,690\nNot a Monday.\n\n74\n00:03:13,140 --> 00:03:13,880\nNot a new year.\n\n75\n00:03:14,580 --> 00:03:16,200\nA bad, rainy Tuesday.\n\n76\n00:03:17,020 --> 00:03:19,730\n\"On a bad Tuesday.\" I love that, Mia.\n\n77\n00:03:20,280 --> 00:03:22,140\nBecause we think starting needs a good day.\n\n78\n00:03:22,720 --> 00:03:23,210\nA Monday.\n\n79\n00:03:23,760 --> 00:03:24,840\nA new year.\n\n80\n00:03:24,900 --> 00:03:26,800\nA sunny morning with energy.\n\n81\n00:03:26,820 --> 00:03:28,920\nBut most starts happen on bad Tuesdays.\n\n82\n00:03:29,780 --> 00:03:31,100\nLet me tell you my story now.\n\n83\n00:03:32,060 --> 00:03:34,360\nFor three years, I wanted to make this podcast.\n\n84\n00:03:34,960 --> 00:03:35,980\nThree years, Ben?\n\n85\n00:03:36,500 --> 00:03:37,680\nLonger than my swimming.\n\n86\n00:03:37,980 --> 00:03:39,040\nWhat were you waiting for?\n\n87\n00:03:39,440 --> 00:03:40,320\nA better voice?\n\n88\n00:03:40,660 --> 00:03:41,500\nA better plan?\n\n89\n00:03:42,360 --> 00:03:43,720\nI was waiting to be ready, Mia.\n\n90\n00:03:44,420 --> 00:03:45,260\nI had the idea.\n\n91\n00:03:45,760 --> 00:03:46,460\nI had the name.\n\n92\n00:03:47,040 --> 00:03:48,120\nI had a list of topics.\n\n93\n00:03:48,630 --> 00:03:59,689\nAnd I said, \"I need a better microphone.\" Then, \"I need to speak better first.\" Then, \"I need to be ready.\" Three years of \"I need to be ready.\"\n\n94\n00:04:00,380 --> 00:04:04,420\n\"I need to be ready.\" Ben, that is the sentence, isn't it?\n\n95\n00:04:04,500 --> 00:04:05,820\nEveryone says it.\n\n96\n00:04:05,880 --> 00:04:06,840\nWhat does ready mean?\n\n97\n00:04:07,800 --> 00:04:08,720\nI did not know, Mia.\n\n98\n00:04:09,280 --> 00:04:10,120\nThat was the problem.\n\n99\n00:04:10,900 --> 00:04:13,320\nReady was a feeling I was waiting for.\n\n100\n00:04:13,380 --> 00:04:15,240\nA feeling of, \"Now.\n\n101\n00:04:15,320 --> 00:04:18,080\nNow I can do it.\" And the feeling never came.\n\n102\n00:04:18,860 --> 00:04:21,680\nBecause ready is not a feeling that comes before.\n\n103\n00:04:21,740 --> 00:04:24,020\nReady is a feeling that comes after.\n\n104\n00:04:24,140 --> 00:04:24,860\nAfter the start.\n\n105\n00:04:25,640 --> 00:04:27,020\nAfter the first bad episode.\n\n106\n00:04:27,700 --> 00:04:29,340\nAfter your first ten minutes in the pool.\n\n107\n00:04:30,170 --> 00:04:34,320\n\"Ready comes after the start.\" Ben, I want to write that down.\n\n108\n00:04:34,380 --> 00:04:36,290\nNobody feels ready before.\n\n109\n00:04:36,360 --> 00:04:37,840\nEveryone feels ready after.\n\n110\n00:04:38,580 --> 00:04:40,640\nSo how did you start the podcast, in the end?\n\n111\n00:04:42,160 --> 00:04:43,420\nMy sister, Mia.\n\n112\n00:04:43,480 --> 00:04:45,280\nShe was tired of hearing about it.\n\n113\n00:04:45,380 --> 00:04:52,580\nOne evening, she said, \"Ben, record one episode tonight, with your phone, or stop talking about it.\" I was angry.\n\n114\n00:04:53,280 --> 00:04:55,380\nThen I recorded one episode with my phone.\n\n115\n00:04:56,140 --> 00:04:57,400\nWith your phone!\n\n116\n00:04:58,020 --> 00:04:59,140\nIn your kitchen, I guess.\n\n117\n00:04:59,720 --> 00:05:00,260\nWas it good?\n\n118\n00:05:00,740 --> 00:05:01,640\nBe honest, Ben.\n\n119\n00:05:02,520 --> 00:05:04,120\nIt was terrible, Mia.\n\n120\n00:05:04,180 --> 00:05:05,160\nMy voice was shaking.\n\n121\n00:05:05,760 --> 00:05:07,340\nI said \"um\" fifty times.\n\n122\n00:05:07,920 --> 00:05:09,220\nBut it existed.\n\n123\n00:05:09,280 --> 00:05:10,600\nIt was real.\n\n124\n00:05:10,610 --> 00:05:13,500\nAnd the next day, I recorded another one.\n\n125\n00:05:13,560 --> 00:05:15,480\nA little less terrible.\n\n126\n00:05:15,520 --> 00:05:17,580\nThe perfect moment did not come.\n\n127\n00:05:17,640 --> 00:05:18,940\nMy sister came.\n\n128\n00:05:18,970 --> 00:05:20,160\nAnd a phone.\n\n129\n00:05:20,220 --> 00:05:20,820\nThat was enough.\n\n130\n00:05:21,820 --> 00:05:23,300\nA sister and a phone.\n\n131\n00:05:23,360 --> 00:05:25,220\nThat is a perfect moment, actually, Ben.\n\n132\n00:05:25,880 --> 00:05:27,860\nJust not the kind we imagine.\n\n133\n00:05:27,899 --> 00:05:29,360\nWe imagine a big beginning.\n\n134\n00:05:29,760 --> 00:05:30,350\nA big day.\n\n135\n00:05:30,980 --> 00:05:33,070\nBut the real beginning is a phone in the kitchen.\n\n136\n00:05:33,660 --> 00:05:35,620\nTen minutes in a pool.\n\n137\n00:05:35,680 --> 00:05:37,260\nDid you post that first episode?\n\n138\n00:05:38,500 --> 00:05:39,580\nI wanted to delete it, Mia.\n\n139\n00:05:40,320 --> 00:05:41,640\nIt was not ready for the world.\n\n140\n00:05:42,409 --> 00:05:45,200\nMy sister said, \"Post it.\" I posted it.\n\n141\n00:05:45,940 --> 00:05:46,870\nNine people listened.\n\n142\n00:05:47,420 --> 00:05:50,969\nOne wrote, \"Thank you, this helped me.\" One person.\n\n143\n00:05:51,920 --> 00:05:54,870\nAnd that one person was worth more than three years of waiting.\n\n144\n00:05:55,720 --> 00:05:57,790\nIn three years of waiting, I helped nobody.\n\n145\n00:05:58,600 --> 00:06:01,220\nIn one bad episode, I helped one person.\n\n146\n00:06:02,189 --> 00:06:07,040\n\"In three years of waiting, I helped nobody.\" Ben, that is the price of waiting.\n\n147\n00:06:07,580 --> 00:06:09,500\nAnd we do not see the price until later.\n\n148\n00:06:10,260 --> 00:06:11,920\nCan I tell you about my \"later\" list?\n\n149\n00:06:12,880 --> 00:06:13,620\nA \"later\" list?\n\n150\n00:06:14,160 --> 00:06:15,440\nI think I have one too, Mia.\n\n151\n00:06:15,980 --> 00:06:17,040\nBut I never wrote it down.\n\n152\n00:06:17,440 --> 00:06:18,450\nPlease, tell us.\n\n153\n00:06:19,200 --> 00:06:21,760\nI had one, on paper, Ben.\n\n154\n00:06:22,600 --> 00:06:23,980\nLearn to swim, later.\n\n155\n00:06:24,660 --> 00:06:26,940\nCall my old friend, later.\n\n156\n00:06:26,980 --> 00:06:29,120\nFix the shelf, later.\n\n157\n00:06:29,140 --> 00:06:31,080\nWrite to my grandmother, later.\n\n158\n00:06:32,860 --> 00:06:34,360\nMy grandmother died last year.\n\n159\n00:06:35,190 --> 00:06:36,820\nThe letter was still on the list.\n\n160\n00:06:38,220 --> 00:06:39,140\nI am sorry, Mia.\n\n161\n00:06:40,150 --> 00:06:41,380\nAnd thank you for telling us that.\n\n162\n00:06:42,380 --> 00:06:44,400\nBecause that is the real reason for today.\n\n163\n00:06:45,260 --> 00:06:45,980\nNot success.\n\n164\n00:06:46,840 --> 00:06:47,360\nNot work.\n\n165\n00:06:48,350 --> 00:06:53,520\n\"Later\" has a price, and the price is the things we never do.\n\n166\n00:06:53,600 --> 00:06:54,459\nYes, Ben.\n\n167\n00:06:54,600 --> 00:06:56,520\nI do not say it to make everyone sad.\n\n168\n00:06:57,140 --> 00:07:00,280\nI say it because \"later\" is not a real place.\n\n169\n00:07:00,920 --> 00:07:03,300\nLater is where all the perfect moments live.\n\n170\n00:07:03,820 --> 00:07:05,160\nAnd nobody lives there.\n\n171\n00:07:06,760 --> 00:07:08,440\nOkay, so that was part one.\n\n172\n00:07:09,040 --> 00:07:17,060\nTwo years of swimming, three years of a podcast, a bad Tuesday, a sister with a phone, and a letter that stayed on a list.\n\n173\n00:07:17,940 --> 00:07:19,260\nNow, part two.\n\n174\n00:07:20,380 --> 00:07:22,580\nWhy does the perfect moment never come?\n\n175\n00:07:23,460 --> 00:07:24,980\nBen, you have a true story.\n\n176\n00:07:26,020 --> 00:07:27,560\nI do, Mia.\n\n177\n00:07:27,640 --> 00:07:29,580\nOkay, part two.\n\n178\n00:07:29,640 --> 00:07:30,740\nThis is a true story.\n\n179\n00:07:31,380 --> 00:07:37,120\nIn January 2007, Steve Jobs stood on a stage and showed the first iPhone to the world.\n\n180\n00:07:38,020 --> 00:07:39,099\nEveryone remembers that.\n\n181\n00:07:39,760 --> 00:07:40,560\nIt looked perfect.\n\n182\n00:07:41,550 --> 00:07:48,340\nBut a writer named Fred Vogelstein later told the story behind it, in The New York Times Magazine, in 2013.\n\n183\n00:07:49,220 --> 00:07:52,040\nAnd the iPhone on that stage was not finished.\n\n184\n00:07:52,860 --> 00:07:53,760\nNot finished?\n\n185\n00:07:54,110 --> 00:07:55,640\nThe first iPhone, Ben?\n\n186\n00:07:56,080 --> 00:07:57,560\nThe one everyone remembers?\n\n187\n00:07:58,180 --> 00:07:59,940\nI thought that phone was perfect.\n\n188\n00:08:00,940 --> 00:08:02,040\nNot finished, Mia.\n\n189\n00:08:02,680 --> 00:08:04,860\nThe phone on the stage had many problems.\n\n190\n00:08:05,500 --> 00:08:06,260\nIt could crash.\n\n191\n00:08:06,900 --> 00:08:09,260\nThe engineers had a special order for the demo.\n\n192\n00:08:09,960 --> 00:08:12,180\nDo this, then this, then this.\n\n193\n00:08:13,120 --> 00:08:17,800\nIf Jobs did something in a different order, the phone could stop working.\n\n194\n00:08:17,920 --> 00:08:19,850\nSome parts of the software were not done.\n\n195\n00:08:20,980 --> 00:08:22,520\nSo the team was afraid, Ben.\n\n196\n00:08:22,980 --> 00:08:24,840\nAnd Jobs went on the stage anyway?\n\n197\n00:08:25,980 --> 00:08:27,080\nHe went anyway, Mia.\n\n198\n00:08:27,840 --> 00:08:29,620\nHe did the demo in the right order.\n\n199\n00:08:30,440 --> 00:08:30,930\nIt worked.\n\n200\n00:08:31,740 --> 00:08:34,780\nAnd six months later, the finished phone went to the shops.\n\n201\n00:08:35,820 --> 00:08:39,720\nSo the most famous product of our time started before it was ready.\n\n202\n00:08:40,690 --> 00:08:44,420\n\"Before it was ready.\" Ben, I think about this a lot.\n\n203\n00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:49,040\nIf Apple waited for the perfect iPhone, we might still be waiting.\n\n204\n00:08:49,760 --> 00:08:51,380\nEvery product has problems.\n\n205\n00:08:51,860 --> 00:08:56,480\nEvery first episode has \"um.\" Every first swim is bad.\n\n206\n00:08:57,400 --> 00:08:58,669\nSo what was the difference?\n\n207\n00:08:59,140 --> 00:09:01,780\nWhy could Jobs start, and I could not?\n\n208\n00:09:02,840 --> 00:09:04,620\nHe had a plan for the problems, Mia.\n\n209\n00:09:05,400 --> 00:09:06,440\nNot a perfect phone.\n\n210\n00:09:07,120 --> 00:09:08,960\nA plan for an imperfect phone.\n\n211\n00:09:09,949 --> 00:09:11,000\nThe order for the demo.\n\n212\n00:09:11,650 --> 00:09:12,600\nThat was his plan.\n\n213\n00:09:13,500 --> 00:09:16,100\nThe people who start are not the people who are ready.\n\n214\n00:09:17,040 --> 00:09:20,590\nThey are the people who start anyway, with a plan for the problems.\n\n215\n00:09:21,380 --> 00:09:23,920\nA plan for an imperfect phone.\n\n216\n00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:24,780\nI like that, Ben.\n\n217\n00:09:25,740 --> 00:09:27,560\nLet me say one more thing about waiting.\n\n218\n00:09:28,320 --> 00:09:30,500\nWaiting feels like something.\n\n219\n00:09:30,560 --> 00:09:31,970\nIt feels like preparing.\n\n220\n00:09:32,570 --> 00:09:35,920\nWhen I waited two years, I did not feel lazy.\n\n221\n00:09:36,020 --> 00:09:37,199\nI read about swimming.\n\n222\n00:09:37,280 --> 00:09:38,560\nI bought a swimsuit.\n\n223\n00:09:38,700 --> 00:09:40,120\nI looked at pools on the map.\n\n224\n00:09:41,020 --> 00:09:42,740\nTwo years of \"preparing.\"\n\n225\n00:09:43,780 --> 00:09:45,000\nReading about swimming, Mia.\n\n226\n00:09:45,720 --> 00:09:47,120\nBuying a swimsuit.\n\n227\n00:09:47,200 --> 00:09:49,100\nLooking at pools.\n\n228\n00:09:49,140 --> 00:09:50,380\nIt looks like work, doesn't it?\n\n229\n00:09:51,290 --> 00:09:52,680\nI did the same.\n\n230\n00:09:52,740 --> 00:09:54,130\nVideos about microphones.\n\n231\n00:09:54,560 --> 00:09:56,380\nArticles about speaking.\n\n232\n00:09:56,540 --> 00:09:58,580\nI was \"getting ready\" every evening.\n\n233\n00:09:59,460 --> 00:10:00,970\nAnd I was not getting ready.\n\n234\n00:10:01,689 --> 00:10:02,480\nI was waiting.\n\n235\n00:10:03,360 --> 00:10:04,189\nDid you feel that too?\n\n236\n00:10:05,800 --> 00:10:07,060\nI felt careful, Ben.\n\n237\n00:10:07,540 --> 00:10:08,900\nNot lazy.\n\n238\n00:10:08,980 --> 00:10:09,470\nCareful.\n\n239\n00:10:10,360 --> 00:10:11,860\nThat is the trick of waiting.\n\n240\n00:10:12,380 --> 00:10:14,360\nIt never feels like waiting.\n\n241\n00:10:14,380 --> 00:10:16,560\nIt feels like being smart.\n\n242\n00:10:16,590 --> 00:10:21,440\n\"I am not ready yet, so I prepare.\" But the pool does not care how much I read.\n\n243\n00:10:21,940 --> 00:10:24,980\nThe pool only knows if I am in the water or not.\n\n244\n00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:28,500\nTwo years of preparing, Mia.\n\n245\n00:10:29,260 --> 00:10:29,870\nI know it well.\n\n246\n00:10:30,800 --> 00:10:32,860\nThree years of reading about microphones.\n\n247\n00:10:33,880 --> 00:10:37,380\nBut waiting looks like work, and it is not work.\n\n248\n00:10:38,260 --> 00:10:42,600\nTen minutes in the water taught you more than two years of reading, right?\n\n249\n00:10:42,680 --> 00:10:43,720\nMuch more, Ben.\n\n250\n00:10:44,260 --> 00:10:45,810\nBecause the water is the teacher.\n\n251\n00:10:46,280 --> 00:10:47,400\nNot the book.\n\n252\n00:10:47,460 --> 00:10:49,160\nThe pool is the teacher.\n\n253\n00:10:49,200 --> 00:10:50,780\nThe microphone is the teacher.\n\n254\n00:10:51,260 --> 00:10:53,140\nThe thing itself is the teacher.\n\n255\n00:10:53,760 --> 00:10:56,060\nAnd the thing can only teach when we start.\n\n256\n00:10:57,090 --> 00:11:01,600\n\"The thing itself is the teacher.\" Mia, this is true for English, too.\n\n257\n00:11:02,440 --> 00:11:04,560\nMany of our listeners are waiting.\n\n258\n00:11:04,610 --> 00:11:12,760\nThey say, \"I will speak English when my grammar is better.\" \"I will talk to people when I know more words.\" I understand.\n\n259\n00:11:13,440 --> 00:11:14,740\nI said the same about Spanish.\n\n260\n00:11:15,780 --> 00:11:17,900\nAnd how did you start speaking Spanish, Ben?\n\n261\n00:11:18,600 --> 00:11:19,880\nWas there a perfect moment?\n\n262\n00:11:20,350 --> 00:11:23,240\nA class, a teacher, a good day?\n\n263\n00:11:24,220 --> 00:11:26,180\nAt a bus stop, Mia.\n\n264\n00:11:26,300 --> 00:11:29,700\nOne day, I said hello to a man from Mexico at the bus stop.\n\n265\n00:11:30,499 --> 00:11:31,860\nMy Spanish was terrible.\n\n266\n00:11:32,680 --> 00:11:33,260\nHe smiled.\n\n267\n00:11:33,920 --> 00:11:35,080\nWe talked for two minutes.\n\n268\n00:11:35,920 --> 00:11:36,820\nThat was my start.\n\n269\n00:11:37,780 --> 00:11:39,100\nTwo minutes at a bus stop.\n\n270\n00:11:40,080 --> 00:11:41,480\nTwo minutes at a bus stop.\n\n271\n00:11:42,020 --> 00:11:43,620\nThat is a start, Ben.\n\n272\n00:11:43,660 --> 00:11:45,420\nAnd I think this is important.\n\n273\n00:11:45,450 --> 00:11:47,200\nThe start is small.\n\n274\n00:11:47,240 --> 00:11:49,380\nThe perfect moment we wait for is big.\n\n275\n00:11:49,870 --> 00:11:54,890\nBut the real start is two minutes, or ten minutes, or one bad episode.\n\n276\n00:11:55,540 --> 00:11:58,640\nIf the start is small, we do not need a perfect moment.\n\n277\n00:11:59,040 --> 00:12:00,220\nWe need any moment.\n\n278\n00:12:00,630 --> 00:12:01,200\nThis one.\n\n279\n00:12:01,720 --> 00:12:02,140\nNow.\n\n280\n00:12:03,200 --> 00:12:03,740\n\"This one.\n\n281\n00:12:04,580 --> 00:12:07,720\nNow.\" Mia, that reminds me of a small thing.\n\n282\n00:12:08,660 --> 00:12:10,600\nI wanted to call my father every Sunday.\n\n283\n00:12:11,400 --> 00:12:14,840\nAnd every Sunday, I waited for the right time.\n\n284\n00:12:14,940 --> 00:12:16,200\nAfter lunch?\n\n285\n00:12:16,240 --> 00:12:17,960\nNo, he sleeps.\n\n286\n00:12:18,080 --> 00:12:18,380\nEvening?\n\n287\n00:12:19,160 --> 00:12:19,990\nNo, the football.\n\n288\n00:12:21,060 --> 00:12:21,420\nMorning?\n\n289\n00:12:22,100 --> 00:12:22,620\nToo early.\n\n290\n00:12:23,700 --> 00:12:24,780\nAnd the Sunday passed.\n\n291\n00:12:25,580 --> 00:12:27,100\nEvery Sunday.\n\n292\n00:12:27,140 --> 00:12:27,750\nFor months.\n\n293\n00:12:28,860 --> 00:12:31,540\nWaiting for the right hour to make a five-minute call.\n\n294\n00:12:32,700 --> 00:12:33,700\nFive minutes, Ben.\n\n295\n00:12:34,400 --> 00:12:36,580\nAnd months of waiting for the right hour.\n\n296\n00:12:37,280 --> 00:12:37,970\nWhat do you do now?\n\n297\n00:12:38,860 --> 00:12:40,260\nI call when I think of it, Mia.\n\n298\n00:12:40,790 --> 00:12:42,560\nThat is the rule now.\n\n299\n00:12:42,600 --> 00:12:46,820\nWhen the thought comes, \"I should call Dad,\" I call.\n\n300\n00:12:46,880 --> 00:12:47,410\nNot later.\n\n301\n00:12:48,340 --> 00:12:48,630\nThen.\n\n302\n00:12:49,580 --> 00:12:52,060\nSometimes he is sleeping, and he does not answer.\n\n303\n00:12:52,940 --> 00:12:53,240\nFine.\n\n304\n00:12:53,840 --> 00:12:54,560\nI call again.\n\n305\n00:12:55,380 --> 00:12:57,080\nThe thought is the moment.\n\n306\n00:12:58,040 --> 00:13:00,380\nAnd that is the word I want to talk about.\n\n307\n00:13:01,370 --> 00:13:05,620\n\"Now.\" Now is the only moment we have.\n\n308\n00:13:05,660 --> 00:13:08,100\nBut now always feels wrong for starting.\n\n309\n00:13:09,060 --> 00:13:11,040\nSo we push the start to \"later.\"\n\n310\n00:13:11,840 --> 00:13:13,660\nAnd later does not exist, Ben.\n\n311\n00:13:14,060 --> 00:13:14,780\nWe said that.\n\n312\n00:13:15,820 --> 00:13:16,880\nCan I ask about fear?\n\n313\n00:13:17,360 --> 00:13:21,460\nBecause I think fear is what we are really talking about.\n\n314\n00:13:21,480 --> 00:13:23,440\nYou are right, Mia.\n\n315\n00:13:23,500 --> 00:13:26,080\nWhen I waited for the perfect moment, I was afraid.\n\n316\n00:13:26,860 --> 00:13:28,840\nAfraid of being bad.\n\n317\n00:13:28,900 --> 00:13:30,320\nAfraid of people laughing.\n\n318\n00:13:31,290 --> 00:13:34,120\nWaiting was a way to hide from the fear.\n\n319\n00:13:34,180 --> 00:13:37,100\nAs long as I was \"getting ready,\" I could not fail.\n\n320\n00:13:38,140 --> 00:13:39,580\nBut I could not start, either.\n\n321\n00:13:40,500 --> 00:13:43,700\nThe perfect moment was a wall I built to protect myself.\n\n322\n00:13:44,460 --> 00:13:46,520\nA wall we build to protect ourselves.\n\n323\n00:13:47,020 --> 00:13:47,580\nYes, Ben.\n\n324\n00:13:48,220 --> 00:13:50,260\nAnd behind the wall, it feels safe.\n\n325\n00:13:51,120 --> 00:13:54,740\nBehind the wall, I am a person who will swim one day.\n\n326\n00:13:54,750 --> 00:13:55,730\nA future swimmer.\n\n327\n00:13:56,420 --> 00:13:58,160\nThat is comfortable.\n\n328\n00:13:58,200 --> 00:14:01,380\nThe moment I go into the pool, I am a bad swimmer.\n\n329\n00:14:02,140 --> 00:14:03,860\nA real, bad swimmer.\n\n330\n00:14:04,740 --> 00:14:06,520\nA real, bad swimmer, Mia.\n\n331\n00:14:07,360 --> 00:14:08,500\nThat is not comfortable.\n\n332\n00:14:09,400 --> 00:14:10,140\nBut it is real.\n\n333\n00:14:11,120 --> 00:14:14,620\nAnd a real bad swimmer becomes a real okay swimmer.\n\n334\n00:14:15,600 --> 00:14:17,520\nA future swimmer becomes nothing.\n\n335\n00:14:18,560 --> 00:14:20,340\nFuture is where nothing happens.\n\n336\n00:14:21,160 --> 00:14:26,100\n\"Future is where nothing happens.\" Ben, that is the big idea of today.\n\n337\n00:14:26,780 --> 00:14:28,700\nThe perfect moment does not exist.\n\n338\n00:14:29,360 --> 00:14:30,700\nWhat exists is now.\n\n339\n00:14:31,380 --> 00:14:34,460\nAnd now is the only moment where anything ever started.\n\n340\n00:14:35,340 --> 00:14:36,860\nOkay, so that was part two.\n\n341\n00:14:37,640 --> 00:14:39,460\nWhy the perfect moment never comes.\n\n342\n00:14:40,320 --> 00:14:42,940\nWe talked about the iPhone that was not finished.\n\n343\n00:14:42,980 --> 00:14:44,800\nWe said ready comes after the start.\n\n344\n00:14:45,440 --> 00:14:46,849\nAnd we said waiting is a wall.\n\n345\n00:14:47,700 --> 00:14:49,399\nNow, part three.\n\n346\n00:14:49,460 --> 00:14:50,960\nSimple ideas for starting now.\n\n347\n00:14:51,620 --> 00:14:52,700\nBen, you go first.\n\n348\n00:14:53,500 --> 00:14:54,629\nOkay, part three.\n\n349\n00:14:55,680 --> 00:14:57,600\nMy first idea is very simple, Mia.\n\n350\n00:14:58,420 --> 00:14:59,660\nMake the start tiny.\n\n351\n00:15:00,600 --> 00:15:01,180\nTwo minutes.\n\n352\n00:15:01,980 --> 00:15:02,540\nTen minutes.\n\n353\n00:15:03,580 --> 00:15:05,700\nOne episode with a phone.\n\n354\n00:15:05,760 --> 00:15:07,460\nNot the whole thing.\n\n355\n00:15:07,500 --> 00:15:09,180\nThe smallest piece of the thing.\n\n356\n00:15:10,120 --> 00:15:13,580\nWhen the start is tiny, you do not need a perfect moment.\n\n357\n00:15:14,480 --> 00:15:16,820\nAny moment is big enough for two minutes.\n\n358\n00:15:18,560 --> 00:15:20,640\nAny moment is big enough for two minutes.\n\n359\n00:15:21,280 --> 00:15:22,020\nI like that, Ben.\n\n360\n00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:25,280\nMy first idea is a strange one.\n\n361\n00:15:25,340 --> 00:15:27,700\nChoose a bad moment on purpose.\n\n362\n00:15:27,760 --> 00:15:29,120\nA rainy Tuesday.\n\n363\n00:15:29,160 --> 00:15:30,280\nA tired evening.\n\n364\n00:15:30,880 --> 00:15:34,280\nBecause if I start on a bad moment, every other moment is better.\n\n365\n00:15:35,200 --> 00:15:37,220\nMy bad Tuesday was my swimming start.\n\n366\n00:15:37,820 --> 00:15:39,940\nNow every swim is easier than that one.\n\n367\n00:15:40,760 --> 00:15:42,880\nEvery swim is easier than the first, Mia.\n\n368\n00:15:43,540 --> 00:15:44,420\nThat is smart.\n\n369\n00:15:45,120 --> 00:15:49,230\nAnd I have a trick for the evening, because evenings are where I lose my starts.\n\n370\n00:15:50,060 --> 00:16:01,280\nIn the morning, I say, \"Tonight, I will start.\" Then evening comes, and I am tired, and I say, \"Tomorrow.\" So now I start in the morning, for two minutes.\n\n371\n00:16:02,320 --> 00:16:03,060\nI open the file.\n\n372\n00:16:03,480 --> 00:16:05,200\nI write one line.\n\n373\n00:16:05,240 --> 00:16:07,420\nAnd in the evening, the thing is already started.\n\n374\n00:16:08,290 --> 00:16:12,200\n\"Start with energy, continue without it.\" That is smart, Ben.\n\n375\n00:16:12,760 --> 00:16:13,880\nContinuing is easy.\n\n376\n00:16:14,380 --> 00:16:15,520\nStarting is hard.\n\n377\n00:16:16,220 --> 00:16:20,020\nSo you start when you have energy, and you continue when you do not.\n\n378\n00:16:20,160 --> 00:16:21,660\nI want to try that with my messages.\n\n379\n00:16:22,260 --> 00:16:23,440\nTwo lines in the morning.\n\n380\n00:16:23,980 --> 00:16:26,880\nThen the message is started, and I only need to finish it.\n\n381\n00:16:28,580 --> 00:16:30,420\nChoose a bad moment on purpose.\n\n382\n00:16:30,900 --> 00:16:32,120\nThat is brave, Mia.\n\n383\n00:16:33,020 --> 00:16:34,720\nMy second idea is about things.\n\n384\n00:16:35,420 --> 00:16:37,560\nMake the thing ready, not yourself.\n\n385\n00:16:38,460 --> 00:16:42,000\nWe spend years making ourselves ready, and we are never ready.\n\n386\n00:16:42,780 --> 00:16:44,040\nBut a swimsuit can be ready.\n\n387\n00:16:44,640 --> 00:16:45,780\nA phone can be ready.\n\n388\n00:16:46,360 --> 00:16:47,820\nAn open file can be ready.\n\n389\n00:16:48,620 --> 00:16:49,540\nThings can be ready.\n\n390\n00:16:50,080 --> 00:16:50,820\nPeople start.\n\n391\n00:16:51,690 --> 00:16:52,590\n\"Things can be ready.\n\n392\n00:16:52,950 --> 00:16:56,590\nPeople start.\" Ben, that is exactly what I did.\n\n393\n00:16:57,200 --> 00:17:00,260\nWhen I wanted to swim, I put my swimsuit in my work bag.\n\n394\n00:17:00,800 --> 00:17:02,150\nEvery day.\n\n395\n00:17:02,160 --> 00:17:06,080\nSo on the bad Tuesday, when I passed the pool, the swimsuit was there.\n\n396\n00:17:06,780 --> 00:17:08,220\nI had no reason to wait.\n\n397\n00:17:08,780 --> 00:17:12,360\nThe perfect moment did not come, but the swimsuit was ready.\n\n398\n00:17:13,300 --> 00:17:14,960\nThe swimsuit was ready.\n\n399\n00:17:15,040 --> 00:17:15,980\nThat was enough, Mia.\n\n400\n00:17:16,830 --> 00:17:18,740\nAnd my third idea comes from my sister.\n\n401\n00:17:19,540 --> 00:17:21,220\nTell one person.\n\n402\n00:17:21,300 --> 00:17:22,220\nA sister.\n\n403\n00:17:22,280 --> 00:17:22,750\nA friend.\n\n404\n00:17:23,230 --> 00:17:23,860\nA colleague.\n\n405\n00:17:24,700 --> 00:17:30,980\nSay, \"I am going to start this week.\" Because a person will push you when the moment will not.\n\n406\n00:17:31,040 --> 00:17:32,020\nDo you have a third idea?\n\n407\n00:17:32,940 --> 00:17:33,580\nI do, Ben.\n\n408\n00:17:34,100 --> 00:17:36,360\nMy third idea is a question for the voice.\n\n409\n00:17:37,200 --> 00:17:41,320\nTonight, when we want to start something, the voice will say, \"Not yet.\n\n410\n00:17:41,740 --> 00:17:42,360\nNot today.\n\n411\n00:17:42,800 --> 00:17:45,940\nNot ready.\" And I have one answer for that voice.\n\n412\n00:17:46,580 --> 00:17:52,390\nAsk it, \"When?\" Not \"why.\" \"When?\" And the voice has no answer.\n\n413\n00:17:52,920 --> 00:17:54,020\nIt never has a date.\n\n414\n00:17:54,540 --> 00:17:59,800\nIt only has \"later.\" And when we see that the voice has no date, we can start.\n\n415\n00:18:00,700 --> 00:18:02,000\nThe voice has no date.\n\n416\n00:18:02,720 --> 00:18:04,380\nThat is true, Mia.\n\n417\n00:18:04,480 --> 00:18:09,280\nI asked my voice, \"When will I be ready for the podcast?\" And it said nothing.\n\n418\n00:18:10,020 --> 00:18:11,300\nThree years of nothing.\n\n419\n00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:13,360\nThe voice is not a plan.\n\n420\n00:18:13,760 --> 00:18:14,459\nIt is a feeling.\n\n421\n00:18:15,100 --> 00:18:18,660\nAnd a feeling with no date is just fear wearing a nice coat.\n\n422\n00:18:19,570 --> 00:18:24,920\n\"Fear wearing a nice coat.\" Ben, I will remember that.\n\n423\n00:18:24,940 --> 00:18:26,630\nAnd I have one more small idea.\n\n424\n00:18:27,300 --> 00:18:28,480\nSay a sentence out loud.\n\n425\n00:18:29,110 --> 00:18:33,230\n\"Now is not perfect, and now is enough.\" I say it in the kitchen.\n\n426\n00:18:33,600 --> 00:18:34,960\nIt sounds silly.\n\n427\n00:18:35,020 --> 00:18:35,500\nIt works.\n\n428\n00:18:37,260 --> 00:18:39,400\nIt sounds silly, and it works, Mia.\n\n429\n00:18:39,940 --> 00:18:42,500\nLike the sentence before bed in our slow day episode.\n\n430\n00:18:43,160 --> 00:18:44,720\nCan I add a small one for messages?\n\n431\n00:18:45,280 --> 00:18:48,040\nWhen I think, \"I should write to my friend,\" I write.\n\n432\n00:18:48,500 --> 00:18:48,960\nRight then.\n\n433\n00:18:49,580 --> 00:18:50,180\nTwo lines.\n\n434\n00:18:50,660 --> 00:18:52,100\nNot a beautiful long message.\n\n435\n00:18:52,580 --> 00:18:56,800\nBecause if I wait for the time to write a beautiful long message, I write nothing.\n\n436\n00:18:57,420 --> 00:18:58,060\nFor a year.\n\n437\n00:18:59,300 --> 00:19:02,020\nTwo lines now is better than a perfect letter never.\n\n438\n00:19:02,540 --> 00:19:03,100\nYes, Ben.\n\n439\n00:19:03,580 --> 00:19:05,100\nI learned that from my grandmother's letter.\n\n440\n00:19:05,830 --> 00:19:07,340\nThe perfect letter never came.\n\n441\n00:19:08,120 --> 00:19:10,060\nTwo lines would have come.\n\n442\n00:19:10,160 --> 00:19:12,320\nSo now, two lines, today.\n\n443\n00:19:12,980 --> 00:19:13,620\nNot perfect.\n\n444\n00:19:14,440 --> 00:19:14,740\nSent.\n\n445\n00:19:15,580 --> 00:19:22,240\n\"Now is not perfect, and now is enough.\" Mia, I want to give that sentence to our listeners who are learning English.\n\n446\n00:19:22,820 --> 00:19:24,840\nBecause many of you are waiting to speak.\n\n447\n00:19:25,520 --> 00:19:27,100\nSo here is a tiny start.\n\n448\n00:19:27,780 --> 00:19:30,060\nSay one sentence out loud, right now.\n\n449\n00:19:30,660 --> 00:19:31,380\nWherever you are.\n\n450\n00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:32,640\nIn the kitchen.\n\n451\n00:19:33,060 --> 00:19:34,880\nOn the bus, quietly.\n\n452\n00:19:34,960 --> 00:19:37,050\nSay, \"Today I started.\"\n\n453\n00:19:38,150 --> 00:19:41,420\n\"Today I started.\" That is all, everyone.\n\n454\n00:19:42,140 --> 00:19:42,650\nNot ready.\n\n455\n00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:44,020\nGrammar not perfect.\n\n456\n00:19:44,400 --> 00:19:45,140\nAccent your own.\n\n457\n00:19:45,980 --> 00:19:47,440\nAnd you started.\n\n458\n00:19:47,500 --> 00:19:49,800\nOn a normal Tuesday, with a podcast.\n\n459\n00:19:50,510 --> 00:19:51,140\nThat counts.\n\n460\n00:19:52,280 --> 00:19:53,780\nBen, is there one last idea?\n\n461\n00:19:54,740 --> 00:19:55,860\nOne last idea, Mia.\n\n462\n00:19:56,420 --> 00:19:57,640\nIt is about being bad.\n\n463\n00:19:58,380 --> 00:19:59,699\nEveryone is bad at first.\n\n464\n00:20:00,280 --> 00:20:01,220\nYou were bad at swimming.\n\n465\n00:20:01,840 --> 00:20:03,040\nI was bad at podcasts.\n\n466\n00:20:03,660 --> 00:20:05,580\nSteve Jobs had a phone that could crash.\n\n467\n00:20:06,320 --> 00:20:08,040\nBeing bad is not a sign to wait.\n\n468\n00:20:08,520 --> 00:20:10,780\nBeing bad is the first step of being good.\n\n469\n00:20:11,560 --> 00:20:13,400\nThere is no road from waiting to good.\n\n470\n00:20:13,970 --> 00:20:16,880\nThe only road goes through bad.\n\n471\n00:20:16,910 --> 00:20:21,600\n\"The only road goes through bad.\" Ben, that is our message today, I think.\n\n472\n00:20:22,440 --> 00:20:23,160\nDo not wait.\n\n473\n00:20:23,640 --> 00:20:24,500\nStart small.\n\n474\n00:20:24,580 --> 00:20:25,140\nStart bad.\n\n475\n00:20:25,440 --> 00:20:25,970\nStart now.\n\n476\n00:20:26,760 --> 00:20:28,180\nIt is a Tuesday somewhere.\n\n477\n00:20:28,200 --> 00:20:29,660\nIt is raining somewhere.\n\n478\n00:20:29,680 --> 00:20:31,120\nSomebody is tired.\n\n479\n00:20:31,140 --> 00:20:32,940\nAnd somebody is starting anyway.\n\n480\n00:20:33,740 --> 00:20:34,700\nBe that somebody.\n\n481\n00:20:35,620 --> 00:20:36,520\nBe that somebody.\n\n482\n00:20:37,420 --> 00:20:42,470\nMia, let's repeat today's important words, slowly, because our listeners want to remember them.\n\n483\n00:20:43,560 --> 00:20:44,639\nWait.\n\n484\n00:20:44,649 --> 00:20:47,720\nTo stay and do nothing until something happens.\n\n485\n00:20:47,900 --> 00:20:49,700\nI waited two years.\n\n486\n00:20:49,709 --> 00:20:51,520\nStop waiting.\n\n487\n00:20:51,560 --> 00:20:52,000\nPerfect.\n\n488\n00:20:52,550 --> 00:20:54,540\nWith no problems, no mistakes.\n\n489\n00:20:55,120 --> 00:20:56,120\nThe perfect moment.\n\n490\n00:20:57,060 --> 00:20:58,020\nNothing is perfect.\n\n491\n00:20:59,040 --> 00:21:00,020\nStart.\n\n492\n00:21:00,080 --> 00:21:00,640\nTo begin.\n\n493\n00:21:01,460 --> 00:21:02,080\nStart small.\n\n494\n00:21:02,700 --> 00:21:03,340\nStart now.\n\n495\n00:21:04,180 --> 00:21:04,460\nNow.\n\n496\n00:21:05,100 --> 00:21:05,740\nThis moment.\n\n497\n00:21:06,300 --> 00:21:06,840\nNot later.\n\n498\n00:21:07,780 --> 00:21:08,720\nNow is enough.\n\n499\n00:21:09,800 --> 00:21:10,040\nReady.\n\n500\n00:21:10,660 --> 00:21:11,200\nPrepared.\n\n501\n00:21:12,100 --> 00:21:12,840\nI am not ready.\n\n502\n00:21:13,480 --> 00:21:14,340\nNobody is ready.\n\n503\n00:21:15,280 --> 00:21:15,580\nWait.\n\n504\n00:21:16,200 --> 00:21:16,640\nPerfect.\n\n505\n00:21:17,420 --> 00:21:17,760\nStart.\n\n506\n00:21:18,520 --> 00:21:18,800\nNow.\n\n507\n00:21:19,760 --> 00:21:20,060\nReady.\n\n508\n00:21:20,940 --> 00:21:21,580\nFive words.\n\n509\n00:21:22,060 --> 00:21:23,400\nAnd one big idea.\n\n510\n00:21:24,180 --> 00:21:25,860\nStop waiting for the perfect moment.\n\n511\n00:21:26,900 --> 00:21:29,100\nNow, Ben, do you have a question for our listeners?\n\n512\n00:21:29,960 --> 00:21:30,480\nYes, Mia.\n\n513\n00:21:31,020 --> 00:21:32,940\nEveryone listening, here is my question.\n\n514\n00:21:33,560 --> 00:21:34,760\nWhat are you waiting to start?\n\n515\n00:21:35,400 --> 00:21:35,800\nSwimming?\n\n516\n00:21:36,360 --> 00:21:36,760\nA call?\n\n517\n00:21:37,540 --> 00:21:38,240\nSpeaking English?\n\n518\n00:21:38,920 --> 00:21:39,240\nA letter?\n\n519\n00:21:40,040 --> 00:21:42,340\nWrite it in the comments in one short sentence.\n\n520\n00:21:42,890 --> 00:21:50,460\nAnd then, if you want, add three more words: \"I start today.\" For example, \"I am waiting to call my brother.\n\n521\n00:21:51,080 --> 00:21:51,880\nI start today.\"\n\n522\n00:21:52,639 --> 00:21:57,980\n\"I start today.\" Reading those sentences is the best part of our week.\n\n523\n00:21:58,480 --> 00:22:02,379\nAnd if this episode helped you, please subscribe to Two Cups English.\n\n524\n00:22:02,699 --> 00:22:04,919\nWe make a new episode every few days.\n\n525\n00:22:05,480 --> 00:22:09,620\nSlow English, simple words, and one true story each time.\n\n526\n00:22:10,239 --> 00:22:12,739\nThe full text of this episode is in the description.\n\n527\n00:22:13,319 --> 00:22:14,399\nRead while you listen.\n\n528\n00:22:14,999 --> 00:22:16,159\nOr read later.\n\n529\n00:22:16,999 --> 00:22:17,940\nActually, no.\n\n530\n00:22:18,499 --> 00:22:19,039\nNot later.\n\n531\n00:22:19,700 --> 00:22:20,259\nRead now.\n\n532\n00:22:21,459 --> 00:22:23,179\nThank you for spending this time with us.\n\n533\n00:22:23,940 --> 00:22:25,339\nMaybe you are on a bus.\n\n534\n00:22:25,959 --> 00:22:26,859\nMaybe in the kitchen.\n\n535\n00:22:27,779 --> 00:22:29,599\nMaybe it is a bad Tuesday.\n\n536\n00:22:30,739 --> 00:22:30,979\nGood.\n\n537\n00:22:31,540 --> 00:22:33,499\nBad Tuesdays are where things begin.\n\n538\n00:22:34,679 --> 00:22:36,319\nSay one sentence out loud.\n\n539\n00:22:37,399 --> 00:22:38,839\nPut the swimsuit in the bag.\n\n540\n00:22:39,899 --> 00:22:40,699\nCall the person.\n\n541\n00:22:41,919 --> 00:22:45,179\nNow is not perfect, and now is enough.\n\n542\n00:22:47,219 --> 00:22:47,899\nThis is Mia.\n\n543\n00:22:48,259 --> 00:22:49,179\nAnd this is Ben.\n\n544\n00:22:49,899 --> 00:22:51,599\nThis was Two Cups English.\n\n545\n00:22:52,319 --> 00:22:53,499\nStop waiting.\n\n546\n00:22:53,959 --> 00:22:55,200\nWe will see you next time."},{"code":"CUP04","file":"CUP04.srt","srt":"1\n00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,260\nHello everyone, and welcome to Two Cups English.\n\n2\n00:00:03,840 --> 00:00:06,720\nI'm Mia, and Ben is here with me.\n\n3\n00:00:06,780 --> 00:00:09,560\nThis podcast is for people who are learning English.\n\n4\n00:00:10,220 --> 00:00:11,060\nWe speak slowly.\n\n5\n00:00:11,640 --> 00:00:12,970\nWe use simple words.\n\n6\n00:00:13,560 --> 00:00:16,400\nAnd we repeat the important words many times.\n\n7\n00:00:17,320 --> 00:00:20,540\nToday, our topic is one task at a time.\n\n8\n00:00:21,300 --> 00:00:23,300\nOne thing, then the next thing.\n\n9\n00:00:24,400 --> 00:00:26,560\nBen, how long is your list today?\n\n10\n00:00:27,620 --> 00:00:28,280\nHi Mia.\n\n11\n00:00:28,320 --> 00:00:28,860\nHi everyone.\n\n12\n00:00:30,020 --> 00:00:32,060\nMy list today has fourteen things.\n\n13\n00:00:33,260 --> 00:00:33,760\nEmails.\n\n14\n00:00:34,460 --> 00:00:35,300\nA call to the bank.\n\n15\n00:00:36,040 --> 00:00:36,840\nA broken lamp.\n\n16\n00:00:37,960 --> 00:00:41,860\nFourteen things, and so far I have done only two.\n\n17\n00:00:43,200 --> 00:00:46,520\nAnd I looked at the list for a long time before I started.\n\n18\n00:00:47,760 --> 00:00:48,580\nWhat about you, Mia?\n\n19\n00:00:49,240 --> 00:00:50,320\nHow long is your list?\n\n20\n00:00:51,660 --> 00:00:53,180\nToday, only nine, Ben.\n\n21\n00:00:53,980 --> 00:00:55,500\nNine is a good day for me.\n\n22\n00:00:56,120 --> 00:00:58,920\nBut last Thursday, it was twenty-three.\n\n23\n00:00:59,700 --> 00:01:02,040\nTwenty-three things, and one day.\n\n24\n00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:03,600\nEmails.\n\n25\n00:01:03,900 --> 00:01:04,540\nA report.\n\n26\n00:01:05,080 --> 00:01:05,760\nCall the bank.\n\n27\n00:01:06,220 --> 00:01:07,000\nBuy a gift.\n\n28\n00:01:07,520 --> 00:01:08,400\nFix the printer.\n\n29\n00:01:09,300 --> 00:01:11,460\nI looked at the list, and I did nothing.\n\n30\n00:01:12,040 --> 00:01:13,460\nFor one hour.\n\n31\n00:01:14,540 --> 00:01:16,840\nOne hour of nothing, Mia?\n\n32\n00:01:16,920 --> 00:01:18,570\nWith twenty-three things waiting?\n\n33\n00:01:19,580 --> 00:01:20,580\nI know that hour.\n\n34\n00:01:21,520 --> 00:01:26,240\nYou sit, you look, and the list looks back at you.\n\n35\n00:01:26,300 --> 00:01:27,179\nWhat did it feel like?\n\n36\n00:01:28,680 --> 00:01:29,760\nOne hour, Ben.\n\n37\n00:01:30,600 --> 00:01:33,250\nI looked, and I felt heavy, and I did nothing.\n\n38\n00:01:34,040 --> 00:01:36,399\nTwenty-three things is not a list.\n\n39\n00:01:36,480 --> 00:01:37,490\nIt is a wall.\n\n40\n00:01:38,430 --> 00:01:40,540\nAnd I think many people know that wall.\n\n41\n00:01:41,340 --> 00:01:44,400\nYou look at everything, and you cannot start anything.\n\n42\n00:01:45,400 --> 00:01:46,320\nA wall, Mia.\n\n43\n00:01:46,840 --> 00:01:47,200\nYes.\n\n44\n00:01:47,940 --> 00:01:49,580\nI know that wall very well.\n\n45\n00:01:50,610 --> 00:01:53,370\nAnd today, we talk about how to get over it.\n\n46\n00:01:54,580 --> 00:01:55,580\nSo here is our plan.\n\n47\n00:01:56,720 --> 00:01:59,580\nIn part one, we talk about our own lists.\n\n48\n00:02:00,560 --> 00:02:02,280\nWhat happens when the list is long.\n\n49\n00:02:03,720 --> 00:02:07,620\nIn part two, we ask why one task at a time works.\n\n50\n00:02:08,820 --> 00:02:10,600\nAnd I have a true story for that.\n\n51\n00:02:11,900 --> 00:02:15,820\nIn part three, we share simple ideas for the next heavy day.\n\n52\n00:02:16,940 --> 00:02:18,860\nDoes that sound good?\n\n53\n00:02:18,870 --> 00:02:19,880\nThat sounds good, Ben.\n\n54\n00:02:20,560 --> 00:02:24,760\nAnd to everyone listening, please do not worry if you miss something.\n\n55\n00:02:24,820 --> 00:02:26,460\nYou will hear it again.\n\n56\n00:02:26,500 --> 00:02:31,480\nYou can listen while you walk, while you cook, or while you look at your own long list.\n\n57\n00:02:32,620 --> 00:02:33,220\nOkay, Ben.\n\n58\n00:02:33,480 --> 00:02:34,079\nPart one.\n\n59\n00:02:35,140 --> 00:02:38,820\nDo you want to hear what happened after my hour of nothing?\n\n60\n00:02:38,880 --> 00:02:40,160\nOf course, Mia.\n\n61\n00:02:40,340 --> 00:02:43,140\nI have been waiting for this all week.\n\n62\n00:02:43,220 --> 00:02:45,540\nHow did you start, in the end?\n\n63\n00:02:45,580 --> 00:02:47,280\nDid the wall go away by itself?\n\n64\n00:02:48,080 --> 00:02:49,180\nOr did somebody help?\n\n65\n00:02:50,380 --> 00:02:51,540\nMy friend called, Ben.\n\n66\n00:02:52,120 --> 00:02:55,820\nShe asked, \"What are you doing?\" I said, \"Nothing.\n\n67\n00:02:56,230 --> 00:03:00,820\nI have twenty-three things.\" And she said something simple.\n\n68\n00:03:00,900 --> 00:03:08,180\nShe said, \"What is the first one?\" I said, \"Reply to Anna's email.\" She said, \"Do that.\n\n69\n00:03:08,600 --> 00:03:09,700\nThen call me back.\"\n\n70\n00:03:10,780 --> 00:03:15,650\n\"Then call me back.\" Mia, that is a good friend.\n\n71\n00:03:16,810 --> 00:03:19,109\nShe did not ask about the twenty-three things.\n\n72\n00:03:19,880 --> 00:03:21,210\nShe asked about one.\n\n73\n00:03:22,540 --> 00:03:24,520\nSo you did the one email?\n\n74\n00:03:24,620 --> 00:03:24,991\nJust that one?\n\n75\n00:03:26,560 --> 00:03:27,540\nI did, Ben.\n\n76\n00:03:28,240 --> 00:03:29,030\nOne email.\n\n77\n00:03:29,600 --> 00:03:30,280\nFive minutes.\n\n78\n00:03:30,900 --> 00:03:31,800\nThen I called her back.\n\n79\n00:03:32,360 --> 00:03:36,320\nAnd she said, \"What is the next one?\" And we did this all morning.\n\n80\n00:03:36,740 --> 00:03:37,480\nOne task.\n\n81\n00:03:37,920 --> 00:03:38,660\nThen the next.\n\n82\n00:03:39,120 --> 00:03:42,000\nBy lunch, I had finished eleven things.\n\n83\n00:03:42,080 --> 00:03:42,600\nEleven.\n\n84\n00:03:43,040 --> 00:03:46,650\nIn one morning, after one hour of nothing.\n\n85\n00:03:46,680 --> 00:03:47,740\nEleven things, Mia.\n\n86\n00:03:48,380 --> 00:03:50,399\nThat is a very good morning.\n\n87\n00:03:50,440 --> 00:03:52,320\nAnd the question was so simple.\n\n88\n00:03:52,350 --> 00:03:58,220\n\"What is the first one?\" Not, \"How will I do all of this?\" That question makes the wall.\n\n89\n00:03:59,080 --> 00:04:02,440\nBut \"What is the first one?\" That question makes a door.\n\n90\n00:04:03,220 --> 00:04:04,260\nA door, Ben.\n\n91\n00:04:04,620 --> 00:04:05,000\nYes.\n\n92\n00:04:05,540 --> 00:04:07,500\nOne email is not a wall.\n\n93\n00:04:08,080 --> 00:04:10,200\nOne email is a thing with edges.\n\n94\n00:04:10,700 --> 00:04:12,620\nI can see the start and the end.\n\n95\n00:04:13,280 --> 00:04:14,020\nFive minutes.\n\n96\n00:04:14,960 --> 00:04:16,120\nWhat about you?\n\n97\n00:04:16,160 --> 00:04:17,860\nWhat do you do when your list is a wall?\n\n98\n00:04:18,600 --> 00:04:20,020\nI have one question too, Mia.\n\n99\n00:04:20,780 --> 00:04:25,580\nI ask, \"What can I finish in ten minutes?\" Not the most important thing.\n\n100\n00:04:26,120 --> 00:04:27,040\nNot the hardest thing.\n\n101\n00:04:27,700 --> 00:04:29,840\nThe thing I can finish in ten minutes.\n\n102\n00:04:30,800 --> 00:04:31,900\nReply to an email.\n\n103\n00:04:32,640 --> 00:04:33,460\nPay one bill.\n\n104\n00:04:34,300 --> 00:04:36,290\nBecause finishing is the medicine.\n\n105\n00:04:36,980 --> 00:04:38,520\nFinishing is the medicine?\n\n106\n00:04:39,240 --> 00:04:40,940\nThat is a strange sentence, Ben.\n\n107\n00:04:41,500 --> 00:04:44,740\nI thought starting was the hard part, not finishing.\n\n108\n00:04:44,780 --> 00:04:45,360\nWhat do you mean?\n\n109\n00:04:46,160 --> 00:04:49,940\nWhen I finish one small thing, something in my head changes, Mia.\n\n110\n00:04:50,790 --> 00:04:52,580\nThe wall becomes a little smaller.\n\n111\n00:04:53,200 --> 00:04:54,640\nAnd I want to finish another one.\n\n112\n00:04:55,420 --> 00:04:57,250\nFinishing makes the next one easier.\n\n113\n00:04:57,980 --> 00:04:59,540\nSo I start with the easy finish.\n\n114\n00:05:00,160 --> 00:05:00,690\nTen minutes.\n\n115\n00:05:01,400 --> 00:05:02,420\nDone.\n\n116\n00:05:02,480 --> 00:05:03,880\nThen the next one.\n\n117\n00:05:03,890 --> 00:05:08,420\n\"Finishing makes the next one easier.\" Ben, I felt that on Thursday.\n\n118\n00:05:09,020 --> 00:05:11,940\nAfter the first email, there was a small click.\n\n119\n00:05:12,060 --> 00:05:12,310\nDone.\n\n120\n00:05:12,920 --> 00:05:14,820\nAnd the click made me want the next click.\n\n121\n00:05:15,380 --> 00:05:16,880\nI think that is the secret.\n\n122\n00:05:17,280 --> 00:05:18,180\nNot the task.\n\n123\n00:05:18,580 --> 00:05:19,100\nThe click.\n\n124\n00:05:20,280 --> 00:05:21,160\nThe click, Mia.\n\n125\n00:05:21,520 --> 00:05:21,800\nYes.\n\n126\n00:05:22,480 --> 00:05:24,160\nTwenty-three things give no click.\n\n127\n00:05:24,860 --> 00:05:25,880\nThey are never finished.\n\n128\n00:05:26,740 --> 00:05:29,220\nBut one email gives a click in five minutes.\n\n129\n00:05:29,940 --> 00:05:33,740\nAnd eleven clicks in a morning is a very good morning.\n\n130\n00:05:33,780 --> 00:05:36,240\nCan I tell you about a task I carried for four months?\n\n131\n00:05:37,120 --> 00:05:38,560\nFour months, Ben?\n\n132\n00:05:38,620 --> 00:05:40,760\nThat is a long time to carry one task.\n\n133\n00:05:41,260 --> 00:05:42,200\nWas it a big task?\n\n134\n00:05:42,620 --> 00:05:43,200\nA report?\n\n135\n00:05:43,460 --> 00:05:44,660\nA project?\n\n136\n00:05:44,680 --> 00:05:45,580\nWhat kind of task?\n\n137\n00:05:46,280 --> 00:05:47,880\nA form, Mia.\n\n138\n00:05:48,700 --> 00:05:50,920\nOne form for the city, for my car.\n\n139\n00:05:51,780 --> 00:05:52,849\nIt needed twenty minutes.\n\n140\n00:05:53,600 --> 00:05:55,820\nAnd I looked at it every day for four months.\n\n141\n00:05:56,620 --> 00:05:58,820\nEvery day, twenty seconds of guilt.\n\n142\n00:05:59,660 --> 00:06:02,600\nTwenty seconds, times one hundred and twenty days.\n\n143\n00:06:03,400 --> 00:06:06,160\nThat is forty minutes of guilt for a twenty-minute task.\n\n144\n00:06:07,300 --> 00:06:09,770\nForty minutes of guilt for twenty minutes of work.\n\n145\n00:06:10,400 --> 00:06:12,300\nBen, that is a bad trade.\n\n146\n00:06:12,320 --> 00:06:14,560\nAnd I think I have ten forms like that in my life.\n\n147\n00:06:15,300 --> 00:06:16,340\nWhat happened to the form?\n\n148\n00:06:16,700 --> 00:06:17,680\nDid you do it in the end?\n\n149\n00:06:18,600 --> 00:06:20,080\nOne Tuesday, I did it, Mia.\n\n150\n00:06:20,860 --> 00:06:21,540\nTwenty minutes.\n\n151\n00:06:22,320 --> 00:06:22,569\nClick.\n\n152\n00:06:23,300 --> 00:06:24,280\nAnd the guilt was gone.\n\n153\n00:06:25,320 --> 00:06:29,700\nSo now, when a small task knocks for the third time, I do it next.\n\n154\n00:06:30,440 --> 00:06:31,720\nNot because it is important.\n\n155\n00:06:32,280 --> 00:06:34,610\nBecause carrying it costs more than finishing it.\n\n156\n00:06:35,360 --> 00:06:41,840\n\"Carrying it costs more than finishing it.\" Ben, I have a form like that right now, actually.\n\n157\n00:06:42,520 --> 00:06:42,780\nOkay.\n\n158\n00:06:43,280 --> 00:06:45,580\nThat is my next task, after this recording.\n\n159\n00:06:46,130 --> 00:06:48,100\nLet me say it out loud so it is real.\n\n160\n00:06:48,760 --> 00:06:50,280\nNext, the form.\n\n161\n00:06:50,880 --> 00:06:51,600\nTwenty minutes.\n\n162\n00:06:52,120 --> 00:06:52,420\nClick.\n\n163\n00:06:53,180 --> 00:06:54,260\nNext, the form.\n\n164\n00:06:54,760 --> 00:06:55,620\nGood, Mia.\n\n165\n00:06:55,660 --> 00:06:56,680\nWe will ask you next time.\n\n166\n00:06:57,500 --> 00:06:59,260\nAnd what about the other twenty-two things?\n\n167\n00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:01,980\nWhile you do one email, do they go away?\n\n168\n00:07:02,920 --> 00:07:03,879\nNo, Ben.\n\n169\n00:07:03,920 --> 00:07:05,000\nThey wait outside the door.\n\n170\n00:07:05,520 --> 00:07:06,180\nBut they knock.\n\n171\n00:07:06,760 --> 00:07:10,420\nWhile I am doing one thing, my head says, \"What about the report?\n\n172\n00:07:10,640 --> 00:07:14,420\nWhat about the bank?\" And the knocking pulls me out of the one thing.\n\n173\n00:07:15,280 --> 00:07:16,660\nDo you know that knocking?\n\n174\n00:07:17,540 --> 00:07:18,300\nEvery day, Mia.\n\n175\n00:07:19,040 --> 00:07:20,560\nSo here is what I do.\n\n176\n00:07:20,600 --> 00:07:22,280\nI write the knocking down.\n\n177\n00:07:22,320 --> 00:07:23,920\nOn a paper next to me.\n\n178\n00:07:23,970 --> 00:07:24,430\n\"Report.\n\n179\n00:07:25,140 --> 00:07:28,540\nBank.\" And I say to the paper, \"You are next.\n\n180\n00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:32,410\nWait.\" And my head is quiet, because the thing is written.\n\n181\n00:07:32,740 --> 00:07:33,290\nIt is safe.\n\n182\n00:07:33,660 --> 00:07:35,380\nIt will not be forgotten.\n\n183\n00:07:35,500 --> 00:07:36,840\nWrite the knocking down.\n\n184\n00:07:37,420 --> 00:07:38,440\nThat is smart, Ben.\n\n185\n00:07:39,030 --> 00:07:42,800\nThe paper holds the twenty-two, so my head can hold the one.\n\n186\n00:07:43,460 --> 00:07:45,200\nI do something similar with my phone.\n\n187\n00:07:45,860 --> 00:07:47,200\nPhone in another room.\n\n188\n00:07:47,700 --> 00:07:51,000\nBecause the phone is twenty-two more things, knocking.\n\n189\n00:07:51,660 --> 00:07:53,280\nEvery message is a task.\n\n190\n00:07:53,740 --> 00:07:55,640\nEvery message is a task.\n\n191\n00:07:55,660 --> 00:07:56,760\nYes, Mia.\n\n192\n00:07:56,900 --> 00:08:01,760\nSo, paper for the knocking, another room for the phone, and one task on the table.\n\n193\n00:08:02,720 --> 00:08:04,120\nOkay, so that was part one.\n\n194\n00:08:04,560 --> 00:08:05,120\nOur lists.\n\n195\n00:08:05,780 --> 00:08:13,200\nTwenty-three things, one hour of nothing, a good friend, eleven clicks, and a form that waited four months.\n\n196\n00:08:14,260 --> 00:08:15,100\nNow, part two.\n\n197\n00:08:15,820 --> 00:08:17,540\nWhy does one task at a time work?\n\n198\n00:08:18,250 --> 00:08:20,750\nMia, do you want to start, or should I tell my story?\n\n199\n00:08:21,400 --> 00:08:22,960\nTell your story, Ben.\n\n200\n00:08:23,020 --> 00:08:26,430\nYou said it is a true story, and you said it is small.\n\n201\n00:08:26,500 --> 00:08:27,960\nI like small true stories.\n\n202\n00:08:28,290 --> 00:08:30,300\nThey stay in my head longer than big ones.\n\n203\n00:08:31,100 --> 00:08:31,940\nOkay, part two.\n\n204\n00:08:32,660 --> 00:08:34,560\nThis is a true story, Mia.\n\n205\n00:08:34,620 --> 00:08:37,780\nIt is a small story from a book, and I think about it every week.\n\n206\n00:08:38,460 --> 00:08:42,140\nThe book is \"Bird by Bird,\" by an American writer, Anne Lamott.\n\n207\n00:08:42,840 --> 00:08:44,460\nIt came out in 1994.\n\n208\n00:08:45,220 --> 00:08:48,220\nShe tells a story about her brother, when he was ten years old.\n\n209\n00:08:48,860 --> 00:08:49,760\nTen years old?\n\n210\n00:08:50,320 --> 00:08:52,000\nBen, I remember being ten.\n\n211\n00:08:52,540 --> 00:08:54,040\nEverything was big at ten.\n\n212\n00:08:54,540 --> 00:08:56,220\nA school report was a mountain.\n\n213\n00:08:56,780 --> 00:08:57,700\nWhat happened to him?\n\n214\n00:08:58,440 --> 00:09:00,400\nHe had a school report about birds, Mia.\n\n215\n00:09:01,040 --> 00:09:02,420\nHe had three months to do it.\n\n216\n00:09:03,040 --> 00:09:04,540\nAnd he did nothing for three months.\n\n217\n00:09:05,240 --> 00:09:09,500\nThen, the night before the report was due, he sat at the kitchen table.\n\n218\n00:09:09,560 --> 00:09:09,950\nCrying.\n\n219\n00:09:11,160 --> 00:09:13,300\nOh, I know that table, Ben.\n\n220\n00:09:13,360 --> 00:09:14,980\nI have sat at that table.\n\n221\n00:09:15,020 --> 00:09:17,460\nBooks all around, and nothing on the paper.\n\n222\n00:09:18,400 --> 00:09:19,320\nWe all have, Mia.\n\n223\n00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:22,240\nHe had books about birds all around him.\n\n224\n00:09:22,280 --> 00:09:23,080\nPaper.\n\n225\n00:09:23,120 --> 00:09:23,650\nPencils.\n\n226\n00:09:24,220 --> 00:09:26,250\nAnd a whole report to write in one night.\n\n227\n00:09:26,900 --> 00:09:27,840\nHe could not start.\n\n228\n00:09:28,320 --> 00:09:29,160\nIt was too big.\n\n229\n00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:31,800\nAnd then his father sat down next to him.\n\n230\n00:09:32,440 --> 00:09:33,720\nHe put his arm around the boy.\n\n231\n00:09:34,280 --> 00:09:36,620\nAnd he said, \"Bird by bird, buddy.\n\n232\n00:09:37,320 --> 00:09:38,680\nJust take it bird by bird.\"\n\n233\n00:09:39,390 --> 00:09:42,360\n\"Bird by bird.\" Ben, I love that.\n\n234\n00:09:43,180 --> 00:09:43,840\nOne bird.\n\n235\n00:09:44,280 --> 00:09:45,560\nWrite about one bird.\n\n236\n00:09:46,160 --> 00:09:47,180\nThen the next bird.\n\n237\n00:09:47,900 --> 00:09:51,180\nAnd the father did not say, \"You should have started three months ago.\"\n\n238\n00:09:51,740 --> 00:09:53,720\nThat was true, Mia, but it did not help.\n\n239\n00:09:54,300 --> 00:10:00,520\nHe did not say, \"Work faster.\" He said, \"One bird.\" He made the wall into a bird.\n\n240\n00:10:01,180 --> 00:10:02,820\nAnd a boy can write about one bird.\n\n241\n00:10:03,630 --> 00:10:05,320\nThat is the whole story.\n\n242\n00:10:05,400 --> 00:10:10,310\nAnd Anne Lamott made it the title of her book, because it is the best advice she ever heard about work.\n\n243\n00:10:11,010 --> 00:10:12,900\nThe best advice about work, Ben.\n\n244\n00:10:13,480 --> 00:10:15,520\nAnd I think I know why it works.\n\n245\n00:10:15,600 --> 00:10:17,920\nOur heads cannot hold twenty-three things.\n\n246\n00:10:17,960 --> 00:10:18,460\nNot really.\n\n247\n00:10:19,120 --> 00:10:21,160\nWe can hold one thing, maybe two.\n\n248\n00:10:21,940 --> 00:10:25,100\nWhen we look at twenty-three, the head does not see tasks.\n\n249\n00:10:25,380 --> 00:10:26,360\nIt sees a cloud.\n\n250\n00:10:27,080 --> 00:10:27,660\nIs that right?\n\n251\n00:10:28,220 --> 00:10:29,040\nThat is right, Mia.\n\n252\n00:10:29,550 --> 00:10:30,940\nAnd nobody can start a cloud.\n\n253\n00:10:31,800 --> 00:10:33,920\nOne bird is a thing with edges.\n\n254\n00:10:34,020 --> 00:10:36,560\nOne email is a thing with edges.\n\n255\n00:10:36,660 --> 00:10:38,900\nSo the trick is not to work harder.\n\n256\n00:10:38,940 --> 00:10:41,020\nThe trick is to look at one thing with edges.\n\n257\n00:10:41,580 --> 00:10:43,100\nAnd then the next thing with edges.\n\n258\n00:10:43,840 --> 00:10:47,640\n\"Nobody can start a cloud.\" Ben, can I ask about time?\n\n259\n00:10:48,240 --> 00:10:51,000\nBecause on Thursday, I noticed something strange about time.\n\n260\n00:10:51,760 --> 00:10:52,600\nStrange how, Mia?\n\n261\n00:10:53,100 --> 00:10:53,860\nTime is time.\n\n262\n00:10:54,640 --> 00:10:58,100\nTwenty-three things or one thing, the day has the same hours.\n\n263\n00:10:58,760 --> 00:10:59,540\nWhat did you notice?\n\n264\n00:11:00,300 --> 00:11:03,840\nWhen I looked at twenty-three things, I felt I had no time, Ben.\n\n265\n00:11:04,340 --> 00:11:05,240\nNot one minute.\n\n266\n00:11:05,880 --> 00:11:08,220\nBut when I looked at one email, I had time.\n\n267\n00:11:08,660 --> 00:11:09,340\nFive minutes.\n\n268\n00:11:09,730 --> 00:11:10,200\nPlenty.\n\n269\n00:11:10,960 --> 00:11:12,400\nTime did not change.\n\n270\n00:11:12,460 --> 00:11:13,740\nThe day was the same length.\n\n271\n00:11:14,380 --> 00:11:17,280\nBut twenty-three things ate all my time before I started.\n\n272\n00:11:17,860 --> 00:11:20,500\nAnd one task took only its own five minutes.\n\n273\n00:11:21,080 --> 00:11:22,340\n\"A cloud eats time.\n\n274\n00:11:22,920 --> 00:11:26,280\nA task uses time.\" Mia, that is exactly it.\n\n275\n00:11:26,920 --> 00:11:28,660\nAnd I make a mistake with time, too.\n\n276\n00:11:29,440 --> 00:11:32,660\nI think, \"I have eight hours, and twenty-three things.\n\n277\n00:11:33,140 --> 00:11:37,300\nThat is twenty minutes each.\" And then I try to do everything in twenty minutes.\n\n278\n00:11:37,860 --> 00:11:39,100\nAnd nothing gets finished.\n\n279\n00:11:39,880 --> 00:11:41,480\nI am not doing tasks.\n\n280\n00:11:41,560 --> 00:11:42,600\nI am watching a clock.\n\n281\n00:11:43,420 --> 00:11:45,060\nWatching a clock, Ben.\n\n282\n00:11:45,100 --> 00:11:48,559\nAnd the clock makes you fast, and fast makes you bad.\n\n283\n00:11:48,680 --> 00:11:50,540\nSo what is the good way to think about time?\n\n284\n00:11:51,380 --> 00:11:53,420\nOne task, and the time it needs, Mia.\n\n285\n00:11:54,220 --> 00:11:55,840\nNot the time I want to give it.\n\n286\n00:11:55,880 --> 00:11:56,749\nThe time it needs.\n\n287\n00:11:57,529 --> 00:11:59,540\nThe email needs five minutes.\n\n288\n00:11:59,580 --> 00:12:00,980\nThe report needs two hours.\n\n289\n00:12:01,640 --> 00:12:02,560\nI do not mix them.\n\n290\n00:12:03,400 --> 00:12:06,060\nThe time belongs to the task, not to the clock.\n\n291\n00:12:06,940 --> 00:12:10,290\nAnd when I work this way, I am slower, but I finish more.\n\n292\n00:12:11,040 --> 00:12:13,180\nSlower, but you finish more.\n\n293\n00:12:14,160 --> 00:12:17,040\nBecause finished things do not come back, Ben.\n\n294\n00:12:17,780 --> 00:12:21,040\nHalf-finished things come back tomorrow, and knock.\n\n295\n00:12:21,820 --> 00:12:25,480\nMy half-finished things are the loudest knockers.\n\n296\n00:12:26,260 --> 00:12:28,080\nThe report I started and stopped.\n\n297\n00:12:28,710 --> 00:12:30,600\nThe email I opened and closed.\n\n298\n00:12:32,260 --> 00:12:33,660\nThe loudest knockers, Mia.\n\n299\n00:12:33,980 --> 00:12:34,320\nYes.\n\n300\n00:12:34,920 --> 00:12:39,800\nSo maybe the rule is, do not start the next one until this one is finished.\n\n301\n00:12:40,480 --> 00:12:42,080\nOne bird, fully written.\n\n302\n00:12:42,680 --> 00:12:43,579\nThen the next bird.\n\n303\n00:12:44,240 --> 00:12:47,100\nNot five birds, half-written, all crying.\n\n304\n00:12:48,220 --> 00:12:53,480\n\"Not five birds, half-written, all crying.\" Ben, I want to put that on my wall.\n\n305\n00:12:54,580 --> 00:12:56,020\nOkay, so that was part two.\n\n306\n00:12:56,500 --> 00:12:58,640\nWhy one task at a time works.\n\n307\n00:12:59,100 --> 00:13:00,860\nWe talked about the boy and his father.\n\n308\n00:13:01,409 --> 00:13:04,080\nWe said the head cannot hold twenty-three things.\n\n309\n00:13:04,700 --> 00:13:09,170\nAnd we said a cloud eats time, and a task uses time.\n\n310\n00:13:10,020 --> 00:13:11,020\nNow, part three.\n\n311\n00:13:11,760 --> 00:13:14,100\nSimple ideas for the next heavy day.\n\n312\n00:13:14,680 --> 00:13:15,940\nBen, you go first.\n\n313\n00:13:16,960 --> 00:13:17,820\nOkay, part three.\n\n314\n00:13:18,620 --> 00:13:20,820\nMy first idea is for big tasks, Mia.\n\n315\n00:13:21,440 --> 00:13:25,080\nSome people are thinking, \"But Ben, the report is two hours.\n\n316\n00:13:25,360 --> 00:13:28,220\nI cannot finish it in ten minutes.\" Yes.\n\n317\n00:13:28,720 --> 00:13:32,220\nSo for big tasks, I make a small first task.\n\n318\n00:13:32,840 --> 00:13:42,460\nNot \"write the report.\" But \"write the first page.\" Or \"write the title and three lines.\" That is a bird.\n\n319\n00:13:42,470 --> 00:13:43,520\nThat can be finished.\n\n320\n00:13:44,580 --> 00:13:47,000\nA thing with one page finished, Ben.\n\n321\n00:13:47,620 --> 00:13:50,300\nThat is the swimsuit from our last episode.\n\n322\n00:13:50,380 --> 00:13:51,840\nMake the thing ready.\n\n323\n00:13:52,850 --> 00:13:54,510\nMy first idea is a question.\n\n324\n00:13:55,120 --> 00:13:59,410\nWhen a new task comes while I am working, I ask, \"Is it on fire?\"\n\n325\n00:14:00,470 --> 00:14:03,520\n\"Is it on fire?\" Mia, I like that.\n\n326\n00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:07,640\nBecause every new task feels like fire when it arrives.\n\n327\n00:14:08,240 --> 00:14:11,720\nThe message, the call, the small problem.\n\n328\n00:14:11,820 --> 00:14:13,920\nAnd is it on fire?\n\n329\n00:14:14,040 --> 00:14:14,370\nUsually?\n\n330\n00:14:15,720 --> 00:14:17,080\nAlmost never, Ben.\n\n331\n00:14:17,120 --> 00:14:18,900\nIt feels on fire.\n\n332\n00:14:18,960 --> 00:14:20,409\nIt knocks like fire.\n\n333\n00:14:21,009 --> 00:14:24,320\nBut most things can wait twenty minutes while I finish one email.\n\n334\n00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:26,380\nReal fires are rare.\n\n335\n00:14:26,840 --> 00:14:27,760\nMaybe one a month.\n\n336\n00:14:28,580 --> 00:14:30,160\nEverything else goes on the paper.\n\n337\n00:14:30,680 --> 00:14:31,280\nIt waits.\n\n338\n00:14:31,820 --> 00:14:33,400\nI finish my bird first.\n\n339\n00:14:34,560 --> 00:14:35,980\nReal fires are rare, Mia.\n\n340\n00:14:36,509 --> 00:14:39,560\nAnd sometimes the \"fire\" is not even there anymore.\n\n341\n00:14:40,060 --> 00:14:41,080\nIt solved itself.\n\n342\n00:14:41,900 --> 00:14:44,120\nI learned that on my worst day.\n\n343\n00:14:44,240 --> 00:14:48,080\nA deadline, a sick child, a broken car.\n\n344\n00:14:48,140 --> 00:14:54,260\nI tried to do it all at once, and by six o'clock, everything was half-finished, all crying.\n\n345\n00:14:55,040 --> 00:14:56,700\nNow, one at a time.\n\n346\n00:14:57,800 --> 00:15:00,560\nMy second idea is about the moment between tasks.\n\n347\n00:15:01,280 --> 00:15:02,240\nI finish one thing.\n\n348\n00:15:02,920 --> 00:15:03,200\nClick.\n\n349\n00:15:03,840 --> 00:15:05,580\nAnd then there is a small hole.\n\n350\n00:15:06,400 --> 00:15:07,039\nTen seconds.\n\n351\n00:15:08,200 --> 00:15:08,680\nA hole?\n\n352\n00:15:09,620 --> 00:15:10,750\nBen, I know that hole.\n\n353\n00:15:11,260 --> 00:15:15,120\nTen seconds after a click, and then the day can go two ways.\n\n354\n00:15:15,680 --> 00:15:16,910\nWhat happens in your hole?\n\n355\n00:15:18,160 --> 00:15:21,260\nIn the hole, my hand goes to the phone, Mia.\n\n356\n00:15:21,920 --> 00:15:25,780\nOr my eyes go to the whole list, and the cloud comes back.\n\n357\n00:15:26,660 --> 00:15:29,980\nSo now, before the hole, I decide the next task.\n\n358\n00:15:30,800 --> 00:15:36,729\nWhen I finish one thing, I say, out loud, \"Next: the bank.\" One sentence.\n\n359\n00:15:37,460 --> 00:15:40,280\nAnd I start the next task before the hole opens.\n\n360\n00:15:41,080 --> 00:15:45,320\n\"Next: the bank.\" Ben, I do that in a different way.\n\n361\n00:15:45,880 --> 00:15:48,160\nI keep the next task next to me.\n\n362\n00:15:48,760 --> 00:15:53,720\nIf the next task is the bank, the bank letter is on my desk, next to my hand.\n\n363\n00:15:54,300 --> 00:15:56,920\nWhen I finish the email, my hand finds the letter.\n\n364\n00:15:57,380 --> 00:15:58,440\nI do not need to think.\n\n365\n00:15:58,860 --> 00:16:00,740\nThe next thing is already here.\n\n366\n00:16:02,100 --> 00:16:04,320\nThe next thing is already here.\n\n367\n00:16:04,380 --> 00:16:05,320\nThat is smart, Mia.\n\n368\n00:16:06,160 --> 00:16:11,160\nThen the hole between tasks has something in it, and the cloud has no room.\n\n369\n00:16:11,180 --> 00:16:12,190\nDo you have a third idea?\n\n370\n00:16:12,600 --> 00:16:14,880\nSomething for the evening, maybe?\n\n371\n00:16:15,020 --> 00:16:15,680\nYes, Ben.\n\n372\n00:16:16,060 --> 00:16:18,520\nMy third idea is for the evening.\n\n373\n00:16:18,580 --> 00:16:20,540\nAt the end of the day, I look at my list.\n\n374\n00:16:21,020 --> 00:16:22,300\nSome things are finished.\n\n375\n00:16:22,570 --> 00:16:23,490\nSome are not.\n\n376\n00:16:24,240 --> 00:16:28,360\nIn the past, I looked at the unfinished things, and I felt bad.\n\n377\n00:16:28,460 --> 00:16:30,880\nNow I choose one thing for tomorrow morning.\n\n378\n00:16:31,620 --> 00:16:31,960\nOne.\n\n379\n00:16:32,490 --> 00:16:33,700\nThe first bird.\n\n380\n00:16:34,400 --> 00:16:37,320\nAnd I write it on a small paper, and I put it on the table.\n\n381\n00:16:38,540 --> 00:16:41,610\nThe first bird, on the table, ready for the morning.\n\n382\n00:16:42,470 --> 00:16:45,120\nSo in the morning, there is no cloud, Mia.\n\n383\n00:16:45,180 --> 00:16:46,090\nThere is one paper.\n\n384\n00:16:47,280 --> 00:16:49,440\nI do the same with my notebook, from our first episode.\n\n385\n00:16:50,020 --> 00:16:50,510\nOne line.\n\n386\n00:16:51,280 --> 00:16:52,140\nAnd I noticed something.\n\n387\n00:16:52,780 --> 00:16:57,020\nWhen I know the first bird the night before, I sleep better.\n\n388\n00:16:57,120 --> 00:16:58,500\nYou sleep better, Ben.\n\n389\n00:16:58,520 --> 00:17:00,860\nBecause your head is not making a cloud all night.\n\n390\n00:17:01,340 --> 00:17:03,060\nThe cloud is on the paper.\n\n391\n00:17:03,120 --> 00:17:04,160\nYour head is free.\n\n392\n00:17:05,320 --> 00:17:07,060\nAnd do you have a third idea?\n\n393\n00:17:07,120 --> 00:17:09,290\nSomething for our listeners who are learning English?\n\n394\n00:17:10,640 --> 00:17:11,220\nYes, Mia.\n\n395\n00:17:11,660 --> 00:17:13,940\nLearning English is the biggest list of all.\n\n396\n00:17:14,520 --> 00:17:15,680\nTen thousand words.\n\n397\n00:17:16,200 --> 00:17:16,900\nAll the grammar.\n\n398\n00:17:17,580 --> 00:17:19,980\nListening, speaking, reading, writing.\n\n399\n00:17:20,640 --> 00:17:22,520\nIt is not twenty-three things.\n\n400\n00:17:22,580 --> 00:17:24,560\nIt is twenty-three thousand things.\n\n401\n00:17:25,400 --> 00:17:27,630\nAnd people look at the whole thing and do nothing.\n\n402\n00:17:28,180 --> 00:17:29,800\nFor years.\n\n403\n00:17:29,840 --> 00:17:31,670\nWhat was your first bird, in Spanish?\n\n404\n00:17:33,160 --> 00:17:34,460\nOne podcast, Ben.\n\n405\n00:17:35,140 --> 00:17:37,560\nOne episode, every morning, with my tea.\n\n406\n00:17:38,160 --> 00:17:39,080\nThat was the bird.\n\n407\n00:17:39,520 --> 00:17:43,510\nNot \"learn Spanish.\" Not \"grammar.\" One episode.\n\n408\n00:17:44,080 --> 00:17:48,220\nAnd I let the other twenty-three thousand things wait outside the door.\n\n409\n00:17:48,260 --> 00:17:51,700\nAnd one day, months later, I understood the whole episode.\n\n410\n00:17:52,189 --> 00:17:53,840\nThe birds had become a report.\n\n411\n00:17:55,140 --> 00:17:58,920\n\"The birds had become a report.\" Mia, that is how it works.\n\n412\n00:17:59,480 --> 00:18:02,990\nAnd for everyone listening now, this episode is the bird today.\n\n413\n00:18:03,820 --> 00:18:04,520\nOne episode.\n\n414\n00:18:05,280 --> 00:18:07,960\nNobody needs to learn all of English today.\n\n415\n00:18:08,080 --> 00:18:09,680\nJust finish this one.\n\n416\n00:18:09,720 --> 00:18:11,220\nAnd then, click.\n\n417\n00:18:11,280 --> 00:18:12,960\nThat click is English getting better.\n\n418\n00:18:14,210 --> 00:18:16,580\nThat click is English getting better.\n\n419\n00:18:16,720 --> 00:18:17,500\nI like that, Ben.\n\n420\n00:18:18,180 --> 00:18:19,950\nAnd I want to say one honest thing.\n\n421\n00:18:20,640 --> 00:18:23,140\nOne task at a time does not give more time.\n\n422\n00:18:23,710 --> 00:18:25,600\nThe day is still twenty-four hours.\n\n423\n00:18:26,120 --> 00:18:27,860\nTwenty-three things may not fit.\n\n424\n00:18:28,440 --> 00:18:30,160\nSome birds will wait until tomorrow.\n\n425\n00:18:30,660 --> 00:18:31,240\nIs that okay?\n\n426\n00:18:32,080 --> 00:18:32,980\nThat is fine, Mia.\n\n427\n00:18:33,280 --> 00:18:34,860\nThe goal is not to finish everything.\n\n428\n00:18:35,349 --> 00:18:40,960\nThe goal is to finish something, every time, and to stop carrying half-finished things.\n\n429\n00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:46,080\nAt the end of a one-task day, I have maybe eleven finished things and twelve waiting on paper.\n\n430\n00:18:46,740 --> 00:18:51,120\nAt the end of a cloud day, I have zero finished things and twenty-three crying.\n\n431\n00:18:51,740 --> 00:18:52,360\nSame time.\n\n432\n00:18:52,820 --> 00:18:53,300\nDifferent day.\n\n433\n00:18:54,370 --> 00:18:55,080\n\"Same time.\n\n434\n00:18:55,360 --> 00:18:58,840\nDifferent day.\" Ben, that is our message today, I think.\n\n435\n00:18:59,500 --> 00:19:01,600\nA long list is not many tasks.\n\n436\n00:19:01,940 --> 00:19:02,840\nIt is a cloud.\n\n437\n00:19:03,320 --> 00:19:05,120\nAnd nobody can start a cloud.\n\n438\n00:19:05,780 --> 00:19:07,520\nSo we take one thing with edges.\n\n439\n00:19:07,960 --> 00:19:08,720\nWe finish it.\n\n440\n00:19:09,320 --> 00:19:09,640\nClick.\n\n441\n00:19:10,340 --> 00:19:11,200\nThen the next one.\n\n442\n00:19:11,830 --> 00:19:15,320\nThe cloud goes away one bird at a time.\n\n443\n00:19:15,400 --> 00:19:16,540\nOne bird at a time.\n\n444\n00:19:17,440 --> 00:19:22,440\nMia, let's repeat today's important words, slowly, because our listeners want to remember them.\n\n445\n00:19:23,600 --> 00:19:23,860\nOne.\n\n446\n00:19:24,660 --> 00:19:26,460\nNot two, not twenty-three.\n\n447\n00:19:27,160 --> 00:19:27,820\nOne task.\n\n448\n00:19:28,440 --> 00:19:29,140\nOne bird.\n\n449\n00:19:30,180 --> 00:19:30,580\nTask.\n\n450\n00:19:31,120 --> 00:19:31,860\nA piece of work.\n\n451\n00:19:32,250 --> 00:19:33,140\nA small task.\n\n452\n00:19:34,120 --> 00:19:34,980\nFinish the task.\n\n453\n00:19:36,260 --> 00:19:36,580\nTime.\n\n454\n00:19:37,110 --> 00:19:38,200\nMinutes and hours.\n\n455\n00:19:38,800 --> 00:19:40,010\nOne task at a time.\n\n456\n00:19:40,620 --> 00:19:41,520\nThe time it needs.\n\n457\n00:19:42,920 --> 00:19:43,320\nNext.\n\n458\n00:19:43,870 --> 00:19:45,100\nThe one after this one.\n\n459\n00:19:45,660 --> 00:19:46,600\nWhat is the next one?\n\n460\n00:19:47,880 --> 00:19:48,220\nFinish.\n\n461\n00:19:48,780 --> 00:19:49,420\nTo complete.\n\n462\n00:19:50,120 --> 00:19:51,160\nFinish one thing.\n\n463\n00:19:51,860 --> 00:19:53,620\nFinished things do not come back.\n\n464\n00:19:54,960 --> 00:19:55,880\nOne.\n\n465\n00:19:55,980 --> 00:19:56,480\nTask.\n\n466\n00:19:57,440 --> 00:19:57,850\nTime.\n\n467\n00:19:59,000 --> 00:19:59,450\nNext.\n\n468\n00:20:00,700 --> 00:20:01,070\nFinish.\n\n469\n00:20:01,960 --> 00:20:04,240\nFive words, one at a time.\n\n470\n00:20:04,270 --> 00:20:05,820\nAnd one big idea.\n\n471\n00:20:06,440 --> 00:20:07,520\nBird by bird.\n\n472\n00:20:08,660 --> 00:20:10,900\nNow, Ben, do you have a question for our listeners?\n\n473\n00:20:11,720 --> 00:20:12,660\nYes, Mia.\n\n474\n00:20:12,740 --> 00:20:14,660\nEveryone listening, here is my question.\n\n475\n00:20:15,400 --> 00:20:17,040\nWhat is your first bird tomorrow?\n\n476\n00:20:17,660 --> 00:20:18,340\nNot the list.\n\n477\n00:20:18,730 --> 00:20:19,680\nThe first one.\n\n478\n00:20:20,480 --> 00:20:20,960\nAn email?\n\n479\n00:20:21,400 --> 00:20:21,900\nA call?\n\n480\n00:20:22,260 --> 00:20:22,780\nA page?\n\n481\n00:20:23,500 --> 00:20:25,520\nWrite it in the comments in one short sentence.\n\n482\n00:20:25,960 --> 00:20:33,040\nFor example, \"My first bird tomorrow is one email to my boss.\" Reading those sentences is our bird, actually.\n\n483\n00:20:33,580 --> 00:20:35,800\nWe read them one at a time.\n\n484\n00:20:35,960 --> 00:20:37,000\nOne at a time.\n\n485\n00:20:37,700 --> 00:20:41,120\nAnd if this episode helped you, please subscribe to Two Cups English.\n\n486\n00:20:41,580 --> 00:20:43,780\nWe make a new episode every few days.\n\n487\n00:20:44,260 --> 00:20:48,440\nSlow English, simple words, and one true story each time.\n\n488\n00:20:49,140 --> 00:20:51,650\nThe full text of this episode is in the description.\n\n489\n00:20:52,400 --> 00:20:53,700\nOne episode at a time.\n\n490\n00:20:54,170 --> 00:20:55,239\nThat is the plan.\n\n491\n00:20:56,120 --> 00:20:57,760\nThank you for spending this time with us.\n\n492\n00:20:58,360 --> 00:20:59,900\nMaybe you have a long list today.\n\n493\n00:21:00,620 --> 00:21:01,760\nMaybe it looks like a wall.\n\n494\n00:21:02,620 --> 00:21:03,940\nSit down for one minute.\n\n495\n00:21:04,430 --> 00:21:07,680\nAsk, \"What is the first one?\" Do that one.\n\n496\n00:21:08,520 --> 00:21:09,160\nThen the next.\n\n497\n00:21:09,920 --> 00:21:11,430\nNobody needs to do everything.\n\n498\n00:21:12,280 --> 00:21:13,380\nOne thing.\n\n499\n00:21:13,420 --> 00:21:14,880\nAnd then, one more.\n\n500\n00:21:15,840 --> 00:21:16,500\nThis is Mia.\n\n501\n00:21:17,070 --> 00:21:17,959\nAnd this is Ben.\n\n502\n00:21:18,880 --> 00:21:20,440\nThis was Two Cups English.\n\n503\n00:21:20,840 --> 00:21:21,980\nBird by bird.\n\n504\n00:21:22,560 --> 00:21:23,720\nWe will see you next time."},{"code":"CUP05","file":"CUP05.srt","srt":"1\n00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,280\nHello everyone, and welcome to Two Cups English.\n\n2\n00:00:03,840 --> 00:00:06,720\nI'm Mia, and Ben is here with me.\n\n3\n00:00:06,780 --> 00:00:09,540\nThis podcast is for people who are learning English.\n\n4\n00:00:10,220 --> 00:00:11,060\nWe speak slowly.\n\n5\n00:00:11,640 --> 00:00:12,970\nWe use simple words.\n\n6\n00:00:13,560 --> 00:00:16,400\nAnd we repeat the important words many times.\n\n7\n00:00:17,320 --> 00:00:20,070\nToday, our topic is the evening.\n\n8\n00:00:20,071 --> 00:00:21,920\nAnd how the evening shapes tomorrow.\n\n9\n00:00:22,980 --> 00:00:25,370\nBen, what time did you go to bed last night?\n\n10\n00:00:26,520 --> 00:00:27,480\nHi Mia.\n\n11\n00:00:27,560 --> 00:00:28,960\nHi everyone.\n\n12\n00:00:29,020 --> 00:00:32,439\nLast night, I went to bed at eleven thirty.\n\n13\n00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:33,880\nNot bad.\n\n14\n00:00:33,920 --> 00:00:39,200\nBut my phone was next to my pillow, and I looked at it for thirty minutes in the dark.\n\n15\n00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:41,880\nSo, really, midnight.\n\n16\n00:00:43,040 --> 00:00:44,980\nAnd this morning was slow.\n\n17\n00:00:46,060 --> 00:00:46,800\nWhat about you, Mia?\n\n18\n00:00:47,380 --> 00:00:48,020\nHow was your night?\n\n19\n00:00:49,300 --> 00:00:50,120\nBad, Ben.\n\n20\n00:00:50,720 --> 00:00:52,060\nI went to bed at midnight.\n\n21\n00:00:52,620 --> 00:00:53,860\nMy cup was on the desk.\n\n22\n00:00:54,400 --> 00:00:57,840\nMy clothes were on the chair, on the floor, on the bed.\n\n23\n00:00:58,560 --> 00:01:01,350\nMy phone was next to my pillow, full of light.\n\n24\n00:01:02,040 --> 00:01:04,500\nAnd this morning, I could not find my keys.\n\n25\n00:01:05,060 --> 00:01:06,540\nI could not find a clean shirt.\n\n26\n00:01:07,220 --> 00:01:07,980\nI was late.\n\n27\n00:01:09,060 --> 00:01:11,940\nNo keys and no shirt, Mia.\n\n28\n00:01:12,060 --> 00:01:14,460\nThat is a hard morning.\n\n29\n00:01:14,520 --> 00:01:16,350\nBut let me ask a strange question.\n\n30\n00:01:17,260 --> 00:01:18,400\nWas it the morning's fault?\n\n31\n00:01:19,440 --> 00:01:19,980\nNo, Ben.\n\n32\n00:01:20,410 --> 00:01:22,100\nThat is the thing I understood today.\n\n33\n00:01:22,800 --> 00:01:24,960\nThis morning did not start this morning.\n\n34\n00:01:25,400 --> 00:01:26,640\nIt started last night.\n\n35\n00:01:27,350 --> 00:01:29,720\nThe messy evening made the messy morning.\n\n36\n00:01:30,470 --> 00:01:33,100\nAnd I think many people know this feeling.\n\n37\n00:01:33,160 --> 00:01:35,270\nThe morning pays for the evening before.\n\n38\n00:01:36,350 --> 00:01:41,860\n\"The morning pays for the evening before.\" Mia, that is our topic today.\n\n39\n00:01:42,720 --> 00:01:47,040\nAnd it is good news, because the evening is easy to change.\n\n40\n00:01:48,080 --> 00:01:49,120\nSo here is our plan.\n\n41\n00:01:50,040 --> 00:01:53,580\nIn part one, we talk about our own evenings.\n\n42\n00:01:53,640 --> 00:01:55,900\nThe bad ones and the good ones.\n\n43\n00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:01,060\nIn part two, we ask why the evening shapes tomorrow.\n\n44\n00:02:01,880 --> 00:02:03,720\nAnd I have a true story for that.\n\n45\n00:02:04,980 --> 00:02:08,880\nIn part three, we share five small things for tonight.\n\n46\n00:02:09,600 --> 00:02:10,380\nDoes that sound good?\n\n47\n00:02:11,300 --> 00:02:12,220\nThat sounds good, Ben.\n\n48\n00:02:12,920 --> 00:02:16,520\nAnd to everyone listening, please do not worry if you miss something.\n\n49\n00:02:17,020 --> 00:02:17,880\nYou will hear it again.\n\n50\n00:02:18,540 --> 00:02:24,640\nYou can listen while you cook dinner, while you wash the dishes, or while you get ready for bed.\n\n51\n00:02:24,700 --> 00:02:25,240\nOkay, Ben.\n\n52\n00:02:25,600 --> 00:02:26,820\nPart one.\n\n53\n00:02:26,900 --> 00:02:28,100\nTell me about your evenings.\n\n54\n00:02:28,450 --> 00:02:30,640\nWhat were they like before?\n\n55\n00:02:30,700 --> 00:02:33,480\nBefore, my evenings were too full, Mia.\n\n56\n00:02:34,480 --> 00:02:35,760\nI did not disappear.\n\n57\n00:02:36,700 --> 00:02:37,260\nI worked.\n\n58\n00:02:38,420 --> 00:02:40,480\nI answered emails at ten at night.\n\n59\n00:02:41,680 --> 00:02:43,360\nI planned tomorrow at eleven.\n\n60\n00:02:44,480 --> 00:02:47,040\nI \"used\" my evening, as I thought.\n\n61\n00:02:48,240 --> 00:02:53,140\nAnd then I lay in bed with a head full of tomorrow, and I did not sleep.\n\n62\n00:02:54,420 --> 00:02:55,520\nAnd yours?\n\n63\n00:02:55,560 --> 00:02:57,420\nWere they full, too?\n\n64\n00:02:57,780 --> 00:02:58,740\nThe opposite, Ben.\n\n65\n00:02:59,329 --> 00:03:00,750\nMy evening was a hole.\n\n66\n00:03:01,380 --> 00:03:04,019\nWork ended, and I fell into the hole.\n\n67\n00:03:04,120 --> 00:03:04,520\nSofa.\n\n68\n00:03:05,040 --> 00:03:05,440\nPhone.\n\n69\n00:03:05,900 --> 00:03:06,400\nSnacks.\n\n70\n00:03:06,740 --> 00:03:07,329\nTelevision.\n\n71\n00:03:08,140 --> 00:03:10,300\nThen it was midnight, and I was surprised.\n\n72\n00:03:11,040 --> 00:03:12,360\nWhere did the evening go?\n\n73\n00:03:13,160 --> 00:03:15,500\nI did not rest, and I did not prepare.\n\n74\n00:03:16,140 --> 00:03:17,970\nI just disappeared for four hours.\n\n75\n00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:22,180\nSo your evening was empty, Mia, and mine was too full.\n\n76\n00:03:22,960 --> 00:03:24,680\nAnd both of us were tired in the morning.\n\n77\n00:03:25,740 --> 00:03:26,660\nThat is interesting.\n\n78\n00:03:27,460 --> 00:03:28,990\nThe problem is not doing nothing.\n\n79\n00:03:29,510 --> 00:03:30,750\nAnd it is not doing a lot.\n\n80\n00:03:31,400 --> 00:03:33,480\nThe problem is doing the wrong things at night.\n\n81\n00:03:34,990 --> 00:03:37,340\n\"The wrong things at night.\" Yes, Ben.\n\n82\n00:03:38,060 --> 00:03:39,220\nSo what are the right things?\n\n83\n00:03:40,130 --> 00:03:42,359\nLet me tell you what changed for me.\n\n84\n00:03:42,420 --> 00:03:44,560\nIt was a small thing, again.\n\n85\n00:03:44,660 --> 00:03:47,500\nOne evening, I was tired of losing my keys.\n\n86\n00:03:47,620 --> 00:03:50,700\nSo before bed, I put the keys in a bowl by the door.\n\n87\n00:03:51,260 --> 00:03:51,780\nThat is all.\n\n88\n00:03:52,180 --> 00:03:53,120\nTen seconds.\n\n89\n00:03:54,420 --> 00:03:55,680\nA bowl by the door, Mia.\n\n90\n00:03:56,380 --> 00:03:57,459\nTen seconds at night.\n\n91\n00:03:58,000 --> 00:03:58,620\nAnd in the morning?\n\n92\n00:03:59,200 --> 00:03:59,920\nWere the keys there?\n\n93\n00:04:01,280 --> 00:04:02,280\nThey were there, Ben.\n\n94\n00:04:02,700 --> 00:04:03,380\nNo searching.\n\n95\n00:04:03,680 --> 00:04:04,380\nNo stress.\n\n96\n00:04:05,020 --> 00:04:08,820\nAnd I thought, \"What else can I put in its place tonight?\" The cup.\n\n97\n00:04:09,240 --> 00:04:09,880\nThe clothes.\n\n98\n00:04:10,190 --> 00:04:10,720\nThe bag.\n\n99\n00:04:11,600 --> 00:04:13,920\nFive small things, five minutes.\n\n100\n00:04:14,580 --> 00:04:15,940\nAnd the morning was different.\n\n101\n00:04:16,460 --> 00:04:17,240\nNot perfect.\n\n102\n00:04:17,720 --> 00:04:18,160\nDifferent.\n\n103\n00:04:18,840 --> 00:04:19,260\nEasier.\n\n104\n00:04:20,480 --> 00:04:21,160\nNot perfect.\n\n105\n00:04:21,680 --> 00:04:22,060\nEasier.\n\n106\n00:04:22,920 --> 00:04:24,200\nThat is the right goal, Mia.\n\n107\n00:04:25,200 --> 00:04:26,820\nSo what are your five small things now?\n\n108\n00:04:27,480 --> 00:04:29,300\nHow do you get ready for tomorrow?\n\n109\n00:04:29,340 --> 00:04:30,310\nTell us the whole evening.\n\n110\n00:04:31,720 --> 00:04:32,360\nOkay, Ben.\n\n111\n00:04:32,920 --> 00:04:35,520\nFirst, when I come home, the bag goes on the hook.\n\n112\n00:04:35,940 --> 00:04:36,840\nNot on the floor.\n\n113\n00:04:37,220 --> 00:04:37,700\nThe hook.\n\n114\n00:04:38,720 --> 00:04:40,980\nSecond, I put tomorrow's clothes on the chair.\n\n115\n00:04:41,600 --> 00:04:43,460\nOne shirt, one pair of trousers.\n\n116\n00:04:44,400 --> 00:04:47,920\nThird, the cup goes in the sink, washed, and back on the shelf.\n\n117\n00:04:49,040 --> 00:04:51,500\nFourth, the phone goes on the table in the kitchen.\n\n118\n00:04:51,820 --> 00:04:52,840\nNot in the bedroom.\n\n119\n00:04:53,900 --> 00:04:56,060\nFifth, I write one line for tomorrow.\n\n120\n00:04:56,460 --> 00:04:57,080\nJust one.\n\n121\n00:04:57,860 --> 00:04:58,860\nThat is my evening.\n\n122\n00:04:59,260 --> 00:05:00,040\nTen minutes.\n\n123\n00:05:01,180 --> 00:05:01,960\nTen minutes, Mia.\n\n124\n00:05:02,720 --> 00:05:07,379\nAnd I want to say something about the clothes, because it sounds small, and it is not.\n\n125\n00:05:08,040 --> 00:05:11,900\nWhen I choose tomorrow's shirt tonight, I make a decision when I have energy.\n\n126\n00:05:12,760 --> 00:05:15,440\nIn the morning, I have no energy for decisions.\n\n127\n00:05:16,280 --> 00:05:16,760\nWhich shirt?\n\n128\n00:05:17,320 --> 00:05:17,920\nWhich shoes?\n\n129\n00:05:18,500 --> 00:05:20,140\nBreakfast or not?\n\n130\n00:05:20,180 --> 00:05:23,680\nBy eight o'clock, I have spent all my energy on small choices.\n\n131\n00:05:25,240 --> 00:05:29,660\n\"Small choices at night, big work in the morning.\" I like that, Ben.\n\n132\n00:05:30,440 --> 00:05:33,000\nAnd I think the phone is the biggest small choice.\n\n133\n00:05:33,660 --> 00:05:36,120\nMy phone lived next to my pillow for years.\n\n134\n00:05:36,529 --> 00:05:38,300\nThe last thing I saw at night.\n\n135\n00:05:38,330 --> 00:05:40,140\nThe first thing I saw in the morning.\n\n136\n00:05:41,340 --> 00:05:42,800\nWhere does your phone sleep now?\n\n137\n00:05:44,440 --> 00:05:45,600\nStill in the bedroom, Mia.\n\n138\n00:05:46,240 --> 00:05:47,040\nI know.\n\n139\n00:05:47,180 --> 00:05:48,680\nI know it is a problem.\n\n140\n00:05:48,740 --> 00:05:51,140\nMy phone is my alarm, that is my excuse.\n\n141\n00:05:51,920 --> 00:05:52,800\nWhere does yours sleep?\n\n142\n00:05:54,020 --> 00:05:54,880\nIn the kitchen, Ben.\n\n143\n00:05:55,500 --> 00:05:58,180\nAnd the first thing I see in the morning is the window.\n\n144\n00:05:58,220 --> 00:05:58,760\nThe light.\n\n145\n00:05:59,120 --> 00:06:00,100\nThe room.\n\n146\n00:06:00,160 --> 00:06:01,800\nIt is quiet.\n\n147\n00:06:01,830 --> 00:06:05,240\nThat one change did more for my mornings than anything else.\n\n148\n00:06:06,220 --> 00:06:07,840\nBuy a small alarm clock, Ben.\n\n149\n00:06:08,240 --> 00:06:09,000\nFive dollars.\n\n150\n00:06:09,580 --> 00:06:11,460\nThen the phone can sleep in the kitchen too.\n\n151\n00:06:12,100 --> 00:06:14,300\nAnd tomorrow morning, you wake up ready.\n\n152\n00:06:16,080 --> 00:06:17,040\nFive dollars, Mia.\n\n153\n00:06:17,440 --> 00:06:17,740\nOkay.\n\n154\n00:06:18,420 --> 00:06:19,680\nThat is my task for tonight.\n\n155\n00:06:20,420 --> 00:06:21,970\nCan I tell you about my worst evening?\n\n156\n00:06:22,360 --> 00:06:23,570\nBecause it shows the other side.\n\n157\n00:06:24,360 --> 00:06:25,080\nPlease, Ben.\n\n158\n00:06:25,600 --> 00:06:26,680\nYour worst evening.\n\n159\n00:06:27,200 --> 00:06:30,059\nI think everyone has one of those, and nobody talks about it.\n\n160\n00:06:30,700 --> 00:06:31,340\nWhat happened?\n\n161\n00:06:32,940 --> 00:06:35,360\nLast month, I had a big day coming, Mia.\n\n162\n00:06:35,390 --> 00:06:36,180\nA presentation.\n\n163\n00:06:36,920 --> 00:06:37,560\nI was nervous.\n\n164\n00:06:38,080 --> 00:06:39,280\nSo at night, I worked on it.\n\n165\n00:06:39,840 --> 00:06:40,600\nUntil midnight.\n\n166\n00:06:41,160 --> 00:06:42,000\nThen one o'clock.\n\n167\n00:06:42,740 --> 00:06:45,380\nI changed one word, then changed it back.\n\n168\n00:06:46,220 --> 00:06:47,440\nI was not making it better.\n\n169\n00:06:48,220 --> 00:06:51,600\nI was just afraid, and working was a way to hold the fear.\n\n170\n00:06:52,840 --> 00:06:54,940\nWorking as a way to hold the fear.\n\n171\n00:06:55,900 --> 00:06:57,380\nBen, I know that night.\n\n172\n00:06:58,000 --> 00:06:58,740\nAnd in the morning?\n\n173\n00:06:59,860 --> 00:07:01,460\nIn the morning, I was tired, Mia.\n\n174\n00:07:02,020 --> 00:07:03,820\nAnd the presentation was not better.\n\n175\n00:07:04,600 --> 00:07:06,820\nIt was the same, and I was worse.\n\n176\n00:07:07,840 --> 00:07:08,840\nSo now I have a rule.\n\n177\n00:07:09,540 --> 00:07:12,160\nThe night before a big day, I stop at eight.\n\n178\n00:07:12,800 --> 00:07:15,440\nWhatever the work is, at eight, I close it.\n\n179\n00:07:16,540 --> 00:07:17,740\nYou stop at eight, Ben?\n\n180\n00:07:18,520 --> 00:07:19,680\nEven when you are nervous?\n\n181\n00:07:20,360 --> 00:07:21,970\nEven when the work is not finished?\n\n182\n00:07:22,740 --> 00:07:24,080\nThat sounds very hard.\n\n183\n00:07:25,200 --> 00:07:27,460\nEspecially when I am nervous, Mia.\n\n184\n00:07:27,540 --> 00:07:29,060\nAt eight, I close the work.\n\n185\n00:07:29,660 --> 00:07:31,740\nI do my small evening things.\n\n186\n00:07:31,800 --> 00:07:33,320\nThen something quiet.\n\n187\n00:07:33,360 --> 00:07:33,800\nA book.\n\n188\n00:07:34,560 --> 00:07:34,840\nTea.\n\n189\n00:07:35,550 --> 00:07:36,600\nAnd I go to bed at ten.\n\n190\n00:07:37,460 --> 00:07:40,380\nAnd the strange thing is, the presentation is better.\n\n191\n00:07:41,300 --> 00:07:42,540\nNot because I worked more.\n\n192\n00:07:43,200 --> 00:07:44,080\nBecause I slept.\n\n193\n00:07:44,980 --> 00:07:51,160\nA rested head at nine in the morning is better than a tired head that worked until one.\n\n194\n00:07:51,190 --> 00:07:57,930\n\"A rested head is better than a tired head that worked more.\" Ben, I did not believe that for years.\n\n195\n00:07:58,420 --> 00:07:59,160\nNow I know it.\n\n196\n00:08:00,380 --> 00:08:02,340\nOkay, so that was part one.\n\n197\n00:08:02,380 --> 00:08:03,080\nOur evenings.\n\n198\n00:08:03,740 --> 00:08:10,540\nA hole, a too-full night, a bowl by the door, five small things, and a rule to stop at eight.\n\n199\n00:08:11,460 --> 00:08:13,359\nNow, part two.\n\n200\n00:08:13,460 --> 00:08:15,400\nWhy does the evening shape tomorrow?\n\n201\n00:08:16,600 --> 00:08:20,440\nBen, you have a true story, and it is a very old one.\n\n202\n00:08:21,420 --> 00:08:22,260\nVery old, Mia.\n\n203\n00:08:23,140 --> 00:08:24,160\nOkay, part two.\n\n204\n00:08:24,900 --> 00:08:26,060\nThis is a true story.\n\n205\n00:08:26,760 --> 00:08:28,160\nIt is about Benjamin Franklin.\n\n206\n00:08:28,880 --> 00:08:31,160\nHe lived in America in the seventeen hundreds.\n\n207\n00:08:31,940 --> 00:08:35,380\nHe was a writer, a scientist, and a very busy man.\n\n208\n00:08:36,100 --> 00:08:40,120\nAnd in his autobiography, he shows his daily plan.\n\n209\n00:08:40,340 --> 00:08:40,880\nOne page.\n\n210\n00:08:41,600 --> 00:08:42,830\nEvery hour of the day.\n\n211\n00:08:44,060 --> 00:08:45,260\nEvery hour, Ben?\n\n212\n00:08:46,040 --> 00:08:48,800\nOne page for the whole day, from morning to night?\n\n213\n00:08:49,440 --> 00:08:50,520\nWhat did the page say?\n\n214\n00:08:51,760 --> 00:08:55,480\nIn the morning, at five o'clock, he asked himself one question, Mia.\n\n215\n00:08:56,430 --> 00:09:02,920\n\"What good shall I do this day?\" And in the evening, at six o'clock, he wrote something very simple.\n\n216\n00:09:03,260 --> 00:09:05,440\n\"Put things in their places.\"\n\n217\n00:09:06,990 --> 00:09:10,789\n\"Put things in their places.\" Ben, that is my bowl.\n\n218\n00:09:11,420 --> 00:09:13,960\nTwo hundred years before my bowl.\n\n219\n00:09:15,700 --> 00:09:17,700\nTwo hundred and fifty years, actually, Mia.\n\n220\n00:09:18,440 --> 00:09:21,540\nAnd at nine o'clock at night, he asked a second question.\n\n221\n00:09:22,380 --> 00:09:25,920\n\"What good have I done today?\" Then he slept.\n\n222\n00:09:27,020 --> 00:09:28,820\nSo Franklin had three evening things.\n\n223\n00:09:29,540 --> 00:09:30,740\nPut things in their places.\n\n224\n00:09:31,420 --> 00:09:33,210\nAsk what went well.\n\n225\n00:09:33,280 --> 00:09:33,440\nSleep.\n\n226\n00:09:34,260 --> 00:09:35,460\nThree things, Ben.\n\n227\n00:09:35,940 --> 00:09:40,680\nThat is the whole evening plan of one of the busiest people in history.\n\n228\n00:09:40,760 --> 00:09:41,680\nNot a long list.\n\n229\n00:09:42,120 --> 00:09:43,079\nNot a big system.\n\n230\n00:09:43,580 --> 00:09:44,450\nThree things.\n\n231\n00:09:45,220 --> 00:09:46,930\nWhy do you think he kept it so small?\n\n232\n00:09:48,060 --> 00:09:50,420\nI think he knew what we are learning today, Mia.\n\n233\n00:09:50,480 --> 00:09:55,200\nThe morning question, \"What good shall I do?\", only works if the evening is ready.\n\n234\n00:09:56,000 --> 00:09:58,060\nA messy evening gives a messy morning.\n\n235\n00:09:58,760 --> 00:09:59,500\nEven to Franklin.\n\n236\n00:10:00,600 --> 00:10:02,039\nAnd I think there is a second reason.\n\n237\n00:10:02,810 --> 00:10:04,760\nThe evening has a strange kind of time.\n\n238\n00:10:05,680 --> 00:10:07,360\nA strange kind of time, Ben?\n\n239\n00:10:08,020 --> 00:10:09,420\nTime is time, I think.\n\n240\n00:10:09,960 --> 00:10:12,000\nAn hour at night is an hour in the morning.\n\n241\n00:10:12,440 --> 00:10:12,960\nWhat do you mean?\n\n242\n00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:15,960\nIn the morning, time is fast, Mia.\n\n243\n00:10:16,580 --> 00:10:17,590\nEverything is urgent.\n\n244\n00:10:18,300 --> 00:10:21,400\nIn the evening, time is slow and soft.\n\n245\n00:10:21,430 --> 00:10:22,520\nThere is no bus to catch.\n\n246\n00:10:23,460 --> 00:10:26,540\nSo the evening is the right time for small things.\n\n247\n00:10:26,600 --> 00:10:28,860\nWashing a cup at seven in the morning is stress.\n\n248\n00:10:29,660 --> 00:10:32,200\nWashing a cup at nine at night is almost nice.\n\n249\n00:10:33,080 --> 00:10:34,540\nSame cup, same water.\n\n250\n00:10:35,220 --> 00:10:35,880\nDifferent time.\n\n251\n00:10:37,120 --> 00:10:40,860\n\"The evening is where small things are cheap.\" Yes, Ben.\n\n252\n00:10:41,380 --> 00:10:44,100\nAnd the morning is where the same things are expensive.\n\n253\n00:10:45,020 --> 00:10:46,680\nCan I ask about the word \"ready\"?\n\n254\n00:10:47,140 --> 00:10:49,040\nBecause I think we misunderstand it.\n\n255\n00:10:50,060 --> 00:10:51,360\nHow do we misunderstand it, Mia?\n\n256\n00:10:52,080 --> 00:10:52,760\nReady is ready.\n\n257\n00:10:53,430 --> 00:10:57,440\nThe bag is packed, the shirt is clean, you are ready.\n\n258\n00:10:57,480 --> 00:10:57,840\nOr not?\n\n259\n00:10:58,980 --> 00:11:01,560\nWe think ready is a feeling, Ben.\n\n260\n00:11:01,570 --> 00:11:07,239\n\"I feel ready for tomorrow.\" I spent years trying to feel ready at night, and I never did.\n\n261\n00:11:07,840 --> 00:11:10,080\nI lay in bed and thought, \"Am I ready?\n\n262\n00:11:10,640 --> 00:11:13,960\nAm I ready?\" And the answer was always no.\n\n263\n00:11:15,040 --> 00:11:16,720\nBecause ready is not a feeling, Mia.\n\n264\n00:11:17,340 --> 00:11:18,660\nReady is a state of things.\n\n265\n00:11:19,340 --> 00:11:20,780\nThe bag is ready.\n\n266\n00:11:20,840 --> 00:11:21,740\nThe shirt is ready.\n\n267\n00:11:22,360 --> 00:11:23,520\nThe line is ready.\n\n268\n00:11:24,440 --> 00:11:25,540\nThings can be ready.\n\n269\n00:11:26,420 --> 00:11:29,720\nAnd when the things are ready, the feeling comes by itself.\n\n270\n00:11:30,680 --> 00:11:33,600\nOr it does not, and it does not matter.\n\n271\n00:11:33,610 --> 00:11:34,820\nThe house is ready for you.\n\n272\n00:11:36,030 --> 00:11:39,670\n\"The house is ready for me.\" Ben, I love that.\n\n273\n00:11:40,340 --> 00:11:42,400\nNow I do not ask if I feel ready.\n\n274\n00:11:42,849 --> 00:11:43,700\nI look at the chair.\n\n275\n00:11:44,180 --> 00:11:45,120\nThe shirt is there.\n\n276\n00:11:45,860 --> 00:11:47,140\nI look at the table.\n\n277\n00:11:47,200 --> 00:11:48,180\nThe line is there.\n\n278\n00:11:48,800 --> 00:11:52,220\nReady is on the chair and on the table, not in my head.\n\n279\n00:11:52,920 --> 00:11:54,240\nAnd my head can sleep.\n\n280\n00:11:55,360 --> 00:11:56,700\nAnd your head can sleep, Mia.\n\n281\n00:11:57,540 --> 00:12:00,020\nThat is the last reason the evening shapes tomorrow.\n\n282\n00:12:00,860 --> 00:12:02,910\nSleep is the last thing the evening does.\n\n283\n00:12:03,700 --> 00:12:08,990\nA good evening makes a good sleep, the way a good morning makes a good day.\n\n284\n00:12:09,020 --> 00:12:11,560\nCan I add Franklin's second question here?\n\n285\n00:12:11,620 --> 00:12:12,980\nBecause it changed my nights.\n\n286\n00:12:14,100 --> 00:12:17,080\n\"What good have I done today?\" That one, Ben?\n\n287\n00:12:17,780 --> 00:12:19,640\nI thought it was just a nice old sentence.\n\n288\n00:12:20,200 --> 00:12:21,530\nDoes it really change something?\n\n289\n00:12:22,360 --> 00:12:23,020\nIt does, Mia.\n\n290\n00:12:23,520 --> 00:12:31,080\nAt night, I ask, \"What went well today?\" Not \"What did I miss?\" Not \"What is left?\" What went well.\n\n291\n00:12:31,180 --> 00:12:31,699\nOne thing.\n\n292\n00:12:32,300 --> 00:12:32,860\nMaybe two.\n\n293\n00:12:33,580 --> 00:12:34,740\nI said hello to a neighbor.\n\n294\n00:12:35,380 --> 00:12:36,360\nI finished the page.\n\n295\n00:12:37,100 --> 00:12:38,020\nI made a good soup.\n\n296\n00:12:38,780 --> 00:12:39,560\nAnd then I sleep.\n\n297\n00:12:40,360 --> 00:12:41,580\nIt sounds small.\n\n298\n00:12:41,600 --> 00:12:43,420\nBut I sleep differently after that question.\n\n299\n00:12:43,940 --> 00:12:47,040\nBen, my old question was the opposite.\n\n300\n00:12:47,660 --> 00:12:51,100\nAt night, I made a list of everything I did not do.\n\n301\n00:12:51,200 --> 00:12:53,140\nA long list, in the dark.\n\n302\n00:12:53,960 --> 00:12:56,280\nAnd then I wondered why I could not sleep.\n\n303\n00:12:57,200 --> 00:12:59,610\nFranklin's question turns the head to the good things.\n\n304\n00:13:00,300 --> 00:13:01,780\nAnd the good things are quiet.\n\n305\n00:13:02,590 --> 00:13:04,380\nThe missing things are loud.\n\n306\n00:13:05,050 --> 00:13:06,640\n\"The good things are quiet.\n\n307\n00:13:07,140 --> 00:13:12,100\nThe missing things are loud.\" Mia, that is the sentence for the wall tonight.\n\n308\n00:13:13,080 --> 00:13:14,660\nOkay, so that was part two.\n\n309\n00:13:15,120 --> 00:13:16,720\nWhy the evening shapes tomorrow.\n\n310\n00:13:17,420 --> 00:13:19,640\nWe talked about Franklin's three evening things.\n\n311\n00:13:20,340 --> 00:13:23,160\nWe said the evening is where small things are cheap.\n\n312\n00:13:23,860 --> 00:13:27,300\nAnd we said ready is a state of things, not a feeling.\n\n313\n00:13:28,200 --> 00:13:29,540\nNow, part three.\n\n314\n00:13:29,580 --> 00:13:31,560\nFive small things for tonight.\n\n315\n00:13:31,600 --> 00:13:32,780\nMia, you go first.\n\n316\n00:13:33,560 --> 00:13:34,660\nOkay, part three.\n\n317\n00:13:35,280 --> 00:13:36,960\nMy first thing is Franklin's rule.\n\n318\n00:13:37,480 --> 00:13:39,140\nPut things in their places.\n\n319\n00:13:39,720 --> 00:13:40,700\nKeys in the bowl.\n\n320\n00:13:41,220 --> 00:13:42,260\nBag on the hook.\n\n321\n00:13:42,800 --> 00:13:44,020\nCup on the shelf.\n\n322\n00:13:44,840 --> 00:13:46,220\nEach one is ten seconds.\n\n323\n00:13:46,600 --> 00:13:47,660\nEach one is nothing.\n\n324\n00:13:48,380 --> 00:13:52,279\nBut each one is also a small trap that the morning would fall into.\n\n325\n00:13:52,940 --> 00:13:56,690\nSo the evening removes the traps before the morning walks in.\n\n326\n00:13:56,720 --> 00:13:58,640\nAnd the house is ready.\n\n327\n00:13:58,720 --> 00:14:01,760\nRemoving the traps before the morning walks in, Mia.\n\n328\n00:14:01,900 --> 00:14:02,440\nI like that.\n\n329\n00:14:03,390 --> 00:14:04,730\nMy first thing is shoes.\n\n330\n00:14:05,420 --> 00:14:07,320\nIt took me years to find it.\n\n331\n00:14:07,360 --> 00:14:08,850\nMy shoes were always somewhere.\n\n332\n00:14:09,480 --> 00:14:10,580\nUnder the sofa.\n\n333\n00:14:10,600 --> 00:14:11,840\nBehind the door.\n\n334\n00:14:11,920 --> 00:14:12,600\nOne in the kitchen.\n\n335\n00:14:13,420 --> 00:14:17,240\nEvery morning, thirty seconds of looking, and a small bad feeling.\n\n336\n00:14:18,040 --> 00:14:20,960\nNow, at night, the shoes go by the door.\n\n337\n00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:21,540\nBoth of them.\n\n338\n00:14:22,080 --> 00:14:22,830\nNext to each other.\n\n339\n00:14:23,520 --> 00:14:24,170\nFive seconds.\n\n340\n00:14:25,700 --> 00:14:27,580\nBoth shoes, next to each other, Ben.\n\n341\n00:14:28,120 --> 00:14:29,340\nSmall is exact.\n\n342\n00:14:30,240 --> 00:14:32,520\nMy second thing is tomorrow's clothes on the chair.\n\n343\n00:14:33,160 --> 00:14:35,060\nOne shirt, one pair of trousers.\n\n344\n00:14:35,580 --> 00:14:37,140\nAnd I iron the shirt.\n\n345\n00:14:37,220 --> 00:14:37,789\nOnly one.\n\n346\n00:14:38,600 --> 00:14:40,440\nWhile I iron, I think about nothing.\n\n347\n00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:42,720\nThe iron goes back and forward.\n\n348\n00:14:43,220 --> 00:14:47,900\nAnd when the shirt is on the chair, the first hour of tomorrow is already made.\n\n349\n00:14:47,910 --> 00:14:54,020\n\"The first hour of tomorrow is on the chair.\" Mia, my second thing is for the hole after dinner.\n\n350\n00:14:54,840 --> 00:14:57,120\nThe dishes are done, and there are two hours.\n\n351\n00:14:57,720 --> 00:14:59,780\nAnd the hole wants the phone.\n\n352\n00:14:59,860 --> 00:15:01,640\nSo I have a small rule.\n\n353\n00:15:01,720 --> 00:15:02,880\nOne thing with my hands.\n\n354\n00:15:03,500 --> 00:15:04,340\nI fix a drawer.\n\n355\n00:15:04,860 --> 00:15:05,800\nI water the plants.\n\n356\n00:15:06,360 --> 00:15:07,060\nI write a card.\n\n357\n00:15:07,760 --> 00:15:08,860\nTwenty minutes with hands.\n\n358\n00:15:09,620 --> 00:15:11,750\nAnd after that, the phone is less loud.\n\n359\n00:15:12,680 --> 00:15:14,220\nSomething with hands, Ben.\n\n360\n00:15:14,610 --> 00:15:15,760\nMy ironing is that.\n\n361\n00:15:16,560 --> 00:15:18,749\nAnd my third thing is the one line.\n\n362\n00:15:19,420 --> 00:15:22,300\nEvery evening, I write one line on a small paper.\n\n363\n00:15:22,780 --> 00:15:23,970\nTomorrow's first thing.\n\n364\n00:15:24,570 --> 00:15:28,980\n\"Call the dentist.\" \"Walk before work.\" One line.\n\n365\n00:15:29,040 --> 00:15:31,680\nI put it on the kitchen table, next to the phone.\n\n366\n00:15:31,720 --> 00:15:33,760\nSo in the morning, I do not think.\n\n367\n00:15:34,250 --> 00:15:35,740\nThe paper already knows.\n\n368\n00:15:36,220 --> 00:15:37,500\nThe evening decided.\n\n369\n00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:39,840\nThe morning only does.\n\n370\n00:15:41,220 --> 00:15:42,320\n\"The evening decided.\n\n371\n00:15:42,850 --> 00:15:47,880\nThe morning only does.\" Mia, my third thing is Franklin's question.\n\n372\n00:15:48,760 --> 00:15:49,640\nWhat went well today?\n\n373\n00:15:50,500 --> 00:15:52,200\nSay it out loud, or in your head.\n\n374\n00:15:52,780 --> 00:15:53,320\nOne thing.\n\n375\n00:15:54,500 --> 00:15:56,760\nAnd my fourth thing is an end time.\n\n376\n00:15:57,520 --> 00:15:58,020\nTen thirty.\n\n377\n00:15:58,860 --> 00:16:00,080\nNot because I am a child.\n\n378\n00:16:00,680 --> 00:16:03,640\nBecause after ten thirty, nothing good happens.\n\n379\n00:16:04,420 --> 00:16:05,140\nNo good work.\n\n380\n00:16:05,740 --> 00:16:06,640\nNo good thinking.\n\n381\n00:16:07,360 --> 00:16:09,100\nOnly the phone, and worry.\n\n382\n00:16:10,340 --> 00:16:16,100\n\"After ten thirty, nothing good happens.\" Ben, that is true in my house too.\n\n383\n00:16:17,160 --> 00:16:18,190\nCan I ask you something?\n\n384\n00:16:19,080 --> 00:16:21,560\nWhat do you do when the evening goes wrong?\n\n385\n00:16:21,640 --> 00:16:27,240\nWhen you come home late, or tired, or sad, and the five things do not happen?\n\n386\n00:16:28,380 --> 00:16:29,240\nThat happens, Mia.\n\n387\n00:16:30,060 --> 00:16:31,460\nMaybe one night in five.\n\n388\n00:16:32,320 --> 00:16:34,900\nI come home at eleven, and I have nothing left.\n\n389\n00:16:35,820 --> 00:16:37,020\nSo I do one thing.\n\n390\n00:16:37,700 --> 00:16:38,360\nNot five.\n\n391\n00:16:39,140 --> 00:16:39,420\nOne.\n\n392\n00:16:40,440 --> 00:16:42,180\nUsually the one line for tomorrow.\n\n393\n00:16:43,140 --> 00:16:43,800\nTen seconds.\n\n394\n00:16:44,480 --> 00:16:49,360\n\"First thing: coffee, then the email to Sarah.\" And I sleep.\n\n395\n00:16:50,760 --> 00:16:52,400\nSo the clothes are not on the chair.\n\n396\n00:16:52,790 --> 00:16:54,140\nThe cup is on the desk.\n\n397\n00:16:54,780 --> 00:16:57,120\nBut the morning has a first line, Ben.\n\n398\n00:16:57,800 --> 00:17:01,500\nAnd a morning with a first line is a morning that starts.\n\n399\n00:17:02,740 --> 00:17:03,580\nExactly, Mia.\n\n400\n00:17:04,320 --> 00:17:06,180\nThe five things are the good version.\n\n401\n00:17:07,020 --> 00:17:09,060\nOne thing is the enough version.\n\n402\n00:17:09,880 --> 00:17:11,720\nTomorrow does not need five things.\n\n403\n00:17:12,380 --> 00:17:13,420\nTomorrow needs one.\n\n404\n00:17:14,300 --> 00:17:15,640\nAnd enough is enough.\n\n405\n00:17:16,980 --> 00:17:17,890\nDo you have a fifth thing?\n\n406\n00:17:18,600 --> 00:17:19,810\nSomething for the weekend, maybe?\n\n407\n00:17:21,020 --> 00:17:21,680\nYes, Ben.\n\n408\n00:17:22,059 --> 00:17:22,999\nSunday evening.\n\n409\n00:17:23,800 --> 00:17:26,680\nMany people feel sad on Sunday evening.\n\n410\n00:17:26,690 --> 00:17:27,890\nThe weekend is ending.\n\n411\n00:17:28,280 --> 00:17:30,000\nMonday is coming.\n\n412\n00:17:30,080 --> 00:17:31,300\nI used to feel that, too.\n\n413\n00:17:31,940 --> 00:17:35,030\nSunday at six, a small grey feeling.\n\n414\n00:17:35,780 --> 00:17:38,610\nNow I use Sunday evening to make Monday.\n\n415\n00:17:39,180 --> 00:17:40,060\nNot the whole week.\n\n416\n00:17:40,580 --> 00:17:41,180\nMonday.\n\n417\n00:17:41,940 --> 00:17:43,380\nI choose Monday's clothes.\n\n418\n00:17:43,860 --> 00:17:45,600\nI write Monday's one line.\n\n419\n00:17:46,020 --> 00:17:47,860\nI put Monday's bag by the door.\n\n420\n00:17:49,240 --> 00:17:51,700\nAnd the grey feeling gets smaller, Mia?\n\n421\n00:17:51,760 --> 00:17:54,780\nBecause Monday is not a wall anymore?\n\n422\n00:17:54,800 --> 00:17:55,920\nMuch smaller, Ben.\n\n423\n00:17:56,620 --> 00:18:00,000\nMonday is a shirt on a chair and a line on a paper.\n\n424\n00:18:00,569 --> 00:18:02,120\nThat is all Monday is.\n\n425\n00:18:02,800 --> 00:18:04,780\nAnd for our listeners who are learning English?\n\n426\n00:18:05,380 --> 00:18:07,260\nIs the evening a good time for English?\n\n427\n00:18:08,600 --> 00:18:11,340\nThe best time, Mia, and nobody uses it.\n\n428\n00:18:12,200 --> 00:18:13,860\nMany learners study late at night.\n\n429\n00:18:14,569 --> 00:18:16,349\nThey are tired, and they push.\n\n430\n00:18:17,260 --> 00:18:18,060\nOne more page.\n\n431\n00:18:18,700 --> 00:18:20,060\nOne more list of words.\n\n432\n00:18:20,920 --> 00:18:22,300\nAnd the words do not stay.\n\n433\n00:18:23,160 --> 00:18:25,240\nA tired head does not keep words.\n\n434\n00:18:25,840 --> 00:18:26,540\nIt drops them.\n\n435\n00:18:27,560 --> 00:18:28,760\nSo here is my idea.\n\n436\n00:18:29,800 --> 00:18:34,460\nStudy early in the evening, for twenty minutes, when the head is still open.\n\n437\n00:18:35,440 --> 00:18:36,080\nThen stop.\n\n438\n00:18:36,940 --> 00:18:37,300\nSleep.\n\n439\n00:18:38,260 --> 00:18:39,080\nAnd the words stay.\n\n440\n00:18:39,840 --> 00:18:45,120\n\"The night does the work, not you.\" Ben, that is beautiful.\n\n441\n00:18:45,740 --> 00:18:48,200\nAnd this podcast is an evening thing, actually.\n\n442\n00:18:48,900 --> 00:18:50,260\nPut it on while you wash the cup.\n\n443\n00:18:50,880 --> 00:18:53,580\nLet the words go in while your hands are busy.\n\n444\n00:18:53,620 --> 00:18:56,139\nTomorrow morning, the words are still there.\n\n445\n00:18:56,200 --> 00:18:57,300\nThe night kept them.\n\n446\n00:18:58,940 --> 00:18:59,810\nThe night kept them.\n\n447\n00:19:00,660 --> 00:19:06,380\nAnd to everyone listening at night right now, thank you for choosing this for your evening.\n\n448\n00:19:06,440 --> 00:19:07,280\nYou could be scrolling.\n\n449\n00:19:08,080 --> 00:19:08,740\nYou are listening.\n\n450\n00:19:09,420 --> 00:19:10,680\nThat is a small good thing.\n\n451\n00:19:11,480 --> 00:19:12,940\nPut it on Franklin's list tonight.\n\n452\n00:19:13,580 --> 00:19:14,540\n\"What went well today?\n\n453\n00:19:15,240 --> 00:19:18,930\nI listened to twenty minutes of slow English.\" That counts.\n\n454\n00:19:20,550 --> 00:19:21,480\nThat counts, Ben.\n\n455\n00:19:22,340 --> 00:19:24,880\nAnd I think that is our message today.\n\n456\n00:19:24,920 --> 00:19:27,280\nThe morning does not start in the morning.\n\n457\n00:19:27,300 --> 00:19:29,380\nIt starts the night before.\n\n458\n00:19:29,460 --> 00:19:31,700\nA messy evening gives a messy morning.\n\n459\n00:19:32,300 --> 00:19:35,000\nA ready evening gives an easy morning.\n\n460\n00:19:35,040 --> 00:19:41,860\nAnd a ready evening is five small things, ten minutes, when time is cheap and soft.\n\n461\n00:19:41,870 --> 00:19:44,700\nTomorrow is on the chair.\n\n462\n00:19:44,779 --> 00:19:46,260\nTomorrow is on the chair.\n\n463\n00:19:47,260 --> 00:19:52,710\nMia, let's repeat today's important words, slowly, because our listeners want to remember them.\n\n464\n00:19:54,040 --> 00:19:54,390\nEvening.\n\n465\n00:19:55,400 --> 00:19:58,480\nThe end of the day, after work, before sleep.\n\n466\n00:19:59,440 --> 00:20:00,220\nA good evening.\n\n467\n00:20:00,990 --> 00:20:02,640\nThe evening shapes tomorrow.\n\n468\n00:20:04,020 --> 00:20:05,200\nTomorrow.\n\n469\n00:20:05,240 --> 00:20:06,320\nThe day after today.\n\n470\n00:20:07,280 --> 00:20:08,180\nTomorrow's shirt.\n\n471\n00:20:09,580 --> 00:20:09,920\nNight.\n\n472\n00:20:10,680 --> 00:20:12,300\nWhen it is dark and we sleep.\n\n473\n00:20:13,680 --> 00:20:15,080\nLast night.\n\n474\n00:20:15,160 --> 00:20:16,280\nThe night does the work.\n\n475\n00:20:17,760 --> 00:20:18,980\nReady.\n\n476\n00:20:19,010 --> 00:20:19,580\nPrepared.\n\n477\n00:20:20,620 --> 00:20:21,460\nA ready evening.\n\n478\n00:20:22,410 --> 00:20:23,420\nThe house is ready.\n\n479\n00:20:24,900 --> 00:20:25,940\nSmall.\n\n480\n00:20:26,000 --> 00:20:26,560\nNot big.\n\n481\n00:20:27,400 --> 00:20:28,680\nFive small things.\n\n482\n00:20:30,460 --> 00:20:30,960\nEvening.\n\n483\n00:20:31,500 --> 00:20:32,040\nTomorrow.\n\n484\n00:20:32,700 --> 00:20:33,080\nNight.\n\n485\n00:20:33,920 --> 00:20:34,280\nReady.\n\n486\n00:20:35,120 --> 00:20:35,580\nSmall.\n\n487\n00:20:36,720 --> 00:20:38,100\nFive words for tonight.\n\n488\n00:20:38,460 --> 00:20:39,880\nAnd one big idea.\n\n489\n00:20:40,540 --> 00:20:41,900\nPut things in their places.\n\n490\n00:20:42,740 --> 00:20:44,900\nNow, Ben, do you have a question for our listeners?\n\n491\n00:20:46,380 --> 00:20:46,900\nYes, Mia.\n\n492\n00:20:47,460 --> 00:20:49,510\nEveryone listening, here is my question.\n\n493\n00:20:50,320 --> 00:20:52,180\nWhat is your one evening thing?\n\n494\n00:20:53,080 --> 00:20:54,120\nThe bowl for the keys?\n\n495\n00:20:54,820 --> 00:20:55,680\nThe shirt on the chair?\n\n496\n00:20:56,640 --> 00:20:57,650\nThe phone in the kitchen?\n\n497\n00:20:58,640 --> 00:21:01,380\nWrite it in the comments in one short sentence.\n\n498\n00:21:02,100 --> 00:21:11,460\nFor example, \"My evening thing is one line for tomorrow.\" Reading those sentences is our evening thing, actually.\n\n499\n00:21:11,540 --> 00:21:14,160\nWe read them at night, with tea.\n\n500\n00:21:15,100 --> 00:21:17,180\nWith tea, before the lights go off.\n\n501\n00:21:17,940 --> 00:21:21,880\nAnd if this episode helped you, please subscribe to Two Cups English.\n\n502\n00:21:22,460 --> 00:21:24,680\nWe make a new episode every few days.\n\n503\n00:21:25,300 --> 00:21:30,440\nSlow English, simple words, and one true story each time.\n\n504\n00:21:30,470 --> 00:21:33,060\nThe full text of this episode is in the description.\n\n505\n00:21:33,780 --> 00:21:36,190\nRead it tonight, or read it tomorrow.\n\n506\n00:21:36,840 --> 00:21:39,180\nTomorrow is fine, this time.\n\n507\n00:21:39,240 --> 00:21:40,220\nIt is on the chair.\n\n508\n00:21:41,960 --> 00:21:44,500\nThank you for spending this evening with us.\n\n509\n00:21:44,560 --> 00:21:46,880\nMaybe you are washing the dishes.\n\n510\n00:21:46,940 --> 00:21:48,160\nMaybe you are in bed already.\n\n511\n00:21:49,100 --> 00:21:52,680\nWherever you are, do one small thing before you sleep.\n\n512\n00:21:53,580 --> 00:21:55,200\nPut one thing in its place.\n\n513\n00:21:56,040 --> 00:21:57,040\nWrite one line.\n\n514\n00:21:57,809 --> 00:21:58,680\nAnd then rest.\n\n515\n00:21:59,480 --> 00:22:01,619\nTomorrow is already a little bit made.\n\n516\n00:22:02,460 --> 00:22:02,900\nGood night.\n\n517\n00:22:04,820 --> 00:22:05,480\nThis is Mia.\n\n518\n00:22:06,360 --> 00:22:07,040\nAnd this is Ben.\n\n519\n00:22:07,959 --> 00:22:09,399\nThis was Two Cups English.\n\n520\n00:22:09,720 --> 00:22:11,259\nPut things in their places.\n\n521\n00:22:11,720 --> 00:22:12,819\nWe will see you tomorrow."},{"code":"CUP06","file":"CUP06.srt","srt":"1\n00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,280\nHello everyone, and welcome to Two Cups English.\n\n2\n00:00:03,840 --> 00:00:06,720\nI'm Mia, and Ben is here with me.\n\n3\n00:00:06,780 --> 00:00:09,580\nThis podcast is for people who are learning English.\n\n4\n00:00:10,220 --> 00:00:11,080\nWe speak slowly.\n\n5\n00:00:11,620 --> 00:00:12,980\nWe use simple words.\n\n6\n00:00:13,560 --> 00:00:16,419\nAnd we repeat the important words many times.\n\n7\n00:00:17,300 --> 00:00:19,630\nToday, our topic is kindness.\n\n8\n00:00:20,330 --> 00:00:22,300\nNot kindness to other people.\n\n9\n00:00:22,360 --> 00:00:23,620\nKindness to yourself.\n\n10\n00:00:24,400 --> 00:00:26,740\nBen, did you make a mistake this week?\n\n11\n00:00:28,040 --> 00:00:28,820\nHi Mia.\n\n12\n00:00:28,860 --> 00:00:29,390\nHi everyone.\n\n13\n00:00:30,100 --> 00:00:31,200\nYes, I did.\n\n14\n00:00:32,119 --> 00:00:35,140\nOn Tuesday, I forgot a meeting.\n\n15\n00:00:35,200 --> 00:00:36,960\nCompletely.\n\n16\n00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:40,020\nMy colleague called me, and I was at home, making soup.\n\n17\n00:00:41,740 --> 00:00:47,210\nAnd for the rest of the day, a voice in my head said, \"You are so lazy.\n\n18\n00:00:47,860 --> 00:00:51,400\nYou always do this.\" What about you, Mia?\n\n19\n00:00:51,900 --> 00:00:52,510\nAny mistakes?\n\n20\n00:00:54,020 --> 00:00:54,720\nOne, Ben.\n\n21\n00:00:55,420 --> 00:00:58,620\nYesterday, I sent an email to the wrong person.\n\n22\n00:00:58,760 --> 00:00:59,480\nA small mistake.\n\n23\n00:00:59,980 --> 00:01:00,900\nNobody was angry.\n\n24\n00:01:01,380 --> 00:01:02,980\nIt was fixed in five minutes.\n\n25\n00:01:03,600 --> 00:01:08,360\nBut for the rest of the day, my voice said, \"You are so stupid.\n\n26\n00:01:08,860 --> 00:01:12,240\nYou are not good at your job.\" All day.\n\n27\n00:01:12,820 --> 00:01:14,360\nFor a five-minute mistake.\n\n28\n00:01:15,260 --> 00:01:17,680\nAll day, for five minutes, Mia.\n\n29\n00:01:18,420 --> 00:01:19,940\nThat is a bad trade.\n\n30\n00:01:21,060 --> 00:01:21,940\nAnd that voice.\n\n31\n00:01:22,890 --> 00:01:26,440\n\"You are so stupid.\" I know that voice.\n\n32\n00:01:27,200 --> 00:01:28,039\nWhere does it come from?\n\n33\n00:01:29,040 --> 00:01:30,800\nI think the voice is old, Ben.\n\n34\n00:01:31,400 --> 00:01:32,900\nIt started at school, maybe.\n\n35\n00:01:33,440 --> 00:01:34,100\nA teacher.\n\n36\n00:01:34,460 --> 00:01:35,060\nA parent.\n\n37\n00:01:35,520 --> 00:01:36,120\nA test.\n\n38\n00:01:36,860 --> 00:01:40,670\nSomebody said, \"You can do better,\" and the voice learned it.\n\n39\n00:01:41,280 --> 00:01:44,950\nAnd now it says it every day, even when nobody else does.\n\n40\n00:01:45,680 --> 00:01:49,540\nIt is the least kind person I know, and it lives in my head.\n\n41\n00:01:50,280 --> 00:01:58,120\n\"The least kind person I know, and it lives in my head.\" Mia, that is our topic today.\n\n42\n00:01:59,080 --> 00:02:00,220\nSo here is our plan.\n\n43\n00:02:01,180 --> 00:02:04,580\nIn part one, we talk about our own voices.\n\n44\n00:02:04,620 --> 00:02:06,160\nWhat they say, and when.\n\n45\n00:02:07,160 --> 00:02:12,260\nIn part two, we ask why kindness to yourself works better than the hard voice.\n\n46\n00:02:13,100 --> 00:02:14,880\nAnd I have a true story for that.\n\n47\n00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:20,860\nIn part three, we share simple ways to talk to yourself like a friend.\n\n48\n00:02:20,870 --> 00:02:22,560\nDoes that sound good?\n\n49\n00:02:22,570 --> 00:02:24,240\nThat sounds good, Ben.\n\n50\n00:02:24,320 --> 00:02:28,049\nAnd to everyone listening, please do not worry if you miss something.\n\n51\n00:02:28,540 --> 00:02:30,140\nYou will hear it again.\n\n52\n00:02:30,200 --> 00:02:34,549\nYou can listen while you walk, while you cook, or while you have a hard day.\n\n53\n00:02:35,260 --> 00:02:38,560\nAnd if today is a hard day, this episode is for you.\n\n54\n00:02:39,620 --> 00:02:40,160\nOkay, Ben.\n\n55\n00:02:40,520 --> 00:02:41,060\nPart one.\n\n56\n00:02:41,780 --> 00:02:44,880\nYour voice says \"lazy.\" Tell me more about it.\n\n57\n00:02:45,720 --> 00:02:47,940\nMy voice does not say \"stupid,\" Mia.\n\n58\n00:02:48,560 --> 00:02:59,540\nIt says \"lazy.\" \"You should have done more today.\" \"Other people work harder.\" \"You are wasting your life.\" And the strange thing is this.\n\n59\n00:03:00,520 --> 00:03:13,060\nIf my friend worked all day, made a soup, and called his mother, I would say, \"That is a good day.\" But the voice says to me, \"You did nothing.\" Same day.\n\n60\n00:03:13,700 --> 00:03:14,460\nDifferent judge.\n\n61\n00:03:15,520 --> 00:03:16,280\n\"Same day.\n\n62\n00:03:16,580 --> 00:03:19,960\nDifferent judge.\" Ben, that is exactly it.\n\n63\n00:03:20,660 --> 00:03:22,720\nCan I tell you about the moment I noticed this?\n\n64\n00:03:23,440 --> 00:03:25,350\nIt was last year, with my friend Anna.\n\n65\n00:03:26,240 --> 00:03:27,280\nAnna, from the café?\n\n66\n00:03:27,900 --> 00:03:28,569\nI remember her.\n\n67\n00:03:29,300 --> 00:03:31,040\nShe is the one who always laughs first.\n\n68\n00:03:31,780 --> 00:03:33,500\nWhat happened, Mia?\n\n69\n00:03:33,600 --> 00:03:35,860\nShe failed her driving test, Ben.\n\n70\n00:03:35,920 --> 00:03:37,400\nShe called me, crying.\n\n71\n00:03:37,900 --> 00:03:40,980\nAnd I said, \"Anna, one test is one test.\n\n72\n00:03:41,420 --> 00:03:43,320\nYou drove well for months.\n\n73\n00:03:43,380 --> 00:03:45,360\nYou will pass next time.\n\n74\n00:03:45,380 --> 00:03:48,800\nBe kind to yourself tonight.\" I meant every word.\n\n75\n00:03:49,600 --> 00:03:51,219\nThat is a good friend, Mia.\n\n76\n00:03:51,320 --> 00:03:51,990\nA real friend.\n\n77\n00:03:52,800 --> 00:03:53,530\nAnd after the call?\n\n78\n00:03:55,280 --> 00:03:57,280\nAfter the call, I sat there, Ben.\n\n79\n00:03:57,810 --> 00:04:05,310\nAnd I thought, \"I failed my driving test twice, and I called myself a failure for a year.\" Same words.\n\n80\n00:04:05,720 --> 00:04:06,400\nSame test.\n\n81\n00:04:06,980 --> 00:04:08,060\nKind to Anna.\n\n82\n00:04:08,580 --> 00:04:09,560\nCruel to me.\n\n83\n00:04:10,220 --> 00:04:12,560\nAnd I did not even see it until that call.\n\n84\n00:04:13,600 --> 00:04:15,780\nKind to Anna, cruel to you.\n\n85\n00:04:16,920 --> 00:04:18,480\nMia, I think everyone does this.\n\n86\n00:04:18,960 --> 00:04:20,060\nWe have two languages.\n\n87\n00:04:20,700 --> 00:04:22,800\nOne for friends, one for ourselves.\n\n88\n00:04:23,500 --> 00:04:25,420\nWe forgive a friend's mistake in one minute.\n\n89\n00:04:25,860 --> 00:04:27,720\nWe punish our own mistake for a year.\n\n90\n00:04:28,760 --> 00:04:30,620\nOne minute is enough for a friend's mistake.\n\n91\n00:04:31,280 --> 00:04:32,160\nWhy not for ours?\n\n92\n00:04:33,420 --> 00:04:35,180\nAnd what did you do after the driving tests?\n\n93\n00:04:35,560 --> 00:04:36,360\nDid you try again?\n\n94\n00:04:37,380 --> 00:04:38,060\nNo, Ben.\n\n95\n00:04:38,370 --> 00:04:39,620\nThat is the sad part.\n\n96\n00:04:40,060 --> 00:04:42,480\nI avoided driving for a year.\n\n97\n00:04:42,560 --> 00:04:44,840\nNot because I was kind to myself.\n\n98\n00:04:44,860 --> 00:04:48,020\nBecause I was cruel, and cruel made me afraid.\n\n99\n00:04:48,860 --> 00:04:52,610\nIf somebody shouts at you every time you fail, you stop trying.\n\n100\n00:04:53,280 --> 00:04:56,180\nEven if the somebody is you.\n\n101\n00:04:56,190 --> 00:04:59,120\n\"Even if the somebody is you.\" Yes, Mia.\n\n102\n00:04:59,800 --> 00:05:01,020\nI have a story like that too.\n\n103\n00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:03,640\nTwo years ago, I lost a job.\n\n104\n00:05:04,140 --> 00:05:06,980\nAnd for three weeks, the voice did not stop.\n\n105\n00:05:07,070 --> 00:05:07,690\n\"You failed.\n\n106\n00:05:08,260 --> 00:05:09,240\nYou are not good enough.\n\n107\n00:05:09,780 --> 00:05:12,670\nYou will never find another one.\" Every morning.\n\n108\n00:05:13,280 --> 00:05:13,760\nEvery night.\n\n109\n00:05:14,860 --> 00:05:16,070\nThree weeks, Ben.\n\n110\n00:05:16,540 --> 00:05:20,320\nThat is a long time to live with that voice every morning.\n\n111\n00:05:20,340 --> 00:05:22,140\nAnd what did you do in those three weeks?\n\n112\n00:05:23,520 --> 00:05:24,760\nNothing, Mia.\n\n113\n00:05:24,840 --> 00:05:26,700\nI did not apply for any jobs.\n\n114\n00:05:26,820 --> 00:05:27,380\nNot one.\n\n115\n00:05:28,080 --> 00:05:29,760\nBecause the voice said there was no point.\n\n116\n00:05:30,540 --> 00:05:32,200\nAnd then a friend came to visit.\n\n117\n00:05:32,220 --> 00:05:33,390\nAnd he said one sentence.\n\n118\n00:05:34,120 --> 00:05:35,840\n\"Ben, you lost a job.\n\n119\n00:05:36,400 --> 00:05:37,700\nYou did not lose yourself.\"\n\n120\n00:05:38,870 --> 00:05:39,940\n\"You lost a job.\n\n121\n00:05:40,480 --> 00:05:44,620\nYou did not lose yourself.\" Ben, that is what a friend does.\n\n122\n00:05:45,180 --> 00:05:47,740\nA friend separates the thing from the person.\n\n123\n00:05:48,620 --> 00:05:50,200\nWhat happened after that sentence?\n\n124\n00:05:51,380 --> 00:05:52,100\nI cried, Mia.\n\n125\n00:05:52,750 --> 00:05:55,660\nAnd the next day, I applied for two jobs.\n\n126\n00:05:55,780 --> 00:06:01,389\nOne sentence, from a kind voice, did in one day what three weeks of the hard voice could not do.\n\n127\n00:06:02,580 --> 00:06:05,240\nAnd that is when I started to think about this.\n\n128\n00:06:05,300 --> 00:06:06,940\nMaybe the hard voice does not work.\n\n129\n00:06:07,740 --> 00:06:09,840\nMaybe it never worked.\n\n130\n00:06:09,860 --> 00:06:11,160\nMaybe it never worked.\n\n131\n00:06:12,240 --> 00:06:18,190\nBen, I want to talk about your friend Tom for a moment, because you told me about him, and he taught me something.\n\n132\n00:06:19,580 --> 00:06:20,380\nTom!\n\n133\n00:06:20,440 --> 00:06:21,160\nYes.\n\n134\n00:06:21,220 --> 00:06:22,960\nTom forgets things, Mia.\n\n135\n00:06:23,040 --> 00:06:24,790\nKeys, dates, names.\n\n136\n00:06:25,420 --> 00:06:27,100\nAnd when he forgets, he laughs.\n\n137\n00:06:27,720 --> 00:06:31,200\nHe says, \"Classic Tom.\" And then he fixes it.\n\n138\n00:06:31,940 --> 00:06:32,540\nNo speech.\n\n139\n00:06:32,960 --> 00:06:33,700\nNo punishment.\n\n140\n00:06:34,229 --> 00:06:36,290\n\"Classic Tom,\" and on with the day.\n\n141\n00:06:37,980 --> 00:06:40,480\n\"Classic Tom.\" I love that, Ben.\n\n142\n00:06:41,100 --> 00:06:43,440\nIt is kind and honest at the same time.\n\n143\n00:06:44,080 --> 00:06:53,380\nIt does not say, \"I never make mistakes.\" It says, \"I made one, and I am still me.\" And is Tom lazy about his mistakes?\n\n144\n00:06:53,940 --> 00:06:54,980\nThe opposite, Mia.\n\n145\n00:06:55,360 --> 00:06:57,360\nHe fixes them faster than I fix mine.\n\n146\n00:06:58,060 --> 00:07:01,260\nBecause he does not spend an hour in the punishment room first.\n\n147\n00:07:01,320 --> 00:07:02,610\nHe goes straight to the fixing.\n\n148\n00:07:03,340 --> 00:07:06,480\nBeing kind to himself saves him an hour every time.\n\n149\n00:07:06,540 --> 00:07:08,040\nAnd the evening, too.\n\n150\n00:07:08,050 --> 00:07:10,420\nThe punishment room does not close at five.\n\n151\n00:07:10,460 --> 00:07:12,320\nIt stays open all night.\n\n152\n00:07:12,380 --> 00:07:15,560\nAnd one hour in that room is enough to ruin a day.\n\n153\n00:07:15,589 --> 00:07:20,800\n\"The punishment room stays open all night.\" Ben, that is true.\n\n154\n00:07:21,720 --> 00:07:23,240\nOkay, so that was part one.\n\n155\n00:07:23,500 --> 00:07:24,200\nOur voices.\n\n156\n00:07:24,700 --> 00:07:31,060\n\"Stupid\" and \"lazy,\" Anna's driving test, a lost job, and Classic Tom.\n\n157\n00:07:31,900 --> 00:07:33,000\nNow, part two.\n\n158\n00:07:33,820 --> 00:07:36,780\nWhy does kindness work better than the hard voice?\n\n159\n00:07:37,560 --> 00:07:39,520\nBen, you said there is real research.\n\n160\n00:07:40,040 --> 00:07:40,640\nThere is, Mia.\n\n161\n00:07:41,380 --> 00:07:42,199\nOkay, part two.\n\n162\n00:07:42,980 --> 00:07:44,000\nThis is a true story.\n\n163\n00:07:44,680 --> 00:07:46,900\nThere is a scientist named Kristin Neff.\n\n164\n00:07:47,520 --> 00:07:49,300\nShe works at the University of Texas.\n\n165\n00:07:50,120 --> 00:07:54,320\nIn 2003, she started to study something she called self-compassion.\n\n166\n00:07:55,220 --> 00:07:57,790\nCompassion is kindness for someone who is suffering.\n\n167\n00:07:58,640 --> 00:08:01,680\nSelf-compassion is that same kindness, for yourself.\n\n168\n00:08:02,240 --> 00:08:05,519\nKindness for yourself, as a science, Ben?\n\n169\n00:08:05,740 --> 00:08:08,020\nI did not know you could measure that.\n\n170\n00:08:08,080 --> 00:08:09,200\nWhat did she ask people?\n\n171\n00:08:09,670 --> 00:08:10,820\nA simple question, Mia.\n\n172\n00:08:11,540 --> 00:08:15,430\nWhen you fail, do you talk to yourself like a friend, or like an enemy?\n\n173\n00:08:16,210 --> 00:08:19,240\nAnd she measured it, in thousands of people.\n\n174\n00:08:19,270 --> 00:08:21,660\nAnd what she found surprised a lot of people.\n\n175\n00:08:22,240 --> 00:08:23,180\nWhat did she find?\n\n176\n00:08:23,980 --> 00:08:26,540\nI think many people believe the hard voice helps.\n\n177\n00:08:27,230 --> 00:08:32,460\n\"If I am kind to myself, I will become lazy.\" That is the fear, isn't it?\n\n178\n00:08:32,470 --> 00:08:33,580\nThat is the fear, Mia.\n\n179\n00:08:34,300 --> 00:08:35,810\nBut the research found the opposite.\n\n180\n00:08:36,560 --> 00:08:40,060\nPeople who were kind to themselves after a mistake were less afraid.\n\n181\n00:08:40,590 --> 00:08:41,810\nThey had less anxiety.\n\n182\n00:08:42,640 --> 00:08:44,680\nAnd here is the important part.\n\n183\n00:08:44,740 --> 00:08:49,300\nIn a later study, in 2012, researchers gave people a hard test.\n\n184\n00:08:49,820 --> 00:08:51,200\nA hard test, Ben.\n\n185\n00:08:51,820 --> 00:08:53,780\nI already feel nervous for those people.\n\n186\n00:08:54,540 --> 00:08:55,020\nAnd then?\n\n187\n00:08:55,660 --> 00:08:57,200\nWhat happened after they failed?\n\n188\n00:08:57,780 --> 00:09:01,120\nSome people were told to be kind to themselves after they failed, Mia.\n\n189\n00:09:01,850 --> 00:09:04,200\nAnd those people studied more for the next test.\n\n190\n00:09:04,680 --> 00:09:05,240\nNot less.\n\n191\n00:09:05,960 --> 00:09:06,240\nMore.\n\n192\n00:09:07,080 --> 00:09:09,280\nKindness did not make them lazy.\n\n193\n00:09:09,340 --> 00:09:11,320\nKindness made them try again.\n\n194\n00:09:11,330 --> 00:09:16,949\n\"Kindness made them try again.\" Ben, that is the opposite of what the voice says.\n\n195\n00:09:17,540 --> 00:09:25,480\nThe voice says, \"If I am hard on you, you will improve.\" But the hard voice makes me afraid, and afraid people hide.\n\n196\n00:09:25,520 --> 00:09:26,660\nThey avoid the test.\n\n197\n00:09:27,280 --> 00:09:28,330\nLike me, with driving.\n\n198\n00:09:29,160 --> 00:09:30,780\nLike you, with driving, Mia.\n\n199\n00:09:31,350 --> 00:09:32,830\nAnd like me, with the jobs.\n\n200\n00:09:33,860 --> 00:09:36,360\nSo the question is, how do we change the voice?\n\n201\n00:09:37,040 --> 00:09:39,650\nBecause it is old, and it has its own key.\n\n202\n00:09:40,540 --> 00:09:42,240\nLet me tell you what I do.\n\n203\n00:09:42,280 --> 00:09:46,260\nWhen the voice starts, I say the sentence again, but I put a friend's name in it.\n\n204\n00:09:46,900 --> 00:09:48,680\nA friend's name, Ben?\n\n205\n00:09:48,760 --> 00:09:49,480\nHow does that work?\n\n206\n00:09:49,920 --> 00:09:50,500\nAny friend?\n\n207\n00:09:51,140 --> 00:09:53,660\nThe voice says, \"You are lazy,\" Mia.\n\n208\n00:09:54,660 --> 00:09:58,250\nI say, \"Tom is lazy.\" And immediately, I feel it.\n\n209\n00:09:59,060 --> 00:09:59,880\nThat is not true.\n\n210\n00:10:00,360 --> 00:10:02,020\nTom worked all day.\n\n211\n00:10:02,080 --> 00:10:02,980\nTom made soup.\n\n212\n00:10:03,620 --> 00:10:04,240\nThat is enough.\n\n213\n00:10:04,900 --> 00:10:06,040\nTom is not lazy.\n\n214\n00:10:06,880 --> 00:10:09,960\nAnd if it is not true for Tom, it is not true for me.\n\n215\n00:10:10,820 --> 00:10:11,380\nSame day.\n\n216\n00:10:11,930 --> 00:10:13,360\nThe name changes the judge.\n\n217\n00:10:14,080 --> 00:10:17,080\n\"The name changes the judge.\" I like that, Ben.\n\n218\n00:10:18,020 --> 00:10:20,320\nI do something similar, but with the friend's face.\n\n219\n00:10:21,060 --> 00:10:23,840\nWhen the voice comes, I imagine Anna sitting next to me.\n\n220\n00:10:24,200 --> 00:10:29,140\nAnd I ask, \"What would I say to Anna right now?\" And the answer comes fast.\n\n221\n00:10:29,930 --> 00:10:30,470\n\"It's okay.\n\n222\n00:10:31,040 --> 00:10:31,620\nYou fixed it.\n\n223\n00:10:32,260 --> 00:10:33,540\nHave some tea.\"\n\n224\n00:10:34,310 --> 00:10:38,680\n\"Have some tea.\" Mia, that is the most Two Cups answer.\n\n225\n00:10:38,720 --> 00:10:39,390\nAnd do you say it?\n\n226\n00:10:39,900 --> 00:10:40,340\nOut loud?\n\n227\n00:10:42,080 --> 00:10:44,860\nOut loud, Ben, if nobody is there.\n\n228\n00:10:44,920 --> 00:10:46,710\nIt feels strange the first time.\n\n229\n00:10:46,740 --> 00:10:47,980\nLike talking to a child.\n\n230\n00:10:48,600 --> 00:10:49,860\nBut maybe that is the point.\n\n231\n00:10:50,410 --> 00:10:52,220\nThe part of me that is hurt is small.\n\n232\n00:10:52,680 --> 00:10:54,800\nAnd small things need kind words.\n\n233\n00:10:55,580 --> 00:11:02,500\n\"Small things need kind words.\" Mia, let me talk about the word \"mistake,\" because the voice uses it wrong.\n\n234\n00:11:03,340 --> 00:11:05,140\nA mistake is a thing that happened.\n\n235\n00:11:05,740 --> 00:11:07,220\nAn email to the wrong person.\n\n236\n00:11:07,800 --> 00:11:08,560\nA wrong turn.\n\n237\n00:11:09,160 --> 00:11:10,020\nA burned soup.\n\n238\n00:11:10,980 --> 00:11:13,340\nThe voice takes the mistake and makes it a person.\n\n239\n00:11:14,080 --> 00:11:17,900\n\"You are a mistake.\" One email becomes a whole life.\n\n240\n00:11:18,640 --> 00:11:20,640\nBut a mistake is not a person, Ben.\n\n241\n00:11:21,160 --> 00:11:24,060\nIt is one thing, on one day.\n\n242\n00:11:24,120 --> 00:11:26,200\nYesterday, Mia made one mistake.\n\n243\n00:11:26,800 --> 00:11:28,220\nMia is not a mistake.\n\n244\n00:11:28,800 --> 00:11:31,140\nThose are two very different sentences.\n\n245\n00:11:31,150 --> 00:11:32,900\nAnd the voice only knows one of them.\n\n246\n00:11:33,640 --> 00:11:37,680\n\"A mistake is a thing, not a person.\" Mia, write that down.\n\n247\n00:11:38,500 --> 00:11:40,840\nAnd there is a second word the voice uses wrong.\n\n248\n00:11:41,550 --> 00:11:47,700\n\"Always.\" \"You always do this.\" \"You never learn.\" Always and never.\n\n249\n00:11:48,220 --> 00:11:50,040\nThose are the voice's favorite words.\n\n250\n00:11:50,840 --> 00:11:52,580\nBut I do not live in \"always,\" Ben.\n\n251\n00:11:53,160 --> 00:11:54,680\nI live in today.\n\n252\n00:11:54,740 --> 00:11:56,160\nToday I made one mistake.\n\n253\n00:11:56,780 --> 00:12:00,200\nToday I also finished two reports and helped a new colleague.\n\n254\n00:12:00,940 --> 00:12:01,880\nToday is a mix.\n\n255\n00:12:02,460 --> 00:12:03,560\nEvery day is a mix.\n\n256\n00:12:04,260 --> 00:12:09,320\nWhen I say \"today\" instead of \"always,\" the mistake becomes one thing in a full day.\n\n257\n00:12:09,880 --> 00:12:10,900\nNot the whole story.\n\n258\n00:12:11,700 --> 00:12:16,660\n\"Today, not always.\" That is a small word change with a big effect, Mia.\n\n259\n00:12:17,460 --> 00:12:18,440\nAnd one more word.\n\n260\n00:12:19,100 --> 00:12:22,110\n\"Enough.\" The hard voice never says a number.\n\n261\n00:12:22,890 --> 00:12:24,409\n\"Not enough\" has no number.\n\n262\n00:12:25,300 --> 00:12:26,560\nHow much English is enough?\n\n263\n00:12:27,189 --> 00:12:28,340\nHow much work is enough?\n\n264\n00:12:29,080 --> 00:12:29,930\nIt never says.\n\n265\n00:12:30,600 --> 00:12:31,620\nSo we can never reach it.\n\n266\n00:12:32,400 --> 00:12:33,420\nSo what do you do, Ben?\n\n267\n00:12:34,080 --> 00:12:37,390\nWhen the voice says \"not enough\" and gives you no number?\n\n268\n00:12:38,140 --> 00:12:39,100\nToday, for example.\n\n269\n00:12:39,680 --> 00:12:45,020\nToday I studied ten minutes, and the voice said, \"Not enough.\" Do you argue with it?\n\n270\n00:12:45,920 --> 00:12:49,540\nI ask it, \"How much is enough, then?\" And it has no answer, Mia.\n\n271\n00:12:50,080 --> 00:12:50,860\nIt never had one.\n\n272\n00:12:51,700 --> 00:12:54,240\nThen I decide the number myself, like a friend would.\n\n273\n00:12:54,950 --> 00:12:56,760\n\"You studied twenty minutes today.\n\n274\n00:12:56,820 --> 00:12:59,550\nThat is enough for a Tuesday.\" Twenty minutes.\n\n275\n00:13:00,040 --> 00:13:01,110\nA Tuesday.\n\n276\n00:13:01,120 --> 00:13:01,770\nReal things.\n\n277\n00:13:02,610 --> 00:13:04,380\n\"The voice lives in fog.\n\n278\n00:13:05,120 --> 00:13:08,650\nKindness lives in numbers.\" Ben, I love that.\n\n279\n00:13:09,340 --> 00:13:11,060\nOkay, so that was part two.\n\n280\n00:13:11,100 --> 00:13:12,240\nWhy kindness works.\n\n281\n00:13:12,730 --> 00:13:14,840\nWe talked about Kristin Neff and the test.\n\n282\n00:13:15,300 --> 00:13:17,720\nWe said kindness made people try again.\n\n283\n00:13:17,760 --> 00:13:20,500\nAnd we said a mistake is a thing, not a person.\n\n284\n00:13:21,280 --> 00:13:22,260\nNow, part three.\n\n285\n00:13:22,960 --> 00:13:25,100\nSimple ways to talk to yourself like a friend.\n\n286\n00:13:25,600 --> 00:13:26,840\nBen, you go first.\n\n287\n00:13:27,580 --> 00:13:28,620\nOkay, part three.\n\n288\n00:13:29,420 --> 00:13:31,760\nMy first idea is for three in the afternoon, Mia.\n\n289\n00:13:32,340 --> 00:13:34,060\nBecause that is when my voice is loudest.\n\n290\n00:13:34,740 --> 00:13:35,500\nI look at my list.\n\n291\n00:13:36,140 --> 00:13:36,830\nHalf is done.\n\n292\n00:13:37,419 --> 00:13:39,080\nAnd the voice says, \"Half?\n\n293\n00:13:39,700 --> 00:13:40,290\nOnly half?\n\n294\n00:13:40,940 --> 00:13:44,720\nOther people finish everything.\" So now I look at the done half.\n\n295\n00:13:45,320 --> 00:13:46,120\nNot the other half.\n\n296\n00:13:46,700 --> 00:13:51,280\nAnd I say, \"That is enough for one morning.\" Today, half is enough.\n\n297\n00:13:51,500 --> 00:13:58,700\n\"Same list, different counting.\" Ben, my three o'clock voice is about my body.\n\n298\n00:13:58,710 --> 00:14:00,140\n\"You are tired again.\n\n299\n00:14:00,180 --> 00:14:00,780\nYou are weak.\n\n300\n00:14:01,180 --> 00:14:05,020\nYou should have more energy.\" As if being tired is a mistake.\n\n301\n00:14:05,780 --> 00:14:07,100\nAnd what do you say to it now, Mia?\n\n302\n00:14:07,720 --> 00:14:09,240\nToday, for example.\n\n303\n00:14:09,320 --> 00:14:11,150\nYou look tired today.\n\n304\n00:14:11,160 --> 00:14:12,420\nWhat I would say to Anna, Ben.\n\n305\n00:14:13,300 --> 00:14:14,800\n\"You are tired because you worked.\n\n306\n00:14:15,280 --> 00:14:16,420\nSit down for ten minutes.\n\n307\n00:14:16,960 --> 00:14:18,040\nTen minutes is enough.\n\n308\n00:14:18,340 --> 00:14:24,520\nDrink some water.\" And the strange thing is, when I am kind to my tired body, it gets less tired.\n\n309\n00:14:25,100 --> 00:14:27,200\nWhen I shout at it, it gets more tired.\n\n310\n00:14:27,860 --> 00:14:29,580\nThe body listens to the voice, too.\n\n311\n00:14:30,390 --> 00:14:35,190\n\"The body listens to the voice, too.\" Mia, my second idea is for the evening.\n\n312\n00:14:35,900 --> 00:14:39,870\nAt night, in bed, the voice reads everything I did wrong today.\n\n313\n00:14:39,920 --> 00:14:40,350\nSlowly.\n\n314\n00:14:40,910 --> 00:14:41,600\nWith details.\n\n315\n00:14:42,519 --> 00:14:43,880\nI used to listen to the whole list.\n\n316\n00:14:44,520 --> 00:14:45,880\nNow I have a small rule.\n\n317\n00:14:46,560 --> 00:14:48,780\nWhen the list starts, I say, \"Thank you.\n\n318\n00:14:49,220 --> 00:14:50,630\nNow tell me one thing I did well.\"\n\n319\n00:14:51,610 --> 00:14:52,920\nAnd what does the voice say, Ben?\n\n320\n00:14:53,480 --> 00:14:54,890\nDoes it find one good thing, today?\n\n321\n00:14:56,400 --> 00:14:56,980\nNothing, Mia.\n\n322\n00:14:57,680 --> 00:14:59,770\nIt goes quiet, because it does not know how.\n\n323\n00:15:00,589 --> 00:15:01,500\nSo I answer for it.\n\n324\n00:15:02,250 --> 00:15:02,980\n\"I called my mother.\n\n325\n00:15:03,520 --> 00:15:05,420\nThat was good.\" One thing.\n\n326\n00:15:06,020 --> 00:15:07,060\nOne thing is enough.\n\n327\n00:15:07,760 --> 00:15:08,820\nToday, I did enough.\n\n328\n00:15:09,400 --> 00:15:10,180\nThat is the sentence.\n\n329\n00:15:10,860 --> 00:15:11,640\nAnd then I sleep.\n\n330\n00:15:12,520 --> 00:15:15,680\nIt is Franklin's question from our last episode, actually.\n\n331\n00:15:15,740 --> 00:15:16,860\nWhat good did I do today?\n\n332\n00:15:18,080 --> 00:15:19,340\nI want to try that tonight, Ben.\n\n333\n00:15:19,920 --> 00:15:22,980\nBecause my night list is long, and it never has a good thing on it.\n\n334\n00:15:23,740 --> 00:15:25,180\nMy second idea is a letter.\n\n335\n00:15:25,780 --> 00:15:26,999\nYou told me about it, actually.\n\n336\n00:15:27,600 --> 00:15:29,790\nA letter to yourself, from a kind friend.\n\n337\n00:15:30,680 --> 00:15:31,340\nThe letter, Mia.\n\n338\n00:15:31,640 --> 00:15:31,900\nYes.\n\n339\n00:15:32,860 --> 00:15:36,620\nAfter my friend's sentence, I wrote myself a letter, as my friend.\n\n340\n00:15:37,540 --> 00:15:39,640\n\"Dear Ben, you lost a job.\n\n341\n00:15:40,220 --> 00:15:41,380\nIt happens to good people.\n\n342\n00:15:42,140 --> 00:15:43,240\nYou are good at your work.\n\n343\n00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:47,980\nTake a week, then start.\" I read it every morning for a month.\n\n344\n00:15:48,520 --> 00:15:50,280\nKristin Neff actually suggests this.\n\n345\n00:15:50,840 --> 00:15:51,940\nDid it feel silly, Ben?\n\n346\n00:15:52,450 --> 00:15:57,880\nWriting a letter to yourself, from a friend who is you, and reading it with your coffee every morning?\n\n347\n00:15:58,740 --> 00:16:00,440\nFor three days, Mia.\n\n348\n00:16:00,520 --> 00:16:02,460\nOn day four, it felt true.\n\n349\n00:16:03,460 --> 00:16:04,860\nI found a job in two months.\n\n350\n00:16:05,350 --> 00:16:06,900\nThe letter did not find the job.\n\n351\n00:16:07,520 --> 00:16:10,060\nBut the letter got me out of bed, and I found it.\n\n352\n00:16:10,570 --> 00:16:14,820\n\"The letter got me out of bed.\" Ben, I want to write one tonight.\n\n353\n00:16:15,440 --> 00:16:19,440\nBecause I can be that friend for Anna in five minutes on the phone.\n\n354\n00:16:19,460 --> 00:16:20,680\nFive minutes is enough.\n\n355\n00:16:21,260 --> 00:16:24,400\nSo I can be that friend for me on paper.\n\n356\n00:16:24,500 --> 00:16:25,200\nSame words.\n\n357\n00:16:25,530 --> 00:16:26,410\nSame kindness.\n\n358\n00:16:26,780 --> 00:16:27,630\nDifferent address.\n\n359\n00:16:28,460 --> 00:16:30,040\nDo you have a third idea?\n\n360\n00:16:30,060 --> 00:16:32,220\nSomething for our listeners who are learning English?\n\n361\n00:16:32,800 --> 00:16:33,880\nYes, Mia.\n\n362\n00:16:33,960 --> 00:16:37,220\nBecause this is where the voice is loudest for learners.\n\n363\n00:16:37,260 --> 00:16:38,520\nYou say a sentence in English.\n\n364\n00:16:39,200 --> 00:16:40,240\nOne word is wrong.\n\n365\n00:16:40,870 --> 00:16:42,540\nAnd the voice says, \"See?\n\n366\n00:16:43,080 --> 00:16:43,990\nYou cannot speak.\n\n367\n00:16:44,620 --> 00:16:48,660\nEveryone noticed.\" I hear this from learners every week.\n\n368\n00:16:48,700 --> 00:16:52,140\nAnd I want to say, clearly, that voice is lying.\n\n369\n00:16:52,720 --> 00:16:53,820\nIt is lying, Ben.\n\n370\n00:16:54,600 --> 00:16:56,720\nOne wrong word is one wrong word.\n\n371\n00:16:57,300 --> 00:16:59,300\nThe person you were talking to understood you.\n\n372\n00:16:59,820 --> 00:17:02,000\nThey did not go home and think about your mistake.\n\n373\n00:17:02,470 --> 00:17:03,620\nThey thought about their dinner.\n\n374\n00:17:04,280 --> 00:17:05,840\nOne conversation is enough.\n\n375\n00:17:06,500 --> 00:17:08,500\nOnly you are still thinking about the word.\n\n376\n00:17:09,190 --> 00:17:11,440\n\"They thought about their dinner.\" Yes, Mia.\n\n377\n00:17:12,200 --> 00:17:14,960\nWhen you make a mistake in Spanish, I do not remember it.\n\n378\n00:17:15,600 --> 00:17:17,380\nI remember that you talked to me in Spanish.\n\n379\n00:17:18,040 --> 00:17:19,780\nThat is what people remember.\n\n380\n00:17:19,820 --> 00:17:20,360\nThe trying.\n\n381\n00:17:20,900 --> 00:17:21,700\nNot the mistake.\n\n382\n00:17:22,420 --> 00:17:26,480\nSo for everyone learning English, here is a kind sentence for today.\n\n383\n00:17:26,490 --> 00:17:27,100\n\"I spoke.\n\n384\n00:17:27,560 --> 00:17:31,910\nThat is enough.\" Not \"I spoke perfectly.\" \"I spoke.\n\n385\n00:17:32,310 --> 00:17:36,100\nThat is enough.\" Say it after every conversation.\n\n386\n00:17:36,160 --> 00:17:37,430\nToday, I spoke.\n\n387\n00:17:38,040 --> 00:17:39,760\nThat is enough for today.\n\n388\n00:17:39,820 --> 00:17:42,520\nIt is true, and the voice hates true things.\n\n389\n00:17:42,910 --> 00:17:46,720\n\"The voice hates true things.\" And one last idea, Mia.\n\n390\n00:17:47,300 --> 00:17:50,040\nIt is for the people who think they do not deserve kindness.\n\n391\n00:17:50,810 --> 00:17:51,940\n\"Other people deserve it.\n\n392\n00:17:52,480 --> 00:17:53,020\nI do not.\n\n393\n00:17:53,620 --> 00:17:58,280\nMy mistakes are worse.\" That is the voice again, wearing a serious coat.\n\n394\n00:17:58,960 --> 00:18:01,490\nToday, that voice is loud for many people.\n\n395\n00:18:02,209 --> 00:18:04,580\nEveryone's voice says their mistakes are worse, Ben.\n\n396\n00:18:05,160 --> 00:18:06,340\nEveryone.\n\n397\n00:18:06,420 --> 00:18:07,390\nAnna's voice says it.\n\n398\n00:18:07,940 --> 00:18:09,280\nYour voice says it.\n\n399\n00:18:09,400 --> 00:18:10,980\nMy voice says it.\n\n400\n00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:12,840\nSo it cannot be true for all of us.\n\n401\n00:18:13,320 --> 00:18:14,770\nIt is just what the voice says.\n\n402\n00:18:15,360 --> 00:18:17,180\nYou are not the exception.\n\n403\n00:18:17,280 --> 00:18:19,920\nYou are a person, and persons get kindness.\n\n404\n00:18:20,429 --> 00:18:21,260\nThat is the rule.\n\n405\n00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:23,200\n\"Persons get kindness.\n\n406\n00:18:23,750 --> 00:18:27,280\nThat is the rule.\" Mia, that is our message today.\n\n407\n00:18:27,960 --> 00:18:30,600\nThere is a voice in your head, and it is not kind.\n\n408\n00:18:31,240 --> 00:18:36,559\nIt says \"always\" and \"never\" and \"not enough.\" And it is lying.\n\n409\n00:18:37,460 --> 00:18:40,640\nA mistake is one thing, on one day.\n\n410\n00:18:40,700 --> 00:18:41,940\nYou are not the mistake.\n\n411\n00:18:42,830 --> 00:18:44,360\nSo talk to yourself like a friend.\n\n412\n00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:45,840\nIt is not weak.\n\n413\n00:18:46,560 --> 00:18:50,300\nIt is the thing that lets you try again tomorrow.\n\n414\n00:18:50,310 --> 00:18:52,240\nThe thing that lets you try again tomorrow.\n\n415\n00:18:52,990 --> 00:18:58,260\nBen, let's repeat today's important words, slowly, because our listeners want to remember them.\n\n416\n00:18:59,560 --> 00:18:59,960\nKind.\n\n417\n00:19:00,560 --> 00:19:01,260\nGood to someone.\n\n418\n00:19:01,940 --> 00:19:02,660\nBe kind.\n\n419\n00:19:03,320 --> 00:19:04,200\nKind to yourself.\n\n420\n00:19:05,340 --> 00:19:05,820\nMistake.\n\n421\n00:19:06,380 --> 00:19:07,540\nSomething that went wrong.\n\n422\n00:19:08,160 --> 00:19:09,060\nI made a mistake.\n\n423\n00:19:10,280 --> 00:19:11,900\nA mistake is not a person.\n\n424\n00:19:12,900 --> 00:19:13,260\nFriend.\n\n425\n00:19:13,750 --> 00:19:15,360\nA person you like and trust.\n\n426\n00:19:16,120 --> 00:19:17,500\nTalk to yourself like a friend.\n\n427\n00:19:18,620 --> 00:19:19,780\nToday.\n\n428\n00:19:19,860 --> 00:19:21,380\nThis day, not always.\n\n429\n00:19:21,980 --> 00:19:23,080\nToday is a mix.\n\n430\n00:19:24,340 --> 00:19:24,780\nEnough.\n\n431\n00:19:25,360 --> 00:19:26,590\nAs much as is needed.\n\n432\n00:19:27,260 --> 00:19:28,050\nI did enough.\n\n433\n00:19:28,700 --> 00:19:29,600\nThat is enough.\n\n434\n00:19:31,000 --> 00:19:31,460\nKind.\n\n435\n00:19:32,180 --> 00:19:32,740\nMistake.\n\n436\n00:19:33,800 --> 00:19:34,220\nFriend.\n\n437\n00:19:35,280 --> 00:19:35,680\nToday.\n\n438\n00:19:36,840 --> 00:19:37,240\nEnough.\n\n439\n00:19:38,840 --> 00:19:39,800\nFive kind words.\n\n440\n00:19:39,970 --> 00:19:41,080\nAnd one big idea.\n\n441\n00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:43,510\nTalk to yourself like a friend.\n\n442\n00:19:44,640 --> 00:19:47,540\nNow, Mia, do you have a question for our listeners?\n\n443\n00:19:47,680 --> 00:19:48,700\nYes, Ben.\n\n444\n00:19:48,780 --> 00:19:50,930\nEveryone listening, here is my question.\n\n445\n00:19:51,600 --> 00:19:54,670\nWhat is one kind sentence you can say to yourself today?\n\n446\n00:19:55,340 --> 00:19:56,400\nNot a big one.\n\n447\n00:19:56,540 --> 00:19:57,820\nA small one.\n\n448\n00:19:57,850 --> 00:19:58,390\n\"I spoke.\n\n449\n00:19:58,800 --> 00:20:05,920\nThat is enough.\" \"I was tired because I worked.\" \"One test is one test.\" Write it in the comments.\n\n450\n00:20:06,560 --> 00:20:08,840\nToday, one kind sentence is enough.\n\n451\n00:20:10,040 --> 00:20:12,920\nReading those sentences is a kind thing for us, too.\n\n452\n00:20:12,960 --> 00:20:15,180\nAnd sometimes we borrow them.\n\n453\n00:20:15,300 --> 00:20:16,500\nSometimes we borrow them.\n\n454\n00:20:17,260 --> 00:20:20,620\nAnd if this episode helped you, please subscribe to Two Cups English.\n\n455\n00:20:21,120 --> 00:20:23,200\nWe make a new episode every few days.\n\n456\n00:20:23,820 --> 00:20:27,640\nSlow English, simple words, and one true story each time.\n\n457\n00:20:28,440 --> 00:20:30,709\nThe full text of this episode is in the description.\n\n458\n00:20:31,280 --> 00:20:34,320\nAnd if you missed some words today, that is fine.\n\n459\n00:20:34,360 --> 00:20:35,720\nYou listened today.\n\n460\n00:20:35,840 --> 00:20:37,840\nThat is enough.\n\n461\n00:20:37,920 --> 00:20:39,580\nThank you for spending this time with us.\n\n462\n00:20:40,160 --> 00:20:41,960\nMaybe you had a hard day.\n\n463\n00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:43,120\nMaybe you made a mistake.\n\n464\n00:20:43,860 --> 00:20:46,360\nMaybe the voice is loud right now.\n\n465\n00:20:46,460 --> 00:20:49,740\nHere is what I would say to a friend, so I am saying it to you.\n\n466\n00:20:50,560 --> 00:20:51,120\nIt's okay.\n\n467\n00:20:51,660 --> 00:20:52,320\nIt happens.\n\n468\n00:20:53,060 --> 00:20:54,000\nYou are still here.\n\n469\n00:20:54,800 --> 00:20:55,500\nHave some tea.\n\n470\n00:20:56,240 --> 00:20:57,680\nBe kind to yourself today.\n\n471\n00:20:58,800 --> 00:20:59,420\nThis is Ben.\n\n472\n00:21:00,120 --> 00:21:01,100\nAnd this is Mia.\n\n473\n00:21:01,780 --> 00:21:03,200\nThis was Two Cups English.\n\n474\n00:21:04,040 --> 00:21:05,220\nBe kind to yourself.\n\n475\n00:21:05,980 --> 00:21:07,020\nWe will see you next time."},{"code":"CUP07","file":"CUP07.srt","srt":"1\n00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,280\nHello everyone, and welcome to Two Cups English.\n\n2\n00:00:03,840 --> 00:00:06,699\nI'm Mia, and Ben is here with me.\n\n3\n00:00:06,780 --> 00:00:09,540\nThis podcast is for people who are learning English.\n\n4\n00:00:10,220 --> 00:00:11,060\nWe speak slowly.\n\n5\n00:00:11,620 --> 00:00:12,980\nWe use simple words.\n\n6\n00:00:13,560 --> 00:00:16,419\nAnd we repeat the important words many times.\n\n7\n00:00:17,360 --> 00:00:19,360\nToday, our topic is hello.\n\n8\n00:00:19,910 --> 00:00:20,940\nThe first hello.\n\n9\n00:00:21,940 --> 00:00:23,180\nSaying hello first.\n\n10\n00:00:24,280 --> 00:00:26,360\nBen, who did you say hello to today?\n\n11\n00:00:27,640 --> 00:00:28,400\nHi Mia.\n\n12\n00:00:28,460 --> 00:00:29,060\nHi everyone.\n\n13\n00:00:29,700 --> 00:00:32,860\nToday, I said hello to the bus driver, and to you.\n\n14\n00:00:33,700 --> 00:00:34,120\nThat is all.\n\n15\n00:00:35,880 --> 00:00:38,560\nAnd there is a man at the corner shop.\n\n16\n00:00:39,440 --> 00:00:42,000\nI have bought bread from him for four years.\n\n17\n00:00:42,880 --> 00:00:43,840\nFour years.\n\n18\n00:00:44,500 --> 00:00:46,420\nAnd I have never asked his name.\n\n19\n00:00:47,480 --> 00:00:48,260\nWhat about you, Mia?\n\n20\n00:00:48,920 --> 00:00:49,720\nAny hellos today?\n\n21\n00:00:50,820 --> 00:00:52,000\nOne big one, Ben.\n\n22\n00:00:52,760 --> 00:00:54,700\nThere is a woman who lives next door to me.\n\n23\n00:00:55,200 --> 00:00:58,240\nFor two years, we met in the hallway almost every day.\n\n24\n00:00:58,900 --> 00:01:01,220\nAnd for two years, we said nothing.\n\n25\n00:01:01,800 --> 00:01:02,580\nA small nod.\n\n26\n00:01:02,960 --> 00:01:03,640\nEyes down.\n\n27\n00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:04,640\nKeys out.\n\n28\n00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:05,820\nDoor closed.\n\n29\n00:01:06,360 --> 00:01:07,400\nTwo years.\n\n30\n00:01:08,440 --> 00:01:10,080\nTwo years of nodding, Mia.\n\n31\n00:01:10,880 --> 00:01:14,660\nTwo years is a long time to stand in a hallway and say nothing.\n\n32\n00:01:15,650 --> 00:01:16,110\nAnd today?\n\n33\n00:01:16,900 --> 00:01:17,790\nDid something change?\n\n34\n00:01:19,140 --> 00:01:20,380\nLast week, actually, Ben.\n\n35\n00:01:20,980 --> 00:01:24,980\nI was tired, and I did not think, and I said, \"Hello.\n\n36\n00:01:25,440 --> 00:01:27,520\nLong day?\" And she stopped.\n\n37\n00:01:28,020 --> 00:01:29,179\nAnd she smiled.\n\n38\n00:01:29,200 --> 00:01:34,300\nAnd she said, \"The longest.\" And we talked for five minutes about nothing.\n\n39\n00:01:34,840 --> 00:01:36,920\nAnd now, every day, we say hello.\n\n40\n00:01:37,540 --> 00:01:38,580\nWe are almost friends.\n\n41\n00:01:39,200 --> 00:01:41,850\nTwo years of silence, and one word fixed it.\n\n42\n00:01:42,930 --> 00:01:48,920\n\"Two years of silence, and one word fixed it.\" Mia, that is our topic today.\n\n43\n00:01:49,960 --> 00:01:51,100\nSo here is our plan.\n\n44\n00:01:52,040 --> 00:01:55,460\nIn part one, we talk about our own silent people.\n\n45\n00:01:56,260 --> 00:01:57,890\nThe neighbor, the bread man.\n\n46\n00:01:59,040 --> 00:02:02,620\nIn part two, we ask why hello matters so much.\n\n47\n00:02:03,460 --> 00:02:05,900\nAnd I have a true story for that.\n\n48\n00:02:05,960 --> 00:02:09,280\nThe longest study in the world.\n\n49\n00:02:09,340 --> 00:02:13,180\nIn part three, we share simple ways to say hello first.\n\n50\n00:02:14,060 --> 00:02:14,840\nDoes that sound good?\n\n51\n00:02:16,140 --> 00:02:17,100\nThat sounds good, Ben.\n\n52\n00:02:17,720 --> 00:02:21,360\nAnd to everyone listening, please do not worry if you miss something.\n\n53\n00:02:21,800 --> 00:02:23,280\nYou will hear it again.\n\n54\n00:02:23,320 --> 00:02:29,200\nYou can listen while you walk, while you cook, or on the bus, next to a person you have never talked to.\n\n55\n00:02:30,140 --> 00:02:30,660\nOkay, Ben.\n\n56\n00:02:30,960 --> 00:02:31,440\nPart one.\n\n57\n00:02:32,220 --> 00:02:33,920\nWhy did it take me two years?\n\n58\n00:02:34,460 --> 00:02:36,180\nWhat do you think?\n\n59\n00:02:36,400 --> 00:02:38,680\nI think hello is small, Mia.\n\n60\n00:02:38,690 --> 00:02:40,520\nAnd small things feel like they can wait.\n\n61\n00:02:41,400 --> 00:02:43,680\nSo people wait, and nobody talks.\n\n62\n00:02:44,710 --> 00:02:54,760\n\"I will say hello tomorrow.\" \"Next time.\" \"When it feels natural.\" And it never feels natural, because the silence gets heavier every day.\n\n63\n00:02:55,880 --> 00:02:57,240\nDay one, hello is easy.\n\n64\n00:02:58,220 --> 00:03:00,480\nDay one hundred, hello is strange.\n\n65\n00:03:01,560 --> 00:03:05,060\nDay seven hundred, hello feels almost impossible.\n\n66\n00:03:06,080 --> 00:03:07,560\n\"The silence was not empty.\n\n67\n00:03:07,940 --> 00:03:10,140\nIt was growing.\" Yes, Ben.\n\n68\n00:03:10,520 --> 00:03:13,410\nAnd I was feeding it every morning, with my eyes down.\n\n69\n00:03:14,160 --> 00:03:14,940\nAnd your bread man?\n\n70\n00:03:15,720 --> 00:03:16,560\nFour years.\n\n71\n00:03:17,200 --> 00:03:18,280\nWhy not ask his name?\n\n72\n00:03:19,680 --> 00:03:23,400\nBecause after four years, asking feels strange, Mia.\n\n73\n00:03:24,510 --> 00:03:27,690\n\"Sorry, what is your name?\" After four years?\n\n74\n00:03:28,760 --> 00:03:29,980\nSo I do not ask.\n\n75\n00:03:30,680 --> 00:03:32,380\nAnd the silence gets another day.\n\n76\n00:03:33,360 --> 00:03:35,120\nFour years and one day.\n\n77\n00:03:37,320 --> 00:03:39,120\nFour years and one day.\n\n78\n00:03:40,080 --> 00:03:42,440\nBen, you are going to ask him tomorrow.\n\n79\n00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:44,920\nI am, Mia.\n\n80\n00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:46,860\nI promise, in front of everyone.\n\n81\n00:03:47,480 --> 00:03:49,860\nTomorrow, the bread man gets a name.\n\n82\n00:03:50,800 --> 00:03:52,680\nAnd I know a trick for the late name, actually.\n\n83\n00:03:53,100 --> 00:03:53,620\nYou taught me.\n\n84\n00:03:54,040 --> 00:03:54,620\nI did, Ben.\n\n85\n00:03:55,400 --> 00:03:55,960\nSay this.\n\n86\n00:03:56,860 --> 00:03:57,920\n\"I am terrible.\n\n87\n00:03:58,660 --> 00:04:02,200\nI have been coming here for years, and I never asked your name.\n\n88\n00:04:03,160 --> 00:04:05,890\nI am Ben.\" And people love that.\n\n89\n00:04:06,490 --> 00:04:08,120\nThey do not think you are strange.\n\n90\n00:04:08,660 --> 00:04:10,439\nThey think you are honest.\n\n91\n00:04:10,460 --> 00:04:13,460\nAnd they say their name, and now you are two people.\n\n92\n00:04:14,020 --> 00:04:15,520\nNot a customer and a shop.\n\n93\n00:04:16,120 --> 00:04:17,280\nMaybe friends, one day.\n\n94\n00:04:17,780 --> 00:04:23,560\n\"Two people, not a customer and a shop.\" I am going to use exactly that sentence, Mia.\n\n95\n00:04:24,660 --> 00:04:26,640\nCan I tell you about a party last winter?\n\n96\n00:04:27,160 --> 00:04:29,160\nBecause it taught me something about \"first.\"\n\n97\n00:04:29,700 --> 00:04:30,480\nA party, Ben?\n\n98\n00:04:31,300 --> 00:04:32,510\nYou do not like parties.\n\n99\n00:04:33,090 --> 00:04:35,200\nYou told me you hide in the kitchen at parties.\n\n100\n00:04:36,500 --> 00:04:37,020\nI do, Mia.\n\n101\n00:04:37,780 --> 00:04:39,080\nTwenty people.\n\n102\n00:04:39,160 --> 00:04:40,880\nEveryone looking at phones.\n\n103\n00:04:40,940 --> 00:04:41,760\nNobody talking.\n\n104\n00:04:42,620 --> 00:04:45,500\nAnd I remembered a rule my grandfather told me when I was a boy.\n\n105\n00:04:46,450 --> 00:04:49,440\n\"Ben, in a room, be the first to say hello.\n\n106\n00:04:50,140 --> 00:04:51,170\nEveryone is waiting.\n\n107\n00:04:51,800 --> 00:04:53,600\nBe the one who stops the waiting.\"\n\n108\n00:04:54,020 --> 00:04:55,080\n\"Everyone is waiting.\n\n109\n00:04:55,720 --> 00:04:59,800\nBe the one who stops the waiting.\" Ben, that is beautiful.\n\n110\n00:05:00,700 --> 00:05:01,940\nSo what did you do at the party?\n\n111\n00:05:02,420 --> 00:05:04,400\nI said hello to the man next to me, Mia.\n\n112\n00:05:05,100 --> 00:05:05,480\nFirst.\n\n113\n00:05:06,300 --> 00:05:08,440\nAnd he almost fell off his chair with relief.\n\n114\n00:05:09,080 --> 00:05:10,520\nHe had been waiting for an hour.\n\n115\n00:05:11,200 --> 00:05:12,430\nWe talked all evening.\n\n116\n00:05:13,400 --> 00:05:14,310\nEveryone is waiting.\n\n117\n00:05:14,900 --> 00:05:16,900\nBe the one who goes first.\n\n118\n00:05:16,910 --> 00:05:18,680\nAnd that makes the fear smaller, Ben.\n\n119\n00:05:19,440 --> 00:05:23,080\nBecause if everyone is waiting, then my hello is not strange.\n\n120\n00:05:23,740 --> 00:05:25,360\nIt is the thing everyone wants.\n\n121\n00:05:26,240 --> 00:05:28,700\nI am not the strange person who talks.\n\n122\n00:05:28,840 --> 00:05:31,140\nI am the person who ends the waiting.\n\n123\n00:05:31,170 --> 00:05:34,740\nThe first hello is a gift to the whole room, not a risk for me.\n\n124\n00:05:35,300 --> 00:05:36,860\nA gift, not a risk.\n\n125\n00:05:37,340 --> 00:05:37,860\nYes, Mia.\n\n126\n00:05:38,660 --> 00:05:41,500\nBut let me ask about the fear, because it is real.\n\n127\n00:05:42,480 --> 00:05:43,840\nWhy is hello hard for you?\n\n128\n00:05:44,680 --> 00:05:45,480\nWhat is the fear?\n\n129\n00:05:46,500 --> 00:05:47,480\nSmall fears, Ben.\n\n130\n00:05:48,200 --> 00:05:49,400\nWhat if she does not answer?\n\n131\n00:05:50,300 --> 00:05:51,820\nWhat if she thinks I am strange?\n\n132\n00:05:52,600 --> 00:05:54,100\nWhat if she does not want a friend?\n\n133\n00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:57,660\nWhat if she wants to talk for an hour and I am tired?\n\n134\n00:05:58,700 --> 00:06:00,740\nSmall fears, but they are enough.\n\n135\n00:06:01,620 --> 00:06:05,560\nBecause hello is small, and a small fear can stop a small thing.\n\n136\n00:06:06,340 --> 00:06:08,340\nA big fear cannot stop a big thing.\n\n137\n00:06:09,140 --> 00:06:13,500\nBut a small fear can stop hello, every day, for two years.\n\n138\n00:06:13,870 --> 00:06:18,270\n\"A small fear can stop a small thing.\" Mia, that is true.\n\n139\n00:06:19,140 --> 00:06:20,560\nAnd I think there is another reason.\n\n140\n00:06:21,280 --> 00:06:22,680\nWe wait for the other person.\n\n141\n00:06:23,400 --> 00:06:26,240\nI waited four years for the bread man to ask my name.\n\n142\n00:06:27,020 --> 00:06:28,800\nMaybe he waited four years for me.\n\n143\n00:06:29,780 --> 00:06:33,749\nTwo people, both waiting, both polite, both silent.\n\n144\n00:06:34,500 --> 00:06:37,020\nBoth waiting, both silent.\n\n145\n00:06:38,360 --> 00:06:42,060\nSo the word \"first\" in today's title is the important word, Ben.\n\n146\n00:06:42,760 --> 00:06:44,340\nSay hello first.\n\n147\n00:06:44,800 --> 00:06:48,020\nNot \"say hello when they do.\" First.\n\n148\n00:06:48,700 --> 00:06:51,680\nSomebody has to go first, and it might as well be you.\n\n149\n00:06:53,260 --> 00:06:54,280\nIt should be you, Mia.\n\n150\n00:06:54,640 --> 00:06:56,860\nBecause then it happens today.\n\n151\n00:06:56,870 --> 00:06:59,190\nAnd tell me, what happened after the hallway hello?\n\n152\n00:06:59,660 --> 00:07:01,180\nDid it stop there?\n\n153\n00:07:01,260 --> 00:07:03,020\nIt did not stop, Ben.\n\n154\n00:07:03,060 --> 00:07:05,260\nThe next day, she said hello first.\n\n155\n00:07:05,660 --> 00:07:08,140\nAnd the day after, she talked about my plants.\n\n156\n00:07:08,620 --> 00:07:11,100\nAnd last Sunday, she brought me a piece of cake.\n\n157\n00:07:11,600 --> 00:07:16,280\nA whole piece of cake, from a person who was a closed door for two years.\n\n158\n00:07:16,760 --> 00:07:18,020\nNow she is a friend.\n\n159\n00:07:18,700 --> 00:07:20,820\nAll of that was inside one hello.\n\n160\n00:07:22,160 --> 00:07:22,780\nCake, Mia.\n\n161\n00:07:23,200 --> 00:07:25,520\nBehind the door was cake and a friend.\n\n162\n00:07:25,950 --> 00:07:27,500\nThat is what first hellos do.\n\n163\n00:07:28,040 --> 00:07:30,000\nThey open something you cannot see yet.\n\n164\n00:07:31,100 --> 00:07:32,560\nOkay, so that was part one.\n\n165\n00:07:33,120 --> 00:07:33,989\nOur silent people.\n\n166\n00:07:34,510 --> 00:07:38,530\nA neighbor, a bread man, a party, and a piece of cake.\n\n167\n00:07:39,400 --> 00:07:41,220\nNow, part two.\n\n168\n00:07:41,300 --> 00:07:42,940\nWhy does hello matter so much?\n\n169\n00:07:44,060 --> 00:07:46,500\nMia, this is the true story I promised.\n\n170\n00:07:46,560 --> 00:07:48,320\nThe longest study in the world.\n\n171\n00:07:49,160 --> 00:07:51,739\nThe longest study in the world, Ben?\n\n172\n00:07:51,820 --> 00:07:52,990\nHow long is long?\n\n173\n00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:55,060\nEighty years, Mia.\n\n174\n00:07:56,080 --> 00:07:57,460\nOkay, part two.\n\n175\n00:07:57,520 --> 00:07:58,800\nThis is a true story.\n\n176\n00:07:59,780 --> 00:08:06,040\nIn 1938, in America, scientists at Harvard University started to follow a group of young men.\n\n177\n00:08:06,690 --> 00:08:07,980\nThey wanted to know one thing.\n\n178\n00:08:08,760 --> 00:08:09,950\nWhat makes a good life?\n\n179\n00:08:10,700 --> 00:08:13,020\nAnd they followed the same people for their whole lives.\n\n180\n00:08:13,620 --> 00:08:15,300\nEvery two years, questions.\n\n181\n00:08:15,880 --> 00:08:17,700\nEvery five years, health checks.\n\n182\n00:08:18,300 --> 00:08:20,220\nThey are still following the families today.\n\n183\n00:08:20,910 --> 00:08:25,940\nThe man who leads the study now, Robert Waldinger, gave a talk about it in 2015.\n\n184\n00:08:26,960 --> 00:08:28,660\nEighty years of questions, Ben.\n\n185\n00:08:29,140 --> 00:08:30,000\nWhat did they find?\n\n186\n00:08:30,460 --> 00:08:30,760\nMoney?\n\n187\n00:08:31,260 --> 00:08:31,620\nWork?\n\n188\n00:08:32,060 --> 00:08:32,480\nFame?\n\n189\n00:08:33,700 --> 00:08:34,480\nNot money, Mia.\n\n190\n00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:36,040\nNot fame.\n\n191\n00:08:36,100 --> 00:08:36,890\nNot hard work.\n\n192\n00:08:37,580 --> 00:08:40,370\nThe clearest message from eighty years of data was this.\n\n193\n00:08:41,080 --> 00:08:43,760\nGood relationships keep us happier and healthier.\n\n194\n00:08:44,520 --> 00:08:45,780\nNot many relationships.\n\n195\n00:08:46,180 --> 00:08:46,680\nGood ones.\n\n196\n00:08:47,440 --> 00:08:48,140\nWarm ones.\n\n197\n00:08:48,820 --> 00:08:52,280\nPeople with warm connections at fifty were the healthiest at eighty.\n\n198\n00:08:53,070 --> 00:08:56,150\nAnd lonely people were less healthy, and they died earlier.\n\n199\n00:08:57,130 --> 00:09:00,560\nLoneliness, he said, was as bad for the body as smoking.\n\n200\n00:09:01,970 --> 00:09:05,680\n\"As bad as smoking.\" Ben, that is a strong sentence.\n\n201\n00:09:06,200 --> 00:09:09,560\nSo the hello in the hallway is not a small polite thing.\n\n202\n00:09:09,620 --> 00:09:10,290\nIt is health.\n\n203\n00:09:10,780 --> 00:09:12,610\nIt is the thing the study was measuring.\n\n204\n00:09:13,480 --> 00:09:14,880\nExactly, Mia.\n\n205\n00:09:14,940 --> 00:09:16,420\nWe think of hello as manners.\n\n206\n00:09:16,820 --> 00:09:17,520\nSomething nice.\n\n207\n00:09:18,060 --> 00:09:18,480\nOptional.\n\n208\n00:09:19,140 --> 00:09:22,740\nBut eighty years of science says that warm connection is the main thing.\n\n209\n00:09:23,320 --> 00:09:24,420\nNot one of the things.\n\n210\n00:09:24,900 --> 00:09:25,620\nThe main one.\n\n211\n00:09:26,400 --> 00:09:29,380\nAnd warm connection does not start with a deep conversation.\n\n212\n00:09:29,920 --> 00:09:31,090\nIt starts with hello.\n\n213\n00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:35,540\nEvery good relationship in my life started with a first hello, Ben.\n\n214\n00:09:36,260 --> 00:09:37,460\nSomebody said it first.\n\n215\n00:09:37,960 --> 00:09:38,760\nMy best friend.\n\n216\n00:09:39,240 --> 00:09:40,060\nMy sister-in-law.\n\n217\n00:09:40,800 --> 00:09:44,300\nSomebody, one day, said the first small word.\n\n218\n00:09:45,660 --> 00:09:46,820\nAnd your friends, Ben?\n\n219\n00:09:47,580 --> 00:09:49,040\nDid they start with hello too?\n\n220\n00:09:50,400 --> 00:09:52,340\nOne of them started on a train, Mia.\n\n221\n00:09:53,060 --> 00:09:58,320\nEight years ago, a man asked me, \"Is this seat free?\" I said, \"Yes.\n\n222\n00:09:58,980 --> 00:10:01,860\nHello.\" Just hello, after yes.\n\n223\n00:10:02,680 --> 00:10:06,000\nAnd we talked for two hours, like old friends.\n\n224\n00:10:06,120 --> 00:10:06,830\nAbout nothing.\n\n225\n00:10:07,540 --> 00:10:08,010\nFootball.\n\n226\n00:10:08,700 --> 00:10:09,080\nBread.\n\n227\n00:10:09,760 --> 00:10:10,350\nHis daughter.\n\n228\n00:10:11,260 --> 00:10:13,100\nAnd at the end, he gave me his number.\n\n229\n00:10:13,740 --> 00:10:15,000\nDid you call him, Ben?\n\n230\n00:10:15,740 --> 00:10:17,020\nI almost did not, Mia.\n\n231\n00:10:17,480 --> 00:10:18,380\nIt felt strange.\n\n232\n00:10:19,180 --> 00:10:19,960\nBut I called.\n\n233\n00:10:20,620 --> 00:10:22,360\nAnd that man was at my wedding.\n\n234\n00:10:23,060 --> 00:10:25,210\nHe is one of my three real friends.\n\n235\n00:10:25,760 --> 00:10:29,300\nFrom \"Is this seat free?\" and \"Yes.\n\n236\n00:10:30,100 --> 00:10:30,340\nHello.\"\n\n237\n00:10:31,100 --> 00:10:33,840\nFrom \"Is this seat free\" to a wedding, Ben.\n\n238\n00:10:34,450 --> 00:10:36,460\nThat is the whole study in one train.\n\n239\n00:10:37,179 --> 00:10:40,380\nAnd I think we make the word \"friend\" too big.\n\n240\n00:10:40,440 --> 00:10:43,260\nWe think a friend is a person we have known for ten years.\n\n241\n00:10:43,840 --> 00:10:46,820\nBut every ten-year friend was a stranger first.\n\n242\n00:10:47,320 --> 00:10:48,240\nA hello first.\n\n243\n00:10:48,760 --> 00:10:49,760\nA coffee second.\n\n244\n00:10:50,580 --> 00:10:52,960\nA friend is a hello that continued.\n\n245\n00:10:53,560 --> 00:10:58,130\n\"A friend is a hello that continued.\" Mia, anyone can start one.\n\n246\n00:10:58,740 --> 00:10:59,120\nToday.\n\n247\n00:11:00,200 --> 00:11:02,060\nCan I ask about your loneliest year?\n\n248\n00:11:02,860 --> 00:11:05,520\nYou told me once, and I think the study explains it.\n\n249\n00:11:06,460 --> 00:11:09,670\nWhen I moved to this city, Ben, I knew nobody.\n\n250\n00:11:09,700 --> 00:11:12,690\nFor six months, I talked to nobody except at work.\n\n251\n00:11:13,290 --> 00:11:14,780\nAnd I told myself it was fine.\n\n252\n00:11:15,220 --> 00:11:16,180\nI was busy.\n\n253\n00:11:16,320 --> 00:11:18,060\nI was independent.\n\n254\n00:11:18,070 --> 00:11:19,960\nBut I was not fine.\n\n255\n00:11:20,020 --> 00:11:22,080\nI was tired all the time.\n\n256\n00:11:22,120 --> 00:11:23,320\nI got sick twice.\n\n257\n00:11:23,860 --> 00:11:24,860\nI did not sleep.\n\n258\n00:11:25,480 --> 00:11:27,040\nLoneliness and the body, Mia.\n\n259\n00:11:27,470 --> 00:11:28,580\nThe study connects them.\n\n260\n00:11:29,420 --> 00:11:31,360\nAnd you did not connect them, at the time?\n\n261\n00:11:32,060 --> 00:11:32,900\nNot at all, Ben.\n\n262\n00:11:33,440 --> 00:11:34,660\nI thought I was just tired.\n\n263\n00:11:35,300 --> 00:11:38,120\nAnd then, after six months, a hello.\n\n264\n00:11:38,800 --> 00:11:39,560\nOf course.\n\n265\n00:11:40,200 --> 00:11:45,920\nA woman at the gym said, \"Hello, you are here every Tuesday, aren't you?\" And that was it.\n\n266\n00:11:46,660 --> 00:11:47,720\nShe said it first.\n\n267\n00:11:48,160 --> 00:11:50,620\nAnd we had coffee, and we talked for an hour.\n\n268\n00:11:51,100 --> 00:11:52,280\nAnd she had friends.\n\n269\n00:11:52,810 --> 00:11:55,310\nAnd by the end of the year, I had people.\n\n270\n00:11:56,100 --> 00:11:59,240\nFrom one hello that you did not even say, Mia.\n\n271\n00:11:59,420 --> 00:12:00,140\nShe said it.\n\n272\n00:12:00,820 --> 00:12:01,930\nSomebody went first.\n\n273\n00:12:02,530 --> 00:12:04,840\nAnd I think she did not know what she was doing.\n\n274\n00:12:05,480 --> 00:12:06,940\nShe thought she was being polite.\n\n275\n00:12:07,620 --> 00:12:09,120\nShe was saving your year.\n\n276\n00:12:09,840 --> 00:12:11,520\n\"She thought she was being polite.\n\n277\n00:12:12,100 --> 00:12:16,620\nShe was saving my year.\" Ben, that is the whole episode, I think.\n\n278\n00:12:17,440 --> 00:12:20,820\nWe never know what our hello is doing on the other side.\n\n279\n00:12:20,900 --> 00:12:22,100\nIt looks like nothing.\n\n280\n00:12:22,860 --> 00:12:24,240\nIt might be everything.\n\n281\n00:12:25,520 --> 00:12:27,200\nOkay, so that was part two.\n\n282\n00:12:27,800 --> 00:12:29,120\nWhy hello matters.\n\n283\n00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:32,530\nWe said good relationships are the main thing.\n\n284\n00:12:33,069 --> 00:12:36,760\nAnd we said a friend is a hello that continued.\n\n285\n00:12:36,840 --> 00:12:37,849\nNow, part three.\n\n286\n00:12:38,700 --> 00:12:40,690\nSimple ways to say hello first.\n\n287\n00:12:41,460 --> 00:12:42,640\nBen, you go first.\n\n288\n00:12:43,800 --> 00:12:44,540\nOf course, Mia.\n\n289\n00:12:45,580 --> 00:12:46,649\nOkay, part three.\n\n290\n00:12:47,460 --> 00:12:49,460\nMy first idea is counting.\n\n291\n00:12:49,540 --> 00:12:53,180\nLast month, I said hello to everyone I passed at work for one week.\n\n292\n00:12:53,220 --> 00:12:53,980\nThe cleaner.\n\n293\n00:12:54,040 --> 00:12:54,900\nThe security man.\n\n294\n00:12:55,220 --> 00:12:56,160\nThe people in the lift.\n\n295\n00:12:56,640 --> 00:12:57,960\nNot a long talk.\n\n296\n00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:00,260\nHello, and their name if I knew it.\n\n297\n00:13:00,320 --> 00:13:02,680\nAnd by Friday, the building felt different.\n\n298\n00:13:02,720 --> 00:13:03,100\nWarmer.\n\n299\n00:13:03,640 --> 00:13:05,510\nPeople smiled at me before I smiled.\n\n300\n00:13:06,040 --> 00:13:07,120\nSome of them are friends now.\n\n301\n00:13:07,960 --> 00:13:10,280\nTen seconds a day changed the building, Ben.\n\n302\n00:13:11,120 --> 00:13:14,620\nMy first idea is for shy people, because I am one.\n\n303\n00:13:15,440 --> 00:13:18,000\nSaying hello first is harder for us.\n\n304\n00:13:18,020 --> 00:13:19,040\nIt is a real fear.\n\n305\n00:13:19,820 --> 00:13:22,000\nSo here is a very small hello.\n\n306\n00:13:22,660 --> 00:13:25,060\nA nod, and a smile, and the word.\n\n307\n00:13:25,990 --> 00:13:28,000\n\"Hello.\" One word.\n\n308\n00:13:28,360 --> 00:13:29,180\nNot a question.\n\n309\n00:13:29,700 --> 00:13:31,460\nJust the word, with a smile.\n\n310\n00:13:32,080 --> 00:13:33,500\nYou do not need to talk.\n\n311\n00:13:34,280 --> 00:13:35,620\nAnd then keep walking.\n\n312\n00:13:36,320 --> 00:13:37,840\nAnd nobody needs to answer, Mia.\n\n313\n00:13:38,420 --> 00:13:40,100\nIt is a gift, not a request.\n\n314\n00:13:40,740 --> 00:13:41,780\nAnd most people smile.\n\n315\n00:13:42,380 --> 00:13:43,360\nShy hellos count.\n\n316\n00:13:43,780 --> 00:13:44,560\nThey count fully.\n\n317\n00:13:45,460 --> 00:13:47,400\nMy second idea is about the other side.\n\n318\n00:13:48,040 --> 00:13:50,220\nSometimes we say hello, and it does not work.\n\n319\n00:13:50,820 --> 00:13:52,020\nThe person does not answer.\n\n320\n00:13:52,600 --> 00:13:53,340\nOr they look away.\n\n321\n00:13:53,890 --> 00:13:55,360\nAnd the fear says, \"See?\n\n322\n00:13:55,720 --> 00:13:56,680\nYou should not have tried.\"\n\n323\n00:13:57,620 --> 00:13:59,240\nAnd what do you say to that fear, Ben?\n\n324\n00:13:59,820 --> 00:14:01,080\nBecause it is loud.\n\n325\n00:14:01,440 --> 00:14:02,780\nIt says, \"See?\n\n326\n00:14:03,260 --> 00:14:04,440\nDo not try again.\"\n\n327\n00:14:05,400 --> 00:14:07,500\nIt happens, maybe one time in ten, Mia.\n\n328\n00:14:08,180 --> 00:14:09,780\nAnd it is almost never about you.\n\n329\n00:14:10,380 --> 00:14:11,420\nThe person is tired.\n\n330\n00:14:11,880 --> 00:14:12,420\nOr shy.\n\n331\n00:14:13,080 --> 00:14:14,740\nOr having the worst day of their year.\n\n332\n00:14:15,500 --> 00:14:17,060\nTheir silence is their silence.\n\n333\n00:14:17,660 --> 00:14:18,920\nYour hello was still good.\n\n334\n00:14:19,830 --> 00:14:22,780\n\"Their silence is their silence.\" Yes, Ben.\n\n335\n00:14:23,460 --> 00:14:26,960\nAnd the one time in ten is not a reason to stop the nine.\n\n336\n00:14:27,600 --> 00:14:29,620\nSo I keep a small count.\n\n337\n00:14:29,680 --> 00:14:30,220\nIn my head.\n\n338\n00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:33,740\nThis week, I said hello first eight times.\n\n339\n00:14:34,280 --> 00:14:37,000\nSeven smiles, and maybe one new friend.\n\n340\n00:14:37,540 --> 00:14:38,520\nOne nothing.\n\n341\n00:14:39,320 --> 00:14:40,840\nSeven is the number.\n\n342\n00:14:40,880 --> 00:14:42,140\nNot one.\n\n343\n00:14:42,300 --> 00:14:43,260\nI count the seven.\n\n344\n00:14:44,320 --> 00:14:47,160\n\"I count the seven.\" Mia, that is a good rule.\n\n345\n00:14:48,060 --> 00:14:49,590\nMy third idea is about home.\n\n346\n00:14:50,280 --> 00:14:52,780\nBecause there is a hello I always forget.\n\n347\n00:14:52,819 --> 00:14:56,660\nI say hello to the bread man, and then I come home, and I say nothing to my wife.\n\n348\n00:14:57,120 --> 00:14:57,900\nMy best friend.\n\n349\n00:14:58,600 --> 00:15:01,320\nI walk in, put the bag down, look at the phone.\n\n350\n00:15:01,800 --> 00:15:02,340\nNo hello.\n\n351\n00:15:03,320 --> 00:15:06,880\nAnd she is the most important person in your whole study, Ben.\n\n352\n00:15:07,600 --> 00:15:11,079\nThe one warm connection the Harvard study is really about.\n\n353\n00:15:11,820 --> 00:15:14,340\nAnd I give her the smallest hello of the day, Mia.\n\n354\n00:15:15,020 --> 00:15:15,500\nOr none.\n\n355\n00:15:16,420 --> 00:15:17,860\nSo now, I stop at the door.\n\n356\n00:15:18,560 --> 00:15:19,740\nI put the bag down.\n\n357\n00:15:19,780 --> 00:15:23,720\nAnd I say hello first, like she is a person I want to talk to.\n\n358\n00:15:23,780 --> 00:15:24,520\nBecause she is.\n\n359\n00:15:25,410 --> 00:15:32,960\n\"Like she is a person I want to talk to.\" Ben, that is beautiful, and a little sad, because we all do this.\n\n360\n00:15:33,620 --> 00:15:39,020\nThe hello goes to strangers, and the people at home get the tired face, and no talk.\n\n361\n00:15:39,880 --> 00:15:44,139\nMy mother calls me, and I say, \"Yes, what?\" Not hello.\n\n362\n00:15:44,850 --> 00:15:46,790\n\"Yes, what?\" To my mother.\n\n363\n00:15:47,609 --> 00:15:49,960\n\"Yes, what?\" To your mother, Mia.\n\n364\n00:15:50,760 --> 00:15:52,300\nI think my mother gets the same from me.\n\n365\n00:15:53,140 --> 00:15:54,260\nSo what will you do this week?\n\n366\n00:15:55,140 --> 00:15:58,400\nThis week, I am starting my calls with hello, Ben.\n\n367\n00:15:59,200 --> 00:16:00,120\nA real one.\n\n368\n00:16:00,750 --> 00:16:01,500\n\"Hello, Mom.\n\n369\n00:16:01,740 --> 00:16:04,620\nHow are you?\" And waiting for the answer.\n\n370\n00:16:05,500 --> 00:16:07,020\nWaiting is part of hello.\n\n371\n00:16:07,740 --> 00:16:10,240\nA hello without waiting is just a noise.\n\n372\n00:16:11,580 --> 00:16:13,070\nDo you have a fourth idea?\n\n373\n00:16:13,680 --> 00:16:16,300\nSomething for the evening, maybe?\n\n374\n00:16:16,340 --> 00:16:16,920\nYes, Mia.\n\n375\n00:16:17,480 --> 00:16:20,780\nFor many people, the evening is the loneliest time.\n\n376\n00:16:20,900 --> 00:16:21,980\nSo, a message.\n\n377\n00:16:22,680 --> 00:16:24,310\nOne line, to one person.\n\n378\n00:16:25,330 --> 00:16:25,640\n\"Hello.\n\n379\n00:16:26,200 --> 00:16:28,570\nI thought about you today.\" That is all.\n\n380\n00:16:29,340 --> 00:16:30,009\nNo question.\n\n381\n00:16:30,520 --> 00:16:31,040\nNo plan.\n\n382\n00:16:31,840 --> 00:16:33,160\nI do this three times a week.\n\n383\n00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:34,980\nMy cousin.\n\n384\n00:16:35,020 --> 00:16:35,680\nAn old friend.\n\n385\n00:16:36,280 --> 00:16:37,280\nA colleague who left.\n\n386\n00:16:38,080 --> 00:16:41,060\nAnd most of the time, they write back, and we talk a little.\n\n387\n00:16:42,360 --> 00:16:44,119\nThree messages a week, Ben.\n\n388\n00:16:44,600 --> 00:16:47,400\nThat is nothing, and it is a lot.\n\n389\n00:16:47,960 --> 00:16:49,720\nI want to try that.\n\n390\n00:16:49,780 --> 00:16:52,220\nAnd for the people who are learning English?\n\n391\n00:16:52,260 --> 00:16:56,180\nBecause I think hello is the best English lesson nobody teaches.\n\n392\n00:16:57,120 --> 00:16:57,720\nIt is, Mia.\n\n393\n00:16:58,480 --> 00:17:01,020\nMany learners are afraid to talk to people in English.\n\n394\n00:17:01,780 --> 00:17:03,790\nThey wait until they are ready.\n\n395\n00:17:03,840 --> 00:17:05,100\nUntil the grammar is good.\n\n396\n00:17:05,940 --> 00:17:07,720\nBut hello does not need grammar.\n\n397\n00:17:08,480 --> 00:17:09,860\nHello is one word.\n\n398\n00:17:10,910 --> 00:17:11,220\n\"Hello.\n\n399\n00:17:11,700 --> 00:17:13,560\nNice day.\" Two words.\n\n400\n00:17:14,380 --> 00:17:14,680\n\"Hello.\n\n401\n00:17:15,200 --> 00:17:17,090\nLong day?\" Three words.\n\n402\n00:17:17,700 --> 00:17:19,420\nAnd that is a real conversation.\n\n403\n00:17:20,260 --> 00:17:22,120\nYou can make a friend with three words.\n\n404\n00:17:23,520 --> 00:17:26,380\nMy first Spanish conversation was two words, Ben.\n\n405\n00:17:27,080 --> 00:17:27,480\n\"Hola.\n\n406\n00:17:27,860 --> 00:17:29,840\nQué calor.\" Hello.\n\n407\n00:17:30,360 --> 00:17:31,540\nSo hot.\n\n408\n00:17:31,580 --> 00:17:33,280\nTo a man at a bus stop.\n\n409\n00:17:33,320 --> 00:17:35,960\nAnd he said, \"Mucho.\" A lot.\n\n410\n00:17:36,510 --> 00:17:37,240\nAnd that was it.\n\n411\n00:17:37,880 --> 00:17:39,480\nMy first real Spanish.\n\n412\n00:17:40,200 --> 00:17:41,590\nI went home so happy.\n\n413\n00:17:42,820 --> 00:17:44,500\nTwo words, and you went home happy.\n\n414\n00:17:45,340 --> 00:17:48,820\nMia, if you can say hello, you can speak English today.\n\n415\n00:17:49,660 --> 00:17:50,660\nNot after the course.\n\n416\n00:17:51,240 --> 00:17:52,440\nToday.\n\n417\n00:17:52,450 --> 00:17:54,620\nYou can make a friend in English today.\n\n418\n00:17:55,560 --> 00:17:59,580\nAnd after hello comes talk, and some people are afraid of that part.\n\n419\n00:18:00,460 --> 00:18:02,860\n\"What if they want to talk, and I have nothing to say?\"\n\n420\n00:18:04,100 --> 00:18:05,760\nThat is my fear exactly, Ben.\n\n421\n00:18:06,360 --> 00:18:07,280\nWhat is the secret?\n\n422\n00:18:08,400 --> 00:18:10,080\nYou do not need something to say, Mia.\n\n423\n00:18:10,900 --> 00:18:12,820\nYou need one question.\n\n424\n00:18:12,850 --> 00:18:20,480\n\"Long day?\" \"Busy today?\" \"How is your morning?\" One small question, and then you listen.\n\n425\n00:18:21,360 --> 00:18:23,860\nPeople love to talk about their day.\n\n426\n00:18:23,920 --> 00:18:24,900\nNobody asks them.\n\n427\n00:18:25,760 --> 00:18:34,860\nYou ask, and they talk, and you say \"really?\" and \"that sounds hard.\" That is a conversation, and now you have a friend.\n\n428\n00:18:35,820 --> 00:18:37,580\nYou said ten words.\n\n429\n00:18:37,640 --> 00:18:38,900\nThey said two hundred.\n\n430\n00:18:40,340 --> 00:18:41,880\n\"You said ten words.\n\n431\n00:18:41,960 --> 00:18:48,660\nThey said two hundred.\" Ben, that is the best English conversation advice I have ever heard.\n\n432\n00:18:48,720 --> 00:18:51,270\nBecause the listener does not need grammar.\n\n433\n00:18:51,300 --> 00:18:55,000\nThe listener needs \"really?\" and a face that cares.\n\n434\n00:18:56,220 --> 00:18:57,320\nA face that cares.\n\n435\n00:18:58,120 --> 00:19:02,280\nAnd one last idea, Mia, for the people who are new somewhere.\n\n436\n00:19:02,360 --> 00:19:05,130\nA new city, a new job, a new country.\n\n437\n00:19:06,020 --> 00:19:09,480\nWhen you are new, everyone else looks like they already have friends.\n\n438\n00:19:10,340 --> 00:19:11,600\nThey do not.\n\n439\n00:19:11,680 --> 00:19:14,040\nMost people are waiting, like at the party.\n\n440\n00:19:14,860 --> 00:19:16,380\nLike at the party, Ben.\n\n441\n00:19:17,020 --> 00:19:18,000\nI saw this at work.\n\n442\n00:19:18,500 --> 00:19:21,780\nA new colleague, from Brazil, English not perfect.\n\n443\n00:19:22,360 --> 00:19:25,540\nShe said hello to everyone, by name, in week one.\n\n444\n00:19:26,160 --> 00:19:30,880\nBy week three, she had more friends than I had after five years.\n\n445\n00:19:30,939 --> 00:19:32,440\nFirst beats perfect.\n\n446\n00:19:33,830 --> 00:19:37,120\n\"First beats perfect.\" Mia, that is our message today.\n\n447\n00:19:37,800 --> 00:19:41,400\nGood relationships are the main thing, for your health and your happiness.\n\n448\n00:19:42,080 --> 00:19:44,380\nAnd every good relationship starts with a first hello.\n\n449\n00:19:45,300 --> 00:19:46,979\nEvery friend was a stranger first.\n\n450\n00:19:47,540 --> 00:19:48,550\nSomebody says it first.\n\n451\n00:19:49,280 --> 00:19:50,720\nPeople remember who said hello.\n\n452\n00:19:51,620 --> 00:19:52,840\nLet it be you.\n\n453\n00:19:52,860 --> 00:19:53,180\nToday.\n\n454\n00:19:53,910 --> 00:19:57,060\nTo the neighbor, the bread man, your mother, your wife.\n\n455\n00:19:57,860 --> 00:19:58,380\nOne word.\n\n456\n00:19:59,160 --> 00:20:00,680\nIt looks like nothing.\n\n457\n00:20:00,720 --> 00:20:01,760\nIt might be everything.\n\n458\n00:20:02,760 --> 00:20:04,200\nIt might be everything.\n\n459\n00:20:04,960 --> 00:20:10,200\nBen, let's repeat today's important words, slowly, because our listeners want to remember them.\n\n460\n00:20:11,420 --> 00:20:11,800\nHello.\n\n461\n00:20:12,400 --> 00:20:13,220\nThe first word.\n\n462\n00:20:13,860 --> 00:20:14,680\nSay hello.\n\n463\n00:20:15,380 --> 00:20:16,700\nA hello is a door.\n\n464\n00:20:18,020 --> 00:20:18,380\nPeople.\n\n465\n00:20:19,020 --> 00:20:20,300\nPersons, others.\n\n466\n00:20:20,800 --> 00:20:21,860\nThe people we pass.\n\n467\n00:20:22,540 --> 00:20:23,900\nPeople love to talk.\n\n468\n00:20:25,240 --> 00:20:25,640\nFirst.\n\n469\n00:20:26,180 --> 00:20:27,620\nBefore others.\n\n470\n00:20:27,700 --> 00:20:28,460\nSay it first.\n\n471\n00:20:29,220 --> 00:20:30,580\nSomebody goes first.\n\n472\n00:20:31,820 --> 00:20:32,190\nFriend.\n\n473\n00:20:32,880 --> 00:20:34,540\nA person you know and like.\n\n474\n00:20:35,120 --> 00:20:36,540\nA friend from one hello.\n\n475\n00:20:37,940 --> 00:20:38,280\nTalk.\n\n476\n00:20:38,840 --> 00:20:39,900\nTo speak with someone.\n\n477\n00:20:40,420 --> 00:20:41,640\nTalk for five minutes.\n\n478\n00:20:42,540 --> 00:20:43,940\nAsk, and let them talk.\n\n479\n00:20:45,520 --> 00:20:45,820\nHello.\n\n480\n00:20:46,420 --> 00:20:46,750\nPeople.\n\n481\n00:20:47,740 --> 00:20:48,080\nFirst.\n\n482\n00:20:49,140 --> 00:20:49,460\nFriend.\n\n483\n00:20:50,560 --> 00:20:50,840\nTalk.\n\n484\n00:20:52,060 --> 00:20:53,920\nFive words, and one to say tomorrow.\n\n485\n00:20:54,520 --> 00:20:55,620\nAnd one big idea.\n\n486\n00:20:56,420 --> 00:20:57,360\nSay hello first.\n\n487\n00:20:58,280 --> 00:21:00,800\nNow, Mia, do you have a question for our listeners?\n\n488\n00:21:01,940 --> 00:21:02,520\nYes, Ben.\n\n489\n00:21:02,980 --> 00:21:05,030\nEveryone listening, here is my question.\n\n490\n00:21:05,880 --> 00:21:07,360\nWho is your silent person?\n\n491\n00:21:07,880 --> 00:21:10,460\nThe one you pass every day and never talk to.\n\n492\n00:21:11,200 --> 00:21:13,860\nWrite it in the comments in one short sentence.\n\n493\n00:21:14,320 --> 00:21:15,100\nNot their name.\n\n494\n00:21:15,480 --> 00:21:16,560\nJust who.\n\n495\n00:21:17,270 --> 00:21:27,980\n\"The woman at the bakery.\" \"My neighbor with the dog.\" And then, if you want, add, \"Tomorrow I say hello first.\" And Ben will tell us the bread man's name.\n\n496\n00:21:29,440 --> 00:21:29,920\nI will, Mia.\n\n497\n00:21:30,440 --> 00:21:31,850\nNext episode, first thing.\n\n498\n00:21:32,540 --> 00:21:36,440\nAnd if this episode helped you, please subscribe to Two Cups English.\n\n499\n00:21:36,480 --> 00:21:38,900\nWe make a new episode every few days.\n\n500\n00:21:39,060 --> 00:21:43,760\nSlow English, simple words, and one true story each time.\n\n501\n00:21:43,820 --> 00:21:46,700\nThe full text of this episode is in the description.\n\n502\n00:21:46,760 --> 00:21:49,489\nRead it, and then go say hello to someone.\n\n503\n00:21:49,580 --> 00:21:50,480\nThat is the homework.\n\n504\n00:21:51,300 --> 00:21:51,820\nOne word.\n\n505\n00:21:53,020 --> 00:21:54,880\nThank you for spending this time with us.\n\n506\n00:21:55,280 --> 00:21:57,800\nMaybe you are on a bus right now, next to someone.\n\n507\n00:21:58,500 --> 00:21:59,780\nMaybe you are in a hallway.\n\n508\n00:22:00,460 --> 00:22:01,840\nMaybe your mother is calling.\n\n509\n00:22:02,620 --> 00:22:05,320\nSay hello first, to a stranger or a friend.\n\n510\n00:22:05,820 --> 00:22:07,320\nA real one, with waiting.\n\n511\n00:22:07,960 --> 00:22:09,019\nIt looks like nothing.\n\n512\n00:22:09,560 --> 00:22:10,920\nIt might be everything.\n\n513\n00:22:11,960 --> 00:22:12,580\nThis is Ben.\n\n514\n00:22:13,340 --> 00:22:14,300\nAnd this is Mia.\n\n515\n00:22:15,120 --> 00:22:16,480\nThis was Two Cups English.\n\n516\n00:22:17,180 --> 00:22:18,160\nSay hello first.\n\n517\n00:22:19,000 --> 00:22:19,900\nWe will see you next time."},{"code":"CUP08","file":"CUP08.srt","srt":"1\n00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,280\nHello everyone, and welcome to Two Cups English.\n\n2\n00:00:03,820 --> 00:00:05,840\nI'm Mia, and Ben is here with me.\n\n3\n00:00:06,720 --> 00:00:09,580\nThis podcast is for people who are learning English.\n\n4\n00:00:10,220 --> 00:00:11,060\nWe speak slowly.\n\n5\n00:00:11,640 --> 00:00:13,440\nWe use simple words.\n\n6\n00:00:13,460 --> 00:00:17,279\nAnd we repeat the important words many times.\n\n7\n00:00:17,360 --> 00:00:19,500\nToday, our topic is the walk.\n\n8\n00:00:20,160 --> 00:00:23,440\nA slow walk, outside, without your phone.\n\n9\n00:00:24,160 --> 00:00:25,780\nBen, did you walk today?\n\n10\n00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:27,420\nHi Mia.\n\n11\n00:00:27,820 --> 00:00:28,340\nHi everyone.\n\n12\n00:00:28,960 --> 00:00:30,900\nYes, I walked to the bus, Mia.\n\n13\n00:00:31,380 --> 00:00:32,140\nTwelve minutes.\n\n14\n00:00:32,689 --> 00:00:34,720\nAnd I looked at my phone for eleven of them.\n\n15\n00:00:35,940 --> 00:00:38,600\nI cannot tell you one thing I saw on the street.\n\n16\n00:00:39,180 --> 00:00:40,100\nNot one.\n\n17\n00:00:40,860 --> 00:00:41,380\nWhat about you?\n\n18\n00:00:41,800 --> 00:00:42,420\nDid you walk?\n\n19\n00:00:43,680 --> 00:00:44,500\nI did, Ben.\n\n20\n00:00:45,180 --> 00:00:49,220\nAnd last Tuesday, something strange happened on my walk.\n\n21\n00:00:49,300 --> 00:00:50,460\nI lost my phone.\n\n22\n00:00:51,280 --> 00:00:51,920\nNot forever.\n\n23\n00:00:52,600 --> 00:00:55,180\nI left it at home, on the kitchen table.\n\n24\n00:00:55,220 --> 00:00:59,769\nAnd I did not notice until I was outside, on the street, walking to work.\n\n25\n00:01:00,420 --> 00:01:01,900\nMy hand went to my pocket.\n\n26\n00:01:02,640 --> 00:01:03,120\nNothing.\n\n27\n00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:05,300\nNothing in the pocket, Mia.\n\n28\n00:01:06,080 --> 00:01:06,900\nI know that feeling.\n\n29\n00:01:07,560 --> 00:01:08,580\nI think everyone does.\n\n30\n00:01:09,380 --> 00:01:11,100\nIt is like a small fall.\n\n31\n00:01:12,040 --> 00:01:12,740\nWhat did you do?\n\n32\n00:01:13,140 --> 00:01:13,880\nDid you go back?\n\n33\n00:01:14,760 --> 00:01:17,240\nFor one second, I felt panic, Ben.\n\n34\n00:01:18,100 --> 00:01:20,060\nThen I thought, \"I can go back.\n\n35\n00:01:20,520 --> 00:01:23,120\nOr I can walk.\" And I walked.\n\n36\n00:01:23,960 --> 00:01:24,960\nTwenty minutes.\n\n37\n00:01:25,470 --> 00:01:26,540\nWithout the phone.\n\n38\n00:01:27,420 --> 00:01:29,240\nAnd I saw a tree.\n\n39\n00:01:29,340 --> 00:01:33,620\nA big tree, on my street, that I have passed a thousand times.\n\n40\n00:01:34,280 --> 00:01:35,320\nI had never seen it.\n\n41\n00:01:36,150 --> 00:01:42,410\n\"A tree you passed a thousand times and never saw.\" Mia, that is our topic today.\n\n42\n00:01:43,020 --> 00:01:44,940\nSo here is our plan.\n\n43\n00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:47,710\nIn part one, we talk about our own walks.\n\n44\n00:01:48,420 --> 00:01:50,280\nWith the phone, and without.\n\n45\n00:01:51,400 --> 00:01:56,480\nIn part two, we ask why a slow walk without a phone helps the head.\n\n46\n00:01:57,360 --> 00:02:00,140\nAnd I have a true story for that.\n\n47\n00:02:00,200 --> 00:02:04,900\nIn part three, we share simple ideas for a slow ten-minute walk.\n\n48\n00:02:05,660 --> 00:02:06,440\nDoes that sound good?\n\n49\n00:02:07,320 --> 00:02:08,340\nThat sounds good, Ben.\n\n50\n00:02:09,140 --> 00:02:12,640\nAnd to everyone listening, please do not worry if you miss something.\n\n51\n00:02:13,180 --> 00:02:14,180\nYou will hear it again.\n\n52\n00:02:15,020 --> 00:02:19,880\nYou can listen while you cook, while you clean, or, yes, while you walk.\n\n53\n00:02:20,600 --> 00:02:21,640\nWe will talk about that.\n\n54\n00:02:22,620 --> 00:02:23,200\nOkay, Ben.\n\n55\n00:02:23,500 --> 00:02:23,960\nPart one.\n\n56\n00:02:24,620 --> 00:02:26,020\nWhat did I see on my street?\n\n57\n00:02:26,560 --> 00:02:27,280\nDo you want to know?\n\n58\n00:02:28,340 --> 00:02:29,220\nI want to know, Mia.\n\n59\n00:02:29,880 --> 00:02:32,260\nI think I know your street a little.\n\n60\n00:02:32,290 --> 00:02:34,320\nWhat did you see, besides the tree?\n\n61\n00:02:35,400 --> 00:02:36,980\nEverything, Ben.\n\n62\n00:02:37,060 --> 00:02:39,280\nA bakery with a blue door.\n\n63\n00:02:39,360 --> 00:02:42,030\nA man who waters his flowers at eight in the morning.\n\n64\n00:02:42,820 --> 00:02:45,540\nA cat that sits on the same car every day.\n\n65\n00:02:46,420 --> 00:02:50,240\nMy street was full of things, outside, all the time.\n\n66\n00:02:51,020 --> 00:02:55,080\nAnd I had walked through it for three years, looking at a small screen.\n\n67\n00:02:55,920 --> 00:02:57,140\nThree years.\n\n68\n00:02:58,060 --> 00:03:00,400\nThree years of the same walk, Mia.\n\n69\n00:03:01,260 --> 00:03:01,920\nSame street.\n\n70\n00:03:02,680 --> 00:03:04,580\nSame slow twenty minutes.\n\n71\n00:03:05,640 --> 00:03:09,210\nBut this one was different, because your head was outside.\n\n72\n00:03:09,920 --> 00:03:11,180\nNot inside the phone.\n\n73\n00:03:12,080 --> 00:03:13,799\nOutside, where the walk was.\n\n74\n00:03:14,700 --> 00:03:18,420\n\"My head was outside, where the walk was.\" Yes, Ben.\n\n75\n00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:19,840\nAnd your walks?\n\n76\n00:03:20,580 --> 00:03:21,960\nYou said you walk to think.\n\n77\n00:03:22,680 --> 00:03:23,360\nI do, Mia.\n\n78\n00:03:24,100 --> 00:03:29,420\nWhen I have a problem, a work problem, a life problem, I do not sit at my desk.\n\n79\n00:03:30,020 --> 00:03:31,200\nI go outside and walk.\n\n80\n00:03:31,940 --> 00:03:32,920\nNo phone.\n\n81\n00:03:32,960 --> 00:03:33,560\nNo music.\n\n82\n00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:35,560\nJust the street and the problem.\n\n83\n00:03:36,460 --> 00:03:39,160\nAnd somewhere in the walk, the problem gets smaller.\n\n84\n00:03:39,760 --> 00:03:40,680\nOr the answer comes.\n\n85\n00:03:41,480 --> 00:03:42,500\nI did not know why.\n\n86\n00:03:43,260 --> 00:03:44,200\nThen I read the science.\n\n87\n00:03:44,900 --> 00:03:45,900\nThe science, Ben?\n\n88\n00:03:46,420 --> 00:03:48,080\nThere is science about walking?\n\n89\n00:03:48,680 --> 00:03:50,380\nI thought walking was just walking.\n\n90\n00:03:51,740 --> 00:03:53,000\nThere is, Mia.\n\n91\n00:03:53,040 --> 00:03:57,270\nBut first, let me tell you about the day I noticed the phone was eating my walks.\n\n92\n00:03:58,200 --> 00:03:58,960\nIt was a Sunday.\n\n93\n00:03:59,740 --> 00:04:01,549\nI walked to the market, twenty minutes.\n\n94\n00:04:02,200 --> 00:04:05,860\nAnd when I arrived, I could not remember one thing from the walk.\n\n95\n00:04:06,560 --> 00:04:07,180\nNot one.\n\n96\n00:04:08,080 --> 00:04:08,660\nWas it sunny?\n\n97\n00:04:09,340 --> 00:04:10,400\nDid I cross the bridge?\n\n98\n00:04:11,180 --> 00:04:12,640\nI had no picture in my head.\n\n99\n00:04:13,300 --> 00:04:14,980\nNo picture in your head at all, Ben?\n\n100\n00:04:15,500 --> 00:04:16,480\nNot even the weather?\n\n101\n00:04:17,260 --> 00:04:18,940\nI think that is the strangest part.\n\n102\n00:04:19,430 --> 00:04:21,640\nTwenty minutes happened, and you were not there.\n\n103\n00:04:22,200 --> 00:04:22,920\nWhere were you?\n\n104\n00:04:23,600 --> 00:04:24,620\nIn a group chat, Mia.\n\n105\n00:04:25,380 --> 00:04:29,380\nTwenty minutes in a small room with six people who were not there.\n\n106\n00:04:29,410 --> 00:04:32,060\nAnd when I think about it now, that is the trade.\n\n107\n00:04:32,860 --> 00:04:37,140\nI gave away a bridge and a sky for six messages I do not remember either.\n\n108\n00:04:38,020 --> 00:04:38,760\nNothing stayed.\n\n109\n00:04:39,260 --> 00:04:41,200\nNot the walk, not the messages.\n\n110\n00:04:42,480 --> 00:04:43,940\nNo picture in your head, Ben.\n\n111\n00:04:44,440 --> 00:04:46,300\nTwenty minutes of walking, and nothing.\n\n112\n00:04:46,820 --> 00:04:49,280\nThe walk happened to your legs only.\n\n113\n00:04:49,310 --> 00:04:54,400\n\"The walk happened to my legs only.\" Mia, that is exactly it.\n\n114\n00:04:54,410 --> 00:04:57,680\nThe phone takes the walk away from the head and leaves it to the legs.\n\n115\n00:04:58,540 --> 00:04:59,820\nI did not think once.\n\n116\n00:05:00,740 --> 00:05:03,420\nAnd the sky above the whole walk, unused.\n\n117\n00:05:04,160 --> 00:05:06,640\nTwenty minutes of sky, and I did not look up.\n\n118\n00:05:07,300 --> 00:05:09,660\nThree years of sky, unused, Ben.\n\n119\n00:05:10,280 --> 00:05:11,880\nThat is what I felt on the Tuesday.\n\n120\n00:05:12,430 --> 00:05:17,880\nAnd I think the phone does something to the word \"outside.\" Outside used to mean the world.\n\n121\n00:05:17,960 --> 00:05:23,180\nNow, for many of us, outside means the place where we walk while looking inside.\n\n122\n00:05:23,220 --> 00:05:25,960\nWalking outside while looking inside.\n\n123\n00:05:26,040 --> 00:05:26,500\nYes, Mia.\n\n124\n00:05:27,280 --> 00:05:30,400\nSo the body is outside, and the head is in a small room.\n\n125\n00:05:31,280 --> 00:05:33,309\nAnd a head in a small room cannot think.\n\n126\n00:05:34,360 --> 00:05:36,410\nWhat happened at work that Tuesday?\n\n127\n00:05:36,480 --> 00:05:37,589\nAfter the phone-less walk?\n\n128\n00:05:38,260 --> 00:05:39,480\nSomething strange, Ben.\n\n129\n00:05:40,100 --> 00:05:41,940\nI had a problem with a report.\n\n130\n00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:44,059\nI had been stuck for three days.\n\n131\n00:05:44,100 --> 00:05:47,040\nAnd I sat down, and I looked at it, and the answer was there.\n\n132\n00:05:47,700 --> 00:05:48,240\nJust there.\n\n133\n00:05:48,820 --> 00:05:49,980\nI saw it in two minutes.\n\n134\n00:05:50,480 --> 00:05:52,660\nThree days of nothing, and then two minutes.\n\n135\n00:05:53,160 --> 00:05:56,440\nThree days at a desk, and two minutes after a walk, Mia.\n\n136\n00:05:57,149 --> 00:06:01,100\nThe head was working behind, while you looked at a cat outside.\n\n137\n00:06:01,110 --> 00:06:02,520\nThe walk gave the head room.\n\n138\n00:06:03,270 --> 00:06:04,820\nThe phone had been taking the room.\n\n139\n00:06:05,580 --> 00:06:06,869\nAnd the answer needed room.\n\n140\n00:06:07,600 --> 00:06:10,580\n\"The answer needed room.\" I like that, Ben.\n\n141\n00:06:11,640 --> 00:06:13,520\nOkay, so that was part one.\n\n142\n00:06:13,600 --> 00:06:14,320\nOur walks.\n\n143\n00:06:14,720 --> 00:06:22,840\nA lost phone, a tree, a bakery with a blue door, a market with no picture, and a report that solved itself.\n\n144\n00:06:23,780 --> 00:06:25,100\nNow, part two.\n\n145\n00:06:25,960 --> 00:06:29,100\nWhy does a slow walk without a phone help the head?\n\n146\n00:06:30,140 --> 00:06:31,480\nBen, the science.\n\n147\n00:06:31,920 --> 00:06:32,280\nTell me.\n\n148\n00:06:33,060 --> 00:06:33,979\nOkay, part two.\n\n149\n00:06:34,680 --> 00:06:35,900\nThis is a true story, Mia.\n\n150\n00:06:36,600 --> 00:06:44,400\nIn 2014, two researchers at Stanford University, Marily Oppezzo and Daniel Schwartz, did a simple experiment.\n\n151\n00:06:44,430 --> 00:06:46,220\nThey asked people to think of new ideas.\n\n152\n00:06:46,860 --> 00:06:48,680\nNew uses for an object, for example.\n\n153\n00:06:49,580 --> 00:06:50,380\nSome people sat.\n\n154\n00:06:50,880 --> 00:06:51,760\nSome people walked.\n\n155\n00:06:52,500 --> 00:06:55,320\nAnd the people who walked had more ideas, Ben?\n\n156\n00:06:56,120 --> 00:06:56,940\nMany more, Mia.\n\n157\n00:06:57,350 --> 00:06:59,780\nAbout sixty percent more.\n\n158\n00:06:59,810 --> 00:07:01,170\nAnd here is the strange part.\n\n159\n00:07:01,880 --> 00:07:03,920\nSome people walked outside.\n\n160\n00:07:03,960 --> 00:07:08,000\nSome people walked on a treadmill, inside, looking at a wall.\n\n161\n00:07:08,020 --> 00:07:10,020\nAnd even the wall people had more ideas.\n\n162\n00:07:10,780 --> 00:07:12,259\nIt was not the trees.\n\n163\n00:07:12,320 --> 00:07:13,120\nIt was the walking.\n\n164\n00:07:13,940 --> 00:07:18,060\nSixty percent more ideas, even walking towards a wall, Ben.\n\n165\n00:07:18,920 --> 00:07:21,540\nSo the body walks, and the head opens.\n\n166\n00:07:22,210 --> 00:07:27,500\n\"The body walks, and the head opens.\" That is the whole study, Mia.\n\n167\n00:07:27,510 --> 00:07:29,450\nAnd the researchers found one more thing.\n\n168\n00:07:30,280 --> 00:07:35,100\nAfter the walk, sitting down again, the ideas kept coming for a while.\n\n169\n00:07:35,200 --> 00:07:37,840\nSo the walk does not only help during the walk.\n\n170\n00:07:37,860 --> 00:07:38,620\nIt helps after.\n\n171\n00:07:39,390 --> 00:07:41,630\nTen minutes of legs for one hour of head.\n\n172\n00:07:42,460 --> 00:07:45,380\nTen minutes of legs for one hour of head.\n\n173\n00:07:46,100 --> 00:07:47,430\nBen, that is a good trade.\n\n174\n00:07:48,180 --> 00:07:51,580\nBut then why did my three years of walking give me nothing?\n\n175\n00:07:51,660 --> 00:07:54,300\nNo tree, no ideas.\n\n176\n00:07:54,360 --> 00:07:56,740\nBecause your head was not on the walk, Mia.\n\n177\n00:07:56,800 --> 00:07:57,540\nIt was in the phone.\n\n178\n00:07:58,310 --> 00:08:00,460\nThe study people were walking with an open head.\n\n179\n00:08:01,220 --> 00:08:02,660\nYou were walking with a full head.\n\n180\n00:08:03,540 --> 00:08:04,520\nMessages.\n\n181\n00:08:04,580 --> 00:08:05,380\nNews.\n\n182\n00:08:05,460 --> 00:08:06,380\nOther people's days.\n\n183\n00:08:07,180 --> 00:08:07,960\nThe legs walked.\n\n184\n00:08:08,530 --> 00:08:09,800\nThe head never left the room.\n\n185\n00:08:10,850 --> 00:08:13,820\n\"The head never left the room.\" Yes, Ben.\n\n186\n00:08:14,380 --> 00:08:16,700\nSo a walk with a phone is half a walk.\n\n187\n00:08:17,240 --> 00:08:18,360\nLess than half, maybe.\n\n188\n00:08:18,900 --> 00:08:21,300\nThe legs move, and the head stays inside.\n\n189\n00:08:22,180 --> 00:08:23,360\nCan I ask about thinking?\n\n190\n00:08:23,760 --> 00:08:26,819\nBecause I think people misunderstand what happens on a walk.\n\n191\n00:08:27,460 --> 00:08:29,560\nMisunderstand how, Mia?\n\n192\n00:08:29,700 --> 00:08:30,770\nI thought a walk was simple.\n\n193\n00:08:31,580 --> 00:08:34,400\nYou walk, you think, you come back.\n\n194\n00:08:34,410 --> 00:08:37,160\nThey think you go out to think about the problem, Ben.\n\n195\n00:08:37,200 --> 00:08:38,620\nTo work on it while walking.\n\n196\n00:08:39,220 --> 00:08:41,360\nBut that is not what happened on my Tuesday.\n\n197\n00:08:41,880 --> 00:08:43,480\nI did not think about the report.\n\n198\n00:08:43,920 --> 00:08:44,980\nI thought about a cat.\n\n199\n00:08:45,440 --> 00:08:47,160\nAnd the report solved itself.\n\n200\n00:08:47,849 --> 00:08:53,900\n\"I thought about a cat, and the report solved itself.\" Mia, that is exactly how it works for me, too.\n\n201\n00:08:54,760 --> 00:08:56,800\nI go out, and I do not think about the problem.\n\n202\n00:08:57,500 --> 00:08:58,380\nI think about the street.\n\n203\n00:08:59,020 --> 00:08:59,440\nA door.\n\n204\n00:09:00,080 --> 00:09:00,700\nA dog.\n\n205\n00:09:01,440 --> 00:09:04,480\nAnd the problem sits in the back, and it gets sorted quietly.\n\n206\n00:09:05,480 --> 00:09:07,040\nThinking on a walk is letting go.\n\n207\n00:09:07,840 --> 00:09:08,820\nLetting go, Ben.\n\n208\n00:09:09,420 --> 00:09:10,160\nBut how?\n\n209\n00:09:10,760 --> 00:09:14,100\nWhen I have a problem, I cannot stop thinking about it.\n\n210\n00:09:14,180 --> 00:09:16,480\nIt follows me out of the door.\n\n211\n00:09:16,500 --> 00:09:17,820\nIt follows everyone, Mia.\n\n212\n00:09:18,620 --> 00:09:19,570\nSo I do not fight it.\n\n213\n00:09:20,180 --> 00:09:21,260\nI give the eyes a job.\n\n214\n00:09:21,940 --> 00:09:23,420\nCount blue doors.\n\n215\n00:09:23,480 --> 00:09:24,940\nLook for cats.\n\n216\n00:09:25,020 --> 00:09:25,980\nRead the shop signs.\n\n217\n00:09:26,800 --> 00:09:29,460\nThe eyes are busy, and the front of the head goes quiet.\n\n218\n00:09:30,140 --> 00:09:34,440\nAnd the problem, with nobody watching it, starts to move on its own.\n\n219\n00:09:34,520 --> 00:09:35,560\nI think that is the trick.\n\n220\n00:09:36,220 --> 00:09:37,040\nDo not think.\n\n221\n00:09:37,940 --> 00:09:38,260\nLook.\n\n222\n00:09:39,470 --> 00:09:40,300\n\"Do not think.\n\n223\n00:09:41,160 --> 00:09:44,580\nLook.\" I think the head has two rooms, Ben.\n\n224\n00:09:45,180 --> 00:09:47,660\nThe front room, where I push and try.\n\n225\n00:09:48,290 --> 00:09:52,600\nAnd the back room, where things get sorted quietly.\n\n226\n00:09:52,720 --> 00:09:55,500\nSitting at a desk keeps me in the front room.\n\n227\n00:09:55,560 --> 00:09:57,740\nThe walk opens the back room.\n\n228\n00:09:58,480 --> 00:10:00,080\nThe walk opens the back room.\n\n229\n00:10:00,780 --> 00:10:02,460\nAnd the phone closes it again, Mia.\n\n230\n00:10:03,220 --> 00:10:05,560\nBecause the phone is the front room in your hand.\n\n231\n00:10:06,500 --> 00:10:11,819\nLet me talk about English for a moment, because many of our listeners listen to this podcast while walking.\n\n232\n00:10:11,860 --> 00:10:12,580\nAnd that is good.\n\n233\n00:10:13,280 --> 00:10:16,720\nBut then, after the episode, walk five minutes with nothing.\n\n234\n00:10:17,580 --> 00:10:19,360\nFive minutes with nothing, Ben?\n\n235\n00:10:20,060 --> 00:10:20,480\nWhy?\n\n236\n00:10:21,140 --> 00:10:24,320\nBecause the words get sorted on the quiet walk, Mia.\n\n237\n00:10:24,400 --> 00:10:25,780\nYou listened for twenty minutes.\n\n238\n00:10:26,380 --> 00:10:28,320\nYour head is full of new words.\n\n239\n00:10:28,980 --> 00:10:32,220\nAnd the quiet five minutes is where the head puts them in the right place.\n\n240\n00:10:32,860 --> 00:10:34,180\nThe listening is the input.\n\n241\n00:10:34,690 --> 00:10:37,380\nThe quiet walk is where the learning becomes yours.\n\n242\n00:10:38,110 --> 00:10:43,320\n\"The quiet walk is where the learning becomes yours.\" I believe that, Ben.\n\n243\n00:10:44,040 --> 00:10:47,710\nWhen I was learning Spanish, my best days were not study days.\n\n244\n00:10:48,250 --> 00:10:54,050\nThey were days when I walked home without headphones, and suddenly a Spanish sentence appeared in my head.\n\n245\n00:10:54,600 --> 00:10:55,900\nFrom nowhere.\n\n246\n00:10:55,910 --> 00:10:57,490\nThe head was sorting.\n\n247\n00:10:58,140 --> 00:10:59,780\nFrom nowhere, Mia.\n\n248\n00:11:00,420 --> 00:11:01,680\nIt is never from nowhere.\n\n249\n00:11:02,300 --> 00:11:04,560\nIt is from the back room.\n\n250\n00:11:04,600 --> 00:11:05,900\nOkay, so that was part two.\n\n251\n00:11:06,240 --> 00:11:07,340\nWhy the walk helps.\n\n252\n00:11:08,060 --> 00:11:10,540\nWe talked about the Stanford study and sixty percent.\n\n253\n00:11:11,180 --> 00:11:13,500\nWe said the body walks and the head opens.\n\n254\n00:11:14,100 --> 00:11:16,419\nAnd we said thinking on a walk is letting go.\n\n255\n00:11:17,320 --> 00:11:18,220\nNow, part three.\n\n256\n00:11:18,900 --> 00:11:22,240\nSimple ideas for a slow ten-minute walk.\n\n257\n00:11:22,300 --> 00:11:23,380\nMia, you go first.\n\n258\n00:11:24,320 --> 00:11:25,400\nOkay, part three.\n\n259\n00:11:26,160 --> 00:11:28,280\nMy first idea is about fear, Ben.\n\n260\n00:11:28,940 --> 00:11:33,080\nBecause when I first walked without a phone, I felt naked.\n\n261\n00:11:33,140 --> 00:11:34,470\nWhat if someone calls?\n\n262\n00:11:34,960 --> 00:11:36,120\nWhat if I need the map?\n\n263\n00:11:36,580 --> 00:11:37,960\nWhat if I am bored?\n\n264\n00:11:38,700 --> 00:11:40,280\nBored was the big one.\n\n265\n00:11:40,800 --> 00:11:43,220\nTwenty minutes outside with nothing.\n\n266\n00:11:43,900 --> 00:11:46,540\nAnd how long did bored last, Mia?\n\n267\n00:11:46,570 --> 00:11:49,180\nBecause I think most people stop on day one.\n\n268\n00:11:49,240 --> 00:11:49,660\nHonestly.\n\n269\n00:11:50,820 --> 00:11:51,769\nTwo days, Ben.\n\n270\n00:11:52,260 --> 00:11:54,640\nBored lasted two days.\n\n271\n00:11:54,680 --> 00:11:56,800\nAnd most people stop on day one.\n\n272\n00:11:56,829 --> 00:12:04,180\nThey feel the empty pocket, they feel bored, and they say, \"This is not for me.\" But bored is the door.\n\n273\n00:12:04,860 --> 00:12:12,960\nBored is the head saying, \"There is nothing to grab.\" And then, when there is nothing to grab, the head looks up.\n\n274\n00:12:13,620 --> 00:12:15,080\nAnd there is the tree.\n\n275\n00:12:15,900 --> 00:12:21,220\n\"Bored is the door.\" Mia, that is the sentence for the wall.\n\n276\n00:12:21,260 --> 00:12:23,380\nMy first idea is about the hands.\n\n277\n00:12:23,440 --> 00:12:25,460\nBecause the hand wants the phone.\n\n278\n00:12:25,480 --> 00:12:27,980\nIt goes to the pocket, again and again.\n\n279\n00:12:28,680 --> 00:12:30,880\nIt is a habit of the hand, not the head.\n\n280\n00:12:31,760 --> 00:12:33,720\nSo I carry my keys, and I turn them.\n\n281\n00:12:34,200 --> 00:12:35,740\nThe hand needs a small job.\n\n282\n00:12:37,160 --> 00:12:39,560\nThe hand needs a small job.\n\n283\n00:12:39,660 --> 00:12:41,900\nI carry a cup of tea on the morning walk, Ben.\n\n284\n00:12:42,320 --> 00:12:43,260\nA cup with a lid.\n\n285\n00:12:43,780 --> 00:12:46,080\nThe hand is busy, and the head is free.\n\n286\n00:12:47,280 --> 00:12:49,380\nMy second idea is about the afternoon.\n\n287\n00:12:50,060 --> 00:12:52,280\nAt two o'clock, my head is heavy.\n\n288\n00:12:52,840 --> 00:12:53,480\nI have eaten.\n\n289\n00:12:53,880 --> 00:12:55,600\nI have sat for four hours.\n\n290\n00:12:56,100 --> 00:12:58,720\nSo I walk outside, once around the building.\n\n291\n00:12:59,340 --> 00:13:00,120\nSeven minutes.\n\n292\n00:13:00,900 --> 00:13:03,540\nOnce around the building, seven minutes, Mia.\n\n293\n00:13:04,420 --> 00:13:07,780\nI do the same, but I go to the small park near my office.\n\n294\n00:13:07,810 --> 00:13:08,700\nAnd I have a rule.\n\n295\n00:13:09,380 --> 00:13:10,320\nNo destination.\n\n296\n00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:12,349\nI am not going anywhere.\n\n297\n00:13:13,100 --> 00:13:16,520\nWhen I walk to a place, my head is at the place already.\n\n298\n00:13:16,529 --> 00:13:18,780\nThe walk is just the road to it.\n\n299\n00:13:18,800 --> 00:13:22,500\nBut when I walk nowhere, the walk is the place.\n\n300\n00:13:23,420 --> 00:13:29,570\nI walk to the park and then I think, \"Now what?\" Ben, that is my problem with nowhere.\n\n301\n00:13:30,180 --> 00:13:32,800\nStanding in a park with nothing to do.\n\n302\n00:13:32,880 --> 00:13:33,800\nWhat do you do there?\n\n303\n00:13:34,540 --> 00:13:35,200\nNothing, Mia.\n\n304\n00:13:35,860 --> 00:13:37,340\nI stand there for one minute.\n\n305\n00:13:37,900 --> 00:13:39,120\nI look at one tree.\n\n306\n00:13:39,700 --> 00:13:40,740\nAnd then I walk back.\n\n307\n00:13:41,500 --> 00:13:42,780\nThat is the whole park.\n\n308\n00:13:43,560 --> 00:13:46,260\nAnd I think the \"now what\" is the head asking for the phone.\n\n309\n00:13:46,940 --> 00:13:47,680\nLet it ask.\n\n310\n00:13:48,180 --> 00:13:49,120\nDo not answer.\n\n311\n00:13:50,020 --> 00:13:52,960\nAfter a minute, it stops asking and starts looking.\n\n312\n00:13:53,510 --> 00:13:55,740\nThe head can only be bored for one minute.\n\n313\n00:13:56,250 --> 00:13:57,600\nThen it gets curious.\n\n314\n00:13:58,330 --> 00:14:03,080\n\"The head can only be bored for one minute.\" Ben, that is beautiful.\n\n315\n00:14:03,960 --> 00:14:05,660\nMy third idea is about slow.\n\n316\n00:14:06,320 --> 00:14:08,620\nMy phone-less walk was slow.\n\n317\n00:14:08,740 --> 00:14:09,840\nI did not decide that.\n\n318\n00:14:10,400 --> 00:14:15,460\nWhen there is nothing to look at in my hand, I look around, and looking around makes me slow.\n\n319\n00:14:15,980 --> 00:14:17,900\nAnd slow is where the tree is.\n\n320\n00:14:18,820 --> 00:14:20,720\nSlow is where the tree is, Mia.\n\n321\n00:14:21,460 --> 00:14:24,500\nLet me tell you about a slow walk with my father, last spring.\n\n322\n00:14:25,180 --> 00:14:28,560\nHe is old now, and he walks very slowly.\n\n323\n00:14:28,700 --> 00:14:30,120\nA block takes ten minutes.\n\n324\n00:14:30,760 --> 00:14:32,880\nAnd at first, I was impatient.\n\n325\n00:14:33,060 --> 00:14:33,960\nI wanted to walk.\n\n326\n00:14:34,660 --> 00:14:36,280\nBut he stopped at everything.\n\n327\n00:14:36,860 --> 00:14:37,840\nA wall with moss.\n\n328\n00:14:38,320 --> 00:14:39,560\nA window with a cat.\n\n329\n00:14:40,120 --> 00:14:41,760\nA tree with a strange shape.\n\n330\n00:14:42,460 --> 00:14:44,780\nAnd you saw the street, Ben, at his speed?\n\n331\n00:14:45,460 --> 00:14:47,140\nFor the first time, Mia.\n\n332\n00:14:47,240 --> 00:14:50,420\nI had walked that block a hundred times at my speed.\n\n333\n00:14:50,540 --> 00:14:52,500\nA speed where the world cannot be seen.\n\n334\n00:14:53,180 --> 00:14:55,420\nAt his slow speed, the block was full.\n\n335\n00:14:56,260 --> 00:15:00,760\nSo now I think a slow walk of ten minutes is worth more than a fast walk of thirty.\n\n336\n00:15:01,560 --> 00:15:05,960\nA slow walk of ten is worth more than a fast walk of thirty.\n\n337\n00:15:05,980 --> 00:15:08,400\nAnd I think slow is a kind of respect, Ben.\n\n338\n00:15:09,040 --> 00:15:15,050\nWhen I walk slowly, I am saying to the street, \"You are worth looking at.\" And the street answers.\n\n339\n00:15:15,490 --> 00:15:16,330\nThe bakery door.\n\n340\n00:15:16,780 --> 00:15:17,869\nThe man with flowers.\n\n341\n00:15:18,320 --> 00:15:19,650\nThey were always answering.\n\n342\n00:15:20,220 --> 00:15:21,720\nI was walking too fast to hear.\n\n343\n00:15:22,440 --> 00:15:29,320\n\"I was walking too fast to hear.\" Mia, my third idea is for the people who say, \"I cannot leave the phone.\n\n344\n00:15:29,520 --> 00:15:32,080\nMy work needs me.\" I understand that.\n\n345\n00:15:32,580 --> 00:15:34,060\nSo here is a smaller version.\n\n346\n00:15:34,780 --> 00:15:35,460\nTake the phone.\n\n347\n00:15:35,990 --> 00:15:38,220\nBut put it in the bag, not the pocket.\n\n348\n00:15:38,800 --> 00:15:40,740\nSound on, so you hear a real call.\n\n349\n00:15:41,300 --> 00:15:43,560\nAnd the hand cannot find it every ten seconds.\n\n350\n00:15:44,620 --> 00:15:46,200\nBag, not pocket.\n\n351\n00:15:46,860 --> 00:15:48,840\nPut one small effort in the way, Ben.\n\n352\n00:15:49,340 --> 00:15:50,619\nThat is a rule I can do.\n\n353\n00:15:51,420 --> 00:15:52,540\nAnd the evening?\n\n354\n00:15:52,600 --> 00:15:53,640\nDo you walk in the evening?\n\n355\n00:15:54,659 --> 00:15:56,100\nEvery evening, Mia.\n\n356\n00:15:56,110 --> 00:16:00,419\nAfter dinner, a slow fifteen minutes outside, around the block.\n\n357\n00:16:00,480 --> 00:16:02,420\nAnd I sleep better after the evening walk.\n\n358\n00:16:02,900 --> 00:16:04,040\nNot because I am tired.\n\n359\n00:16:04,400 --> 00:16:05,900\nBecause my head is finished.\n\n360\n00:16:06,440 --> 00:16:09,880\nIt did its sorting on the walk, so it does not need to do it in bed.\n\n361\n00:16:10,570 --> 00:16:12,449\nThe walk puts the day in its place.\n\n362\n00:16:13,550 --> 00:16:20,580\n\"The walk puts the day in its place.\" Ben, that is the difference between a slow walk and a phone at night.\n\n363\n00:16:20,590 --> 00:16:22,340\nThe phone gives the head more to sort.\n\n364\n00:16:22,830 --> 00:16:25,160\nThe walk lets the head close its files.\n\n365\n00:16:26,040 --> 00:16:29,240\nMy fourth idea is for people who work at home all day.\n\n366\n00:16:29,300 --> 00:16:32,370\nBecause some days, they do not go outside at all.\n\n367\n00:16:33,700 --> 00:16:35,240\nI did that for two years, Mia.\n\n368\n00:16:35,700 --> 00:16:37,740\nBed, desk, kitchen, bed.\n\n369\n00:16:38,520 --> 00:16:40,180\nSome days, not outside once.\n\n370\n00:16:40,840 --> 00:16:42,790\nAnd my head got smaller every day.\n\n371\n00:16:43,410 --> 00:16:44,220\nLess ideas.\n\n372\n00:16:44,680 --> 00:16:45,300\nMore worry.\n\n373\n00:16:46,280 --> 00:16:52,820\nI did not connect it to outside until a friend asked, \"When were you last outside?\" And I could not answer.\n\n374\n00:16:53,930 --> 00:16:57,680\n\"When were you last outside?\" That is a good question, Ben.\n\n375\n00:16:58,380 --> 00:17:00,160\nI think I would not know the answer either.\n\n376\n00:17:01,140 --> 00:17:02,320\nSo what is the rule now?\n\n377\n00:17:03,500 --> 00:17:04,860\nOutside before noon, Mia.\n\n378\n00:17:05,220 --> 00:17:05,780\nEvery day.\n\n379\n00:17:06,440 --> 00:17:10,680\nA slow five minutes with a cup, on the step, on the balcony, on the street.\n\n380\n00:17:11,330 --> 00:17:13,000\nThat is the smallest version.\n\n381\n00:17:13,100 --> 00:17:15,740\nThe sky is the biggest room, and it is free.\n\n382\n00:17:16,560 --> 00:17:19,000\nFive minutes in the biggest room before noon.\n\n383\n00:17:19,970 --> 00:17:23,160\n\"The sky is the biggest room.\" I love that, Ben.\n\n384\n00:17:23,850 --> 00:17:27,720\nAnd I want to say, the outside does not need to be beautiful.\n\n385\n00:17:27,760 --> 00:17:29,680\nMy street is not beautiful.\n\n386\n00:17:29,700 --> 00:17:32,640\nIt is a normal street with cars and a bakery.\n\n387\n00:17:32,660 --> 00:17:33,440\nBut it was full.\n\n388\n00:17:34,440 --> 00:17:35,760\nDo you have one last idea?\n\n389\n00:17:36,880 --> 00:17:43,580\nOne last idea, Mia, for the people who live in a city with no park, no trees, only noise and cars.\n\n390\n00:17:44,230 --> 00:17:46,540\nThe Stanford study says it does not matter.\n\n391\n00:17:47,129 --> 00:17:49,820\nThey walked towards a wall, and the ideas came.\n\n392\n00:17:50,520 --> 00:17:52,740\nSo a noisy street is fine.\n\n393\n00:17:52,800 --> 00:17:53,940\nA car park is fine.\n\n394\n00:17:54,480 --> 00:17:55,720\nA hallway is fine.\n\n395\n00:17:56,420 --> 00:17:57,620\nYou do not need the tree.\n\n396\n00:17:58,220 --> 00:17:59,440\nYou need the ten minutes.\n\n397\n00:18:00,480 --> 00:18:01,740\n\"You do not need the tree.\n\n398\n00:18:02,120 --> 00:18:06,140\nYou need the ten minutes.\" Ben, that is our message today.\n\n399\n00:18:06,860 --> 00:18:08,720\nA walk is not only for the legs.\n\n400\n00:18:09,160 --> 00:18:10,760\nIt is the room where the head thinks.\n\n401\n00:18:11,220 --> 00:18:13,620\nAnd the phone takes that room away.\n\n402\n00:18:13,640 --> 00:18:14,580\nSo walk without it.\n\n403\n00:18:15,240 --> 00:18:15,920\nTen minutes.\n\n404\n00:18:16,460 --> 00:18:16,900\nSlow.\n\n405\n00:18:17,460 --> 00:18:17,940\nNowhere.\n\n406\n00:18:18,480 --> 00:18:19,140\nOutside.\n\n407\n00:18:19,700 --> 00:18:20,600\nEmpty hands.\n\n408\n00:18:21,200 --> 00:18:24,060\nAnd the head goes outside, where it has been waiting to go.\n\n409\n00:18:24,800 --> 00:18:26,530\nWhere it has been waiting to go.\n\n410\n00:18:27,500 --> 00:18:32,990\nMia, let's repeat today's important words, slowly, because our listeners want to remember them.\n\n411\n00:18:34,420 --> 00:18:34,740\nWalk.\n\n412\n00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:37,060\nTo go on foot.\n\n413\n00:18:38,200 --> 00:18:39,220\nA slow walk.\n\n414\n00:18:40,740 --> 00:18:41,880\nWalk without the phone.\n\n415\n00:18:43,420 --> 00:18:44,180\nWalk nowhere.\n\n416\n00:18:45,740 --> 00:18:46,100\nPhone.\n\n417\n00:18:47,260 --> 00:18:48,240\nLeave the phone.\n\n418\n00:18:49,680 --> 00:18:50,780\nPhone in the bag.\n\n419\n00:18:52,460 --> 00:18:52,720\nThink.\n\n420\n00:18:53,980 --> 00:18:54,840\nTo use the head.\n\n421\n00:18:56,100 --> 00:18:57,660\nThe head thinks on the walk.\n\n422\n00:18:59,280 --> 00:18:59,860\nOutside.\n\n423\n00:19:00,760 --> 00:19:01,680\nNot in the house.\n\n424\n00:19:03,080 --> 00:19:03,880\nGo outside.\n\n425\n00:19:05,100 --> 00:19:06,640\nThe sky is outside.\n\n426\n00:19:08,420 --> 00:19:08,760\nSlow.\n\n427\n00:19:09,740 --> 00:19:10,500\nNot fast.\n\n428\n00:19:11,780 --> 00:19:12,900\nA slow walk.\n\n429\n00:19:14,380 --> 00:19:15,720\nSlow is where the tree is.\n\n430\n00:19:17,540 --> 00:19:17,960\nWalk.\n\n431\n00:19:19,240 --> 00:19:19,700\nPhone.\n\n432\n00:19:20,960 --> 00:19:21,340\nThink.\n\n433\n00:19:22,800 --> 00:19:23,420\nOutside.\n\n434\n00:19:24,920 --> 00:19:25,280\nSlow.\n\n435\n00:19:26,520 --> 00:19:28,260\nFive words, and one street.\n\n436\n00:19:28,800 --> 00:19:30,000\nAnd one big idea.\n\n437\n00:19:30,780 --> 00:19:31,930\nWalk without your phone.\n\n438\n00:19:32,920 --> 00:19:35,460\nNow, Ben, do you have a question for our listeners?\n\n439\n00:19:36,220 --> 00:19:36,720\nYes, Mia.\n\n440\n00:19:37,360 --> 00:19:39,480\nEveryone listening, here is my question.\n\n441\n00:19:40,560 --> 00:19:44,470\nWhat did you see on your slow walk this week that you never saw before?\n\n442\n00:19:45,540 --> 00:19:45,940\nA door?\n\n443\n00:19:46,910 --> 00:19:47,380\nA tree?\n\n444\n00:19:48,520 --> 00:19:49,620\nA cat on a car?\n\n445\n00:19:50,760 --> 00:19:53,840\nWrite it in the comments in one short sentence.\n\n446\n00:19:53,880 --> 00:20:02,540\nFor example, \"A bakery with a blue door.\" And if you have not walked without the phone yet, tell us what you think you might be missing.\n\n447\n00:20:03,320 --> 00:20:04,520\nWhat you might be missing.\n\n448\n00:20:05,240 --> 00:20:08,960\nAnd if this episode helped you, please subscribe to Two Cups English.\n\n449\n00:20:09,520 --> 00:20:11,720\nWe make a new episode every few days.\n\n450\n00:20:12,260 --> 00:20:17,100\nSlow English, simple words, and one true story each time.\n\n451\n00:20:17,110 --> 00:20:19,510\nThe full text of this episode is in the description.\n\n452\n00:20:20,140 --> 00:20:22,840\nRead it at your desk, and then go for a walk without it.\n\n453\n00:20:23,560 --> 00:20:25,110\nThank you for spending this time with us.\n\n454\n00:20:25,860 --> 00:20:27,350\nMaybe you are walking right now.\n\n455\n00:20:28,220 --> 00:20:29,240\nGood.\n\n456\n00:20:29,360 --> 00:20:34,780\nFinish this episode, and then take the headphones out, and walk the last five minutes with nothing.\n\n457\n00:20:35,880 --> 00:20:36,640\nLook at a door.\n\n458\n00:20:37,620 --> 00:20:38,400\nLook at a tree.\n\n459\n00:20:39,540 --> 00:20:41,120\nLet the head go outside.\n\n460\n00:20:42,080 --> 00:20:42,880\nIt has been waiting.\n\n461\n00:20:43,660 --> 00:20:44,340\nThis is Mia.\n\n462\n00:20:44,820 --> 00:20:45,880\nAnd this is Ben.\n\n463\n00:20:46,600 --> 00:20:48,400\nThis was Two Cups English.\n\n464\n00:20:48,940 --> 00:20:49,720\nWalk slow.\n\n465\n00:20:50,360 --> 00:20:51,400\nWe will see you next time."},{"code":"CUP09","file":"CUP09.srt","srt":"1\n00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,280\nHello everyone, and welcome to Two Cups English.\n\n2\n00:00:03,800 --> 00:00:06,700\nI'm Mia, and Ben is here with me.\n\n3\n00:00:06,720 --> 00:00:09,580\nThis podcast is for people who are learning English.\n\n4\n00:00:10,220 --> 00:00:11,060\nWe speak slowly.\n\n5\n00:00:11,640 --> 00:00:12,970\nWe use simple words.\n\n6\n00:00:13,560 --> 00:00:16,400\nAnd we repeat the important words many times.\n\n7\n00:00:17,320 --> 00:00:19,980\nToday, our topic is sleep.\n\n8\n00:00:20,060 --> 00:00:22,520\nAnd why sleep is not wasted time.\n\n9\n00:00:23,740 --> 00:00:26,360\nBen, how many hours did you sleep last night?\n\n10\n00:00:27,480 --> 00:00:27,920\nHi Mia.\n\n11\n00:00:28,460 --> 00:00:29,040\nHi everyone.\n\n12\n00:00:29,780 --> 00:00:32,060\nLast night, six hours, Mia.\n\n13\n00:00:32,680 --> 00:00:33,480\nNot bad for me.\n\n14\n00:00:34,200 --> 00:00:36,080\nBut I was in bed for eight.\n\n15\n00:00:36,860 --> 00:00:39,920\nTwo hours of phone, and then six hours of sleep.\n\n16\n00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:43,080\nAnd I am tired now.\n\n17\n00:00:43,940 --> 00:00:44,520\nWhat about you?\n\n18\n00:00:45,100 --> 00:00:45,800\nHow did you sleep?\n\n19\n00:00:46,980 --> 00:00:48,180\nSeven hours, Ben.\n\n20\n00:00:48,640 --> 00:00:49,559\nThat is new for me.\n\n21\n00:00:50,280 --> 00:00:52,800\nFor five years, I was proud of one thing.\n\n22\n00:00:53,420 --> 00:00:55,980\nI slept five hours a night.\n\n23\n00:00:56,100 --> 00:00:57,140\nFive.\n\n24\n00:00:57,220 --> 00:00:58,310\nI said it at parties.\n\n25\n00:00:58,910 --> 00:01:02,180\n\"I only need five hours.\" People were impressed.\n\n26\n00:01:02,800 --> 00:01:04,360\nI was impressed with myself.\n\n27\n00:01:05,040 --> 00:01:06,980\nAnd I was tired every day.\n\n28\n00:01:07,960 --> 00:01:10,420\nFive hours, for five years, Mia?\n\n29\n00:01:11,180 --> 00:01:12,280\nAnd you were proud of it?\n\n30\n00:01:13,160 --> 00:01:14,240\nAnd what happened?\n\n31\n00:01:14,980 --> 00:01:15,840\nWhy did you change?\n\n32\n00:01:17,280 --> 00:01:18,240\nI got sick, Ben.\n\n33\n00:01:18,680 --> 00:01:19,220\nLast year.\n\n34\n00:01:19,940 --> 00:01:20,920\nNot very sick.\n\n35\n00:01:21,460 --> 00:01:23,160\nBut sick for a long time.\n\n36\n00:01:23,860 --> 00:01:25,320\nColds that did not go.\n\n37\n00:01:26,020 --> 00:01:27,580\nTired all day.\n\n38\n00:01:27,590 --> 00:01:29,830\nAnd the doctor asked me one question.\n\n39\n00:01:30,590 --> 00:01:33,680\n\"How much do you sleep?\" I said, \"Five hours.\n\n40\n00:01:34,220 --> 00:01:38,340\nI only need five.\" And she looked at me for a long time.\n\n41\n00:01:39,300 --> 00:01:41,040\nA long look from the doctor, Mia.\n\n42\n00:01:41,860 --> 00:01:42,560\nI know that look.\n\n43\n00:01:43,420 --> 00:01:46,700\nDoctors do it before they say something true.\n\n44\n00:01:46,710 --> 00:01:48,180\nWhat did she say?\n\n45\n00:01:48,280 --> 00:01:49,720\nI want the exact words.\n\n46\n00:01:50,820 --> 00:01:54,020\nShe said, \"Your body is not a phone, Mia.\n\n47\n00:01:54,490 --> 00:02:01,640\nYou cannot charge it to forty percent and run all day.\" And she said most people need seven or eight hours.\n\n48\n00:02:02,320 --> 00:02:07,220\nAnd that the five-hour people are almost always tired and do not know it.\n\n49\n00:02:07,300 --> 00:02:10,340\nBecause tired becomes normal.\n\n50\n00:02:10,370 --> 00:02:14,489\n\"Tired becomes normal.\" Mia, that is our topic today.\n\n51\n00:02:15,240 --> 00:02:17,180\nSo here is our plan.\n\n52\n00:02:17,240 --> 00:02:21,100\nIn part one, we talk about our own tired years.\n\n53\n00:02:21,160 --> 00:02:22,680\nWhat five hours feels like.\n\n54\n00:02:23,620 --> 00:02:30,000\nIn part two, we ask what the brain does at night, and why sleep is not wasted.\n\n55\n00:02:30,040 --> 00:02:32,900\nAnd I have a true story for that.\n\n56\n00:02:32,940 --> 00:02:36,180\nIn part three, we share simple ideas for a better night.\n\n57\n00:02:37,080 --> 00:02:37,820\nDoes that sound good?\n\n58\n00:02:38,800 --> 00:02:39,780\nThat sounds good, Ben.\n\n59\n00:02:40,320 --> 00:02:44,080\nAnd to everyone listening, please do not worry if you miss something.\n\n60\n00:02:44,480 --> 00:02:45,480\nYou will hear it again.\n\n61\n00:02:46,100 --> 00:02:51,600\nYou can listen while you cook, while you walk, or in bed, resting, before you sleep.\n\n62\n00:02:52,380 --> 00:02:53,960\nThat is a good place for this one.\n\n63\n00:02:54,960 --> 00:02:55,519\nOkay, Ben.\n\n64\n00:02:55,820 --> 00:02:56,340\nPart one.\n\n65\n00:02:57,300 --> 00:02:59,340\nYou said you had a tired year too.\n\n66\n00:02:59,840 --> 00:03:00,240\nTell me.\n\n67\n00:03:01,700 --> 00:03:02,500\nI did, Mia.\n\n68\n00:03:03,200 --> 00:03:06,740\nTwo jobs, a baby, six hours on a good night.\n\n69\n00:03:07,600 --> 00:03:10,240\nAnd I remember thinking I was just getting old.\n\n70\n00:03:11,120 --> 00:03:12,960\nI was thirty-four.\n\n71\n00:03:13,020 --> 00:03:14,040\nI was not old.\n\n72\n00:03:14,820 --> 00:03:15,570\nI was tired.\n\n73\n00:03:16,160 --> 00:03:17,680\nAnd I did not know the difference.\n\n74\n00:03:18,680 --> 00:03:20,400\nTired and old felt the same.\n\n75\n00:03:21,320 --> 00:03:21,640\nHeavy.\n\n76\n00:03:22,540 --> 00:03:23,880\nSlow.\n\n77\n00:03:23,960 --> 00:03:24,640\nNo ideas.\n\n78\n00:03:25,420 --> 00:03:26,280\nHeavy.\n\n79\n00:03:26,360 --> 00:03:27,760\nThat is my word too, Ben.\n\n80\n00:03:28,580 --> 00:03:32,240\nEvery morning for five years was heavy.\n\n81\n00:03:32,400 --> 00:03:40,820\nI woke up, and the first feeling was not \"good morning.\" It was \"again.\" The alarm, the coffee, the fog.\n\n82\n00:03:41,280 --> 00:03:43,740\nI needed two coffees before I could speak.\n\n83\n00:03:44,540 --> 00:03:45,620\nAnd the afternoon, Mia?\n\n84\n00:03:46,260 --> 00:03:49,700\nBecause the morning is one thing, but three o'clock is the real test.\n\n85\n00:03:50,260 --> 00:03:51,740\nEveryone is tired at three.\n\n86\n00:03:52,380 --> 00:03:53,520\nWhat was three o'clock like?\n\n87\n00:03:54,820 --> 00:03:55,840\nFinished, Ben.\n\n88\n00:03:56,400 --> 00:04:00,140\nBy three, I was finished, and I had four hours of work left.\n\n89\n00:04:00,720 --> 00:04:01,900\nSo I pushed.\n\n90\n00:04:01,960 --> 00:04:02,880\nMore coffee.\n\n91\n00:04:02,920 --> 00:04:03,510\nMore sugar.\n\n92\n00:04:04,180 --> 00:04:06,640\nAnd at night, I was too tired to sleep.\n\n93\n00:04:06,680 --> 00:04:07,990\nStrange, but true.\n\n94\n00:04:08,680 --> 00:04:10,160\nToo tired to sleep.\n\n95\n00:04:10,860 --> 00:04:12,700\nSo I looked at my phone until one.\n\n96\n00:04:13,200 --> 00:04:15,339\nAnd then five hours.\n\n97\n00:04:15,420 --> 00:04:15,900\nAgain.\n\n98\n00:04:16,659 --> 00:04:20,280\n\"Too tired to sleep.\" Mia, I know that exactly.\n\n99\n00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:24,500\nThe body is finished, and the brain is still running.\n\n100\n00:04:24,560 --> 00:04:26,600\nAnd we think sleep is the price of the day.\n\n101\n00:04:27,060 --> 00:04:29,839\nSomething we pay at the end, as little as possible.\n\n102\n00:04:30,570 --> 00:04:32,540\nBut sleep is not the price of the day.\n\n103\n00:04:33,140 --> 00:04:34,160\nIt is part of the day.\n\n104\n00:04:34,780 --> 00:04:37,560\nIt is rest, and rest is work too.\n\n105\n00:04:38,380 --> 00:04:39,680\nRest is work, Ben?\n\n106\n00:04:40,300 --> 00:04:41,880\nThat sounds like a contradiction.\n\n107\n00:04:42,280 --> 00:04:43,800\nMy boss would not agree.\n\n108\n00:04:45,420 --> 00:04:47,140\nYour boss is awake, Mia.\n\n109\n00:04:47,260 --> 00:04:49,000\nHis brain is not.\n\n110\n00:04:49,060 --> 00:04:50,900\nNobody's boss sees the night shift.\n\n111\n00:04:51,520 --> 00:04:54,410\nBut it is there, every night, for everyone.\n\n112\n00:04:55,220 --> 00:04:58,100\nAnd a worker who never rests does bad work.\n\n113\n00:04:58,880 --> 00:05:00,340\nYou know that from three o'clock.\n\n114\n00:05:01,240 --> 00:05:02,680\nSame for the brain.\n\n115\n00:05:02,740 --> 00:05:07,360\nThe night is when the brain does the work it cannot do while you are talking to your boss.\n\n116\n00:05:08,080 --> 00:05:10,080\nSo rest is not the opposite of work.\n\n117\n00:05:10,720 --> 00:05:12,460\nRest is the other half of work.\n\n118\n00:05:13,290 --> 00:05:18,940\n\"Sleep is part of the day, not the price of it.\" Ben, that is the sentence, I think.\n\n119\n00:05:19,620 --> 00:05:21,860\nSo what happened when you started sleeping more?\n\n120\n00:05:22,180 --> 00:05:22,740\nDid it help?\n\n121\n00:05:24,360 --> 00:05:26,280\nNot in the first week, Mia.\n\n122\n00:05:26,360 --> 00:05:27,540\nThat is the strange part.\n\n123\n00:05:28,400 --> 00:05:30,890\nI slept seven hours, and I felt worse.\n\n124\n00:05:31,560 --> 00:05:32,230\nMore tired.\n\n125\n00:05:32,880 --> 00:05:36,120\nLike the body was saying, \"Oh, you are listening now?\n\n126\n00:05:36,240 --> 00:05:41,440\nHere is everything I wanted to say.\" And then, in week two, something opened.\n\n127\n00:05:42,160 --> 00:05:44,179\nLike somebody opened a window in my head.\n\n128\n00:05:44,980 --> 00:05:47,040\nA window in week two, Ben.\n\n129\n00:05:47,570 --> 00:05:48,550\nWhat did it feel like?\n\n130\n00:05:48,880 --> 00:05:52,560\nBecause I want our listeners to know what they are waiting for.\n\n131\n00:05:52,640 --> 00:05:55,220\nLike being fifteen again, Mia.\n\n132\n00:05:55,400 --> 00:05:56,800\nI woke up before the alarm.\n\n133\n00:05:57,660 --> 00:05:59,380\nI was not angry at the alarm.\n\n134\n00:06:00,260 --> 00:06:01,560\nI had ideas in the shower.\n\n135\n00:06:02,060 --> 00:06:03,040\nSmall things.\n\n136\n00:06:03,060 --> 00:06:05,320\nBut I had forgotten they existed.\n\n137\n00:06:05,340 --> 00:06:07,920\nAnd at three in the afternoon, nothing happened.\n\n138\n00:06:08,500 --> 00:06:09,060\nNo cliff.\n\n139\n00:06:09,440 --> 00:06:09,980\nNo sugar.\n\n140\n00:06:10,740 --> 00:06:11,800\nI just kept working.\n\n141\n00:06:12,660 --> 00:06:14,279\nThat was the window.\n\n142\n00:06:14,340 --> 00:06:16,970\nA normal afternoon, for the first time in a year.\n\n143\n00:06:17,780 --> 00:06:24,600\n\"Somebody opened a window in my head.\" Ben, that is exactly what happened to me.\n\n144\n00:06:24,980 --> 00:06:30,640\nThe doctor said, \"Try seven hours, for two weeks.\" Week one was worse.\n\n145\n00:06:31,260 --> 00:06:33,399\nWeek two, the window.\n\n146\n00:06:34,080 --> 00:06:36,920\nAnd I had forgotten what not tired felt like.\n\n147\n00:06:37,520 --> 00:06:41,180\nFive years of tired, and I had forgotten the other thing.\n\n148\n00:06:42,150 --> 00:06:45,000\nWe forget what not tired feels like, Mia.\n\n149\n00:06:45,100 --> 00:06:48,780\nThat is the danger of \"normal.\" And there is something else.\n\n150\n00:06:49,450 --> 00:06:50,800\nWe are proud of tired.\n\n151\n00:06:51,330 --> 00:06:56,320\n\"I only sleep five hours.\" \"I worked until two.\" We say it like a medal.\n\n152\n00:06:57,280 --> 00:06:58,540\nLike a medal, Ben.\n\n153\n00:06:58,960 --> 00:07:02,740\nNobody says, \"I only eat one meal a day,\" with pride.\n\n154\n00:07:03,200 --> 00:07:04,660\nBut we say it about sleep.\n\n155\n00:07:05,360 --> 00:07:07,500\nI wore that medal for five years.\n\n156\n00:07:07,980 --> 00:07:10,080\nAnd the medal was making me sick.\n\n157\n00:07:10,980 --> 00:07:12,240\nTake the medal off, Mia.\n\n158\n00:07:12,860 --> 00:07:14,000\nThat is the first thing.\n\n159\n00:07:15,260 --> 00:07:16,600\nOkay, so that was part one.\n\n160\n00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:18,040\nOur tired years.\n\n161\n00:07:18,700 --> 00:07:25,520\nFive hours and a medal, two jobs and a baby, too tired to sleep, and a window in week two.\n\n162\n00:07:25,600 --> 00:07:27,460\nNow, part two.\n\n163\n00:07:27,520 --> 00:07:29,040\nWhat does the brain do at night?\n\n164\n00:07:30,060 --> 00:07:34,240\nMia, this is my true story, and it changed how I think about sleep.\n\n165\n00:07:35,140 --> 00:07:36,320\nChanged how you think, Ben?\n\n166\n00:07:36,920 --> 00:07:39,040\nThat is a big claim for a story about sleep.\n\n167\n00:07:39,640 --> 00:07:40,000\nTell me.\n\n168\n00:07:40,480 --> 00:07:41,420\nWhat does the brain do?\n\n169\n00:07:42,500 --> 00:07:43,380\nOkay, part two.\n\n170\n00:07:43,980 --> 00:07:45,240\nThis is a true story.\n\n171\n00:07:45,980 --> 00:07:54,080\nIn 2013, a scientist named Maiken Nedergaard, at the University of Rochester in America, published a study in the journal Science.\n\n172\n00:07:54,780 --> 00:07:57,700\nHer team looked at the brains of sleeping mice.\n\n173\n00:07:57,710 --> 00:08:00,080\nAnd they found something nobody had seen before.\n\n174\n00:08:00,980 --> 00:08:02,020\nSleeping mice, Ben?\n\n175\n00:08:02,800 --> 00:08:05,520\nI did not expect mice in a story about my bedtime.\n\n176\n00:08:06,020 --> 00:08:06,740\nWhat did they see?\n\n177\n00:08:07,670 --> 00:08:09,760\nThey saw the brain washing itself, Mia.\n\n178\n00:08:10,490 --> 00:08:13,460\nAll day, the brain works, and work makes waste.\n\n179\n00:08:14,080 --> 00:08:15,970\nLittle pieces of used-up material.\n\n180\n00:08:16,670 --> 00:08:20,820\nAnd during the day, there is no room to clean, because the brain is busy.\n\n181\n00:08:21,540 --> 00:08:25,630\nBut at night, when the mice slept, the space between the brain cells opened.\n\n182\n00:08:26,220 --> 00:08:27,560\nLike streets opening.\n\n183\n00:08:27,600 --> 00:08:30,960\nAnd a clear liquid came in and washed the waste out.\n\n184\n00:08:31,850 --> 00:08:34,360\nThe brain washes itself at night, Ben?\n\n185\n00:08:34,400 --> 00:08:35,250\nLike a street cleaner?\n\n186\n00:08:36,180 --> 00:08:37,940\nExactly like a street cleaner, Mia.\n\n187\n00:08:38,589 --> 00:08:42,240\nThe streets open, the water comes, the rubbish goes.\n\n188\n00:08:42,840 --> 00:08:44,580\nAnd it only happens when we sleep.\n\n189\n00:08:45,160 --> 00:08:46,740\nNot when we rest with eyes open.\n\n190\n00:08:47,190 --> 00:08:48,520\nNot when we sit quietly.\n\n191\n00:08:49,160 --> 00:08:50,100\nOnly in sleep.\n\n192\n00:08:50,820 --> 00:08:53,520\nSo five hours means the cleaning stops halfway.\n\n193\n00:08:54,070 --> 00:08:57,060\nAnd you wake up and think in a brain that was not cleaned.\n\n194\n00:08:58,060 --> 00:09:00,900\nFive hours, and the cleaning stops halfway, Ben.\n\n195\n00:09:01,700 --> 00:09:04,080\nSo what does a half-cleaned brain feel like?\n\n196\n00:09:04,500 --> 00:09:05,209\nIs that the fog?\n\n197\n00:09:06,220 --> 00:09:07,500\nThat is the fog, Mia.\n\n198\n00:09:08,140 --> 00:09:10,300\nThe waste from yesterday is still on the streets.\n\n199\n00:09:11,080 --> 00:09:12,399\nSo the brain is slow.\n\n200\n00:09:12,960 --> 00:09:13,920\nIt forgets names.\n\n201\n00:09:14,400 --> 00:09:15,600\nIt cannot find words.\n\n202\n00:09:16,240 --> 00:09:17,920\nIt gets angry at small things.\n\n203\n00:09:18,800 --> 00:09:23,040\nNedergaard's team even thinks that years of bad sleep let the waste build up.\n\n204\n00:09:23,180 --> 00:09:25,480\nSo five hours is not only a tired morning.\n\n205\n00:09:26,110 --> 00:09:29,160\nIt is a dirty brain, every day, for years.\n\n206\n00:09:30,260 --> 00:09:32,580\nThinking in a brain that was not cleaned, Ben.\n\n207\n00:09:33,240 --> 00:09:35,080\nFor five years.\n\n208\n00:09:35,180 --> 00:09:39,860\nI was thinking in a dirty room for five years, and I was proud of it.\n\n209\n00:09:40,580 --> 00:09:42,040\nAnd now the room is clean, Mia.\n\n210\n00:09:42,760 --> 00:09:44,980\nThat is the window in week two.\n\n211\n00:09:45,020 --> 00:09:46,030\nThe washing caught up.\n\n212\n00:09:46,760 --> 00:09:50,200\nLet me say what sleep is not, because I had it wrong.\n\n213\n00:09:50,280 --> 00:09:52,960\nI thought sleep was like turning off a computer.\n\n214\n00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:53,740\nNothing happens.\n\n215\n00:09:54,320 --> 00:09:55,240\nThe screen is black.\n\n216\n00:09:55,960 --> 00:09:58,220\nEvery hour asleep was an hour of nothing.\n\n217\n00:09:58,880 --> 00:10:01,160\nBut the brain is not off at night, Ben.\n\n218\n00:10:01,760 --> 00:10:02,780\nIt is not tired.\n\n219\n00:10:03,240 --> 00:10:03,880\nIt is busy.\n\n220\n00:10:04,420 --> 00:10:05,120\nWashing.\n\n221\n00:10:05,240 --> 00:10:05,780\nSorting.\n\n222\n00:10:06,320 --> 00:10:07,340\nIt is the night shift.\n\n223\n00:10:08,060 --> 00:10:10,820\n\"Sleep is the night shift.\" Mia, I like that.\n\n224\n00:10:11,460 --> 00:10:13,840\nBecause it makes sleep a job, not a hole.\n\n225\n00:10:14,480 --> 00:10:16,080\nAnd a job you do not need to do.\n\n226\n00:10:16,760 --> 00:10:17,579\nThe workers do it.\n\n227\n00:10:18,200 --> 00:10:20,920\nYour only job is to close the doors and leave the building.\n\n228\n00:10:21,920 --> 00:10:23,280\nCan I add something about English?\n\n229\n00:10:23,820 --> 00:10:26,180\nBecause the night shift does something with words.\n\n230\n00:10:26,820 --> 00:10:27,680\nWith words, Ben?\n\n231\n00:10:28,420 --> 00:10:30,980\nI did not know the night had anything to do with words.\n\n232\n00:10:31,640 --> 00:10:32,300\nWhat does it do?\n\n233\n00:10:33,300 --> 00:10:38,060\nWhen you learn a word in the day, Mia, it goes into a small, temporary place in the brain.\n\n234\n00:10:38,820 --> 00:10:40,120\nA waiting room.\n\n235\n00:10:40,240 --> 00:10:43,500\nAnd at night, during sleep, the brain moves it.\n\n236\n00:10:43,540 --> 00:10:46,580\nFrom the waiting room to the long-term room, where it stays.\n\n237\n00:10:47,280 --> 00:10:48,720\nNo sleep, no moving.\n\n238\n00:10:49,480 --> 00:10:53,640\nThe words stay in the waiting room, and in the morning, the waiting room is empty.\n\n239\n00:10:54,990 --> 00:11:00,060\n\"The words are gone because you did not sleep.\" Ben, that explains so much.\n\n240\n00:11:00,740 --> 00:11:03,140\nWhen I was learning Spanish, I had a bad month.\n\n241\n00:11:03,550 --> 00:11:06,380\nI studied every night until one in the morning.\n\n242\n00:11:06,500 --> 00:11:07,520\nHard.\n\n243\n00:11:07,560 --> 00:11:08,700\nAnd nothing stayed.\n\n244\n00:11:09,360 --> 00:11:10,360\nI thought I was stupid.\n\n245\n00:11:11,060 --> 00:11:12,860\nI was only tired.\n\n246\n00:11:12,900 --> 00:11:13,500\nAnd then, Mia?\n\n247\n00:11:13,980 --> 00:11:14,600\nWhat changed?\n\n248\n00:11:15,580 --> 00:11:16,460\nI got busy, Ben.\n\n249\n00:11:17,040 --> 00:11:17,870\nI studied less.\n\n250\n00:11:18,340 --> 00:11:20,539\nTwenty minutes at seven, and then I slept more.\n\n251\n00:11:21,060 --> 00:11:22,700\nAnd suddenly the words stayed.\n\n252\n00:11:23,380 --> 00:11:25,920\nLess study, more sleep, more Spanish.\n\n253\n00:11:26,520 --> 00:11:27,980\nI did not understand it then.\n\n254\n00:11:28,560 --> 00:11:29,200\nNow I do.\n\n255\n00:11:29,680 --> 00:11:31,600\nThe night shift was moving the words.\n\n256\n00:11:32,400 --> 00:11:38,900\n\"Less study, more sleep, more Spanish.\" Mia, that should be on the first page of every language book.\n\n257\n00:11:39,720 --> 00:11:41,580\nAnd it is the same for this podcast.\n\n258\n00:11:42,320 --> 00:11:48,060\nIf you listen at midnight and sleep five hours, most of what we said goes to the empty room.\n\n259\n00:11:48,140 --> 00:11:52,069\nIf you listen at eight, and sleep at ten thirty, the night keeps the words.\n\n260\n00:11:52,880 --> 00:11:54,340\nThe night keeps the words.\n\n261\n00:11:55,200 --> 00:11:59,320\nAnd the night does it for free, Ben, while you rest.\n\n262\n00:11:59,350 --> 00:12:02,400\nThe teacher that works for free while you sleep.\n\n263\n00:12:03,760 --> 00:12:05,700\nOkay, so that was part two.\n\n264\n00:12:05,760 --> 00:12:07,460\nWhat the brain does at night.\n\n265\n00:12:08,360 --> 00:12:12,120\nWe talked about the sleeping mice and the street cleaner.\n\n266\n00:12:12,130 --> 00:12:14,400\nWe said sleep is the night shift.\n\n267\n00:12:15,240 --> 00:12:17,880\nAnd we said the night keeps the words.\n\n268\n00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:20,960\nNow, part three.\n\n269\n00:12:21,060 --> 00:12:23,020\nSimple ideas for a better night.\n\n270\n00:12:23,840 --> 00:12:25,400\nBen, you go first.\n\n271\n00:12:26,340 --> 00:12:28,060\nOkay, part three.\n\n272\n00:12:28,100 --> 00:12:30,340\nMy first idea is about the evening, Mia.\n\n273\n00:12:31,080 --> 00:12:33,940\nBecause sleep is decided in the evening, not at night.\n\n274\n00:12:34,880 --> 00:12:37,600\nMy sleep was bad because my evening was a cliff.\n\n275\n00:12:38,480 --> 00:12:43,200\nLaptop until eleven, then dark, then lying there with a brain full of email.\n\n276\n00:12:44,260 --> 00:12:46,020\nSo now the evening slopes down.\n\n277\n00:12:46,760 --> 00:12:47,980\nEight, nine, ten.\n\n278\n00:12:48,840 --> 00:12:51,350\nEach hour a little darker, a little quieter.\n\n279\n00:12:52,130 --> 00:12:53,480\nThe evening is for rest.\n\n280\n00:12:54,650 --> 00:13:04,040\n\"The evening slopes down instead of falling off a cliff.\" Ben, my first idea is about the word \"tired,\" from the body's side.\n\n281\n00:13:04,900 --> 00:13:08,080\nBecause the body talks, and we do not listen.\n\n282\n00:13:09,180 --> 00:13:10,440\nTired has a sound.\n\n283\n00:13:11,310 --> 00:13:12,160\nHeavy eyes.\n\n284\n00:13:12,740 --> 00:13:13,360\nA yawn.\n\n285\n00:13:14,140 --> 00:13:16,300\nCold hands at four in the afternoon.\n\n286\n00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:18,220\nA wish for sugar.\n\n287\n00:13:19,180 --> 00:13:20,220\nThose are messages.\n\n288\n00:13:20,920 --> 00:13:21,700\nMessages, Mia.\n\n289\n00:13:22,370 --> 00:13:25,520\nAnd for years, I answered every message with coffee.\n\n290\n00:13:26,490 --> 00:13:32,810\nThe body said, \"Rest,\" and I said, \"Coffee.\" And after a while, the body stopped expecting an answer.\n\n291\n00:13:33,620 --> 00:13:35,940\nThe body stops expecting an answer, Ben.\n\n292\n00:13:36,880 --> 00:13:39,100\nMine stopped years ago.\n\n293\n00:13:39,140 --> 00:13:43,900\nSo now, when I feel the four o'clock cold hands, I do not drink coffee.\n\n294\n00:13:44,660 --> 00:13:46,800\nI close my eyes for ten minutes.\n\n295\n00:13:47,560 --> 00:13:49,320\nOn the bus, at my desk.\n\n296\n00:13:50,080 --> 00:13:51,500\nTen minutes of rest.\n\n297\n00:13:51,960 --> 00:13:52,660\nNot sleep.\n\n298\n00:13:53,340 --> 00:13:53,820\nRest.\n\n299\n00:13:54,600 --> 00:13:56,420\nAnd the body says, \"Oh.\n\n300\n00:13:57,060 --> 00:13:57,560\nThank you.\"\n\n301\n00:13:58,540 --> 00:13:59,920\nTen minutes of rest, Mia.\n\n302\n00:14:00,990 --> 00:14:02,820\nMy second idea is the nap.\n\n303\n00:14:03,779 --> 00:14:05,980\nIn many countries, people rest after lunch.\n\n304\n00:14:06,760 --> 00:14:08,520\nTwenty minutes in a chair.\n\n305\n00:14:08,620 --> 00:14:09,670\nNot an hour in bed.\n\n306\n00:14:10,200 --> 00:14:10,840\nTwenty minutes.\n\n307\n00:14:11,770 --> 00:14:12,820\nAnd the brain rests.\n\n308\n00:14:13,420 --> 00:14:14,640\nIt is not the big wash.\n\n309\n00:14:15,180 --> 00:14:16,500\nBut it is a small one.\n\n310\n00:14:17,520 --> 00:14:19,780\nA small wash is better than a dirty room.\n\n311\n00:14:20,780 --> 00:14:22,500\nTwenty minutes in a chair, Ben.\n\n312\n00:14:23,090 --> 00:14:24,040\nAnd not an hour?\n\n313\n00:14:24,920 --> 00:14:30,120\nBecause when I nap, I wake up at five in the evening, and I do not know what year it is.\n\n314\n00:14:31,620 --> 00:14:32,720\nThat is the hour, Mia.\n\n315\n00:14:33,420 --> 00:14:37,560\nThe hour takes you into deep sleep, and you wake up in the middle of the wash.\n\n316\n00:14:38,520 --> 00:14:40,120\nTwenty minutes stays near the door.\n\n317\n00:14:40,920 --> 00:14:42,370\nYou rest, and you come back.\n\n318\n00:14:43,280 --> 00:14:44,080\nSo set a timer.\n\n319\n00:14:44,760 --> 00:14:48,020\nTwenty minutes, in a chair, not a bed.\n\n320\n00:14:48,080 --> 00:14:49,180\nThe chair is the trick.\n\n321\n00:14:49,930 --> 00:14:51,100\nThe bed says night.\n\n322\n00:14:51,849 --> 00:14:53,320\nThe chair says rest.\n\n323\n00:14:54,200 --> 00:15:01,400\n\"A small wash is better than a dirty room.\" Ben, my second idea is about the phone in bed.\n\n324\n00:15:02,200 --> 00:15:04,920\nBecause the phone is the biggest street-closer.\n\n325\n00:15:05,760 --> 00:15:09,340\nThe phone is light, and light says \"day\" to the brain.\n\n326\n00:15:10,260 --> 00:15:15,000\nAnd the phone is people, and people say \"party\" to the brain.\n\n327\n00:15:15,100 --> 00:15:18,340\nA small sun and a small party, in the dark room.\n\n328\n00:15:19,140 --> 00:15:22,070\nA small sun and a small party, in the dark room.\n\n329\n00:15:22,730 --> 00:15:26,380\nMia, that is exactly what my two hours of phone were last night.\n\n330\n00:15:26,420 --> 00:15:28,960\nAnd I am tired now because of it.\n\n331\n00:15:28,980 --> 00:15:30,300\nSo where does your phone sleep now?\n\n332\n00:15:30,900 --> 00:15:31,880\nIn the kitchen, Ben.\n\n333\n00:15:32,560 --> 00:15:33,720\nFrom our evening episode.\n\n334\n00:15:34,300 --> 00:15:36,180\nAnd my bedroom is dark by ten thirty.\n\n335\n00:15:36,520 --> 00:15:38,840\nBecause the brain does not read clocks.\n\n336\n00:15:39,160 --> 00:15:39,980\nIt reads light.\n\n337\n00:15:40,660 --> 00:15:43,620\nIf the room is bright, the brain thinks it is noon.\n\n338\n00:15:43,700 --> 00:15:45,500\nSo make the night dark again.\n\n339\n00:15:45,580 --> 00:15:47,300\nThat is the whole trick.\n\n340\n00:15:47,350 --> 00:15:52,320\n\"Make the night dark again.\" Mia, my third idea is for the people who cannot sleep.\n\n341\n00:15:52,620 --> 00:15:54,180\nBecause some are listening.\n\n342\n00:15:54,220 --> 00:15:57,020\nThey go to bed at ten thirty, and they lie there until two.\n\n343\n00:15:57,580 --> 00:16:00,350\nAnd they hear us say \"sleep more,\" and they feel worse.\n\n344\n00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:01,980\nWhat do you say to them, Ben?\n\n345\n00:16:02,800 --> 00:16:04,320\nI say, do not try, Mia.\n\n346\n00:16:05,080 --> 00:16:07,900\nSleep is a walk to nowhere, like our walking episode.\n\n347\n00:16:08,400 --> 00:16:09,390\nNo destination.\n\n348\n00:16:10,040 --> 00:16:12,600\nThe brain closes when it stops trying to arrive.\n\n349\n00:16:13,220 --> 00:16:15,500\nSo lie there, in the dark, and rest.\n\n350\n00:16:16,200 --> 00:16:18,480\nResting with closed eyes is not nothing.\n\n351\n00:16:18,980 --> 00:16:20,800\nThe streets open a little.\n\n352\n00:16:20,810 --> 00:16:24,160\nAnd most nights, sleep comes when you stop chasing it.\n\n353\n00:16:24,250 --> 00:16:28,980\n\"Sleep comes when you stop chasing it.\" Ben, that is kind.\n\n354\n00:16:29,040 --> 00:16:30,480\nMy third idea is about the medal.\n\n355\n00:16:30,980 --> 00:16:31,640\nTake it off.\n\n356\n00:16:32,060 --> 00:16:33,720\nFive hours is not strong.\n\n357\n00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:35,080\nIt is a dirty room.\n\n358\n00:16:35,620 --> 00:16:37,040\nSo tonight, choose a time.\n\n359\n00:16:37,600 --> 00:16:38,600\nTen thirty.\n\n360\n00:16:38,640 --> 00:16:39,520\nAnd say it out loud.\n\n361\n00:16:39,910 --> 00:16:43,040\n\"Tonight, ten thirty.\" Not because you are a child.\n\n362\n00:16:43,420 --> 00:16:45,460\nBecause your brain has a lot to wash.\n\n363\n00:16:46,140 --> 00:16:51,200\n\"Your brain has a lot to wash.\" Mia, can I tell you about my worst night?\n\n364\n00:16:51,220 --> 00:16:52,480\nBecause it shows the cost.\n\n365\n00:16:53,100 --> 00:16:53,740\nPlease, Ben.\n\n366\n00:16:54,220 --> 00:16:55,040\nThe worst night.\n\n367\n00:16:56,020 --> 00:16:59,740\nTwo years ago, before a big meeting, I did not sleep, Mia.\n\n368\n00:16:59,800 --> 00:17:00,840\nAt all.\n\n369\n00:17:00,880 --> 00:17:02,600\nI prepared until four in the morning.\n\n370\n00:17:03,110 --> 00:17:06,220\nAnd I went into the meeting with a clean shirt and a dirty brain.\n\n371\n00:17:06,820 --> 00:17:09,360\nAnd I forgot the name of my own project.\n\n372\n00:17:09,740 --> 00:17:10,460\nIn the meeting.\n\n373\n00:17:10,820 --> 00:17:12,080\nMy own project.\n\n374\n00:17:12,780 --> 00:17:15,480\nI stood there, and the word was gone.\n\n375\n00:17:15,580 --> 00:17:17,720\nYou could not say your own project's name, Ben.\n\n376\n00:17:18,340 --> 00:17:19,880\nThat is the cost.\n\n377\n00:17:19,980 --> 00:17:23,660\nAll night of preparing, and the tired brain could not find one word.\n\n378\n00:17:24,140 --> 00:17:27,699\nBecause the waiting room was full, Mia, and nobody had moved the words.\n\n379\n00:17:28,539 --> 00:17:32,180\nNow, before a big day, I stop at eight and sleep at ten.\n\n380\n00:17:32,750 --> 00:17:34,440\nSame rule as our evening episode.\n\n381\n00:17:34,960 --> 00:17:36,540\nAnd the words are there in the morning.\n\n382\n00:17:36,980 --> 00:17:37,440\nAll of them.\n\n383\n00:17:38,460 --> 00:17:39,269\nAnd your last idea?\n\n384\n00:17:39,900 --> 00:17:41,880\nMy last idea is about rest, Ben.\n\n385\n00:17:42,420 --> 00:17:44,200\nBecause rest is bigger than sleep.\n\n386\n00:17:44,800 --> 00:17:47,840\nRest is any time the brain closes its streets a little.\n\n387\n00:17:48,560 --> 00:17:50,140\nSleep is the big one.\n\n388\n00:17:50,180 --> 00:17:53,100\nBut sitting with a cup and looking out of the window is rest.\n\n389\n00:17:53,660 --> 00:17:55,180\nThe slow walk is rest.\n\n390\n00:17:55,700 --> 00:17:58,400\nTen minutes with closed eyes on the bus is rest.\n\n391\n00:17:59,040 --> 00:18:01,120\nSmall doors, all day.\n\n392\n00:18:01,160 --> 00:18:02,380\nAnd the big door at night.\n\n393\n00:18:03,200 --> 00:18:07,630\n\"Small doors all day, and the big door at night.\" Mia, I love that.\n\n394\n00:18:08,150 --> 00:18:11,960\nAnd I want to say something to the parents, the nurses, the night workers.\n\n395\n00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:13,860\nThe people who cannot choose their sleep.\n\n396\n00:18:14,660 --> 00:18:16,290\nI had a year like that, with a baby.\n\n397\n00:18:16,880 --> 00:18:18,380\nAnd the science is not a judge.\n\n398\n00:18:18,920 --> 00:18:19,580\nIt is a map.\n\n399\n00:18:20,380 --> 00:18:24,460\nIf you cannot have seven hours tonight, take the small doors.\n\n400\n00:18:24,520 --> 00:18:26,340\nTen minutes of rest is still rest.\n\n401\n00:18:27,260 --> 00:18:29,240\nSome people are in a hard season, Ben.\n\n402\n00:18:29,320 --> 00:18:30,720\nThank you for saying that.\n\n403\n00:18:30,730 --> 00:18:32,300\nAnd I think that is our message today.\n\n404\n00:18:32,960 --> 00:18:34,500\nSleep is not wasted time.\n\n405\n00:18:35,120 --> 00:18:40,520\nIt is the part of the day where the brain washes itself, sorts the words, and puts the day away.\n\n406\n00:18:41,440 --> 00:18:43,440\nTired is a message, not a medal.\n\n407\n00:18:43,980 --> 00:18:46,220\nSo tonight, close the doors early.\n\n408\n00:18:46,280 --> 00:18:47,260\nMake the night dark.\n\n409\n00:18:47,720 --> 00:18:49,120\nLet the body rest.\n\n410\n00:18:49,160 --> 00:18:51,160\nAnd let the night shift work.\n\n411\n00:18:51,220 --> 00:18:52,400\nLet the night shift work.\n\n412\n00:18:53,360 --> 00:18:58,140\nMia, let's repeat today's important words, slowly, because our listeners want to remember them.\n\n413\n00:18:59,320 --> 00:18:59,640\nSleep.\n\n414\n00:19:00,480 --> 00:19:02,280\nTo rest with eyes closed at night.\n\n415\n00:19:03,500 --> 00:19:04,640\nSleep seven hours.\n\n416\n00:19:05,720 --> 00:19:07,040\nSleep is not wasted.\n\n417\n00:19:08,300 --> 00:19:08,680\nTired.\n\n418\n00:19:09,640 --> 00:19:10,340\nNeeding rest.\n\n419\n00:19:11,360 --> 00:19:12,100\nI am tired.\n\n420\n00:19:12,720 --> 00:19:13,840\nTired is a message.\n\n421\n00:19:15,220 --> 00:19:15,540\nNight.\n\n422\n00:19:16,440 --> 00:19:17,140\nWhen it is dark.\n\n423\n00:19:18,280 --> 00:19:19,940\nAt night, the brain washes.\n\n424\n00:19:20,860 --> 00:19:22,150\nThe night keeps the words.\n\n425\n00:19:23,460 --> 00:19:23,860\nBrain.\n\n426\n00:19:24,700 --> 00:19:26,140\nThe brain washes itself.\n\n427\n00:19:26,920 --> 00:19:27,800\nA clean brain.\n\n428\n00:19:29,140 --> 00:19:29,500\nRest.\n\n429\n00:19:30,400 --> 00:19:31,560\nTen minutes of rest.\n\n430\n00:19:32,400 --> 00:19:33,800\nRest is bigger than sleep.\n\n431\n00:19:35,200 --> 00:19:35,560\nSleep.\n\n432\n00:19:36,360 --> 00:19:36,800\nTired.\n\n433\n00:19:37,720 --> 00:19:38,060\nNight.\n\n434\n00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:39,400\nBrain.\n\n435\n00:19:40,360 --> 00:19:40,780\nRest.\n\n436\n00:19:41,760 --> 00:19:43,070\nFive words, and one number.\n\n437\n00:19:43,560 --> 00:19:43,920\nSeven.\n\n438\n00:19:44,410 --> 00:19:45,560\nAnd one big idea.\n\n439\n00:19:46,200 --> 00:19:48,400\nSleep is not wasted time.\n\n440\n00:19:48,460 --> 00:19:50,740\nNow, Ben, do you have a question for our listeners?\n\n441\n00:19:51,520 --> 00:19:52,420\nYes, Mia.\n\n442\n00:19:52,500 --> 00:19:54,420\nEveryone listening, here is my question.\n\n443\n00:19:55,120 --> 00:19:56,860\nWhat time will you go to bed tonight?\n\n444\n00:19:57,640 --> 00:19:59,860\nNot what time you usually do.\n\n445\n00:19:59,920 --> 00:20:00,320\nTonight.\n\n446\n00:20:01,270 --> 00:20:03,520\nWrite it in the comments in one short sentence.\n\n447\n00:20:04,230 --> 00:20:09,620\n\"Tonight, ten thirty.\" And if you want, add what you are moving out of the bedroom.\n\n448\n00:20:09,680 --> 00:20:10,110\nThe phone?\n\n449\n00:20:10,800 --> 00:20:11,380\nThe laptop?\n\n450\n00:20:12,239 --> 00:20:13,340\nWe read every one.\n\n451\n00:20:14,680 --> 00:20:15,800\nAfter seven hours.\n\n452\n00:20:16,720 --> 00:20:17,880\nAfter seven hours.\n\n453\n00:20:18,380 --> 00:20:21,600\nAnd if this episode helped you, please subscribe to Two Cups English.\n\n454\n00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:23,809\nWe make a new episode every few days.\n\n455\n00:20:24,250 --> 00:20:28,040\nSlow English, simple words, and one true story each time.\n\n456\n00:20:28,540 --> 00:20:31,430\nThe full text of this episode is in the description.\n\n457\n00:20:31,460 --> 00:20:34,040\nRead it tomorrow, after the night has kept the words.\n\n458\n00:20:34,510 --> 00:20:36,040\nThat is the best time to read it.\n\n459\n00:20:36,720 --> 00:20:38,340\nThank you for spending this time with us.\n\n460\n00:20:38,980 --> 00:20:40,280\nMaybe you are in bed right now.\n\n461\n00:20:41,080 --> 00:20:41,300\nGood.\n\n462\n00:20:41,860 --> 00:20:42,800\nThis is the right place.\n\n463\n00:20:43,640 --> 00:20:48,040\nFinish this episode, put the phone away from the pillow, and let the streets open.\n\n464\n00:20:48,840 --> 00:20:50,680\nThe brain has a lot to wash tonight.\n\n465\n00:20:51,380 --> 00:20:51,660\nLet it.\n\n466\n00:20:52,400 --> 00:20:53,300\nYou did enough today.\n\n467\n00:20:54,200 --> 00:20:55,680\nSleep is the rest of the work.\n\n468\n00:20:56,480 --> 00:20:57,720\nThis is Mia.\n\n469\n00:20:57,750 --> 00:20:58,560\nAnd this is Ben.\n\n470\n00:20:59,190 --> 00:21:00,640\nThis was Two Cups English.\n\n471\n00:21:01,100 --> 00:21:01,930\nSleep well.\n\n472\n00:21:01,960 --> 00:21:02,920\nWe will see you next time."},{"code":"CUP10","file":"CUP10.srt","srt":"1\n00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,280\nHello everyone, and welcome to Two Cups English.\n\n2\n00:00:03,800 --> 00:00:06,720\nI'm Mia, and Ben is here with me.\n\n3\n00:00:06,780 --> 00:00:09,560\nThis podcast is for people who are learning English.\n\n4\n00:00:10,220 --> 00:00:11,060\nWe speak slowly.\n\n5\n00:00:11,640 --> 00:00:12,970\nWe use simple words.\n\n6\n00:00:13,560 --> 00:00:17,220\nAnd we repeat the important words many times.\n\n7\n00:00:17,280 --> 00:00:19,380\nToday, our topic is writing.\n\n8\n00:00:20,080 --> 00:00:21,560\nOne line a day.\n\n9\n00:00:21,660 --> 00:00:22,150\nOn paper.\n\n10\n00:00:23,300 --> 00:00:25,170\nBen, did you write anything today?\n\n11\n00:00:26,320 --> 00:00:26,740\nHi Mia.\n\n12\n00:00:27,200 --> 00:00:28,260\nHi everyone.\n\n13\n00:00:28,320 --> 00:00:28,830\nYes, Mia.\n\n14\n00:00:29,300 --> 00:00:31,640\nThree lines, this morning, with my coffee.\n\n15\n00:00:32,870 --> 00:00:35,480\n\"Yesterday: fixed the bike, finally.\n\n16\n00:00:36,460 --> 00:00:38,910\nNow: a little nervous about the meeting.\n\n17\n00:00:39,960 --> 00:00:45,420\nToday: call Dad.\" Three lines, four minutes, every morning.\n\n18\n00:00:46,260 --> 00:00:46,810\nWhat about you?\n\n19\n00:00:47,420 --> 00:00:48,300\nDid you write your line?\n\n20\n00:00:49,160 --> 00:00:49,920\nNot yet, Ben.\n\n21\n00:00:50,589 --> 00:00:52,580\nMy line is at night, before bed.\n\n22\n00:00:53,380 --> 00:00:56,720\nBut I want to tell you about a notebook I found last week.\n\n23\n00:00:56,780 --> 00:00:59,440\nA small one, in a box, from 2019.\n\n24\n00:01:00,280 --> 00:01:03,560\nAnd on every page, there was one line on the paper.\n\n25\n00:01:04,120 --> 00:01:04,660\nOnly one.\n\n26\n00:01:05,660 --> 00:01:06,500\n\"Rain all day.\n\n27\n00:01:07,080 --> 00:01:09,260\nCalled Mom.\" \"Bad meeting.\n\n28\n00:01:09,580 --> 00:01:10,180\nAte soup.\n\n29\n00:01:10,550 --> 00:01:13,260\nBetter.\" \"Saw a fox on the street.\"\n\n30\n00:01:14,040 --> 00:01:14,940\nA fox, Mia?\n\n31\n00:01:15,480 --> 00:01:16,300\nOn your street?\n\n32\n00:01:16,860 --> 00:01:18,530\nAnd one line, every day?\n\n33\n00:01:19,560 --> 00:01:20,680\nI want to hear the whole notebook.\n\n34\n00:01:21,540 --> 00:01:23,490\nEvery day, Ben, for a year.\n\n35\n00:01:24,260 --> 00:01:27,550\nAnd I sat on the floor and read the whole year in twenty minutes.\n\n36\n00:01:28,220 --> 00:01:29,430\nAnd I cried a little.\n\n37\n00:01:30,140 --> 00:01:31,260\nNot because it was sad.\n\n38\n00:01:31,840 --> 00:01:33,080\nBecause it was mine.\n\n39\n00:01:33,780 --> 00:01:38,000\nA whole year, kept, in three hundred small lines.\n\n40\n00:01:38,780 --> 00:01:39,960\nI had forgotten the fox.\n\n41\n00:01:40,410 --> 00:01:41,540\nThe paper had not.\n\n42\n00:01:42,420 --> 00:01:43,780\n\"I had forgotten the fox.\n\n43\n00:01:44,240 --> 00:01:48,760\nThe paper had not.\" Mia, that is our topic today.\n\n44\n00:01:49,520 --> 00:01:50,399\nSo here is our plan.\n\n45\n00:01:51,240 --> 00:01:54,080\nIn part one, we talk about our own lines.\n\n46\n00:01:54,740 --> 00:01:57,200\nHow we started, and what a line is.\n\n47\n00:01:58,200 --> 00:02:03,370\nIn part two, we ask why writing one line helps the body and the head.\n\n48\n00:02:04,180 --> 00:02:06,640\nAnd I have a true story for that.\n\n49\n00:02:06,720 --> 00:02:11,100\nIn part three, we share simple ideas for tonight's line.\n\n50\n00:02:11,170 --> 00:02:11,920\nDoes that sound good?\n\n51\n00:02:12,740 --> 00:02:13,720\nThat sounds good, Ben.\n\n52\n00:02:14,340 --> 00:02:17,910\nAnd to everyone listening, please do not worry if you miss something.\n\n53\n00:02:18,420 --> 00:02:19,280\nYou will hear it again.\n\n54\n00:02:20,100 --> 00:02:24,020\nYou can listen while you cook, while you walk, or with a pen in your hand.\n\n55\n00:02:25,080 --> 00:02:25,780\nOkay, Ben.\n\n56\n00:02:25,840 --> 00:02:27,220\nPart one.\n\n57\n00:02:27,280 --> 00:02:29,660\nYou asked me why I started, in 2019.\n\n58\n00:02:30,780 --> 00:02:32,240\nDo you want the honest answer?\n\n59\n00:02:33,049 --> 00:02:34,860\nThe honest answer, Mia.\n\n60\n00:02:34,890 --> 00:02:35,410\nAlways.\n\n61\n00:02:36,200 --> 00:02:39,060\nOur listeners like the honest one better than the nice one.\n\n62\n00:02:40,360 --> 00:02:44,360\nI did not want to, Ben. 2019 was a hard year.\n\n63\n00:02:44,900 --> 00:02:45,880\nMy father was sick.\n\n64\n00:02:46,420 --> 00:02:47,320\nWork was heavy.\n\n65\n00:02:47,940 --> 00:02:51,980\nAnd I carried everything in my head, all day and all night.\n\n66\n00:02:52,680 --> 00:02:55,740\nAnd a friend said, \"Write one line before bed.\n\n67\n00:02:56,200 --> 00:02:57,260\nJust one.\n\n68\n00:02:57,340 --> 00:03:02,049\nWhat happened, and how you felt.\" And I said, \"I am not a writer.\"\n\n69\n00:03:03,470 --> 00:03:08,300\n\"I am not a writer.\" Mia, I said the same thing for thirty years.\n\n70\n00:03:09,180 --> 00:03:10,440\nI hated writing at school.\n\n71\n00:03:11,280 --> 00:03:11,769\nEssays.\n\n72\n00:03:12,320 --> 00:03:12,920\nRed pen.\n\n73\n00:03:13,670 --> 00:03:18,060\n\"Be more clear.\" So I did not write anything that was not an email.\n\n74\n00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:19,710\nWhat did your friend say?\n\n75\n00:03:20,880 --> 00:03:25,800\nShe said, \"Nobody is a writer for one line, Mia.\" And she was right.\n\n76\n00:03:26,700 --> 00:03:28,780\nSo that night, I wrote one line.\n\n77\n00:03:29,990 --> 00:03:30,580\n\"Hospital.\n\n78\n00:03:31,360 --> 00:03:31,860\nTired.\n\n79\n00:03:32,670 --> 00:03:36,390\nDad smiled once.\" Eight words.\n\n80\n00:03:37,140 --> 00:03:37,850\nAnd I slept.\n\n81\n00:03:38,600 --> 00:03:41,080\nFor the first time in weeks, I slept.\n\n82\n00:03:41,750 --> 00:03:45,860\nThe paper held it, so my head did not have to.\n\n83\n00:03:45,870 --> 00:03:50,900\n\"The paper held it, so my head did not have to.\" Mia, that is how it works.\n\n84\n00:03:51,630 --> 00:03:55,080\nAnd I started three years ago, with three lines every morning.\n\n85\n00:03:55,750 --> 00:03:57,020\nNot for anyone.\n\n86\n00:03:57,080 --> 00:03:57,440\nFor me.\n\n87\n00:03:58,300 --> 00:04:01,900\nAnd the strange thing is, the day feels different after three lines.\n\n88\n00:04:02,380 --> 00:04:02,820\nSorted.\n\n89\n00:04:03,480 --> 00:04:05,560\nLike the bed is made, but inside.\n\n90\n00:04:06,720 --> 00:04:07,500\nSorted, Ben.\n\n91\n00:04:08,020 --> 00:04:08,760\nI like that word.\n\n92\n00:04:09,460 --> 00:04:12,700\nBut three lines every morning, for three years.\n\n93\n00:04:13,220 --> 00:04:14,260\nDid you ever miss a day?\n\n94\n00:04:14,980 --> 00:04:16,500\nWhat happens on the days you miss?\n\n95\n00:04:17,760 --> 00:04:18,600\nMany days, Mia.\n\n96\n00:04:19,160 --> 00:04:20,560\nAnd nothing terrible happens.\n\n97\n00:04:20,920 --> 00:04:21,820\nThat is important.\n\n98\n00:04:22,390 --> 00:04:23,900\nThe paper does not punish you.\n\n99\n00:04:24,700 --> 00:04:27,320\nThe next morning, I write, \"Missed two days.\n\n100\n00:04:27,640 --> 00:04:30,720\nFelt busy.\" That is a line.\n\n101\n00:04:30,750 --> 00:04:32,380\nThe missed days become the line.\n\n102\n00:04:33,060 --> 00:04:35,240\nThe notebook is not a teacher with a red pen.\n\n103\n00:04:35,760 --> 00:04:37,000\nIt is a friend who waits.\n\n104\n00:04:37,980 --> 00:04:40,640\nLike the bed is made, but inside, Ben.\n\n105\n00:04:41,680 --> 00:04:45,660\nSo let me say what a line is, because people imagine something big.\n\n106\n00:04:46,300 --> 00:04:47,680\nA line is not a poem.\n\n107\n00:04:47,980 --> 00:04:48,980\nIt is not a story.\n\n108\n00:04:49,460 --> 00:04:51,420\nIt is one sentence about the day.\n\n109\n00:04:52,250 --> 00:04:54,750\n\"Rain all day.\" That is a line.\n\n110\n00:04:55,760 --> 00:04:56,800\n\"Fight with my sister.\n\n111\n00:04:57,420 --> 00:04:59,680\nFeel bad.\" That is a line.\n\n112\n00:05:00,440 --> 00:05:01,560\nAnd the feeling part, Mia?\n\n113\n00:05:02,150 --> 00:05:03,840\nBecause the line has two halves.\n\n114\n00:05:04,360 --> 00:05:06,020\nWhat happened, and how you felt.\n\n115\n00:05:06,820 --> 00:05:08,660\nThe first half is easy.\n\n116\n00:05:08,700 --> 00:05:10,620\nThe second half is the one people skip.\n\n117\n00:05:11,430 --> 00:05:13,600\nThe second half is the medicine, Ben.\n\n118\n00:05:14,670 --> 00:05:15,160\n\"Rain.\n\n119\n00:05:15,880 --> 00:05:18,470\nCalled Mom.\" That is what happened.\n\n120\n00:05:19,030 --> 00:05:19,960\nBut how did I feel?\n\n121\n00:05:20,620 --> 00:05:21,150\nTired?\n\n122\n00:05:21,800 --> 00:05:22,180\nWarm?\n\n123\n00:05:22,920 --> 00:05:23,300\nSad?\n\n124\n00:05:24,240 --> 00:05:26,800\nThe feeling word turns the list into a line.\n\n125\n00:05:27,500 --> 00:05:30,559\nAnd I noticed that I did not have many feeling words at first.\n\n126\n00:05:31,129 --> 00:05:31,640\nTired.\n\n127\n00:05:31,960 --> 00:05:32,280\nOkay.\n\n128\n00:05:33,120 --> 00:05:33,520\nBad.\n\n129\n00:05:34,180 --> 00:05:35,840\nThree words for a whole year.\n\n130\n00:05:36,620 --> 00:05:38,940\nThree words for a whole year of feelings, Mia.\n\n131\n00:05:39,680 --> 00:05:41,640\nTired, okay, bad.\n\n132\n00:05:42,580 --> 00:05:44,580\nI think most of us have three.\n\n133\n00:05:44,660 --> 00:05:48,180\nTired, okay, bad, and that is the whole year.\n\n134\n00:05:49,150 --> 00:05:49,480\nAnd then?\n\n135\n00:05:50,400 --> 00:05:52,380\nAnd then the notebook taught me more, Ben.\n\n136\n00:05:53,140 --> 00:05:53,640\nSlowly.\n\n137\n00:05:54,760 --> 00:05:55,140\nHeavy.\n\n138\n00:05:56,020 --> 00:05:56,400\nLight.\n\n139\n00:05:57,380 --> 00:05:57,820\nWarm.\n\n140\n00:05:58,800 --> 00:05:59,300\nSmall.\n\n141\n00:06:00,160 --> 00:06:00,680\nProud.\n\n142\n00:06:01,600 --> 00:06:02,200\nJealous.\n\n143\n00:06:03,060 --> 00:06:04,220\nNot from a book.\n\n144\n00:06:04,260 --> 00:06:06,420\nFrom needing a word for that day.\n\n145\n00:06:06,480 --> 00:06:07,990\nThe paper made me find the word.\n\n146\n00:06:08,580 --> 00:06:10,480\nAnd the word made the feeling smaller.\n\n147\n00:06:11,290 --> 00:06:15,480\n\"A word is smaller than a worry.\" Mia, that is exactly it.\n\n148\n00:06:16,320 --> 00:06:21,580\nLet me tell you how my three lines work, because they are not about feelings, mostly.\n\n149\n00:06:21,660 --> 00:06:23,860\nLine one: what happened yesterday.\n\n150\n00:06:24,800 --> 00:06:27,129\nLine two: what I feel right now.\n\n151\n00:06:28,120 --> 00:06:30,840\nLine three: one thing I want today.\n\n152\n00:06:31,760 --> 00:06:32,380\nFour minutes.\n\n153\n00:06:32,940 --> 00:06:34,880\nAnd the middle line is the important one.\n\n154\n00:06:35,680 --> 00:06:36,720\nThe middle line, Ben?\n\n155\n00:06:37,180 --> 00:06:37,740\nThe feeling?\n\n156\n00:06:38,360 --> 00:06:38,909\nWhy that one?\n\n157\n00:06:39,740 --> 00:06:41,700\nBecause that is the one I would skip, Mia.\n\n158\n00:06:42,449 --> 00:06:45,840\n\"What do I feel right now?\" I do not want to write it.\n\n159\n00:06:46,540 --> 00:06:48,060\nAnd that is why I must.\n\n160\n00:06:48,990 --> 00:06:52,640\nThe line I do not want to write is the line that helps.\n\n161\n00:06:53,640 --> 00:06:55,540\nOkay, so that was part one.\n\n162\n00:06:55,600 --> 00:06:56,240\nOur lines.\n\n163\n00:06:56,880 --> 00:06:57,720\nNow, part two.\n\n164\n00:06:58,540 --> 00:07:00,840\nWhy does writing one line on paper help?\n\n165\n00:07:01,720 --> 00:07:05,280\nMia, my true story is about a scientist and some students.\n\n166\n00:07:06,200 --> 00:07:07,700\nStudents and a scientist, Ben?\n\n167\n00:07:08,340 --> 00:07:10,640\nI did not know science cared about notebooks.\n\n168\n00:07:11,060 --> 00:07:11,740\nWhat did they write?\n\n169\n00:07:12,520 --> 00:07:13,520\nOkay, part two.\n\n170\n00:07:14,240 --> 00:07:15,440\nThis is a true story.\n\n171\n00:07:16,160 --> 00:07:22,460\nIn 1986, a psychologist named James Pennebaker, at the University of Texas, did a simple experiment.\n\n172\n00:07:23,240 --> 00:07:25,460\nHe asked students to write.\n\n173\n00:07:25,620 --> 00:07:27,820\nFifteen minutes a day, for four days.\n\n174\n00:07:28,740 --> 00:07:30,240\nOne group wrote about their day.\n\n175\n00:07:31,040 --> 00:07:31,680\nWhat they did.\n\n176\n00:07:32,150 --> 00:07:34,500\nThe bus, the class, the food.\n\n177\n00:07:35,300 --> 00:07:36,260\nAnd the other group, Ben?\n\n178\n00:07:36,800 --> 00:07:39,680\nThe one that did not write about the bus and the food?\n\n179\n00:07:39,720 --> 00:07:40,680\nWhat did they write about?\n\n180\n00:07:41,430 --> 00:07:43,820\nThe other group wrote about their feelings, Mia.\n\n181\n00:07:44,600 --> 00:07:45,360\nThe hard things.\n\n182\n00:07:46,160 --> 00:07:47,779\nThings they had not told anyone.\n\n183\n00:07:48,760 --> 00:07:50,560\nFifteen minutes, four days.\n\n184\n00:07:51,440 --> 00:07:52,900\nAnd then Pennebaker waited.\n\n185\n00:07:53,860 --> 00:07:54,820\nSix months.\n\n186\n00:07:55,560 --> 00:07:57,020\nAnd he looked at one thing.\n\n187\n00:07:57,840 --> 00:07:59,920\nHow often did the students go to the doctor?\n\n188\n00:08:00,840 --> 00:08:01,740\nThe doctor, Ben?\n\n189\n00:08:02,340 --> 00:08:03,460\nNot their grades?\n\n190\n00:08:03,740 --> 00:08:04,500\nNot their mood?\n\n191\n00:08:04,980 --> 00:08:05,570\nThe doctor?\n\n192\n00:08:06,320 --> 00:08:07,940\nThat is a strange thing to measure.\n\n193\n00:08:08,840 --> 00:08:10,380\nThe doctor, Mia.\n\n194\n00:08:10,390 --> 00:08:13,500\nAnd the students who wrote about their feelings went to the doctor less.\n\n195\n00:08:14,280 --> 00:08:15,070\nMuch less.\n\n196\n00:08:15,840 --> 00:08:17,480\nFor six months.\n\n197\n00:08:17,620 --> 00:08:23,520\nFour days of writing, fifteen minutes each, and the body was healthier for half a year.\n\n198\n00:08:23,560 --> 00:08:27,260\nPennebaker did this study many times after that, with different people.\n\n199\n00:08:27,960 --> 00:08:29,639\nAnd the result kept coming back.\n\n200\n00:08:30,640 --> 00:08:34,700\nFour days of writing, and the body was healthier for six months, Ben.\n\n201\n00:08:35,460 --> 00:08:35,880\nWhy?\n\n202\n00:08:36,480 --> 00:08:38,120\nWhat does the paper do to the body?\n\n203\n00:08:39,060 --> 00:08:42,520\nPennebaker thinks the feeling that stays inside is work, Mia.\n\n204\n00:08:43,350 --> 00:08:44,330\nThe body holds it.\n\n205\n00:08:45,140 --> 00:08:46,500\nAll day, all night.\n\n206\n00:08:47,140 --> 00:08:48,190\nHolding is work.\n\n207\n00:08:49,060 --> 00:08:52,400\nAnd when you write it, the feeling goes outside, on paper.\n\n208\n00:08:53,330 --> 00:08:54,589\nThe body can put it down.\n\n209\n00:08:55,520 --> 00:08:59,300\nThe feeling on paper is smaller than the feeling in the head.\n\n210\n00:08:59,330 --> 00:09:05,480\n\"The feeling on paper is smaller than the feeling in the head.\" Ben, that is what I found in 2019.\n\n211\n00:09:06,130 --> 00:09:06,709\n\"Hospital.\n\n212\n00:09:07,260 --> 00:09:07,770\nTired.\n\n213\n00:09:08,300 --> 00:09:12,500\nDad smiled once.\" In my head, that day was a storm.\n\n214\n00:09:13,120 --> 00:09:15,020\nOn paper, it was eight words.\n\n215\n00:09:15,620 --> 00:09:17,020\nAnd the eight words were true.\n\n216\n00:09:17,640 --> 00:09:19,080\nThe storm was also true.\n\n217\n00:09:19,740 --> 00:09:21,840\nBut the eight words were the part I could hold.\n\n218\n00:09:22,660 --> 00:09:25,380\n\"The part I could hold.\" Yes, Mia.\n\n219\n00:09:26,120 --> 00:09:28,620\nThe paper does not make the day smaller.\n\n220\n00:09:28,680 --> 00:09:30,240\nIt makes it a size you can hold.\n\n221\n00:09:31,220 --> 00:09:35,340\nCan I ask about the days when the feeling is too big for a line?\n\n222\n00:09:35,420 --> 00:09:35,880\nA death.\n\n223\n00:09:36,600 --> 00:09:37,190\nA breakup.\n\n224\n00:09:38,080 --> 00:09:40,890\nOn those days, one line feels like an insult.\n\n225\n00:09:42,200 --> 00:09:43,080\nIt does, Ben.\n\n226\n00:09:43,780 --> 00:09:46,070\nWhen my father died, \"Dad died.\n\n227\n00:09:46,480 --> 00:09:49,039\nFeel empty\" was too small.\n\n228\n00:09:49,080 --> 00:09:50,810\nAnd on that night, I wrote more.\n\n229\n00:09:51,440 --> 00:09:52,980\nNot because I should.\n\n230\n00:09:53,020 --> 00:09:54,460\nBecause it came.\n\n231\n00:09:54,560 --> 00:09:55,900\nFour pages.\n\n232\n00:09:55,980 --> 00:09:57,400\nI did not plan it.\n\n233\n00:09:57,430 --> 00:10:01,740\nThe line had made a road, and the big feeling used the road.\n\n234\n00:10:02,610 --> 00:10:06,990\n\"The line keeps the door open for the page.\" Mia, that is beautiful.\n\n235\n00:10:07,780 --> 00:10:12,240\nThree months of one line, and then, on the hard night, the road was there.\n\n236\n00:10:13,260 --> 00:10:17,060\nIf there had been no notebook that night, the feeling would have had nowhere to go.\n\n237\n00:10:18,020 --> 00:10:19,020\nNowhere to go, Ben.\n\n238\n00:10:19,640 --> 00:10:24,420\nA head without paper is a room where things come in and nothing goes out.\n\n239\n00:10:25,420 --> 00:10:26,700\nDid you ever stop writing?\n\n240\n00:10:27,260 --> 00:10:27,960\nWhat happened?\n\n241\n00:10:29,480 --> 00:10:30,420\nLast winter, Mia.\n\n242\n00:10:31,040 --> 00:10:35,900\nI was busy, I was traveling, and the notebook stayed in the bag for five weeks.\n\n243\n00:10:36,840 --> 00:10:38,520\nAnd I did not notice anything at first.\n\n244\n00:10:39,260 --> 00:10:41,640\nThen, week three, the cloud came.\n\n245\n00:10:42,580 --> 00:10:44,860\nSmall worries that did not leave.\n\n246\n00:10:44,920 --> 00:10:45,570\nBad sleep.\n\n247\n00:10:46,420 --> 00:10:47,380\nAnd I thought it was work.\n\n248\n00:10:48,400 --> 00:10:49,240\nIt was not work.\n\n249\n00:10:50,040 --> 00:10:52,900\nIt was five weeks of feelings with nowhere to go.\n\n250\n00:10:54,080 --> 00:10:56,540\nFive weeks of feelings with nowhere to go.\n\n251\n00:10:57,250 --> 00:10:59,080\nAnd when you opened the notebook again, Ben?\n\n252\n00:11:00,440 --> 00:11:04,020\nThree lines, and the cloud got smaller the same morning, Mia.\n\n253\n00:11:04,800 --> 00:11:05,350\nNot gone.\n\n254\n00:11:06,240 --> 00:11:06,600\nSmaller.\n\n255\n00:11:07,640 --> 00:11:09,600\nThe paper is not a diary.\n\n256\n00:11:09,640 --> 00:11:11,540\nIt is where the body puts things down.\n\n257\n00:11:12,980 --> 00:11:14,220\nOkay, so that was part two.\n\n258\n00:11:14,640 --> 00:11:15,980\nWhy one line helps.\n\n259\n00:11:16,700 --> 00:11:19,980\nWe said the feeling on paper is smaller than the feeling in the head.\n\n260\n00:11:20,780 --> 00:11:23,860\nAnd we said the line keeps the door open for the page.\n\n261\n00:11:24,880 --> 00:11:26,460\nNow, part three.\n\n262\n00:11:26,580 --> 00:11:29,060\nSimple ideas for tonight's line.\n\n263\n00:11:29,120 --> 00:11:30,120\nMia, you go first.\n\n264\n00:11:31,020 --> 00:11:32,280\nOkay, part three.\n\n265\n00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:35,080\nMy first idea is about the fear, Ben.\n\n266\n00:11:35,620 --> 00:11:38,020\nBecause the fear stopped me for years.\n\n267\n00:11:38,540 --> 00:11:44,750\nThe fear says, \"I have nothing to write.\" And here is the answer, and it is the whole trick.\n\n268\n00:11:45,420 --> 00:11:48,120\nIf you have nothing to write, write that.\n\n269\n00:11:48,180 --> 00:11:49,120\nOn any paper.\n\n270\n00:11:49,769 --> 00:11:50,710\n\"Nothing to write.\n\n271\n00:11:51,080 --> 00:11:52,880\nTired.\" That is a line.\n\n272\n00:11:53,500 --> 00:11:53,990\n\"Nothing.\n\n273\n00:11:54,400 --> 00:11:57,460\nQuiet day.\" That is a line.\n\n274\n00:11:58,000 --> 00:11:58,430\n\"Nothing.\n\n275\n00:11:58,960 --> 00:12:00,600\nQuiet day.\" is a good line, Mia.\n\n276\n00:12:01,720 --> 00:12:03,780\nMy first idea is two rules.\n\n277\n00:12:04,680 --> 00:12:07,109\nRule one: no good sentences.\n\n278\n00:12:07,960 --> 00:12:09,120\nThe line can be ugly.\n\n279\n00:12:09,840 --> 00:12:12,780\nNo verb, no capital letter, spelling wrong.\n\n280\n00:12:13,240 --> 00:12:14,260\nNobody is grading it.\n\n281\n00:12:15,300 --> 00:12:17,400\nRule two: nobody reads it.\n\n282\n00:12:18,140 --> 00:12:21,840\nNot my wife, not my mother, not me, for a month.\n\n283\n00:12:22,800 --> 00:12:24,780\nGood is the enemy of one line.\n\n284\n00:12:25,860 --> 00:12:31,980\n\"Good is the enemy of one line.\" Ben, my second idea is for our listeners learning English.\n\n285\n00:12:32,480 --> 00:12:36,840\nBecause this is one of the best English exercises, and it costs nothing.\n\n286\n00:12:37,560 --> 00:12:39,280\nOne line a day, in English.\n\n287\n00:12:39,680 --> 00:12:40,600\nUgly English.\n\n288\n00:12:41,300 --> 00:12:42,220\n\"Today rain.\n\n289\n00:12:42,660 --> 00:12:43,360\nI tired.\n\n290\n00:12:43,760 --> 00:12:46,140\nEat soup.\" That is English.\n\n291\n00:12:46,680 --> 00:12:48,840\nReal English, about your real day.\n\n292\n00:12:49,700 --> 00:12:51,580\nReal English about a real day, Mia.\n\n293\n00:12:52,420 --> 00:12:53,640\nAnd here is what happens.\n\n294\n00:12:54,640 --> 00:12:57,259\nAfter a month, the line gets a little longer.\n\n295\n00:12:58,280 --> 00:13:01,200\nAfter three months, the line has a verb in the right place.\n\n296\n00:13:02,560 --> 00:13:03,919\nAnd you did not study.\n\n297\n00:13:04,820 --> 00:13:06,439\nYou did not notice when it changed.\n\n298\n00:13:07,360 --> 00:13:09,180\nThat is how it works with everything.\n\n299\n00:13:10,620 --> 00:13:14,180\nA friend who waits, Ben, and a line that grows by itself.\n\n300\n00:13:15,140 --> 00:13:18,120\nCan I ask what the English learners should write about?\n\n301\n00:13:18,180 --> 00:13:19,880\nBecause \"the day\" is big.\n\n302\n00:13:20,920 --> 00:13:21,660\nThree things, Mia.\n\n303\n00:13:22,500 --> 00:13:23,040\nWhat I ate.\n\n304\n00:13:23,920 --> 00:13:24,830\nOne person I saw.\n\n305\n00:13:25,820 --> 00:13:26,540\nOne feeling.\n\n306\n00:13:27,860 --> 00:13:28,240\n\"Rice.\n\n307\n00:13:28,900 --> 00:13:29,380\nMy boss.\n\n308\n00:13:30,200 --> 00:13:34,120\nNervous.\" That is a line in English, and it is true.\n\n309\n00:13:35,020 --> 00:13:37,400\nFood, a person, a feeling.\n\n310\n00:13:38,320 --> 00:13:39,820\nEvery day has those three.\n\n311\n00:13:40,740 --> 00:13:43,320\nAnd after a month, the three words become a sentence.\n\n312\n00:13:44,310 --> 00:13:49,680\n\"I ate rice with my boss, and I was nervous.\" You did not study that sentence.\n\n313\n00:13:50,420 --> 00:13:51,060\nYou lived it.\n\n314\n00:13:52,180 --> 00:13:52,870\nYou lived it.\n\n315\n00:13:53,360 --> 00:13:55,360\nAnd one more thing for the learners, Ben.\n\n316\n00:13:55,900 --> 00:13:57,580\nDo not correct the line.\n\n317\n00:13:58,100 --> 00:13:58,830\nNot that day.\n\n318\n00:13:59,540 --> 00:14:01,400\nWrite it wrong and close the book.\n\n319\n00:14:01,870 --> 00:14:04,020\nAnd once a month, read the old lines.\n\n320\n00:14:04,580 --> 00:14:07,780\nYou will see your own mistakes, and you will fix them yourself.\n\n321\n00:14:08,420 --> 00:14:11,960\nMy old ugly lines taught me more than any red pen.\n\n322\n00:14:13,680 --> 00:14:16,500\nMore than any red pen, Mia.\n\n323\n00:14:16,580 --> 00:14:19,580\nMy second idea is about the middle of the day.\n\n324\n00:14:19,720 --> 00:14:21,730\nI keep a small paper in my pocket at work.\n\n325\n00:14:22,330 --> 00:14:29,140\nAnd when something happens, a hard email, a small win, a strange feeling, I write four words on it.\n\n326\n00:14:30,250 --> 00:14:30,640\n\"Angry.\n\n327\n00:14:31,460 --> 00:14:35,620\nEmail from Tom.\" And the four words hold it until night.\n\n328\n00:14:36,960 --> 00:14:39,340\nFour words that hold it until night, Ben.\n\n329\n00:14:40,220 --> 00:14:43,980\nMy third idea is the evening line, because that is my main one.\n\n330\n00:14:44,660 --> 00:14:49,840\nBefore bed, after the five small things from our evening episode, I write the line.\n\n331\n00:14:50,440 --> 00:14:51,080\nTwo parts.\n\n332\n00:14:51,560 --> 00:14:53,200\nWhat happened, and how I felt.\n\n333\n00:14:54,090 --> 00:14:54,840\n\"Long day.\n\n334\n00:14:55,300 --> 00:14:56,230\nFixed the printer.\n\n335\n00:14:56,820 --> 00:15:01,060\nFelt useful.\" And then I close the book, and the day is kept.\n\n336\n00:15:02,120 --> 00:15:07,860\n\"The day is kept.\" Mia, and I noticed that the line is kinder than the day.\n\n337\n00:15:07,940 --> 00:15:10,160\nWhen I write \"Fixed the printer.\n\n338\n00:15:10,200 --> 00:15:13,780\nFelt useful,\" the day was mostly the printer not working.\n\n339\n00:15:14,480 --> 00:15:16,240\nBut the line remembers the fixing.\n\n340\n00:15:17,160 --> 00:15:18,740\nThat is Franklin's question again.\n\n341\n00:15:19,600 --> 00:15:20,480\nWhat went well.\n\n342\n00:15:21,640 --> 00:15:23,520\nThe line is kinder than the day.\n\n343\n00:15:24,140 --> 00:15:25,460\nAnd my morning line, Ben?\n\n344\n00:15:26,040 --> 00:15:27,140\nYou do it in the morning.\n\n345\n00:15:27,600 --> 00:15:28,260\nIs it different?\n\n346\n00:15:29,400 --> 00:15:29,920\nIt is, Mia.\n\n347\n00:15:30,900 --> 00:15:32,320\nAt night, you write what happened.\n\n348\n00:15:33,100 --> 00:15:35,060\nIn the morning, I write what I want.\n\n349\n00:15:36,129 --> 00:15:40,690\n\"Today: finish the page, call Dad.\" One line.\n\n350\n00:15:41,680 --> 00:15:44,580\nIt is the first bird from our list episode, on paper.\n\n351\n00:15:45,600 --> 00:15:48,160\nTwo doors, two lines, two minutes.\n\n352\n00:15:49,320 --> 00:15:50,400\nNight keeps the day.\n\n353\n00:15:50,980 --> 00:15:52,340\nMorning starts the next one.\n\n354\n00:15:53,120 --> 00:15:56,160\nTwo doors, two lines, two minutes.\n\n355\n00:15:56,860 --> 00:16:00,980\nLet me talk about honesty, Ben, because that is the hard part of the line.\n\n356\n00:16:01,580 --> 00:16:10,480\nIt is easy to write \"fine day.\" It is hard to write \"I was jealous of Anna today.\" But the true line is the one that helps.\n\n357\n00:16:11,060 --> 00:16:15,280\nPennebaker's students wrote the true things, and their bodies got better.\n\n358\n00:16:16,540 --> 00:16:18,580\nThe true line is the one that helps, Mia.\n\n359\n00:16:19,420 --> 00:16:23,200\nAnd for the people who are afraid of the true line, because someone might read it.\n\n360\n00:16:23,840 --> 00:16:24,720\nNobody reads it.\n\n361\n00:16:25,320 --> 00:16:26,240\nThat is rule two.\n\n362\n00:16:27,220 --> 00:16:30,780\nAnd if that is not enough, here is what Pennebaker told his students.\n\n363\n00:16:31,540 --> 00:16:33,400\nWrite it, and then throw it away.\n\n364\n00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:34,400\nBurn it.\n\n365\n00:16:35,100 --> 00:16:37,600\nThe paper only needs to exist while you write.\n\n366\n00:16:39,370 --> 00:16:44,800\n\"The paper only needs to exist while you write.\" Ben, that takes the fear away.\n\n367\n00:16:45,520 --> 00:16:46,780\nAnd what about the phone?\n\n368\n00:16:47,160 --> 00:16:49,340\nPeople ask, \"Can I do this on my phone?\"\n\n369\n00:16:50,580 --> 00:16:51,360\nYou can, Mia.\n\n370\n00:16:51,660 --> 00:16:54,070\nBut I think paper is better, for one reason.\n\n371\n00:16:54,750 --> 00:16:56,580\nThe hand is slow.\n\n372\n00:16:56,620 --> 00:17:01,120\nWhen I type, the words come as fast as the feeling, and the feeling stays big.\n\n373\n00:17:01,960 --> 00:17:07,220\nWhen I write by hand, the hand is slow, and the feeling has to slow down to fit the hand.\n\n374\n00:17:08,120 --> 00:17:09,600\nPaper is slow on purpose.\n\n375\n00:17:11,320 --> 00:17:12,860\nSlow on purpose, Ben.\n\n376\n00:17:13,320 --> 00:17:15,560\nAnd there is one more thing about paper.\n\n377\n00:17:15,600 --> 00:17:17,179\nThe paper is patient.\n\n378\n00:17:17,220 --> 00:17:18,180\nIt does not answer.\n\n379\n00:17:18,880 --> 00:17:23,560\nWhen I tell a friend a feeling, the friend answers, and now I have two feelings.\n\n380\n00:17:24,140 --> 00:17:25,820\nThe paper only listens.\n\n381\n00:17:27,120 --> 00:17:29,220\nThe paper only listens, Mia.\n\n382\n00:17:29,260 --> 00:17:29,520\nYes.\n\n383\n00:17:30,140 --> 00:17:36,290\nAnd it never says, \"Have you tried this?\" It takes the feeling and keeps it, and asks nothing back.\n\n384\n00:17:37,250 --> 00:17:38,840\nThat is why the students got better.\n\n385\n00:17:39,560 --> 00:17:41,240\nNot because someone read it.\n\n386\n00:17:41,300 --> 00:17:44,530\nBecause the feeling left the body and landed somewhere quiet.\n\n387\n00:17:46,220 --> 00:17:47,760\nSomewhere quiet.\n\n388\n00:17:47,800 --> 00:17:50,180\nAnd the phone has other things on it, Ben.\n\n389\n00:17:50,220 --> 00:17:54,220\nThe line on the phone sits next to messages and news and the loud room.\n\n390\n00:17:54,820 --> 00:17:56,940\nThe line on paper sits next to nothing.\n\n391\n00:17:57,540 --> 00:18:00,640\nJust the last line, and the empty space for tomorrow.\n\n392\n00:18:01,280 --> 00:18:03,900\nThe notebook is empty every night, waiting.\n\n393\n00:18:05,220 --> 00:18:11,080\n\"The notebook is empty every night, waiting.\" Mia, my third idea is the smallest version.\n\n394\n00:18:11,740 --> 00:18:13,140\nFor the nights when you are finished.\n\n395\n00:18:13,780 --> 00:18:16,120\nNo pen, no book, no words.\n\n396\n00:18:16,900 --> 00:18:18,299\nHere is the smallest line.\n\n397\n00:18:18,940 --> 00:18:19,519\nOne word.\n\n398\n00:18:20,260 --> 00:18:25,800\n\"Tired.\" \"Good.\" \"Scared.\" One word on any paper.\n\n399\n00:18:26,500 --> 00:18:27,010\nA receipt.\n\n400\n00:18:27,700 --> 00:18:28,740\nThe back of an envelope.\n\n401\n00:18:29,480 --> 00:18:31,350\nThat is still showing up to the page.\n\n402\n00:18:33,080 --> 00:18:34,680\nShowing up to the page, Ben.\n\n403\n00:18:35,180 --> 00:18:38,680\nAnd after a month of showing up, the notebook becomes a place.\n\n404\n00:18:39,080 --> 00:18:40,320\nNot a task.\n\n405\n00:18:40,380 --> 00:18:41,700\nA place I go at night.\n\n406\n00:18:42,180 --> 00:18:44,260\nLike the window in the morning, or the tea.\n\n407\n00:18:45,080 --> 00:18:47,080\nOn hard nights, I want to go there.\n\n408\n00:18:47,660 --> 00:18:49,040\nThe first week is a chore.\n\n409\n00:18:49,540 --> 00:18:50,960\nThe fourth week is a friend.\n\n410\n00:18:51,680 --> 00:18:53,030\n\"The first week is a chore.\n\n411\n00:18:53,700 --> 00:18:57,879\nThe fourth week is a friend.\" Mia, one more thing, for parents.\n\n412\n00:18:58,480 --> 00:18:59,400\nMy daughter is seven.\n\n413\n00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:03,450\nShe writes one line every night, in her own notebook, with a drawing.\n\n414\n00:19:04,210 --> 00:19:05,020\n\"Played with Leo.\n\n415\n00:19:05,640 --> 00:19:08,520\nHappy.\" And she reads them to us on Sundays.\n\n416\n00:19:09,140 --> 00:19:12,690\nShe has words for her feelings now, because she needed them for the line.\n\n417\n00:19:13,400 --> 00:19:16,180\nLearning what you feel, at any age, Ben.\n\n418\n00:19:16,320 --> 00:19:17,720\nI love that.\n\n419\n00:19:17,730 --> 00:19:20,860\nAnd I want to say something to the people who think they are not writers.\n\n420\n00:19:21,320 --> 00:19:23,940\nBecause I said that, for years.\n\n421\n00:19:23,960 --> 00:19:25,160\nAnd it kept me from the notebook.\n\n422\n00:19:25,760 --> 00:19:30,160\nAnd the notebook was the thing that helped me most in the hardest year of my life.\n\n423\n00:19:31,240 --> 00:19:32,480\nYou are not a writer, Mia.\n\n424\n00:19:33,800 --> 00:19:34,600\nFine.\n\n425\n00:19:34,680 --> 00:19:37,080\nYou are a person with a day, and a pen.\n\n426\n00:19:38,040 --> 00:19:39,680\nAnd that is our message today, I think.\n\n427\n00:19:40,440 --> 00:19:43,140\nFeelings that stay inside are work for the body.\n\n428\n00:19:44,020 --> 00:19:47,510\nWriting them puts them outside, where the body can put them down.\n\n429\n00:19:48,530 --> 00:19:49,600\nOne line is enough.\n\n430\n00:19:50,480 --> 00:19:51,480\nUgly is enough.\n\n431\n00:19:52,400 --> 00:19:53,600\nOne word is enough.\n\n432\n00:19:54,600 --> 00:19:56,860\nIt is the cheapest way to keep a life.\n\n433\n00:19:57,740 --> 00:19:59,600\nThe cheapest way to keep a life.\n\n434\n00:20:00,610 --> 00:20:05,650\nBen, let's repeat today's important words, slowly, because our listeners want to remember them.\n\n435\n00:20:06,870 --> 00:20:07,180\nWrite.\n\n436\n00:20:07,860 --> 00:20:09,300\nTo put words on paper.\n\n437\n00:20:10,540 --> 00:20:11,620\nWrite one line.\n\n438\n00:20:12,780 --> 00:20:13,500\nWrite it ugly.\n\n439\n00:20:14,960 --> 00:20:15,360\nLine.\n\n440\n00:20:15,960 --> 00:20:16,860\nOne sentence.\n\n441\n00:20:17,540 --> 00:20:18,580\nOne line a day.\n\n442\n00:20:19,680 --> 00:20:21,120\nA one-word line.\n\n443\n00:20:22,400 --> 00:20:22,780\nPaper.\n\n444\n00:20:23,280 --> 00:20:24,240\nThe paper holds it.\n\n445\n00:20:24,880 --> 00:20:25,800\nPut it on paper.\n\n446\n00:20:27,380 --> 00:20:27,720\nFeel.\n\n447\n00:20:28,460 --> 00:20:30,020\nTo have an emotion inside.\n\n448\n00:20:30,900 --> 00:20:31,680\nHow did you feel?\n\n449\n00:20:33,080 --> 00:20:33,900\nFeel tired.\n\n450\n00:20:34,840 --> 00:20:35,560\nFeel useful.\n\n451\n00:20:37,160 --> 00:20:37,470\nDay.\n\n452\n00:20:38,200 --> 00:20:39,600\nFrom morning to night.\n\n453\n00:20:40,400 --> 00:20:41,540\nOne line a day.\n\n454\n00:20:42,280 --> 00:20:43,420\nThe day is kept.\n\n455\n00:20:45,000 --> 00:20:45,320\nWrite.\n\n456\n00:20:46,240 --> 00:20:46,580\nLine.\n\n457\n00:20:47,620 --> 00:20:49,060\nPaper.\n\n458\n00:20:49,200 --> 00:20:49,480\nFeel.\n\n459\n00:20:50,690 --> 00:20:50,960\nDay.\n\n460\n00:20:51,940 --> 00:20:53,660\nFive words, and one page.\n\n461\n00:20:54,120 --> 00:20:55,560\nAnd one big idea.\n\n462\n00:20:56,420 --> 00:20:57,840\nWrite one line a day.\n\n463\n00:20:58,980 --> 00:21:01,139\nNow, Mia, do you have a question for our listeners?\n\n464\n00:21:01,960 --> 00:21:02,520\nYes, Ben.\n\n465\n00:21:03,000 --> 00:21:05,100\nEveryone listening, here is my question.\n\n466\n00:21:05,980 --> 00:21:07,340\nWhat is your line for today?\n\n467\n00:21:08,200 --> 00:21:08,720\nRight now.\n\n468\n00:21:09,520 --> 00:21:11,060\nWhat happened, and how you feel.\n\n469\n00:21:11,860 --> 00:21:14,320\nWrite it in the comments in one short sentence.\n\n470\n00:21:15,060 --> 00:21:15,920\nUgly is fine.\n\n471\n00:21:16,690 --> 00:21:17,980\n\"Listened to a podcast.\n\n472\n00:21:18,580 --> 00:21:19,040\nTired.\n\n473\n00:21:19,760 --> 00:21:21,920\nOkay.\" That is a line.\n\n474\n00:21:22,820 --> 00:21:25,980\nWe read every one, and your line becomes our line.\n\n475\n00:21:27,080 --> 00:21:28,619\nYour line becomes our line.\n\n476\n00:21:29,260 --> 00:21:32,740\nAnd if this episode helped you, please subscribe to Two Cups English.\n\n477\n00:21:33,380 --> 00:21:36,180\nWe make a new episode every few days.\n\n478\n00:21:36,260 --> 00:21:40,320\nSlow English, simple words, and one true story each time.\n\n479\n00:21:41,209 --> 00:21:43,589\nThe full text of this episode is in the description.\n\n480\n00:21:44,360 --> 00:21:48,340\nRead it, and then close it, and write one line of your own.\n\n481\n00:21:48,360 --> 00:21:50,160\nThank you for spending this time with us.\n\n482\n00:21:50,800 --> 00:21:51,860\nMaybe you are in bed.\n\n483\n00:21:52,420 --> 00:21:54,340\nMaybe there is a pen somewhere near you.\n\n484\n00:21:55,140 --> 00:21:55,600\nFind it.\n\n485\n00:21:56,100 --> 00:21:57,260\nFind any paper.\n\n486\n00:21:57,860 --> 00:21:59,560\nWrite one line about today.\n\n487\n00:22:00,360 --> 00:22:00,740\nUgly.\n\n488\n00:22:01,400 --> 00:22:01,800\nTrue.\n\n489\n00:22:02,560 --> 00:22:02,940\nShort.\n\n490\n00:22:03,709 --> 00:22:05,220\nAnd then close it and sleep.\n\n491\n00:22:06,040 --> 00:22:07,160\nThe day is kept.\n\n492\n00:22:07,920 --> 00:22:08,740\nYou are kept.\n\n493\n00:22:09,560 --> 00:22:10,180\nOne line.\n\n494\n00:22:11,120 --> 00:22:11,760\nThis is Ben.\n\n495\n00:22:12,420 --> 00:22:13,320\nAnd this is Mia.\n\n496\n00:22:14,140 --> 00:22:15,800\nThis was Two Cups English.\n\n497\n00:22:16,500 --> 00:22:17,460\nWrite one line.\n\n498\n00:22:18,200 --> 00:22:19,230\nWe will see you next time."},{"code":"CUP11","file":"CUP11.srt","srt":"1\n00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,260\nHello everyone, and welcome to Two Cups English.\n\n2\n00:00:03,840 --> 00:00:06,699\nI'm Mia, and Ben is here with me.\n\n3\n00:00:06,780 --> 00:00:09,580\nThis podcast is for people who are learning English.\n\n4\n00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:11,080\nWe speak slowly.\n\n5\n00:00:11,620 --> 00:00:12,980\nWe use simple words.\n\n6\n00:00:13,560 --> 00:00:16,419\nAnd we repeat the important words many times.\n\n7\n00:00:17,300 --> 00:00:19,460\nToday, our topic is thank you.\n\n8\n00:00:20,300 --> 00:00:22,980\nSaying thank you today, not tomorrow.\n\n9\n00:00:24,200 --> 00:00:25,900\nBen, who did you thank today?\n\n10\n00:00:27,040 --> 00:00:27,900\nHi Mia.\n\n11\n00:00:27,960 --> 00:00:28,500\nHi everyone.\n\n12\n00:00:29,260 --> 00:00:30,960\nToday, I thanked two people.\n\n13\n00:00:31,720 --> 00:00:34,800\nThe bus driver, and my wife, for the coffee.\n\n14\n00:00:36,200 --> 00:00:37,160\nBut I have a bigger one.\n\n15\n00:00:37,880 --> 00:00:41,080\nThere is a message I have wanted to send for years.\n\n16\n00:00:41,980 --> 00:00:42,940\nTo my old boss.\n\n17\n00:00:44,020 --> 00:00:44,960\nI never sent it.\n\n18\n00:00:46,060 --> 00:00:46,800\nWhat about you, Mia?\n\n19\n00:00:48,460 --> 00:00:49,940\nI have a letter, Ben.\n\n20\n00:00:50,040 --> 00:00:50,940\nIt is in my desk.\n\n21\n00:00:51,500 --> 00:00:53,580\nI wrote it two years ago.\n\n22\n00:00:53,640 --> 00:00:55,640\nIt is for my old English teacher, Mrs.\n\n23\n00:00:55,740 --> 00:00:56,040\nPark.\n\n24\n00:00:56,680 --> 00:00:58,920\nShe is the reason I can do this podcast.\n\n25\n00:00:59,640 --> 00:01:02,920\nShe told me, at fifteen, \"Your English is good.\n\n26\n00:01:03,260 --> 00:01:05,820\nKeep going.\" Nobody else said that.\n\n27\n00:01:06,600 --> 00:01:11,600\n\"Keep going.\" Mia, that is a big sentence for a fifteen-year-old.\n\n28\n00:01:12,580 --> 00:01:13,140\nAnd the letter?\n\n29\n00:01:14,120 --> 00:01:15,420\nTwo years in the desk?\n\n30\n00:01:16,400 --> 00:01:17,340\nTwo years, Ben.\n\n31\n00:01:17,720 --> 00:01:18,520\nTwo pages.\n\n32\n00:01:19,080 --> 00:01:20,060\nI wrote it to thank her.\n\n33\n00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:21,840\nAnd I never sent it.\n\n34\n00:01:22,360 --> 00:01:23,660\nBecause it felt strange.\n\n35\n00:01:24,200 --> 00:01:24,780\nToo much.\n\n36\n00:01:25,580 --> 00:01:30,800\nTwenty years later, a letter from a student she probably does not remember.\n\n37\n00:01:30,860 --> 00:01:33,620\nAnd I think many people have a letter like that.\n\n38\n00:01:33,700 --> 00:01:35,480\nIn a desk, or in their head.\n\n39\n00:01:36,220 --> 00:01:38,940\nA small letter that never goes.\n\n40\n00:01:39,290 --> 00:01:45,480\n\"A letter in a desk, or in the head.\" Mia, that is our topic today.\n\n41\n00:01:45,560 --> 00:01:47,540\nSo here is our plan.\n\n42\n00:01:47,600 --> 00:01:52,970\nIn part one, we talk about the thank yous we did not send, and the ones we did.\n\n43\n00:01:54,120 --> 00:01:59,900\nIn part two, we ask why we wait, and why the other side feels different.\n\n44\n00:02:00,940 --> 00:02:03,800\nAnd I have a true story for that.\n\n45\n00:02:03,880 --> 00:02:08,039\nIn part three, we share simple ways to say thank you today.\n\n46\n00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:09,800\nDoes that sound good?\n\n47\n00:02:11,060 --> 00:02:12,120\nThat sounds good, Ben.\n\n48\n00:02:12,800 --> 00:02:16,440\nAnd to everyone listening, please do not worry if you miss something.\n\n49\n00:02:16,840 --> 00:02:17,940\nYou will hear it again.\n\n50\n00:02:18,540 --> 00:02:23,720\nYou can listen while you cook, while you walk, or while you think of one person who helped you.\n\n51\n00:02:24,820 --> 00:02:25,360\nOkay, Ben.\n\n52\n00:02:25,660 --> 00:02:26,180\nPart one.\n\n53\n00:02:26,740 --> 00:02:27,320\nYour boss.\n\n54\n00:02:27,800 --> 00:02:29,020\nWhat did you want to say to him?\n\n55\n00:02:29,980 --> 00:02:31,860\nHe taught me how to work, Mia.\n\n56\n00:02:32,640 --> 00:02:33,780\nTwenty years ago.\n\n57\n00:02:34,740 --> 00:02:37,880\nI was young and lazy, and he did not shout.\n\n58\n00:02:38,780 --> 00:02:39,609\nHe showed me.\n\n59\n00:02:40,670 --> 00:02:42,020\nAnd I never told him that.\n\n60\n00:02:43,080 --> 00:02:45,420\nBut last year, I sent it.\n\n61\n00:02:45,440 --> 00:02:46,730\nBy accident, almost.\n\n62\n00:02:47,920 --> 00:02:51,810\nI was tired and a little sad, and I wrote three lines.\n\n63\n00:02:52,930 --> 00:02:54,370\n\"You taught me how to work.\n\n64\n00:02:55,380 --> 00:02:56,950\nI still use it every day.\n\n65\n00:02:57,850 --> 00:02:58,340\nThank you.\"\n\n66\n00:02:59,640 --> 00:03:00,680\nBy accident, Ben?\n\n67\n00:03:01,220 --> 00:03:01,850\nYou sent it?\n\n68\n00:03:02,520 --> 00:03:05,960\nI want to hear this, because I have never sent one by accident.\n\n69\n00:03:06,580 --> 00:03:07,200\nWhat happened?\n\n70\n00:03:08,260 --> 00:03:09,440\nHe called me, Mia.\n\n71\n00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:11,060\nThe same evening.\n\n72\n00:03:11,820 --> 00:03:18,540\nAnd he said, \"Ben, nobody has thanked me in twenty years.\" Twenty years.\n\n73\n00:03:19,560 --> 00:03:20,880\nHe was almost crying.\n\n74\n00:03:21,980 --> 00:03:26,109\nThree lines, and a man who had been waiting twenty years for them.\n\n75\n00:03:26,920 --> 00:03:28,860\nTwenty years of waiting, Ben.\n\n76\n00:03:29,500 --> 00:03:30,620\nDid he say anything else?\n\n77\n00:03:31,260 --> 00:03:34,120\nWhat do people say when a thank you finally arrives?\n\n78\n00:03:35,380 --> 00:03:37,680\nHe said, \"I thought nobody noticed,\" Mia.\n\n79\n00:03:38,620 --> 00:03:39,800\nThat is what people say.\n\n80\n00:03:40,660 --> 00:03:46,540\nNot \"thank you for the letter.\" They say, \"I thought nobody noticed.\" Every time.\n\n81\n00:03:46,580 --> 00:03:49,060\nThe nurse, the teacher, the boss.\n\n82\n00:03:49,770 --> 00:03:52,180\nThey all thought nobody noticed.\n\n83\n00:03:52,200 --> 00:03:55,180\nAnd one letter tells them that somebody did.\n\n84\n00:03:55,720 --> 00:03:58,660\nNobody thanked him in twenty years, Ben.\n\n85\n00:03:59,460 --> 00:04:03,240\nThat is so sad, and it is so common.\n\n86\n00:04:03,340 --> 00:04:06,500\nI think most people who help us never hear about it.\n\n87\n00:04:06,540 --> 00:04:07,280\nThe teacher.\n\n88\n00:04:07,340 --> 00:04:07,820\nThe nurse.\n\n89\n00:04:08,040 --> 00:04:08,600\nThe boss.\n\n90\n00:04:09,110 --> 00:04:13,420\nThey do the thing, we go on with our lives, and they never know it worked.\n\n91\n00:04:14,450 --> 00:04:15,580\nThey never know it worked.\n\n92\n00:04:16,480 --> 00:04:16,980\nYes, Mia.\n\n93\n00:04:17,940 --> 00:04:18,080\nMrs.\n\n94\n00:04:18,360 --> 00:04:20,760\nPark does not know you make an English podcast.\n\n95\n00:04:21,480 --> 00:04:24,010\nShe does not know that \"keep going\" went anywhere.\n\n96\n00:04:25,340 --> 00:04:26,080\nAnd the small ones?\n\n97\n00:04:27,080 --> 00:04:28,750\nToday, how many people did you thank?\n\n98\n00:04:29,540 --> 00:04:31,190\nReal thank yous, with looking.\n\n99\n00:04:32,500 --> 00:04:33,490\nToday?\n\n100\n00:04:33,600 --> 00:04:35,060\nLet me think, Ben.\n\n101\n00:04:35,100 --> 00:04:36,440\nThe man at the coffee shop.\n\n102\n00:04:36,740 --> 00:04:37,980\nFast, no looking.\n\n103\n00:04:38,520 --> 00:04:39,920\nMy colleague, for the file.\n\n104\n00:04:40,400 --> 00:04:42,040\nBy email, one word.\n\n105\n00:04:42,500 --> 00:04:43,160\nThat is two.\n\n106\n00:04:43,680 --> 00:04:45,740\nAnd both were small in the bad way.\n\n107\n00:04:46,240 --> 00:04:49,720\nSmall like \"thanks\" thrown over a shoulder.\n\n108\n00:04:49,800 --> 00:04:51,580\nNot small like a real thing.\n\n109\n00:04:52,680 --> 00:04:54,060\nSmall in the bad way, Mia.\n\n110\n00:04:54,940 --> 00:04:56,620\nI know that thank you.\n\n111\n00:04:56,630 --> 00:04:59,050\n\"Thanks,\" over the shoulder, already walking.\n\n112\n00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:03,180\nAnd people do real things for us all day.\n\n113\n00:05:03,300 --> 00:05:05,010\nSo what is small in the good way?\n\n114\n00:05:06,540 --> 00:05:08,460\nA thank you with a thing in it, Ben.\n\n115\n00:05:09,290 --> 00:05:15,360\n\"Thank you for the file, I needed it before the meeting.\" That is small, and it is real.\n\n116\n00:05:15,420 --> 00:05:21,060\nIt says, \"I saw what you did.\" And I think that is what people want, more than the word.\n\n117\n00:05:21,680 --> 00:05:22,460\nTo be seen.\n\n118\n00:05:23,320 --> 00:05:26,820\n\"To be seen.\" Mia, that is the whole thing.\n\n119\n00:05:27,660 --> 00:05:31,340\nMy friend who drove three hours when my car broke does not need a letter.\n\n120\n00:05:32,160 --> 00:05:35,140\nHe needs me to say, \"You drove three hours.\n\n121\n00:05:35,780 --> 00:05:39,100\nI have not forgotten.\" That is the whole letter.\n\n122\n00:05:40,280 --> 00:05:43,340\n\"I have not forgotten\" is the whole letter, Ben.\n\n123\n00:05:43,900 --> 00:05:45,339\nAnd I have more than one letter.\n\n124\n00:05:45,800 --> 00:05:47,180\nThere is a note for a nurse.\n\n125\n00:05:47,490 --> 00:05:48,720\nA message for a neighbor.\n\n126\n00:05:49,440 --> 00:05:51,400\nAll in my head or in my desk.\n\n127\n00:05:51,720 --> 00:05:52,560\nNot sent.\n\n128\n00:05:53,120 --> 00:05:54,820\nAnd the reason is always the same.\n\n129\n00:05:55,540 --> 00:05:57,980\nI think it will be strange for those people.\n\n130\n00:05:58,080 --> 00:05:58,740\nToo much.\n\n131\n00:06:00,440 --> 00:06:01,200\nToo much, Mia.\n\n132\n00:06:02,180 --> 00:06:02,790\nI say the same.\n\n133\n00:06:03,520 --> 00:06:05,100\nAnd I still do not send mine.\n\n134\n00:06:05,900 --> 00:06:06,880\nSo we are the same.\n\n135\n00:06:08,280 --> 00:06:09,580\nOkay, so that was part one.\n\n136\n00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:11,500\nThe letters we did not send.\n\n137\n00:06:12,240 --> 00:06:16,320\nA teacher, a boss, a nurse, a friend with a car.\n\n138\n00:06:16,340 --> 00:06:20,400\nAnd one message that went by accident, and a man who waited twenty years.\n\n139\n00:06:21,300 --> 00:06:23,000\nNow, part two.\n\n140\n00:06:23,060 --> 00:06:23,820\nWhy do we wait?\n\n141\n00:06:24,740 --> 00:06:27,330\nAnd I have a true story about exactly this.\n\n142\n00:06:28,580 --> 00:06:30,600\nAbout exactly this, Ben?\n\n143\n00:06:31,180 --> 00:06:33,550\nPeople who do not send their thank yous?\n\n144\n00:06:34,360 --> 00:06:36,650\nI did not know science looked inside desks.\n\n145\n00:06:37,560 --> 00:06:38,660\nExactly that, Mia.\n\n146\n00:06:39,680 --> 00:06:40,620\nOkay, part two.\n\n147\n00:06:41,400 --> 00:06:43,179\nThis is a true story.\n\n148\n00:06:43,260 --> 00:06:48,620\nTwo researchers in Chicago, Amit Kumar and Nicholas Epley, did a study in 2018.\n\n149\n00:06:49,230 --> 00:06:53,220\nThey asked people to write a thank-you letter to someone who had helped them.\n\n150\n00:06:53,280 --> 00:06:56,540\nA real letter, to a real person who had helped them.\n\n151\n00:06:56,640 --> 00:06:57,840\nA real letter, Ben.\n\n152\n00:06:58,270 --> 00:07:00,260\nLike mine, but they actually sent it.\n\n153\n00:07:00,960 --> 00:07:01,720\nAnd then?\n\n154\n00:07:01,760 --> 00:07:02,900\nWhat did the researchers do?\n\n155\n00:07:03,620 --> 00:07:06,300\nBefore sending it, the writers had to guess, Mia.\n\n156\n00:07:07,140 --> 00:07:08,300\nHow will the person feel?\n\n157\n00:07:08,840 --> 00:07:09,380\nHow happy?\n\n158\n00:07:09,979 --> 00:07:10,740\nHow awkward?\n\n159\n00:07:11,560 --> 00:07:15,520\nAnd then the researchers sent the letters, and asked the receivers the same questions.\n\n160\n00:07:16,260 --> 00:07:16,900\nHow did you feel?\n\n161\n00:07:17,480 --> 00:07:18,100\nWas it awkward?\n\n162\n00:07:18,900 --> 00:07:20,860\nAnd the two answers did not match.\n\n163\n00:07:21,560 --> 00:07:22,780\nHow did they not match, Ben?\n\n164\n00:07:23,280 --> 00:07:25,800\nDid the receivers feel less than the writers thought?\n\n165\n00:07:25,860 --> 00:07:26,300\nOr more?\n\n166\n00:07:26,960 --> 00:07:28,040\nMore, Mia.\n\n167\n00:07:28,080 --> 00:07:29,540\nThe writers guessed too low.\n\n168\n00:07:30,100 --> 00:07:30,760\nEvery time.\n\n169\n00:07:31,360 --> 00:07:33,760\nThey thought the receiver would feel a little happy.\n\n170\n00:07:34,360 --> 00:07:36,280\nThe receiver felt very happy.\n\n171\n00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:39,040\nThey thought the letter would be a bit awkward.\n\n172\n00:07:39,629 --> 00:07:42,400\nThe receiver did not find it awkward at all.\n\n173\n00:07:42,459 --> 00:07:44,360\nNot awkward at all, Ben?\n\n174\n00:07:44,380 --> 00:07:46,220\nBut the writers were sure it would be awkward.\n\n175\n00:07:46,810 --> 00:07:48,350\nThat is my desk exactly.\n\n176\n00:07:48,980 --> 00:07:49,940\nI would have guessed the same.\n\n177\n00:07:50,560 --> 00:07:52,940\nAnd here is the part that explains your desk, Mia.\n\n178\n00:07:53,660 --> 00:07:55,750\nThe writers worried a lot about the words.\n\n179\n00:07:56,500 --> 00:07:57,200\nIs it good enough?\n\n180\n00:07:57,800 --> 00:07:58,620\nIs it strange?\n\n181\n00:07:59,450 --> 00:08:01,960\nAnd the receivers did not care about the words at all.\n\n182\n00:08:02,710 --> 00:08:04,480\nThey only cared that somebody thought of them.\n\n183\n00:08:05,250 --> 00:08:06,360\nThe words were small.\n\n184\n00:08:06,960 --> 00:08:08,040\nThe thought was big.\n\n185\n00:08:08,630 --> 00:08:09,800\n\"The words were small.\n\n186\n00:08:10,230 --> 00:08:14,900\nThe thought was big.\" Ben, so the letter in my desk is not too much.\n\n187\n00:08:15,480 --> 00:08:18,320\nIt is, actually, bigger than I think, for her.\n\n188\n00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:20,440\nAnd I am the one making it small.\n\n189\n00:08:21,160 --> 00:08:23,060\nYou are the one making it small, Mia.\n\n190\n00:08:23,860 --> 00:08:27,140\nThat is what the study found, with hundreds of people.\n\n191\n00:08:27,180 --> 00:08:29,300\nWe think our thank you is smaller than it is.\n\n192\n00:08:29,950 --> 00:08:31,660\nAnd we think it is stranger than it is.\n\n193\n00:08:32,360 --> 00:08:33,420\nBoth wrong.\n\n194\n00:08:33,460 --> 00:08:34,740\nBoth in the same direction.\n\n195\n00:08:35,400 --> 00:08:37,860\nBoth wrong, in the same direction.\n\n196\n00:08:37,920 --> 00:08:41,920\nSo the fear says, \"It will be strange.\" And the study says, \"No.\n\n197\n00:08:42,380 --> 00:08:47,460\nIt will be a very good day for that person.\" Ben, can I tell you about a thank you I received?\n\n198\n00:08:47,960 --> 00:08:50,160\nBecause I think it shows the same thing.\n\n199\n00:08:50,180 --> 00:08:50,719\nPlease, Mia.\n\n200\n00:08:51,080 --> 00:08:51,710\nWho thanked you?\n\n201\n00:08:52,420 --> 00:08:54,860\nI did not know you got one of these letters too.\n\n202\n00:08:54,870 --> 00:08:57,040\nA student from my first English class, Ben.\n\n203\n00:08:57,600 --> 00:08:58,940\nFive years ago.\n\n204\n00:08:59,100 --> 00:08:59,660\nA short letter.\n\n205\n00:09:00,200 --> 00:09:00,880\nTwo lines.\n\n206\n00:09:01,570 --> 00:09:03,000\n\"You said my accent was fine.\n\n207\n00:09:03,500 --> 00:09:04,580\nI stopped being afraid.\n\n208\n00:09:05,180 --> 00:09:08,720\nI work in London now.\" And I had no memory of saying it.\n\n209\n00:09:09,280 --> 00:09:09,580\nNone.\n\n210\n00:09:10,340 --> 00:09:12,520\nIt was a small thing on a Tuesday.\n\n211\n00:09:12,540 --> 00:09:14,440\nA small thing on a Tuesday, Mia.\n\n212\n00:09:15,050 --> 00:09:16,260\nAnd it changed a life.\n\n213\n00:09:17,020 --> 00:09:18,670\nAnd he almost did not send it, I guess?\n\n214\n00:09:19,160 --> 00:09:20,290\nBecause it felt too small?\n\n215\n00:09:21,440 --> 00:09:23,000\nHe said exactly that, Ben.\n\n216\n00:09:23,460 --> 00:09:31,320\n\"I almost did not send this.\" The biggest thing anyone has ever sent me, and he almost did not send it because it felt too small.\n\n217\n00:09:32,120 --> 00:09:34,060\nThat is the study in one letter.\n\n218\n00:09:34,920 --> 00:09:36,500\nThe study in one letter, Mia.\n\n219\n00:09:37,340 --> 00:09:39,900\nAnd I think about all the people who did not send theirs.\n\n220\n00:09:40,660 --> 00:09:42,160\nAll the letters in all the desks.\n\n221\n00:09:42,760 --> 00:09:48,340\nSmall things on Tuesdays that changed lives, and the person who said them never knew.\n\n222\n00:09:49,420 --> 00:09:52,500\nThat is a lot of good news, sitting in drawers.\n\n223\n00:09:52,600 --> 00:09:54,960\nA lot of good news, sitting in drawers.\n\n224\n00:09:55,760 --> 00:09:56,910\nBen, why do we wait?\n\n225\n00:09:57,560 --> 00:09:59,380\nBecause I do not think it is laziness.\n\n226\n00:10:00,300 --> 00:10:01,880\nI think it is a kind of shyness, Mia.\n\n227\n00:10:02,720 --> 00:10:06,920\nSaying thank you, really, means showing that someone helped me.\n\n228\n00:10:06,960 --> 00:10:07,810\nThat I needed help.\n\n229\n00:10:08,590 --> 00:10:11,140\nAnd some part of us does not want to be seen needing help.\n\n230\n00:10:11,760 --> 00:10:14,700\nSo we say \"thanks\" fast and walk away.\n\n231\n00:10:15,400 --> 00:10:18,400\nThe real thank you needs one second of standing still.\n\n232\n00:10:19,320 --> 00:10:20,980\nOne second of standing still.\n\n233\n00:10:21,560 --> 00:10:22,220\nYes, Ben.\n\n234\n00:10:22,820 --> 00:10:25,260\nAnd there is the word \"today,\" in our title.\n\n235\n00:10:25,980 --> 00:10:30,220\nNot \"say thank you.\" \"Say thank you today.\" Why today?\n\n236\n00:10:31,120 --> 00:10:34,720\nBecause thank you is the easiest thing in the world to move to tomorrow, Mia.\n\n237\n00:10:35,400 --> 00:10:36,620\nNobody is waiting for it.\n\n238\n00:10:37,360 --> 00:10:40,060\nNobody will be angry if it does not come.\n\n239\n00:10:40,070 --> 00:10:41,760\nThere is no deadline.\n\n240\n00:10:41,780 --> 00:10:44,600\nAnd that is exactly why it never comes.\n\n241\n00:10:44,640 --> 00:10:47,620\nPeople move it to tomorrow for twenty years.\n\n242\n00:10:47,660 --> 00:10:50,700\nToday is the deadline, because nobody else will give you one.\n\n243\n00:10:51,750 --> 00:10:58,350\n\"Today is the deadline, because nobody else will give you one.\" Ben, that is true for every good thing, I think.\n\n244\n00:10:58,860 --> 00:11:00,630\nNo deadline, so no day.\n\n245\n00:11:01,500 --> 00:11:03,200\nOkay, so that was part two.\n\n246\n00:11:03,240 --> 00:11:04,000\nWhy we wait.\n\n247\n00:11:04,600 --> 00:11:07,180\nWe talked about Kumar and Epley and the letters.\n\n248\n00:11:07,710 --> 00:11:10,260\nWe said the words are small and the thought is big.\n\n249\n00:11:10,740 --> 00:11:12,520\nAnd we said today is the deadline.\n\n250\n00:11:13,240 --> 00:11:14,720\nNow, part three.\n\n251\n00:11:14,860 --> 00:11:16,620\nSimple ways to say thank you today.\n\n252\n00:11:17,160 --> 00:11:18,640\nBen, you go first.\n\n253\n00:11:19,600 --> 00:11:20,520\nOkay, part three.\n\n254\n00:11:21,160 --> 00:11:23,400\nMy first idea is the shape of the letter, Mia.\n\n255\n00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:28,740\nBecause some people listening have a desk like yours, and I want to make the letter small.\n\n256\n00:11:28,780 --> 00:11:30,939\nIt does not need two pages.\n\n257\n00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:33,220\nKumar and Epley's letters were short.\n\n258\n00:11:33,360 --> 00:11:34,120\nA few sentences.\n\n259\n00:11:34,980 --> 00:11:35,720\nHere is the shape.\n\n260\n00:11:36,580 --> 00:11:37,860\nOne: what you did.\n\n261\n00:11:38,760 --> 00:11:40,580\nTwo: what it changed for me.\n\n262\n00:11:41,580 --> 00:11:42,820\nThree: thank you.\n\n263\n00:11:43,780 --> 00:11:45,100\nFour: your name.\n\n264\n00:11:46,060 --> 00:11:46,830\nFour sentences.\n\n265\n00:11:47,860 --> 00:11:49,000\nFour sentences, Ben.\n\n266\n00:11:49,510 --> 00:11:50,940\nThe two pages were the fear.\n\n267\n00:11:51,400 --> 00:11:52,990\nThe four sentences are the letter.\n\n268\n00:11:53,500 --> 00:11:54,160\nOkay.\n\n269\n00:11:54,240 --> 00:11:55,130\nI am going to do it.\n\n270\n00:11:55,820 --> 00:11:58,080\nNot today, because I want to say it right.\n\n271\n00:11:58,740 --> 00:11:58,960\nNo.\n\n272\n00:11:59,540 --> 00:11:59,940\nToday.\n\n273\n00:12:00,660 --> 00:12:01,440\nI hear myself.\n\n274\n00:12:01,940 --> 00:12:03,760\n\"Not today\" is the desk again.\n\n275\n00:12:04,590 --> 00:12:07,420\n\"Not today\" is the desk again, Mia.\n\n276\n00:12:08,180 --> 00:12:08,770\nI heard it too.\n\n277\n00:12:09,600 --> 00:12:10,350\nSo, today?\n\n278\n00:12:11,240 --> 00:12:12,540\nFour sentences, to Mrs.\n\n279\n00:12:12,640 --> 00:12:14,240\nPark, in the mail?\n\n280\n00:12:14,360 --> 00:12:16,090\nSay it so our listeners can hold you to it.\n\n281\n00:12:16,900 --> 00:12:17,500\nToday, Ben.\n\n282\n00:12:18,320 --> 00:12:20,730\nI am saying it out loud so it is real.\n\n283\n00:12:21,300 --> 00:12:23,000\nFour sentences, to Mrs.\n\n284\n00:12:23,100 --> 00:12:25,800\nPark, in the mail, today.\n\n285\n00:12:25,840 --> 00:12:27,290\nYou are all my witnesses.\n\n286\n00:12:28,430 --> 00:12:30,820\nMy first idea is the small one.\n\n287\n00:12:30,900 --> 00:12:32,320\nA thank you with a thing in it.\n\n288\n00:12:32,930 --> 00:12:40,100\n\"Thank you for the soup\" is bigger than \"thanks.\" Three of those, to three people, today.\n\n289\n00:12:40,120 --> 00:12:42,000\nThree small ones with a thing in them, Mia.\n\n290\n00:12:42,940 --> 00:12:44,770\nMy second idea is for the people at home.\n\n291\n00:12:45,560 --> 00:12:47,990\nBecause there is a thank you I always miss.\n\n292\n00:12:49,040 --> 00:12:50,980\nMy wife makes coffee every morning.\n\n293\n00:12:51,720 --> 00:12:53,590\nEvery morning, for eight years.\n\n294\n00:12:54,340 --> 00:12:54,980\nAnd I drink it.\n\n295\n00:12:55,720 --> 00:12:56,150\nThat is all.\n\n296\n00:12:57,140 --> 00:12:59,980\nAnd last month, I said, \"Thank you for the coffee.\n\n297\n00:13:00,660 --> 00:13:04,640\nEight years of coffee.\" And she looked at me like I was a stranger.\n\n298\n00:13:06,080 --> 00:13:07,260\nLike a stranger, Ben?\n\n299\n00:13:07,860 --> 00:13:09,440\nAfter eight years of coffee?\n\n300\n00:13:10,160 --> 00:13:11,000\nWhat did she say?\n\n301\n00:13:12,100 --> 00:13:14,480\nBecause it was the first time with the thing in it, Mia.\n\n302\n00:13:15,220 --> 00:13:16,260\nAfter eight years.\n\n303\n00:13:17,200 --> 00:13:20,500\nWe undervalue the thank you most for the people closest to us.\n\n304\n00:13:21,200 --> 00:13:25,240\nBecause they are always there, and thank you feels unnecessary.\n\n305\n00:13:26,010 --> 00:13:30,400\n\"She knows.\" But she does not know, until we say it.\n\n306\n00:13:31,550 --> 00:13:43,600\n\"She knows, but she does not know until we say it.\" Ben, my mother cooked for me for eighteen years, and I said a real thank you for the first time at thirty.\n\n307\n00:13:43,740 --> 00:13:44,140\nThirty.\n\n308\n00:13:44,860 --> 00:13:47,520\nThat is the desk letter at home, every day.\n\n309\n00:13:48,980 --> 00:13:50,360\nThe desk letter at home, Mia.\n\n310\n00:13:51,480 --> 00:13:53,960\nCan I tell you about my worst thank you?\n\n311\n00:13:53,980 --> 00:13:55,660\nBecause it shows the cost of waiting.\n\n312\n00:13:56,890 --> 00:13:57,640\nPlease, Ben.\n\n313\n00:13:58,000 --> 00:13:58,900\nThe worst one.\n\n314\n00:13:59,400 --> 00:14:02,250\nI think everyone has a thank you they almost missed.\n\n315\n00:14:03,680 --> 00:14:05,360\nMy father, Mia.\n\n316\n00:14:05,420 --> 00:14:06,800\nHe is not a man of words.\n\n317\n00:14:07,530 --> 00:14:10,440\nAnd I always thought, \"He knows I am grateful.\n\n318\n00:14:10,450 --> 00:14:15,920\nWe do not say these things.\" And then he got sick, two years ago.\n\n319\n00:14:15,960 --> 00:14:18,020\nAnd in the hospital, I said it.\n\n320\n00:14:18,730 --> 00:14:19,260\nAll of it.\n\n321\n00:14:20,090 --> 00:14:26,410\n\"Thank you for the work, the house, the bicycle, the patience.\" And he cried.\n\n322\n00:14:27,250 --> 00:14:29,540\nAnd he said, \"I did not know.\"\n\n323\n00:14:30,829 --> 00:14:37,320\n\"I did not know.\" Ben, forty years to say it, and one minute to say it.\n\n324\n00:14:38,360 --> 00:14:40,340\nForty years and one minute, Mia.\n\n325\n00:14:41,300 --> 00:14:42,140\nHe is fine now.\n\n326\n00:14:42,960 --> 00:14:44,360\nBut I almost said it too late.\n\n327\n00:14:45,480 --> 00:14:47,480\nSo my third idea is about receiving.\n\n328\n00:14:48,180 --> 00:14:52,440\nBecause receiving thank you is also a skill, and we are bad at it, too.\n\n329\n00:14:53,360 --> 00:14:58,580\nWhen someone thanks me, I say, \"Oh, it was nothing.\" And I push the present back.\n\n330\n00:14:59,780 --> 00:15:01,380\nI do that all the time, Ben.\n\n331\n00:15:01,810 --> 00:15:04,510\n\"It was nothing.\" It comes out before I think.\n\n332\n00:15:05,300 --> 00:15:06,500\nWhat should we say instead?\n\n333\n00:15:07,690 --> 00:15:08,760\n\"You are welcome,\" Mia.\n\n334\n00:15:09,560 --> 00:15:12,840\nOr, \"It was my pleasure.\" And then stop.\n\n335\n00:15:13,620 --> 00:15:14,840\nDo not push it back.\n\n336\n00:15:15,820 --> 00:15:20,620\n\"It was nothing\" says, \"Do not thank me.\" But they want to thank you.\n\n337\n00:15:21,620 --> 00:15:23,230\nThank you is not a confession.\n\n338\n00:15:23,940 --> 00:15:24,720\nIt is a present.\n\n339\n00:15:25,480 --> 00:15:27,960\nIt is theirs to give, and yours to keep.\n\n340\n00:15:28,680 --> 00:15:34,720\n\"It is theirs to give, and yours to keep.\" Ben, I am going to stop pushing it back.\n\n341\n00:15:35,260 --> 00:15:36,860\nMy second idea is for the evening.\n\n342\n00:15:37,480 --> 00:15:40,560\nBefore bed, I think of one person who made the day better.\n\n343\n00:15:41,310 --> 00:15:42,720\nThe bus driver who waited.\n\n344\n00:15:43,310 --> 00:15:44,680\nThe colleague with the file.\n\n345\n00:15:45,340 --> 00:15:47,100\nAnd I say their name.\n\n346\n00:15:47,220 --> 00:15:49,200\nJust the name, and thank you.\n\n347\n00:15:49,920 --> 00:15:51,100\nFor nobody.\n\n348\n00:15:51,220 --> 00:15:52,730\nOr for everybody.\n\n349\n00:15:54,140 --> 00:15:56,800\nFranklin's question, with a name in it, Mia.\n\n350\n00:15:56,900 --> 00:15:57,850\nI like that very much.\n\n351\n00:15:58,460 --> 00:16:01,200\nAnd I think the evening thank you does something to the morning.\n\n352\n00:16:01,940 --> 00:16:04,140\nI go to sleep thinking about a person who helped me.\n\n353\n00:16:04,600 --> 00:16:07,140\nAnd I wake up thinking that people help me.\n\n354\n00:16:07,200 --> 00:16:10,240\nThat is a different morning than waking up thinking about what is missing.\n\n355\n00:16:11,450 --> 00:16:17,760\n\"The good name is the last thing in the room.\" Ben, my third idea is a small list.\n\n356\n00:16:18,020 --> 00:16:20,480\nBecause some people think they have nobody to thank.\n\n357\n00:16:21,110 --> 00:16:22,040\n\"Nobody helped me.\n\n358\n00:16:22,380 --> 00:16:26,340\nI did it alone.\" I said it myself, for years.\n\n359\n00:16:27,480 --> 00:16:28,080\nAnd then, Mia?\n\n360\n00:16:28,420 --> 00:16:29,260\nDid you make the list?\n\n361\n00:16:29,840 --> 00:16:31,300\nHow long was it?\n\n362\n00:16:31,380 --> 00:16:34,460\nLast night, Ben, after we planned this episode.\n\n363\n00:16:34,520 --> 00:16:37,100\nPeople who did one small thing.\n\n364\n00:16:37,160 --> 00:16:39,460\nTwenty-six people, in ten minutes.\n\n365\n00:16:40,090 --> 00:16:41,580\nThe neighbor who lent a ladder.\n\n366\n00:16:42,030 --> 00:16:43,600\nThe teacher who gave me a book.\n\n367\n00:16:44,160 --> 00:16:46,140\nThe stranger who returned my wallet.\n\n368\n00:16:46,660 --> 00:16:47,520\nA bus fare.\n\n369\n00:16:47,780 --> 00:16:49,320\nA word at the right time.\n\n370\n00:16:49,900 --> 00:16:51,600\nA door held on a bad day.\n\n371\n00:16:52,460 --> 00:16:56,580\nSmall things, from small people, that added up to a life.\n\n372\n00:16:57,840 --> 00:17:00,040\nTwenty-six people in ten minutes, Mia.\n\n373\n00:17:00,760 --> 00:17:01,420\nMake the list.\n\n374\n00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:03,120\nIt will not be short.\n\n375\n00:17:04,060 --> 00:17:07,220\nAnd one last idea, for our listeners who are learning English.\n\n376\n00:17:07,920 --> 00:17:13,079\nBecause thank you is one of the first things we learn, and one of the last things we learn to say well.\n\n377\n00:17:14,240 --> 00:17:15,460\nThe last things, Ben?\n\n378\n00:17:16,170 --> 00:17:17,220\n\"Thank you\" is easy.\n\n379\n00:17:17,680 --> 00:17:19,470\nEveryone learns it in the first week.\n\n380\n00:17:20,470 --> 00:17:21,780\n\"Thank you\" is easy, Mia.\n\n381\n00:17:22,610 --> 00:17:27,439\n\"Thank you for\" is the next step, and it is where the real English is.\n\n382\n00:17:27,450 --> 00:17:34,780\n\"Thank you for helping me.\" \"Thank you for waiting.\" \"Thank you for the file.\" Three small words, and the thing.\n\n383\n00:17:35,480 --> 00:17:37,359\nIt is the safest sentence in the language.\n\n384\n00:17:38,040 --> 00:17:40,620\nNobody has ever been angry at \"thank you for.\"\n\n385\n00:17:41,800 --> 00:17:44,040\nThe safest sentence in the language, Ben.\n\n386\n00:17:44,640 --> 00:17:51,940\nMy first real Spanish to a stranger, after \"hola,\" was \"gracias por la ayuda.\" Thank you for the help.\n\n387\n00:17:52,720 --> 00:17:54,560\nAnd the woman held my hand.\n\n388\n00:17:55,060 --> 00:17:56,630\nFor three wrong words.\n\n389\n00:17:57,520 --> 00:18:00,720\nSo for everyone listening, here is today's English.\n\n390\n00:18:00,740 --> 00:18:02,740\n\"Thank you for\" plus the thing.\n\n391\n00:18:03,440 --> 00:18:05,980\nSay it three times today.\n\n392\n00:18:06,100 --> 00:18:07,960\nThree times today, Mia.\n\n393\n00:18:07,970 --> 00:18:11,520\nAnd I want to say something to the people who think it is too late.\n\n394\n00:18:11,530 --> 00:18:17,900\n\"She will not remember me.\" \"It was twenty years ago.\" The study did not find any of that.\n\n395\n00:18:17,940 --> 00:18:19,160\nThe receivers were happy.\n\n396\n00:18:19,780 --> 00:18:21,160\nSurprised and happy.\n\n397\n00:18:21,880 --> 00:18:28,280\nNobody said, \"Why now?\" Nobody said, \"Too late.\" Late is just the fear with a calendar.\n\n398\n00:18:29,040 --> 00:18:34,860\n\"Late is just the fear with a calendar.\" Ben, that is our message today.\n\n399\n00:18:34,920 --> 00:18:37,900\nThe people who helped us mostly never hear about it.\n\n400\n00:18:37,940 --> 00:18:42,980\nWe do not tell them, because we think our thank you is small and strange.\n\n401\n00:18:43,040 --> 00:18:44,660\nThe science says the opposite.\n\n402\n00:18:45,260 --> 00:18:48,860\nIt is big, and it is welcome, every time.\n\n403\n00:18:49,560 --> 00:18:50,580\nSo say it today.\n\n404\n00:18:51,120 --> 00:18:53,340\nFour sentences, or three seconds.\n\n405\n00:18:53,940 --> 00:18:55,399\nIt is bigger than you think.\n\n406\n00:18:56,600 --> 00:18:57,860\nIt is bigger than you think.\n\n407\n00:18:59,220 --> 00:19:04,500\nMia, let's repeat today's important words, slowly, because our listeners want to remember them.\n\n408\n00:19:06,080 --> 00:19:06,420\nThank.\n\n409\n00:19:07,220 --> 00:19:08,280\nTo say you are grateful.\n\n410\n00:19:09,160 --> 00:19:10,240\nThank you for the soup.\n\n411\n00:19:11,320 --> 00:19:12,840\nA thank you with a thing in it.\n\n412\n00:19:14,420 --> 00:19:14,680\nLetter.\n\n413\n00:19:15,760 --> 00:19:17,720\nWords on paper for one person.\n\n414\n00:19:18,640 --> 00:19:19,280\nA thank-you letter.\n\n415\n00:19:20,960 --> 00:19:21,680\nSend the letter.\n\n416\n00:19:23,280 --> 00:19:24,540\nPeople.\n\n417\n00:19:24,600 --> 00:19:25,720\nThe people who help us.\n\n418\n00:19:26,760 --> 00:19:27,880\nTwenty-six people.\n\n419\n00:19:29,500 --> 00:19:29,840\nToday.\n\n420\n00:19:30,760 --> 00:19:31,480\nSay it today.\n\n421\n00:19:32,240 --> 00:19:33,500\nToday is the deadline.\n\n422\n00:19:35,260 --> 00:19:35,600\nSmall.\n\n423\n00:19:36,420 --> 00:19:36,980\nNot big.\n\n424\n00:19:37,800 --> 00:19:38,760\nA small thank you.\n\n425\n00:19:39,740 --> 00:19:41,860\nSmall things, from small people.\n\n426\n00:19:43,880 --> 00:19:44,300\nThank.\n\n427\n00:19:45,180 --> 00:19:45,520\nLetter.\n\n428\n00:19:46,500 --> 00:19:46,880\nPeople.\n\n429\n00:19:48,020 --> 00:19:48,400\nToday.\n\n430\n00:19:49,680 --> 00:19:50,100\nSmall.\n\n431\n00:19:51,100 --> 00:19:52,700\nFive words, and one letter.\n\n432\n00:19:53,080 --> 00:19:54,620\nAnd one big idea.\n\n433\n00:19:55,480 --> 00:19:56,580\nSay thank you today.\n\n434\n00:19:57,620 --> 00:19:59,940\nNow, Ben, do you have a question for our listeners?\n\n435\n00:20:00,560 --> 00:20:01,060\nYes, Mia.\n\n436\n00:20:01,600 --> 00:20:03,680\nEveryone listening, here is my question.\n\n437\n00:20:04,600 --> 00:20:05,680\nWho is your Mrs.\n\n438\n00:20:05,900 --> 00:20:06,200\nPark?\n\n439\n00:20:07,100 --> 00:20:09,820\nThe person who helped you, who never heard about it.\n\n440\n00:20:10,820 --> 00:20:12,400\nWrite who they were in the comments.\n\n441\n00:20:13,190 --> 00:20:25,420\n\"My first boss.\" \"The nurse in 2015.\" \"My grandmother.\" And if you want, add, \"I will thank them today.\" We read every one.\n\n442\n00:20:26,620 --> 00:20:27,620\nAnd my letter went.\n\n443\n00:20:28,040 --> 00:20:29,920\nI mean, it will go.\n\n444\n00:20:29,960 --> 00:20:30,300\nToday.\n\n445\n00:20:30,920 --> 00:20:34,460\nAnd if this episode helped you, please subscribe to Two Cups English.\n\n446\n00:20:34,900 --> 00:20:36,960\nWe make a new episode every few days.\n\n447\n00:20:37,499 --> 00:20:41,380\nSlow English, simple words, and one true story each time.\n\n448\n00:20:42,040 --> 00:20:44,590\nThe full text of this episode is in the description.\n\n449\n00:20:45,200 --> 00:20:48,789\nRead it, and then close it, and write four sentences to someone.\n\n450\n00:20:49,810 --> 00:20:51,540\nThank you for spending this time with us.\n\n451\n00:20:52,320 --> 00:20:56,060\nThank you for choosing slow English on a busy day.\n\n452\n00:20:56,160 --> 00:20:58,300\nThank you for the comments we read every week.\n\n453\n00:20:59,260 --> 00:21:00,460\nWe mean all three.\n\n454\n00:21:01,540 --> 00:21:03,280\nNow go and say one of your own.\n\n455\n00:21:04,200 --> 00:21:04,580\nToday.\n\n456\n00:21:05,440 --> 00:21:07,680\nFour sentences, or three seconds.\n\n457\n00:21:08,660 --> 00:21:10,020\nIt is bigger than you think.\n\n458\n00:21:11,140 --> 00:21:11,860\nThis is Mia.\n\n459\n00:21:12,480 --> 00:21:13,320\nAnd this is Ben.\n\n460\n00:21:14,160 --> 00:21:15,660\nThis was Two Cups English.\n\n461\n00:21:16,240 --> 00:21:17,280\nSay thank you today.\n\n462\n00:21:17,820 --> 00:21:18,820\nWe will see you next time."},{"code":"CUP12","file":"CUP12.srt","srt":"1\n00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,260\nHello everyone, and welcome to Two Cups English.\n\n2\n00:00:03,840 --> 00:00:06,720\nI'm Mia, and Ben is here with me.\n\n3\n00:00:06,780 --> 00:00:10,160\nThis podcast is for people who are learning English.\n\n4\n00:00:10,220 --> 00:00:11,050\nWe speak slowly.\n\n5\n00:00:11,640 --> 00:00:12,970\nWe use simple words.\n\n6\n00:00:13,560 --> 00:00:17,300\nAnd we repeat the important words many times.\n\n7\n00:00:17,360 --> 00:00:19,680\nToday, our topic is words.\n\n8\n00:00:19,780 --> 00:00:21,300\nLearning one word a day.\n\n9\n00:00:22,260 --> 00:00:23,980\nBen, what is your word for today?\n\n10\n00:00:24,960 --> 00:00:25,420\nHi Mia.\n\n11\n00:00:25,860 --> 00:00:26,480\nHi everyone.\n\n12\n00:00:27,560 --> 00:00:30,520\nMy word today is \"shelf,\" Mia.\n\n13\n00:00:31,680 --> 00:00:32,280\nIn Spanish.\n\n14\n00:00:33,170 --> 00:00:41,540\n\"Estante.\" Because yesterday, in a shop, I wanted to ask where the rice was, and I did not have the word.\n\n15\n00:00:42,860 --> 00:00:43,600\nI pointed.\n\n16\n00:00:44,100 --> 00:00:44,820\nLike a child.\n\n17\n00:00:45,700 --> 00:00:46,620\nMy wife laughed.\n\n18\n00:00:47,760 --> 00:00:48,320\nWhat about you?\n\n19\n00:00:48,840 --> 00:00:49,540\nDo you have a word?\n\n20\n00:00:50,800 --> 00:00:51,820\nNot one word, Ben.\n\n21\n00:00:52,300 --> 00:00:53,500\nThree thousand.\n\n22\n00:00:54,220 --> 00:00:56,240\nWhen I was learning Spanish, I had a list.\n\n23\n00:00:56,830 --> 00:01:02,180\n\"The 3,000 most important Spanish words.\" And I learned fifty words every night.\n\n24\n00:01:02,960 --> 00:01:03,250\nFifty.\n\n25\n00:01:03,880 --> 00:01:05,099\nFor two months.\n\n26\n00:01:06,040 --> 00:01:07,940\nAnd at the end, I did a test.\n\n27\n00:01:08,660 --> 00:01:10,640\nI could remember about two hundred.\n\n28\n00:01:11,380 --> 00:01:13,700\nTwo hundred, from three thousand.\n\n29\n00:01:14,460 --> 00:01:15,260\nAnd I cried.\n\n30\n00:01:16,300 --> 00:01:23,390\n\"Two hundred, from three thousand.\" Mia, two months of nights, and the words were gone.\n\n31\n00:01:24,720 --> 00:01:27,120\nThat is our topic today.\n\n32\n00:01:27,200 --> 00:01:28,440\nSo here is our plan.\n\n33\n00:01:29,460 --> 00:01:32,460\nIn part one, we talk about our own lists.\n\n34\n00:01:33,600 --> 00:01:38,380\nIn part two, we ask why words disappear, and how they stay.\n\n35\n00:01:39,250 --> 00:01:43,070\nAnd I have a true story for that, from 1885.\n\n36\n00:01:44,200 --> 00:01:48,230\nIn part three, we share simple ways to learn one word a day.\n\n37\n00:01:49,360 --> 00:01:50,140\nDoes that sound good?\n\n38\n00:01:51,040 --> 00:01:52,040\nThat sounds good, Ben.\n\n39\n00:01:52,700 --> 00:01:56,220\nAnd to everyone listening, please do not worry if you miss something.\n\n40\n00:01:56,740 --> 00:01:57,820\nYou will hear it again.\n\n41\n00:01:58,620 --> 00:02:02,260\nAnd today, \"you will hear it again\" is not only a promise.\n\n42\n00:02:02,880 --> 00:02:04,100\nIt is the whole topic.\n\n43\n00:02:04,880 --> 00:02:07,720\nSome words will come back many times.\n\n44\n00:02:07,820 --> 00:02:08,500\nOkay, Ben.\n\n45\n00:02:08,560 --> 00:02:09,039\nPart one.\n\n46\n00:02:09,860 --> 00:02:10,940\nDid you have a list too?\n\n47\n00:02:12,260 --> 00:02:13,440\nThe same list, Mia.\n\n48\n00:02:14,280 --> 00:02:15,760\nIn English, when I was young.\n\n49\n00:02:16,640 --> 00:02:21,360\nDifferent language, same list, same fifty a night, same tears.\n\n50\n00:02:22,440 --> 00:02:24,800\nAnd for years, I thought I went wrong.\n\n51\n00:02:25,540 --> 00:02:26,160\nMy memory.\n\n52\n00:02:26,540 --> 00:02:27,160\nMy brain.\n\n53\n00:02:27,970 --> 00:02:31,520\n\"I am not a language person.\" Did you think that?\n\n54\n00:02:32,760 --> 00:02:34,100\nThat was my sentence, Ben.\n\n55\n00:02:34,700 --> 00:02:38,140\n\"I am not a language person.\" For years.\n\n56\n00:02:38,930 --> 00:02:42,340\nBut the list did not go wrong, and my brain did not go wrong.\n\n57\n00:02:42,950 --> 00:02:44,049\nThe method went wrong.\n\n58\n00:02:44,980 --> 00:02:48,880\nFifty words in one night is like eating fifty meals in one night.\n\n59\n00:02:49,510 --> 00:02:50,840\nThe body cannot use it.\n\n60\n00:02:51,440 --> 00:02:52,120\nIt goes through.\n\n61\n00:02:52,860 --> 00:02:54,440\nFifty meals, one night.\n\n62\n00:02:55,160 --> 00:02:57,040\nYou cannot remember fifty meals.\n\n63\n00:02:58,060 --> 00:02:59,160\nIt goes through.\n\n64\n00:03:00,380 --> 00:03:03,080\nMia, let me ask something about those two months.\n\n65\n00:03:04,020 --> 00:03:05,900\nDid it feel like learning, at the time?\n\n66\n00:03:06,780 --> 00:03:07,340\nBe honest.\n\n67\n00:03:08,340 --> 00:03:09,580\nIt felt like learning, Ben.\n\n68\n00:03:10,240 --> 00:03:11,220\nThat is the trap.\n\n69\n00:03:11,980 --> 00:03:16,980\nEvery night, fifty words, and at the end of the hour, I could remember all fifty.\n\n70\n00:03:17,820 --> 00:03:19,780\nI closed the list and I felt full.\n\n71\n00:03:20,500 --> 00:03:23,040\nI felt like a person who had learned fifty words.\n\n72\n00:03:23,660 --> 00:03:24,320\nAnd I had.\n\n73\n00:03:24,840 --> 00:03:25,609\nFor one hour.\n\n74\n00:03:26,480 --> 00:03:27,680\nFor one hour, Mia.\n\n75\n00:03:28,360 --> 00:03:29,620\nThe feeling was real.\n\n76\n00:03:30,520 --> 00:03:31,990\nThe learning was not.\n\n77\n00:03:32,720 --> 00:03:35,470\nAnd I think that is why the list is so popular.\n\n78\n00:03:35,480 --> 00:03:37,260\nIt gives the feeling every night.\n\n79\n00:03:38,020 --> 00:03:39,500\nSeeing is not learning.\n\n80\n00:03:40,220 --> 00:03:42,000\nBut seeing feels like learning.\n\n81\n00:03:42,700 --> 00:03:43,500\nNot for a week.\n\n82\n00:03:44,260 --> 00:03:47,110\nAnd remembering for one hour is not remembering.\n\n83\n00:03:48,010 --> 00:03:52,960\n\"Seeing is not learning.\" Ben, that should be on the first page of the list.\n\n84\n00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:55,410\nAnd there is something else from my two hundred words.\n\n85\n00:03:55,940 --> 00:03:57,320\nThe ones I still remember.\n\n86\n00:03:57,940 --> 00:03:58,979\nI looked at them later.\n\n87\n00:03:59,500 --> 00:04:01,700\nThey were not the first two hundred on the list.\n\n88\n00:04:02,520 --> 00:04:03,700\nNot the first two hundred, Mia?\n\n89\n00:04:04,620 --> 00:04:07,780\nI would have guessed the first ones, the easy ones.\n\n90\n00:04:07,820 --> 00:04:08,830\nThen which ones stayed?\n\n91\n00:04:09,489 --> 00:04:10,260\nThe ones you needed?\n\n92\n00:04:10,949 --> 00:04:12,300\nThe words I heard again, Ben.\n\n93\n00:04:12,680 --> 00:04:13,680\nIn songs.\n\n94\n00:04:13,740 --> 00:04:14,360\nIn the shop.\n\n95\n00:04:14,800 --> 00:04:15,400\nIn a film.\n\n96\n00:04:15,970 --> 00:04:21,900\n\"Pan.\" \"Agua.\" \"Gracias.\" Words that life said to me again, after the list.\n\n97\n00:04:22,540 --> 00:04:23,900\nThe list said them once.\n\n98\n00:04:24,380 --> 00:04:25,500\nLife said them again.\n\n99\n00:04:26,120 --> 00:04:27,640\nAnd the again is what stayed.\n\n100\n00:04:28,080 --> 00:04:29,360\nAgain and again.\n\n101\n00:04:29,960 --> 00:04:31,940\nI remember them because I met them again.\n\n102\n00:04:32,710 --> 00:04:34,720\n\"The list said them once.\n\n103\n00:04:34,800 --> 00:04:40,420\nLife said them again.\" Mia, that is the whole episode in two sentences.\n\n104\n00:04:40,440 --> 00:04:45,120\nBut let me tell you about my father before part two, because he did this without science.\n\n105\n00:04:45,980 --> 00:04:48,640\nHe came to this country at forty, with no English.\n\n106\n00:04:49,520 --> 00:04:51,520\nAt forty, Ben, with no English?\n\n107\n00:04:52,200 --> 00:04:54,450\nHow did he learn, with a job and a family?\n\n108\n00:04:55,340 --> 00:04:57,060\nOne word a day, Mia.\n\n109\n00:04:57,100 --> 00:04:58,520\nHe told me this when I was small.\n\n110\n00:04:59,490 --> 00:05:00,260\n\"One word, Ben.\n\n111\n00:05:00,860 --> 00:05:01,330\nEvery day.\n\n112\n00:05:02,320 --> 00:05:03,260\nThen I use it at work.\n\n113\n00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:05,080\nThen I use it at home.\n\n114\n00:05:05,940 --> 00:05:07,100\nThen I remember it.\n\n115\n00:05:07,880 --> 00:05:12,040\nThen it is mine.\" One word, used three times.\n\n116\n00:05:12,860 --> 00:05:14,440\nAnd he did that for thirty years.\n\n117\n00:05:15,190 --> 00:05:15,760\nDo the math.\n\n118\n00:05:16,520 --> 00:05:18,160\nTen thousand words, Ben.\n\n119\n00:05:18,200 --> 00:05:19,420\nFrom one a day.\n\n120\n00:05:19,480 --> 00:05:21,120\nThat is more than my list.\n\n121\n00:05:21,160 --> 00:05:22,720\nAnd he spoke well?\n\n122\n00:05:23,580 --> 00:05:24,900\nBetter than me, in the end, Mia.\n\n123\n00:05:25,540 --> 00:05:27,960\nWith an accent, and with ten thousand words.\n\n124\n00:05:28,700 --> 00:05:30,800\nHe never had a list, and he had no time.\n\n125\n00:05:31,500 --> 00:05:33,520\nHe had a day, and one word from it.\n\n126\n00:05:34,480 --> 00:05:36,040\nOkay, so that was part one.\n\n127\n00:05:36,600 --> 00:05:37,220\nOur lists.\n\n128\n00:05:38,160 --> 00:05:44,150\nThree thousand words, fifty a night, two hundred that stayed, and a father with one word a day.\n\n129\n00:05:45,220 --> 00:05:46,980\nNow, part two.\n\n130\n00:05:47,040 --> 00:05:49,430\nWhy do words disappear, and how do they stay?\n\n131\n00:05:50,360 --> 00:05:53,330\nMia, my true story is from 1885.\n\n132\n00:05:54,320 --> 00:05:55,680\n1885, Ben?\n\n133\n00:05:56,310 --> 00:05:59,920\nNobody was learning English from a podcast in 1885.\n\n134\n00:06:00,520 --> 00:06:02,760\nWho is the story about, and what did he do?\n\n135\n00:06:03,500 --> 00:06:04,300\nA German, Mia.\n\n136\n00:06:05,160 --> 00:06:06,580\nOkay, part two.\n\n137\n00:06:06,660 --> 00:06:08,720\nThis is a true story.\n\n138\n00:06:08,780 --> 00:06:10,280\nHis name was Hermann Ebbinghaus.\n\n139\n00:06:10,950 --> 00:06:12,780\nAnd he wanted to know how forgetting works.\n\n140\n00:06:13,689 --> 00:06:15,380\nSo he did something strange.\n\n141\n00:06:15,440 --> 00:06:16,380\nHe made up words.\n\n142\n00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:17,880\nNonsense words.\n\n143\n00:06:18,610 --> 00:06:23,240\n\"Zof.\" \"Wub.\" \"Kem.\" Hundreds of them.\n\n144\n00:06:23,270 --> 00:06:24,480\nAnd he learned them.\n\n145\n00:06:24,510 --> 00:06:25,030\nHimself.\n\n146\n00:06:25,730 --> 00:06:27,300\nFor years.\n\n147\n00:06:27,380 --> 00:06:29,320\nHe learned made-up words for years, Ben?\n\n148\n00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:31,990\nWhy not real words, like bread and water?\n\n149\n00:06:32,860 --> 00:06:35,780\nBecause real words have meaning, Mia, and meaning helps.\n\n150\n00:06:36,300 --> 00:06:37,610\nHe wanted words with no help.\n\n151\n00:06:38,140 --> 00:06:41,599\nSo he learned a list of these sounds until he could say them perfectly.\n\n152\n00:06:41,640 --> 00:06:42,860\nAnd then he waited.\n\n153\n00:06:43,960 --> 00:06:44,460\nOne hour.\n\n154\n00:06:45,120 --> 00:06:45,620\nOne day.\n\n155\n00:06:46,400 --> 00:06:46,900\nOne week.\n\n156\n00:06:47,680 --> 00:06:49,319\nAnd he tested how much he could remember.\n\n157\n00:06:49,880 --> 00:06:51,300\nAnd he drew a line on paper.\n\n158\n00:06:51,980 --> 00:06:52,760\nA line, Ben?\n\n159\n00:06:53,220 --> 00:06:53,770\nOn paper?\n\n160\n00:06:54,780 --> 00:06:57,460\nI did not know forgetting could be drawn.\n\n161\n00:06:57,560 --> 00:06:59,220\nWhat did the line show?\n\n162\n00:06:59,280 --> 00:07:00,560\nThe line went down, Mia.\n\n163\n00:07:00,980 --> 00:07:01,380\nFast.\n\n164\n00:07:01,940 --> 00:07:04,260\nAfter one hour, he had lost a lot.\n\n165\n00:07:04,340 --> 00:07:06,700\nAfter one day, he remembered less than half.\n\n166\n00:07:07,360 --> 00:07:09,680\nAfter a week, almost nothing.\n\n167\n00:07:09,720 --> 00:07:11,240\nHe called it the forgetting curve.\n\n168\n00:07:11,760 --> 00:07:13,440\nAnd it was the same every time.\n\n169\n00:07:14,100 --> 00:07:16,300\nLearn, and then fall down the curve.\n\n170\n00:07:16,980 --> 00:07:18,960\nMore than half in one day, Ben.\n\n171\n00:07:19,540 --> 00:07:20,820\nThat is fast.\n\n172\n00:07:21,540 --> 00:07:24,460\nDid he find anything that slowed the line down?\n\n173\n00:07:24,600 --> 00:07:26,309\nOr does everything fall like that?\n\n174\n00:07:26,920 --> 00:07:29,220\nEverything falls like that, Mia, the first time.\n\n175\n00:07:29,840 --> 00:07:33,700\nReal words, nonsense words, names, phone numbers.\n\n176\n00:07:33,740 --> 00:07:35,480\nThe first night is the same for everyone.\n\n177\n00:07:36,070 --> 00:07:38,620\nThat is why fifty words in one night can never work.\n\n178\n00:07:39,160 --> 00:07:41,220\nThe curve does not care how hard you tried.\n\n179\n00:07:41,720 --> 00:07:43,640\nIt only cares how many times you came back.\n\n180\n00:07:44,900 --> 00:07:46,200\nThe curve took them, Ben.\n\n181\n00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:49,460\nSo my fifty words were not learned badly.\n\n182\n00:07:50,030 --> 00:07:54,160\nThey were learned, and then they fell down the curve, the same night.\n\n183\n00:07:54,880 --> 00:07:55,860\nExactly, Mia.\n\n184\n00:07:55,890 --> 00:07:56,730\nThe same night.\n\n185\n00:07:57,380 --> 00:08:00,200\nBut Ebbinghaus found a second thing, and this is the good part.\n\n186\n00:08:00,800 --> 00:08:04,760\nWhen he learned the list again, the second time, he remembered it much faster.\n\n187\n00:08:05,430 --> 00:08:07,120\nThe words were not completely gone.\n\n188\n00:08:07,460 --> 00:08:08,180\nThey were sleeping.\n\n189\n00:08:08,760 --> 00:08:11,660\nAnd when he saw them again, they woke up quickly.\n\n190\n00:08:11,670 --> 00:08:15,920\n\"The words were sleeping, not dead.\" Ben, that is kind.\n\n191\n00:08:16,550 --> 00:08:17,580\nAnd the third time?\n\n192\n00:08:18,200 --> 00:08:19,490\nDid the line keep falling?\n\n193\n00:08:20,180 --> 00:08:23,840\nThe third time, a few days later, the line fell more slowly, Mia.\n\n194\n00:08:24,490 --> 00:08:26,360\nAnd the fourth time, slower again.\n\n195\n00:08:27,140 --> 00:08:32,080\nAnd after about five meetings, on different days, the line almost stopped falling.\n\n196\n00:08:32,160 --> 00:08:33,740\nHe remembered almost all of it.\n\n197\n00:08:34,420 --> 00:08:38,300\nFive times on five different days is close to forever.\n\n198\n00:08:38,400 --> 00:08:40,919\nFive times on five different days, Ben.\n\n199\n00:08:41,780 --> 00:08:44,540\nThat is the opposite of fifty times in one night.\n\n200\n00:08:45,130 --> 00:08:45,929\nSame fifty.\n\n201\n00:08:46,450 --> 00:08:47,329\nDifferent days.\n\n202\n00:08:48,060 --> 00:08:50,570\nAnd the day is the thing, not the number.\n\n203\n00:08:51,290 --> 00:08:54,960\n\"The day is the thing, not the number.\" Yes, Mia.\n\n204\n00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:56,720\nSo here is the whole idea of today.\n\n205\n00:08:57,680 --> 00:08:58,800\nOne word a day.\n\n206\n00:08:58,860 --> 00:09:00,000\nNot fifty.\n\n207\n00:09:00,060 --> 00:09:00,320\nOne.\n\n208\n00:09:01,060 --> 00:09:04,860\nAnd you meet that one word again, on purpose, over the next week.\n\n209\n00:09:05,540 --> 00:09:08,300\nBecause one word, met five times, is learned.\n\n210\n00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:12,020\nFifty words, met once, are seen.\n\n211\n00:09:12,049 --> 00:09:17,720\nAnd a year of one word a day is three hundred and sixty-five words, all learned, Ben.\n\n212\n00:09:18,280 --> 00:09:19,030\nNot seen.\n\n213\n00:09:19,700 --> 00:09:20,170\nLearned.\n\n214\n00:09:20,940 --> 00:09:22,580\nThat is more than my two hundred.\n\n215\n00:09:23,080 --> 00:09:24,700\nFrom one a day.\n\n216\n00:09:24,740 --> 00:09:26,670\nThe slow way is the fast way.\n\n217\n00:09:27,480 --> 00:09:35,560\n\"The slow way is the fast way.\" Mia, let me say one thing about the word \"learn,\" because I had it wrong for years.\n\n218\n00:09:36,540 --> 00:09:39,270\nI thought learning was the moment I understood something.\n\n219\n00:09:39,910 --> 00:09:40,390\nThe click.\n\n220\n00:09:41,130 --> 00:09:45,780\n\"Ah, shelf means estante.\" That click felt like learning.\n\n221\n00:09:45,840 --> 00:09:47,460\nAnd it is not, Ben?\n\n222\n00:09:47,520 --> 00:09:48,770\nThe click is not learning?\n\n223\n00:09:49,480 --> 00:09:53,860\nI have lived on that click for years, and it never felt like forgetting.\n\n224\n00:09:53,940 --> 00:09:57,680\nEbbinghaus showed that the click is the start of forgetting, Mia.\n\n225\n00:09:57,700 --> 00:09:58,810\nNot the end of learning.\n\n226\n00:09:59,510 --> 00:10:01,280\nThe click is the top of the curve.\n\n227\n00:10:02,140 --> 00:10:04,919\nLearning is what you do after the click.\n\n228\n00:10:04,980 --> 00:10:06,540\nThe again.\n\n229\n00:10:06,620 --> 00:10:10,140\nSo I learn a word in a week, not in a second.\n\n230\n00:10:10,180 --> 00:10:13,440\nAnd I remember it in a week, not in a second.\n\n231\n00:10:13,480 --> 00:10:19,460\n\"I learn a word in a week, not in a second.\" Ben, that changes what I do after the click.\n\n232\n00:10:20,140 --> 00:10:21,960\nBefore, the click was the end.\n\n233\n00:10:22,380 --> 00:10:23,320\nI closed the list.\n\n234\n00:10:24,060 --> 00:10:25,640\nNow the click is the beginning.\n\n235\n00:10:26,420 --> 00:10:28,080\nAnd it makes me kinder to myself.\n\n236\n00:10:28,720 --> 00:10:30,960\nIf a word falls, it is not my brain.\n\n237\n00:10:31,260 --> 00:10:32,120\nIt is the curve.\n\n238\n00:10:32,540 --> 00:10:33,540\nEveryone's curve.\n\n239\n00:10:34,060 --> 00:10:35,360\nEveryone's curve, Mia.\n\n240\n00:10:35,960 --> 00:10:37,260\nEven Ebbinghaus's curve.\n\n241\n00:10:38,180 --> 00:10:39,300\nAnd one more thing from him.\n\n242\n00:10:40,120 --> 00:10:44,740\nHe read his old lists years later, and the forgotten words came back fast.\n\n243\n00:10:45,460 --> 00:10:46,240\nNot from zero.\n\n244\n00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:48,980\nHe called it saving.\n\n245\n00:10:49,020 --> 00:10:51,190\nThe second learning is cheaper than the first.\n\n246\n00:10:52,080 --> 00:10:54,920\nSo your three thousand words are not gone.\n\n247\n00:10:54,960 --> 00:10:55,880\nThey are asleep.\n\n248\n00:10:56,460 --> 00:10:58,940\nMy three thousand are asleep, Ben.\n\n249\n00:10:59,060 --> 00:10:59,940\nI thought they were dead.\n\n250\n00:11:00,780 --> 00:11:03,180\nOne meeting, and some of them will remember me.\n\n251\n00:11:04,620 --> 00:11:06,020\nOkay, so that was part two.\n\n252\n00:11:06,340 --> 00:11:07,640\nWhy words disappear.\n\n253\n00:11:08,540 --> 00:11:10,860\nWe talked about Ebbinghaus and the forgetting curve.\n\n254\n00:11:11,400 --> 00:11:13,660\nWe said the words are sleeping, not dead.\n\n255\n00:11:14,320 --> 00:11:18,220\nAnd we said five meetings on five days is close to forever.\n\n256\n00:11:19,040 --> 00:11:20,140\nNow, part three.\n\n257\n00:11:20,840 --> 00:11:23,200\nSimple ways to learn one word a day.\n\n258\n00:11:23,820 --> 00:11:25,000\nBen, you go first.\n\n259\n00:11:25,640 --> 00:11:26,620\nOkay, part three.\n\n260\n00:11:27,460 --> 00:11:29,780\nMy first idea is choosing the word, Mia.\n\n261\n00:11:30,390 --> 00:11:32,100\nBecause that is where people get stuck.\n\n262\n00:11:32,850 --> 00:11:35,340\n\"Which word?\" Here is my rule.\n\n263\n00:11:36,120 --> 00:11:37,270\nThe word you missed.\n\n264\n00:11:38,360 --> 00:11:42,960\nYesterday, at some moment, you wanted to say something and could not.\n\n265\n00:11:43,020 --> 00:11:45,470\nLike me, in the shop, pointing at the rice.\n\n266\n00:11:46,320 --> 00:11:47,440\nThat is today's word.\n\n267\n00:11:47,940 --> 00:11:50,040\nThe word you missed, Ben.\n\n268\n00:11:50,050 --> 00:11:50,990\nNot from a list.\n\n269\n00:11:51,270 --> 00:11:52,060\nFrom your day.\n\n270\n00:11:52,850 --> 00:11:54,860\nThe list gives you words you might need.\n\n271\n00:11:55,330 --> 00:11:58,100\nThe day gives you words you did need.\n\n272\n00:11:58,140 --> 00:12:01,000\nAnd a word you needed has a story.\n\n273\n00:12:01,009 --> 00:12:03,040\n\"Shelf\" is not a word on a list.\n\n274\n00:12:03,580 --> 00:12:05,760\nIt is the moment in the shop when you pointed.\n\n275\n00:12:06,460 --> 00:12:07,740\nYou will remember the shop.\n\n276\n00:12:08,400 --> 00:12:10,040\nMy shelf has glue, Mia.\n\n277\n00:12:10,920 --> 00:12:11,800\nThat is the word.\n\n278\n00:12:12,620 --> 00:12:12,940\nGlue.\n\n279\n00:12:13,880 --> 00:12:15,780\nA word from a list has no glue.\n\n280\n00:12:16,720 --> 00:12:18,280\nA word from a shop has glue.\n\n281\n00:12:19,320 --> 00:12:21,840\nSo what do you do with the word, after you choose it?\n\n282\n00:12:22,680 --> 00:12:23,520\nA card, Ben.\n\n283\n00:12:23,870 --> 00:12:24,880\nMy second idea.\n\n284\n00:12:25,480 --> 00:12:27,540\nOne small card, in the pocket.\n\n285\n00:12:28,150 --> 00:12:28,860\n\"Estante.\n\n286\n00:12:29,580 --> 00:12:32,400\nShelf.\" And the \"again\" part.\n\n287\n00:12:33,080 --> 00:12:35,200\nI look at the card five times that day.\n\n288\n00:12:35,820 --> 00:12:36,560\nNot for long.\n\n289\n00:12:37,040 --> 00:12:37,700\nTwo seconds.\n\n290\n00:12:38,260 --> 00:12:38,960\nOn the bus.\n\n291\n00:12:39,360 --> 00:12:39,980\nAt lunch.\n\n292\n00:12:40,420 --> 00:12:41,240\nBefore bed.\n\n293\n00:12:42,020 --> 00:12:43,640\nAnd the next day, a new card.\n\n294\n00:12:44,160 --> 00:12:46,900\nBut the old card stays in the pocket for a week.\n\n295\n00:12:47,920 --> 00:12:51,060\nSo the old words come again by accident, Mia.\n\n296\n00:12:51,120 --> 00:12:56,240\nYour hand finds last Tuesday's card while looking for today's, and you remember last Tuesday's word.\n\n297\n00:12:57,200 --> 00:12:58,940\nYou made your pocket into the shop.\n\n298\n00:12:59,620 --> 00:13:03,880\nThe pocket says the word again, the way life said your two hundred words again.\n\n299\n00:13:05,020 --> 00:13:08,540\n\"The pocket says the word again.\" Yes, Ben.\n\n300\n00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:11,380\nBy accident, but not by accident.\n\n301\n00:13:11,960 --> 00:13:12,740\nAnd your version?\n\n302\n00:13:13,480 --> 00:13:14,680\nYou do not use cards.\n\n303\n00:13:15,200 --> 00:13:16,120\nWhat do you use?\n\n304\n00:13:17,240 --> 00:13:18,280\nI use a wall, Mia.\n\n305\n00:13:18,780 --> 00:13:19,660\nMy second idea.\n\n306\n00:13:20,340 --> 00:13:23,500\nOne word a day, on a small paper, on the kitchen wall.\n\n307\n00:13:24,220 --> 00:13:25,760\nAnd the paper stays for a month.\n\n308\n00:13:26,499 --> 00:13:29,500\nSo every morning, with my coffee, I see thirty words.\n\n309\n00:13:30,260 --> 00:13:31,620\nI do not study them.\n\n310\n00:13:31,660 --> 00:13:32,860\nI see them.\n\n311\n00:13:32,900 --> 00:13:33,640\nThirty seconds.\n\n312\n00:13:34,280 --> 00:13:36,960\nAnd they come again, every day, without trying.\n\n313\n00:13:38,360 --> 00:13:40,540\nThirty words on a kitchen wall, Ben.\n\n314\n00:13:41,160 --> 00:13:41,820\nAnd your wife?\n\n315\n00:13:42,300 --> 00:13:43,280\nDoes she read them too?\n\n316\n00:13:44,840 --> 00:13:46,840\nShe learns Spanish from my wall now, Mia.\n\n317\n00:13:47,340 --> 00:13:48,120\nWithout trying.\n\n318\n00:13:48,780 --> 00:13:51,480\nShe says \"estante\" when she cannot find the rice.\n\n319\n00:13:52,420 --> 00:13:54,120\nThat is the wall.\n\n320\n00:13:54,180 --> 00:13:55,100\nNobody studies it.\n\n321\n00:13:55,840 --> 00:13:57,120\nEverybody remembers it.\n\n322\n00:13:57,870 --> 00:14:00,410\nThe wall does the again for the whole kitchen.\n\n323\n00:14:01,770 --> 00:14:05,060\n\"Without trying.\" Ben, that is the key.\n\n324\n00:14:05,600 --> 00:14:08,880\nThe fifty-a-night method is all trying, and no again.\n\n325\n00:14:09,420 --> 00:14:13,120\nAnd the wall is no trying, and all again.\n\n326\n00:14:13,180 --> 00:14:15,740\nAnd Ebbinghaus says again is the thing.\n\n327\n00:14:16,440 --> 00:14:17,500\nNot trying.\n\n328\n00:14:18,800 --> 00:14:20,160\nAgain is the thing, Mia.\n\n329\n00:14:20,880 --> 00:14:22,540\nMy third idea is the mouth.\n\n330\n00:14:23,340 --> 00:14:26,330\nAt lunch, I take the day's word, and I use it once.\n\n331\n00:14:26,980 --> 00:14:28,640\nOut loud, if I can.\n\n332\n00:14:28,700 --> 00:14:29,330\nIn a sentence.\n\n333\n00:14:30,080 --> 00:14:30,989\nNot a good sentence.\n\n334\n00:14:31,749 --> 00:14:34,540\n\"The bread is on the shelf.\" Ugly is fine.\n\n335\n00:14:35,240 --> 00:14:35,809\nBut said.\n\n336\n00:14:36,560 --> 00:14:38,200\nBecause the mouth remembers.\n\n337\n00:14:39,160 --> 00:14:42,440\nA word that is said stays longer than a word that is seen.\n\n338\n00:14:43,740 --> 00:14:45,720\nThe mouth has its own curve, Ben.\n\n339\n00:14:46,260 --> 00:14:48,720\nThere are words I can read and cannot say.\n\n340\n00:14:49,260 --> 00:14:50,559\nMy mouth never met them.\n\n341\n00:14:51,460 --> 00:14:54,020\nCan I tell you about my worst week with the cards?\n\n342\n00:14:54,520 --> 00:14:56,060\nBecause I went too fast.\n\n343\n00:14:57,240 --> 00:14:58,140\nToo fast, Mia?\n\n344\n00:14:58,740 --> 00:14:59,780\nWith one word a day?\n\n345\n00:15:00,560 --> 00:15:02,200\nHow do you go too fast with one?\n\n346\n00:15:03,920 --> 00:15:05,040\nI got excited, Ben.\n\n347\n00:15:05,640 --> 00:15:07,380\nOne word felt too slow.\n\n348\n00:15:07,440 --> 00:15:09,180\nSo I did five a day.\n\n349\n00:15:09,220 --> 00:15:10,170\nFive cards.\n\n350\n00:15:10,680 --> 00:15:16,040\nAnd by Thursday, I had twenty cards in my pocket, and I could not remember which was which.\n\n351\n00:15:16,700 --> 00:15:17,820\nThe curve came back.\n\n352\n00:15:18,260 --> 00:15:21,040\nThe moment I went fast, the curve came back.\n\n353\n00:15:22,080 --> 00:15:28,440\n\"The curve comes back the moment you go fast.\" Mia, that is Ebbinghaus in one sentence.\n\n354\n00:15:29,220 --> 00:15:31,360\nAnd the fear says that, too.\n\n355\n00:15:31,370 --> 00:15:32,280\n\"One word a day?\n\n356\n00:15:32,860 --> 00:15:37,580\nI will be old before I speak English.\" So let me answer with numbers.\n\n357\n00:15:38,980 --> 00:15:39,800\nNumbers, Ben.\n\n358\n00:15:40,180 --> 00:15:40,560\nGo on.\n\n359\n00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:42,190\nI am ready for the numbers.\n\n360\n00:15:42,920 --> 00:15:46,160\nOne word a day is three hundred and sixty-five words a year, Mia.\n\n361\n00:15:46,900 --> 00:15:48,200\nLearned, not seen.\n\n362\n00:15:48,940 --> 00:15:51,920\nFifty words a night is three thousand words in two months.\n\n363\n00:15:52,630 --> 00:15:54,900\nSeen, and then two hundred remembered.\n\n364\n00:15:55,760 --> 00:15:56,690\nSo which is faster?\n\n365\n00:15:57,260 --> 00:16:00,410\nThree hundred and sixty-five learned, or two hundred remembered?\n\n366\n00:16:01,090 --> 00:16:02,220\nThe slow one wins.\n\n367\n00:16:03,900 --> 00:16:05,340\nThe slow one wins, Ben.\n\n368\n00:16:05,960 --> 00:16:07,180\nAnd it does not stop.\n\n369\n00:16:07,680 --> 00:16:08,580\nThat is the other thing.\n\n370\n00:16:09,100 --> 00:16:12,500\nFifty a night stops after two months, with tears.\n\n371\n00:16:13,240 --> 00:16:16,700\nOne a day goes on for thirty years, like your father.\n\n372\n00:16:17,480 --> 00:16:18,260\nWhich is faster?\n\n373\n00:16:18,860 --> 00:16:20,300\nThe one that does not stop.\n\n374\n00:16:21,460 --> 00:16:30,400\n\"The one that does not stop.\" Mia, my fourth idea is for our listeners, because many of you are doing this already, without knowing.\n\n375\n00:16:30,500 --> 00:16:32,940\nEvery episode, we repeat five words.\n\n376\n00:16:33,860 --> 00:16:34,200\nSmall.\n\n377\n00:16:34,960 --> 00:16:35,240\nStep.\n\n378\n00:16:36,020 --> 00:16:36,400\nMorning.\n\n379\n00:16:37,120 --> 00:16:37,460\nStart.\n\n380\n00:16:38,300 --> 00:16:38,580\nFeel.\n\n381\n00:16:39,420 --> 00:16:41,760\nAnd you meet them thirty times in one episode.\n\n382\n00:16:42,600 --> 00:16:43,420\nThat is the wall.\n\n383\n00:16:44,140 --> 00:16:45,140\nThat is the pocket.\n\n384\n00:16:46,140 --> 00:16:48,740\nAnd then, next episode, again, Ben.\n\n385\n00:16:49,260 --> 00:16:50,500\nOn a different day.\n\n386\n00:16:50,530 --> 00:16:51,879\nAnd the episode after that.\n\n387\n00:16:52,500 --> 00:16:54,580\nSo the five words are not seen.\n\n388\n00:16:55,000 --> 00:16:56,960\nThey are met, again and again.\n\n389\n00:16:57,660 --> 00:16:59,500\nYou remember them without trying.\n\n390\n00:17:00,060 --> 00:17:02,580\nYou are already on the slow fast road.\n\n391\n00:17:03,580 --> 00:17:05,260\nDo you have a fifth idea?\n\n392\n00:17:05,300 --> 00:17:05,890\nFor the evening?\n\n393\n00:17:06,880 --> 00:17:07,360\nYes, Mia.\n\n394\n00:17:08,080 --> 00:17:14,500\nBefore bed, after the one line from our writing episode, take the day's card and look at it one last time.\n\n395\n00:17:15,200 --> 00:17:15,800\nSay it once.\n\n396\n00:17:16,620 --> 00:17:17,270\nAnd then sleep.\n\n397\n00:17:18,220 --> 00:17:20,730\nAnd from our sleep episode, we know what happens.\n\n398\n00:17:21,390 --> 00:17:24,840\nThe brain moves the word from the waiting room to the long-term room.\n\n399\n00:17:25,540 --> 00:17:26,780\nYou remember it in the morning.\n\n400\n00:17:27,660 --> 00:17:30,320\nGive the night shift the word, not the phone.\n\n401\n00:17:31,460 --> 00:17:37,720\n\"Give the night shift the word, not the phone.\" Ben, I like how the episodes connect.\n\n402\n00:17:38,380 --> 00:17:41,820\nAnd one more idea from me, for the words that still do not stay.\n\n403\n00:17:42,260 --> 00:17:43,420\nBecause some do not.\n\n404\n00:17:44,100 --> 00:17:49,920\nI have a Spanish word, \"aunque,\" that I have met fifty times, and I still cannot remember it.\n\n405\n00:17:50,980 --> 00:17:51,960\nFifty times, Mia?\n\n406\n00:17:52,410 --> 00:17:53,300\nAnd it still falls?\n\n407\n00:17:54,080 --> 00:17:55,400\nWhat do you do with a word like that?\n\n408\n00:17:55,920 --> 00:17:56,300\nGive up?\n\n409\n00:17:57,320 --> 00:17:58,700\nI used to think, \"See?\n\n410\n00:17:59,100 --> 00:18:05,560\nMy memory is bad.\" Now I think, \"This word needs more glue.\" So I gave it a story.\n\n411\n00:18:06,160 --> 00:18:14,120\nI wrote one silly sentence about my sister and \"aunque.\" And now, when I see the word, I see my sister, and I remember it.\n\n412\n00:18:14,960 --> 00:18:17,780\nSome words need fifty meetings and a story.\n\n413\n00:18:18,500 --> 00:18:19,200\nAdd glue.\n\n414\n00:18:20,480 --> 00:18:21,300\nAdd glue, Mia.\n\n415\n00:18:22,120 --> 00:18:25,080\nAnd one last idea, for the people who learn with an app.\n\n416\n00:18:25,800 --> 00:18:27,760\nMany apps do this well now.\n\n417\n00:18:27,800 --> 00:18:32,169\nThey show you the word again on day one, day three, day seven.\n\n418\n00:18:32,920 --> 00:18:34,620\nThat is Ebbinghaus in your phone.\n\n419\n00:18:35,340 --> 00:18:35,600\nGood.\n\n420\n00:18:36,020 --> 00:18:36,380\nKeep it.\n\n421\n00:18:36,940 --> 00:18:38,470\nBut the app remembers for you.\n\n422\n00:18:39,240 --> 00:18:41,880\nAnd the word you needed in the shop is not in the app.\n\n423\n00:18:42,640 --> 00:18:43,920\n\"The app gives you again.\n\n424\n00:18:44,400 --> 00:18:49,780\nThe pocket gives you glue.\" Ben, that is exactly the difference.\n\n425\n00:18:50,240 --> 00:18:52,840\nMy app had \"shelf\" in it for two years.\n\n426\n00:18:53,120 --> 00:18:54,860\nI met it again and again.\n\n427\n00:18:55,000 --> 00:18:56,400\nI remembered it in the app.\n\n428\n00:18:56,900 --> 00:18:59,260\nAnd I still could not say it in the shop.\n\n429\n00:18:59,340 --> 00:19:01,200\nIt was a list word.\n\n430\n00:19:01,240 --> 00:19:05,460\nThen the shop happened, and it became a life word.\n\n431\n00:19:05,480 --> 00:19:06,520\nA life word, Mia.\n\n432\n00:19:07,270 --> 00:19:09,200\nAnd that is our message today.\n\n433\n00:19:09,320 --> 00:19:11,640\nSeeing a word is not learning it.\n\n434\n00:19:11,650 --> 00:19:13,960\nThe curve takes what you see, the same night.\n\n435\n00:19:14,560 --> 00:19:16,920\nLearning is meeting the word again.\n\n436\n00:19:16,980 --> 00:19:17,829\nOn different days.\n\n437\n00:19:18,500 --> 00:19:19,160\nFive times.\n\n438\n00:19:19,920 --> 00:19:20,630\nWith a little glue.\n\n439\n00:19:21,570 --> 00:19:23,540\nSo do not learn fifty words tonight.\n\n440\n00:19:24,240 --> 00:19:24,800\nLearn one.\n\n441\n00:19:25,580 --> 00:19:26,540\nPut it in your pocket.\n\n442\n00:19:27,300 --> 00:19:28,380\nMeet it again tomorrow.\n\n443\n00:19:29,340 --> 00:19:31,140\nThe slow way is the fast way.\n\n444\n00:19:32,380 --> 00:19:34,600\nThe slow way is the fast way.\n\n445\n00:19:35,580 --> 00:19:40,840\nBen, let's repeat today's important words, slowly, because our listeners want to remember them.\n\n446\n00:19:42,420 --> 00:19:42,820\nWord.\n\n447\n00:19:43,680 --> 00:19:44,260\nOne word.\n\n448\n00:19:44,920 --> 00:19:45,920\nThe day's word.\n\n449\n00:19:47,320 --> 00:19:48,220\nA word with glue.\n\n450\n00:19:49,780 --> 00:19:50,220\nLearn.\n\n451\n00:19:50,960 --> 00:19:52,180\nTo make something yours.\n\n452\n00:19:53,520 --> 00:19:54,960\nLearn one word a day.\n\n453\n00:19:56,260 --> 00:19:57,700\nSeeing is not learning.\n\n454\n00:19:58,940 --> 00:19:59,320\nAgain.\n\n455\n00:19:59,680 --> 00:20:00,380\nOne more time.\n\n456\n00:20:01,600 --> 00:20:02,820\nMeet the word again.\n\n457\n00:20:03,700 --> 00:20:05,100\nAgain is the thing.\n\n458\n00:20:06,440 --> 00:20:06,920\nRemember.\n\n459\n00:20:07,840 --> 00:20:08,800\nTo keep in the head.\n\n460\n00:20:09,700 --> 00:20:10,640\nRemember the word.\n\n461\n00:20:11,100 --> 00:20:12,520\nThe words remember you.\n\n462\n00:20:13,850 --> 00:20:15,100\nDay.\n\n463\n00:20:15,220 --> 00:20:16,280\nOne word a day.\n\n464\n00:20:16,840 --> 00:20:18,060\nFive different days.\n\n465\n00:20:19,680 --> 00:20:19,980\nWord.\n\n466\n00:20:21,080 --> 00:20:21,400\nLearn.\n\n467\n00:20:22,660 --> 00:20:23,020\nAgain.\n\n468\n00:20:24,360 --> 00:20:24,800\nRemember.\n\n469\n00:20:26,150 --> 00:20:27,640\nDay.\n\n470\n00:20:27,740 --> 00:20:30,420\nFive words, thirty times, on purpose.\n\n471\n00:20:30,920 --> 00:20:32,110\nAnd one big idea.\n\n472\n00:20:33,120 --> 00:20:34,500\nLearn one word a day.\n\n473\n00:20:35,720 --> 00:20:37,880\nNow, Mia, do you have a question for our listeners?\n\n474\n00:20:38,960 --> 00:20:39,500\nYes, Ben.\n\n475\n00:20:39,960 --> 00:20:42,020\nEveryone listening, here is my question.\n\n476\n00:20:42,620 --> 00:20:43,950\nWhat is your word for today?\n\n477\n00:20:44,370 --> 00:20:47,300\nThe one you missed yesterday, or one from this episode.\n\n478\n00:20:47,880 --> 00:20:50,390\nWrite it in the comments in one short sentence.\n\n479\n00:20:50,949 --> 00:20:56,549\n\"My word today is shelf.\" And then, tomorrow, come back and write it again.\n\n480\n00:20:57,060 --> 00:20:58,100\nWe will see it twice.\n\n481\n00:20:58,500 --> 00:20:59,800\nThat is two meetings.\n\n482\n00:21:01,000 --> 00:21:01,820\nTwo meetings, Mia.\n\n483\n00:21:02,609 --> 00:21:05,860\nAnd if this episode helped you, please subscribe to Two Cups English.\n\n484\n00:21:06,480 --> 00:21:09,200\nWe make a new episode every few days.\n\n485\n00:21:09,280 --> 00:21:13,440\nSlow English, simple words, and one true story each time.\n\n486\n00:21:14,400 --> 00:21:16,690\nThe full text of this episode is in the description.\n\n487\n00:21:17,420 --> 00:21:20,400\nRead it once now, and once next week.\n\n488\n00:21:20,440 --> 00:21:22,560\nThat is two meetings with every word in it.\n\n489\n00:21:23,640 --> 00:21:25,520\nThank you for spending this time with us.\n\n490\n00:21:25,940 --> 00:21:27,120\nMaybe you are on the bus.\n\n491\n00:21:27,640 --> 00:21:28,720\nMaybe you have a pen.\n\n492\n00:21:29,360 --> 00:21:30,420\nChoose one word.\n\n493\n00:21:30,920 --> 00:21:31,880\nWrite it on something.\n\n494\n00:21:32,400 --> 00:21:33,340\nPut it in your pocket.\n\n495\n00:21:33,630 --> 00:21:35,060\nAnd meet it again tonight.\n\n496\n00:21:35,600 --> 00:21:36,220\nThat is all.\n\n497\n00:21:36,820 --> 00:21:37,560\nNot fifty.\n\n498\n00:21:38,260 --> 00:21:38,520\nOne.\n\n499\n00:21:39,300 --> 00:21:40,840\nTomorrow, another one.\n\n500\n00:21:41,400 --> 00:21:43,040\nIn a year, a language.\n\n501\n00:21:44,080 --> 00:21:44,740\nThis is Ben.\n\n502\n00:21:45,460 --> 00:21:46,299\nAnd this is Mia.\n\n503\n00:21:47,200 --> 00:21:48,600\nThis was Two Cups English.\n\n504\n00:21:49,140 --> 00:21:50,160\nOne word a day.\n\n505\n00:21:50,940 --> 00:21:51,919\nWe will see you next time."},{"code":"CUP13","file":"CUP13.srt","srt":"1\n00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,260\nHello everyone, and welcome to Two Cups English.\n\n2\n00:00:03,800 --> 00:00:06,699\nI'm Mia, and Ben is here with me.\n\n3\n00:00:06,720 --> 00:00:09,560\nThis podcast is for people who are learning English.\n\n4\n00:00:10,220 --> 00:00:11,060\nWe speak slowly.\n\n5\n00:00:11,620 --> 00:00:12,980\nWe use simple words.\n\n6\n00:00:13,560 --> 00:00:16,419\nAnd we repeat the important words many times.\n\n7\n00:00:17,360 --> 00:00:19,960\nToday, our topic is doing it badly.\n\n8\n00:00:20,690 --> 00:00:21,990\nDoing it badly first.\n\n9\n00:00:22,880 --> 00:00:25,390\nBen, what is the worst thing you ever made?\n\n10\n00:00:26,640 --> 00:00:27,600\nHi Mia.\n\n11\n00:00:27,660 --> 00:00:28,880\nHi everyone.\n\n12\n00:00:28,920 --> 00:00:32,920\nThe worst thing I ever made is my first podcast episode, Mia.\n\n13\n00:00:33,660 --> 00:00:34,680\nMy voice shook.\n\n14\n00:00:35,340 --> 00:00:37,290\nI said \"um\" fifty times.\n\n15\n00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:39,740\nFor weeks, I wanted to delete it.\n\n16\n00:00:40,460 --> 00:00:42,460\nI made it in my kitchen, with a phone.\n\n17\n00:00:43,860 --> 00:00:44,780\nAnd I still have it.\n\n18\n00:00:45,680 --> 00:00:46,880\nWhat about you?\n\n19\n00:00:46,920 --> 00:00:47,980\nWhat is your worst thing?\n\n20\n00:00:49,460 --> 00:00:50,220\nA bowl, Ben.\n\n21\n00:00:50,980 --> 00:00:52,710\nOn my kitchen shelf, there is a bowl.\n\n22\n00:00:53,400 --> 00:00:54,410\nIt is terrible.\n\n23\n00:00:55,140 --> 00:00:58,040\nOne side is high, one side is low.\n\n24\n00:00:58,080 --> 00:01:00,000\nIt does not stand straight.\n\n25\n00:01:00,010 --> 00:01:02,080\nThe color is grey where it should be blue.\n\n26\n00:01:03,020 --> 00:01:06,220\nI made it, three years ago, in my first pottery class.\n\n27\n00:01:06,880 --> 00:01:08,220\nI made it with my own hands.\n\n28\n00:01:08,880 --> 00:01:10,940\nA terrible bowl on the shelf, Mia.\n\n29\n00:01:11,500 --> 00:01:12,340\nNot in a cupboard.\n\n30\n00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:14,900\nOn the shelf, where people can see it.\n\n31\n00:01:15,720 --> 00:01:17,360\nWhy do you keep it there?\n\n32\n00:01:17,400 --> 00:01:20,460\nBecause it is my favorite thing in the house, Ben.\n\n33\n00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:21,200\nIt is proof.\n\n34\n00:01:21,840 --> 00:01:23,120\nProof that I started.\n\n35\n00:01:23,820 --> 00:01:26,230\nFor thirty years, I did not make things.\n\n36\n00:01:26,920 --> 00:01:30,550\nI looked at people who made things, and I thought, \"They are talented.\n\n37\n00:01:30,940 --> 00:01:35,680\nI am not.\" And I did not try, because I knew the first thing would be bad.\n\n38\n00:01:36,420 --> 00:01:42,660\n\"I did not try, because I knew the first thing would be bad.\" Mia, that is our topic today.\n\n39\n00:01:43,520 --> 00:01:45,220\nSo here is our plan.\n\n40\n00:01:45,280 --> 00:01:48,870\nIn part one, we talk about our own bad first things.\n\n41\n00:01:49,760 --> 00:01:54,500\nIn part two, we ask why bad first is the only way to good.\n\n42\n00:01:55,540 --> 00:01:59,260\nAnd I have a true story for that, from a pottery class.\n\n43\n00:01:59,320 --> 00:02:04,040\nIn part three, we share simple ways to do something badly today.\n\n44\n00:02:04,100 --> 00:02:05,580\nDoes that sound good?\n\n45\n00:02:05,600 --> 00:02:06,660\nThat sounds good, Ben.\n\n46\n00:02:07,280 --> 00:02:11,130\nAnd to everyone listening, please do not worry if you miss something.\n\n47\n00:02:11,510 --> 00:02:12,460\nYou will hear it again.\n\n48\n00:02:13,240 --> 00:02:16,400\nAnd if you say a word badly today, good.\n\n49\n00:02:16,460 --> 00:02:17,500\nThat is the topic.\n\n50\n00:02:18,340 --> 00:02:18,900\nOkay, Ben.\n\n51\n00:02:19,200 --> 00:02:20,260\nPart one.\n\n52\n00:02:20,340 --> 00:02:22,160\nYou wanted to ask about my first lesson?\n\n53\n00:02:22,900 --> 00:02:23,480\nI did, Mia.\n\n54\n00:02:24,109 --> 00:02:25,620\nThe first pottery lesson.\n\n55\n00:02:26,540 --> 00:02:28,690\nWhat did you try to make, and what happened?\n\n56\n00:02:29,900 --> 00:02:31,280\nA cup, Ben.\n\n57\n00:02:31,340 --> 00:02:32,160\nA simple cup.\n\n58\n00:02:32,800 --> 00:02:35,260\nAnd the clay went everywhere.\n\n59\n00:02:35,320 --> 00:02:38,820\nIt went up, it went sideways, it fell over.\n\n60\n00:02:38,860 --> 00:02:40,420\nThree times.\n\n61\n00:02:40,440 --> 00:02:42,320\nThe clay does not care what you want.\n\n62\n00:02:42,980 --> 00:02:47,190\nAnd the teacher came, and I said, \"I am sorry, I am not good at this.\"\n\n63\n00:02:47,950 --> 00:02:52,640\n\"I am not good at this.\" Mia, everyone says that in the first lesson.\n\n64\n00:02:53,360 --> 00:02:54,300\nWhat did the teacher say?\n\n65\n00:02:55,060 --> 00:02:56,780\nShe said something I still hear, Ben.\n\n66\n00:02:57,530 --> 00:02:58,780\n\"Nobody is good at this.\n\n67\n00:02:59,300 --> 00:03:03,560\nThat is why it is a class.\" And then she said, \"Make a bad one.\n\n68\n00:03:04,100 --> 00:03:05,530\nThen make another bad one.\n\n69\n00:03:06,109 --> 00:03:10,290\nThe good one is inside the bad ones.\" So I made the bad bowl.\n\n70\n00:03:10,800 --> 00:03:11,490\nAnd I kept it.\n\n71\n00:03:12,200 --> 00:03:19,200\n\"The good one is inside the bad ones.\" Mia, that is exactly what happened with my podcast.\n\n72\n00:03:19,280 --> 00:03:20,540\nThe first one was bad.\n\n73\n00:03:21,040 --> 00:03:22,940\nThe second was a little less bad.\n\n74\n00:03:23,440 --> 00:03:25,040\nAnd the tenth one, I liked.\n\n75\n00:03:25,740 --> 00:03:29,760\nAnd when I listen to the first one now, it sounds worse every year.\n\n76\n00:03:30,600 --> 00:03:31,740\nThe tenth one, Ben.\n\n77\n00:03:32,510 --> 00:03:33,940\nWhat was different about the tenth?\n\n78\n00:03:34,460 --> 00:03:36,320\nDid you learn a trick between one and ten?\n\n79\n00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:37,680\nNo trick, Mia.\n\n80\n00:03:38,260 --> 00:03:39,800\nNine bad ones.\n\n81\n00:03:39,860 --> 00:03:40,720\nThat is the trick.\n\n82\n00:03:41,480 --> 00:03:43,290\nEach bad one taught my mouth something.\n\n83\n00:03:43,760 --> 00:03:44,860\nWhere to breathe.\n\n84\n00:03:44,900 --> 00:03:45,660\nWhen to stop.\n\n85\n00:03:46,400 --> 00:03:48,840\nI could not learn that from a book about podcasts.\n\n86\n00:03:49,440 --> 00:03:51,560\nI learned it from making bad podcasts.\n\n87\n00:03:52,100 --> 00:03:52,760\nNine of them.\n\n88\n00:03:53,420 --> 00:03:55,880\nThe tenth was made by a person who had made nine.\n\n89\n00:03:56,380 --> 00:03:57,740\nYou cannot skip the nine.\n\n90\n00:03:58,220 --> 00:03:58,800\nYou make them.\n\n91\n00:03:59,600 --> 00:04:00,900\nWorse every year, Ben?\n\n92\n00:04:01,500 --> 00:04:02,220\nWhy worse?\n\n93\n00:04:02,900 --> 00:04:04,820\nI thought the old ones get better with time.\n\n94\n00:04:06,220 --> 00:04:07,800\nBecause I got better, Mia.\n\n95\n00:04:08,380 --> 00:04:09,840\nThe bad one does not change.\n\n96\n00:04:10,300 --> 00:04:10,880\nI change.\n\n97\n00:04:11,400 --> 00:04:12,620\nSo the distance grows.\n\n98\n00:04:13,240 --> 00:04:14,460\nThat is why I keep it.\n\n99\n00:04:15,100 --> 00:04:15,780\nIt is a ruler.\n\n100\n00:04:16,440 --> 00:04:18,260\nIt shows how far I walked from there.\n\n101\n00:04:19,100 --> 00:04:21,210\nI look at it when I feel that I am not moving.\n\n102\n00:04:21,970 --> 00:04:26,430\n\"The bad one is a ruler.\" Ben, my bowl is the same.\n\n103\n00:04:27,080 --> 00:04:30,640\nIt looks worse every year, because I got better.\n\n104\n00:04:30,680 --> 00:04:31,740\nCan I talk about the fear?\n\n105\n00:04:32,460 --> 00:04:35,020\nBecause I think the fear is the whole problem.\n\n106\n00:04:35,620 --> 00:04:36,360\nPlease, Mia.\n\n107\n00:04:36,820 --> 00:04:38,080\nWhat is the fear, exactly?\n\n108\n00:04:38,580 --> 00:04:39,760\nThe bad bowl itself?\n\n109\n00:04:40,180 --> 00:04:40,980\nOr something else?\n\n110\n00:04:41,820 --> 00:04:42,360\nNo, Ben.\n\n111\n00:04:42,870 --> 00:04:44,720\nThe fear is not of making the bowl.\n\n112\n00:04:45,340 --> 00:04:47,900\nThe fear is of being seen making a bad bowl.\n\n113\n00:04:48,500 --> 00:04:50,080\nThe teacher watching.\n\n114\n00:04:50,140 --> 00:04:51,040\nThe other students.\n\n115\n00:04:51,740 --> 00:04:58,960\nIn my head, everyone was looking at my grey bowl and thinking, \"She is not talented.\" Nobody was.\n\n116\n00:04:59,460 --> 00:05:01,680\nThey were looking at their own clay.\n\n117\n00:05:02,600 --> 00:05:04,580\nThey were looking at their own clay, Mia.\n\n118\n00:05:05,140 --> 00:05:05,910\nEveryone is.\n\n119\n00:05:06,700 --> 00:05:09,340\nNobody in the class remembers your first bowl.\n\n120\n00:05:09,400 --> 00:05:10,420\nThey remember their own.\n\n121\n00:05:11,050 --> 00:05:12,530\nThat is the secret of the fear.\n\n122\n00:05:13,240 --> 00:05:15,980\nThe audience is not there.\n\n123\n00:05:16,020 --> 00:05:18,070\nOkay, so that was part one.\n\n124\n00:05:18,100 --> 00:05:19,920\nOur bad first things.\n\n125\n00:05:19,960 --> 00:05:20,900\nNow, part two.\n\n126\n00:05:21,620 --> 00:05:24,020\nWhy is bad first the only way to good?\n\n127\n00:05:24,920 --> 00:05:27,360\nMia, my story is from a pottery class, too.\n\n128\n00:05:28,080 --> 00:05:29,720\nAnother pottery class, Ben?\n\n129\n00:05:30,400 --> 00:05:33,480\nA real one, with real clay and a real teacher?\n\n130\n00:05:34,160 --> 00:05:35,100\nA real one, Mia.\n\n131\n00:05:36,080 --> 00:05:37,060\nOkay, part two.\n\n132\n00:05:37,680 --> 00:05:39,000\nThis is a true story.\n\n133\n00:05:39,580 --> 00:05:46,660\nIt comes from a book called \"Art and Fear,\" written in 1993 by two artists, David Bayles and Ted Orland.\n\n134\n00:05:47,270 --> 00:05:50,199\nThe book is about why people stop making things.\n\n135\n00:05:50,300 --> 00:05:52,120\nThey tell a story about a pottery teacher.\n\n136\n00:05:52,960 --> 00:05:56,320\nOn the first day, the teacher divided the class into two groups.\n\n137\n00:05:57,020 --> 00:05:57,900\nTwo groups, Ben?\n\n138\n00:05:58,600 --> 00:05:59,860\nWhat was the difference between them?\n\n139\n00:06:00,680 --> 00:06:02,780\nThe first group was the quantity group, Mia.\n\n140\n00:06:03,340 --> 00:06:04,529\nTheir grade was by weight.\n\n141\n00:06:05,060 --> 00:06:06,760\nFifty pounds of pots, an A.\n\n142\n00:06:07,260 --> 00:06:08,520\nForty pounds, a B.\n\n143\n00:06:09,280 --> 00:06:10,430\nOnly the amount mattered.\n\n144\n00:06:10,720 --> 00:06:11,360\nNot how good.\n\n145\n00:06:11,940 --> 00:06:14,320\nFifty pounds is a lot of clay.\n\n146\n00:06:14,380 --> 00:06:16,640\nThe second group was the quality group.\n\n147\n00:06:16,700 --> 00:06:18,820\nThey had to make only one pot.\n\n148\n00:06:18,840 --> 00:06:20,040\nBut it had to be perfect.\n\n149\n00:06:20,580 --> 00:06:22,380\nOne perfect pot for an A.\n\n150\n00:06:24,120 --> 00:06:27,180\nOne perfect pot, or fifty pounds of pots, Ben.\n\n151\n00:06:28,060 --> 00:06:30,100\nI know which group I would have chosen.\n\n152\n00:06:30,120 --> 00:06:31,800\nThe perfect one.\n\n153\n00:06:31,940 --> 00:06:32,859\nAnd?\n\n154\n00:06:32,940 --> 00:06:35,860\nAt the end of the term, the teacher looked at all the work, Mia.\n\n155\n00:06:36,390 --> 00:06:40,760\nAnd the best pots, the most beautiful ones, all came from the quantity group.\n\n156\n00:06:41,400 --> 00:06:41,900\nAll of them.\n\n157\n00:06:42,450 --> 00:06:45,940\nThe group that was told to make many pots, badly, quickly.\n\n158\n00:06:46,600 --> 00:06:47,420\nAll of them, Ben?\n\n159\n00:06:47,940 --> 00:06:49,380\nFrom the badly-many group?\n\n160\n00:06:50,040 --> 00:06:51,420\nAnd the perfect-pot group?\n\n161\n00:06:51,980 --> 00:06:53,940\nThe perfect-pot group sat, Mia.\n\n162\n00:06:54,400 --> 00:06:55,160\nThey planned.\n\n163\n00:06:55,640 --> 00:06:56,320\nThey drew.\n\n164\n00:06:56,800 --> 00:06:58,480\nThey thought about the perfect pot.\n\n165\n00:06:58,980 --> 00:07:04,900\nAnd at the end, they had a lot of ideas about pots, and one pot that was not very good.\n\n166\n00:07:04,960 --> 00:07:08,620\nBecause you cannot learn to make a pot by thinking about a pot.\n\n167\n00:07:08,630 --> 00:07:10,800\nThey had ideas about pots, Ben.\n\n168\n00:07:11,420 --> 00:07:12,480\nBut no pots.\n\n169\n00:07:13,260 --> 00:07:14,360\nAnd the quantity group?\n\n170\n00:07:15,040 --> 00:07:17,600\nDid they hate their fifty pounds of bad pots?\n\n171\n00:07:19,220 --> 00:07:20,320\nThey laughed at them, Mia.\n\n172\n00:07:20,900 --> 00:07:23,080\nThat is the other half of the story.\n\n173\n00:07:23,090 --> 00:07:27,440\nThe quantity group made a bad pot, laughed, and made the next one.\n\n174\n00:07:27,480 --> 00:07:28,090\nNo shame.\n\n175\n00:07:28,520 --> 00:07:29,540\nNo punishment room.\n\n176\n00:07:30,030 --> 00:07:32,160\nThe pot was bad, and they were fine.\n\n177\n00:07:32,720 --> 00:07:35,540\nAnd by pot number thirty, the hands knew what to do.\n\n178\n00:07:36,020 --> 00:07:36,980\nThe hands learned.\n\n179\n00:07:37,480 --> 00:07:38,160\nNot the head.\n\n180\n00:07:38,810 --> 00:07:46,080\n\"You cannot learn to make a pot by thinking about a pot.\" Ben, that is my class exactly.\n\n181\n00:07:46,140 --> 00:07:49,060\nThere was a woman who made one bowl in six weeks.\n\n182\n00:07:49,740 --> 00:07:50,140\nOne.\n\n183\n00:07:50,840 --> 00:07:52,660\nShe worked on it every lesson.\n\n184\n00:07:53,280 --> 00:07:56,280\nFixing, smoothing, starting again.\n\n185\n00:07:56,320 --> 00:07:58,099\nAnd at the end, it was okay.\n\n186\n00:07:58,920 --> 00:08:06,560\nMine were bad, bad, bad, bad, and then, the eleventh one, the teacher put in the window.\n\n187\n00:08:07,260 --> 00:08:09,200\nThe eleventh one went in the window, Mia.\n\n188\n00:08:09,690 --> 00:08:11,560\nAnd the ten bad ones went in the bin.\n\n189\n00:08:12,140 --> 00:08:13,560\nNobody saw them.\n\n190\n00:08:13,620 --> 00:08:18,340\nSo the story does not say \"quality does not matter.\" Quality matters.\n\n191\n00:08:18,380 --> 00:08:20,120\nThe point is where quality comes from.\n\n192\n00:08:20,680 --> 00:08:22,740\nIt does not come from wanting it.\n\n193\n00:08:22,760 --> 00:08:24,200\nIt comes from the bad ones.\n\n194\n00:08:24,889 --> 00:08:31,500\n\"Quality comes from the bad ones.\" Ben, so the bad bowl is not the opposite of the good bowl.\n\n195\n00:08:32,040 --> 00:08:33,300\nIt is the road to it.\n\n196\n00:08:33,880 --> 00:08:35,520\nAnd there is no other road.\n\n197\n00:08:36,300 --> 00:08:40,700\nNobody walks from \"never tried\" to \"good\" without walking through \"bad.\"\n\n198\n00:08:41,340 --> 00:08:43,160\nThere is no other first step, Mia.\n\n199\n00:08:43,880 --> 00:08:47,880\nAnd let me say something about the word \"try,\" because it is smaller than it sounds.\n\n200\n00:08:48,640 --> 00:08:51,360\nWe say \"I will try\" like it is a promise.\n\n201\n00:08:51,940 --> 00:08:52,540\nIt is not.\n\n202\n00:08:53,280 --> 00:08:54,900\nA try is one attempt.\n\n203\n00:08:55,620 --> 00:08:56,230\nOne bowl.\n\n204\n00:08:56,960 --> 00:08:57,560\nOne sentence.\n\n205\n00:08:58,280 --> 00:08:59,740\nIt has no promise inside it.\n\n206\n00:09:00,400 --> 00:09:02,520\nIt can fail, and it is still a try.\n\n207\n00:09:03,200 --> 00:09:05,100\nA try is small on purpose.\n\n208\n00:09:05,780 --> 00:09:08,490\nYou make a try, you look, you make another.\n\n209\n00:09:09,320 --> 00:09:11,680\nA try that fails is still a try, Ben.\n\n210\n00:09:12,380 --> 00:09:18,100\nI always heard \"try\" as \"succeed, but with effort.\" So trying felt dangerous.\n\n211\n00:09:18,620 --> 00:09:20,880\nIf I try and fail, I failed.\n\n212\n00:09:21,440 --> 00:09:23,720\nBut the quantity group was not making pots.\n\n213\n00:09:24,240 --> 00:09:25,860\nThey were making tries.\n\n214\n00:09:26,720 --> 00:09:28,680\nFifty pounds of tries.\n\n215\n00:09:29,500 --> 00:09:36,840\n\"Fifty pounds of tries.\" Mia, and I want to say what \"better\" means, because it is the kindest word today.\n\n216\n00:09:36,940 --> 00:09:38,430\nNot \"good.\" Better.\n\n217\n00:09:39,280 --> 00:09:40,740\nThe second bowl was not good.\n\n218\n00:09:41,240 --> 00:09:42,540\nIt was better than the first.\n\n219\n00:09:43,140 --> 00:09:44,780\nThe third was better than the second.\n\n220\n00:09:45,740 --> 00:09:46,800\nGood is a wall.\n\n221\n00:09:47,560 --> 00:09:48,760\nBetter is a stair.\n\n222\n00:09:49,810 --> 00:09:50,940\n\"Good is a wall.\n\n223\n00:09:51,560 --> 00:09:56,660\nBetter is a stair.\" Ben, that should be on a poster next to my bowl.\n\n224\n00:09:57,660 --> 00:09:59,120\nOkay, so that was part two.\n\n225\n00:09:59,680 --> 00:10:01,540\nWhy bad first works.\n\n226\n00:10:02,120 --> 00:10:05,200\nWe talked about the two groups and fifty pounds of pots.\n\n227\n00:10:05,760 --> 00:10:08,060\nWe said quality comes from the bad ones.\n\n228\n00:10:08,500 --> 00:10:10,380\nAnd we said better is a stair.\n\n229\n00:10:11,160 --> 00:10:12,240\nNow, part three.\n\n230\n00:10:13,080 --> 00:10:15,480\nSimple ways to do something badly today.\n\n231\n00:10:16,140 --> 00:10:17,420\nBen, you go first.\n\n232\n00:10:18,220 --> 00:10:19,000\nOkay, part three.\n\n233\n00:10:19,590 --> 00:10:23,600\nMy first idea is for English, Mia, because this is where I see the fear most.\n\n234\n00:10:24,420 --> 00:10:27,080\nIn every class, there is a student who does not speak.\n\n235\n00:10:27,780 --> 00:10:28,610\nThey know the grammar.\n\n236\n00:10:28,950 --> 00:10:29,810\nThey know the words.\n\n237\n00:10:30,320 --> 00:10:32,140\nThey are waiting for the good sentence.\n\n238\n00:10:32,810 --> 00:10:35,870\nAnd the students who wait for the good sentence never say one.\n\n239\n00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:38,300\nI was that student, Ben.\n\n240\n00:10:38,680 --> 00:10:39,780\nIn Spanish class.\n\n241\n00:10:40,220 --> 00:10:41,840\nWaiting for the good sentence.\n\n242\n00:10:42,740 --> 00:10:43,980\nWhat is your idea for them?\n\n243\n00:10:45,680 --> 00:10:47,340\nFifty pounds of pots, Mia.\n\n244\n00:10:47,860 --> 00:10:49,480\nFifty bad sentences.\n\n245\n00:10:50,300 --> 00:10:52,500\nSay fifty bad sentences this week.\n\n246\n00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:53,540\nOut loud.\n\n247\n00:10:53,940 --> 00:10:54,440\nTo people.\n\n248\n00:10:55,100 --> 00:10:58,100\nWrong grammar, wrong word, wrong sound.\n\n249\n00:10:58,860 --> 00:10:59,260\nFifty.\n\n250\n00:11:00,020 --> 00:11:03,840\nAnd the good sentence will be inside them, somewhere around number thirty.\n\n251\n00:11:04,840 --> 00:11:06,700\nThat is the quantity group, in English.\n\n252\n00:11:07,660 --> 00:11:09,900\nFifty is not a magic number.\n\n253\n00:11:09,960 --> 00:11:12,060\nIt is a big pile.\n\n254\n00:11:12,700 --> 00:11:15,440\nFifty bad sentences to people, Ben.\n\n255\n00:11:15,480 --> 00:11:17,600\nMy face is already red.\n\n256\n00:11:17,699 --> 00:11:20,300\nHow do you say a bad sentence and not die?\n\n257\n00:11:21,149 --> 00:11:23,400\nYou say it and you make the next one, Mia.\n\n258\n00:11:24,180 --> 00:11:26,200\nThat is the whole trick, again.\n\n259\n00:11:26,240 --> 00:11:28,600\nThe bad sentence is over in three seconds.\n\n260\n00:11:29,190 --> 00:11:30,960\nThe red face is over in ten.\n\n261\n00:11:31,640 --> 00:11:34,100\nAnd the person you spoke to has already answered.\n\n262\n00:11:34,810 --> 00:11:36,500\nThey are not thinking about your grammar.\n\n263\n00:11:37,040 --> 00:11:38,760\nThey are thinking about their answer.\n\n264\n00:11:39,620 --> 00:11:40,940\nMake the next sentence.\n\n265\n00:11:41,680 --> 00:11:43,280\nNumber two is already better.\n\n266\n00:11:44,940 --> 00:11:46,640\nFifty bad sentences, Ben.\n\n267\n00:11:47,240 --> 00:11:49,080\nI can do that in one day at the market.\n\n268\n00:11:49,810 --> 00:11:52,429\nMy first idea is about the afternoon.\n\n269\n00:11:52,480 --> 00:11:56,670\nBecause there is a bad first try hiding in every afternoon.\n\n270\n00:11:56,700 --> 00:11:58,420\nThe email I do not want to write.\n\n271\n00:11:58,910 --> 00:12:00,460\nThe call I am afraid of.\n\n272\n00:12:00,940 --> 00:12:01,570\nThe report.\n\n273\n00:12:02,540 --> 00:12:03,840\nAnd what do you do with them, Mia?\n\n274\n00:12:04,280 --> 00:12:06,059\nThe email, the call?\n\n275\n00:12:06,100 --> 00:12:07,200\nDo you write the good version?\n\n276\n00:12:07,920 --> 00:12:09,440\nI do the bad version, Ben.\n\n277\n00:12:10,080 --> 00:12:10,669\nRight away.\n\n278\n00:12:11,280 --> 00:12:12,100\nA bad email.\n\n279\n00:12:12,580 --> 00:12:13,940\nThree ugly lines.\n\n280\n00:12:14,280 --> 00:12:15,940\nA bad call.\n\n281\n00:12:15,949 --> 00:12:20,040\n\"Hello, it is Mia, I have a question.\" And then it exists.\n\n282\n00:12:21,100 --> 00:12:23,080\nFixing a bad email is easy.\n\n283\n00:12:23,160 --> 00:12:26,820\nMaking a good first email is impossible.\n\n284\n00:12:26,900 --> 00:12:28,140\nEvery writer says this.\n\n285\n00:12:28,490 --> 00:12:30,320\nThe first draft is always bad.\n\n286\n00:12:30,790 --> 00:12:33,040\nThe job is to make it exist.\n\n287\n00:12:33,060 --> 00:12:35,320\nThe bad email takes two minutes.\n\n288\n00:12:35,360 --> 00:12:38,520\nThe good email takes two weeks, and never comes.\n\n289\n00:12:39,650 --> 00:12:45,940\n\"The job is to make it exist.\" Mia, that is my second idea, in different words.\n\n290\n00:12:46,780 --> 00:12:47,900\nMaking beats thinking.\n\n291\n00:12:49,080 --> 00:12:50,440\nI learned that again last year.\n\n292\n00:12:50,880 --> 00:12:53,000\nI was planning a new series for the podcast.\n\n293\n00:12:53,360 --> 00:12:54,440\nThree months of notes.\n\n294\n00:12:54,760 --> 00:12:55,240\nPlans.\n\n295\n00:12:55,620 --> 00:12:56,040\nTitles.\n\n296\n00:12:56,600 --> 00:12:57,460\nNothing recorded.\n\n297\n00:12:58,440 --> 00:12:59,860\nThree months of notes, Ben.\n\n298\n00:13:00,120 --> 00:13:01,340\nYou forgot your own bowl.\n\n299\n00:13:01,720 --> 00:13:02,500\nWhat did you do?\n\n300\n00:13:02,840 --> 00:13:03,640\nDid you make one?\n\n301\n00:13:05,580 --> 00:13:07,300\nI read the pottery story again, Mia.\n\n302\n00:13:07,690 --> 00:13:10,460\nAnd I recorded a bad episode that afternoon.\n\n303\n00:13:10,500 --> 00:13:11,050\nBad title.\n\n304\n00:13:11,550 --> 00:13:12,350\nPhone microphone.\n\n305\n00:13:12,760 --> 00:13:16,330\nFifteen minutes of \"um.\" I made it in one afternoon.\n\n306\n00:13:16,940 --> 00:13:18,400\nAnd I posted it, badly.\n\n307\n00:13:19,110 --> 00:13:21,160\nAnd the second one was less bad.\n\n308\n00:13:21,200 --> 00:13:23,440\nAnd the tenth one, I liked.\n\n309\n00:13:23,520 --> 00:13:26,140\nThe good one is in the tenth bad one.\n\n310\n00:13:26,240 --> 00:13:27,170\nNever in the notes.\n\n311\n00:13:28,220 --> 00:13:34,880\n\"The good one is in the tenth bad one, never in the notes.\" Ben, my second idea is for the evening.\n\n312\n00:13:35,420 --> 00:13:36,560\nI learned to cook this year.\n\n313\n00:13:36,990 --> 00:13:38,080\nBadly.\n\n314\n00:13:38,180 --> 00:13:40,980\nEvery night, I make one thing I have never made.\n\n315\n00:13:41,380 --> 00:13:42,920\nAnd most nights, it is bad.\n\n316\n00:13:43,340 --> 00:13:44,220\nToo salty.\n\n317\n00:13:44,280 --> 00:13:44,920\nToo soft.\n\n318\n00:13:45,440 --> 00:13:46,100\nBut I eat it.\n\n319\n00:13:46,640 --> 00:13:47,880\nI eat the evidence.\n\n320\n00:13:48,830 --> 00:13:55,720\n\"I eat the evidence.\" Mia, the kitchen is the quantity group with a door you can close.\n\n321\n00:13:55,820 --> 00:13:57,160\nNobody sees the bad soup.\n\n322\n00:13:57,660 --> 00:13:59,430\nAnd the fortieth soup is good.\n\n323\n00:14:00,560 --> 00:14:02,780\nMy third idea is about the people who watch.\n\n324\n00:14:03,420 --> 00:14:05,640\nBecause you said the fear is being seen.\n\n325\n00:14:06,830 --> 00:14:07,500\nIt is, Ben.\n\n326\n00:14:07,910 --> 00:14:08,580\nThe teacher.\n\n327\n00:14:08,880 --> 00:14:09,780\nThe other students.\n\n328\n00:14:10,190 --> 00:14:12,740\nThe person at the bus stop when I say a bad sentence.\n\n329\n00:14:13,400 --> 00:14:14,100\nWhat about them?\n\n330\n00:14:15,300 --> 00:14:17,140\nNobody remembers the bad one, Mia.\n\n331\n00:14:17,940 --> 00:14:18,900\nThink about your class.\n\n332\n00:14:19,260 --> 00:14:21,460\nNobody remembers your ten bad bowls.\n\n333\n00:14:22,030 --> 00:14:24,300\nThe teacher put the eleventh in the window.\n\n334\n00:14:24,380 --> 00:14:25,660\nOnly the good one stays.\n\n335\n00:14:26,050 --> 00:14:28,020\nThe bad ones disappear the same day.\n\n336\n00:14:28,580 --> 00:14:31,000\nThey are busy remembering their own bad ones.\n\n337\n00:14:31,700 --> 00:14:35,760\nSo the fear of being seen is a fear of something that nobody keeps.\n\n338\n00:14:37,040 --> 00:14:38,680\nSomething nobody keeps, Ben.\n\n339\n00:14:39,180 --> 00:14:40,730\nBut the teacher keeps the good one.\n\n340\n00:14:41,360 --> 00:14:44,310\nDoes the teacher remember who made ten bad ones first?\n\n341\n00:14:46,080 --> 00:14:48,960\nThe teacher remembers that you came every week, Mia.\n\n342\n00:14:49,540 --> 00:14:51,100\nThat is all teachers remember.\n\n343\n00:14:51,780 --> 00:14:52,639\nNot the grey bowl.\n\n344\n00:14:53,230 --> 00:14:54,859\nThe chair that was never empty.\n\n345\n00:14:55,760 --> 00:14:58,080\nTen bad bowls tell the teacher one thing.\n\n346\n00:14:58,680 --> 00:15:00,660\nThis person is going to be good.\n\n347\n00:15:01,370 --> 00:15:05,000\nThe perfect-pot group had a beautiful plan and an empty chair.\n\n348\n00:15:05,720 --> 00:15:10,590\n\"A beautiful plan and an empty chair.\" Ben, that is true.\n\n349\n00:15:11,500 --> 00:15:14,570\nNobody at the market remembers my bad Spanish from Tuesday.\n\n350\n00:15:15,300 --> 00:15:17,280\nThey remember that I came back on Wednesday.\n\n351\n00:15:18,660 --> 00:15:27,360\nMy third idea is about the word \"first.\" Because the title is \"do it badly first.\" Not \"do it badly forever.\"\n\n352\n00:15:28,280 --> 00:15:28,880\nFirst, Mia.\n\n353\n00:15:29,680 --> 00:15:30,380\nThen the stair.\n\n354\n00:15:31,440 --> 00:15:33,580\nBad first, better second, better third.\n\n355\n00:15:34,280 --> 00:15:35,440\nNobody stays at bad.\n\n356\n00:15:36,070 --> 00:15:37,780\nBad is the door, not the room.\n\n357\n00:15:38,399 --> 00:15:39,900\nYou walk through it.\n\n358\n00:15:39,940 --> 00:15:43,490\nAnd the only people who stay at bad are the people who never make the second one.\n\n359\n00:15:44,909 --> 00:15:47,420\nThe people who never make the second one, Ben.\n\n360\n00:15:48,440 --> 00:15:49,440\nWhy do they stop?\n\n361\n00:15:50,170 --> 00:15:52,800\nThe first one was bad, but they knew it would be bad.\n\n362\n00:15:53,760 --> 00:15:56,500\nBecause they thought bad meant something about them, Mia.\n\n363\n00:15:57,140 --> 00:15:57,980\nNot about the bowl.\n\n364\n00:15:58,920 --> 00:16:04,760\n\"The bowl is bad\" becomes \"I am bad at this.\" That is the kindness episode again.\n\n365\n00:16:05,440 --> 00:16:07,540\nThe bowl is a thing, not a person.\n\n366\n00:16:08,300 --> 00:16:09,300\nMake the second bowl.\n\n367\n00:16:10,140 --> 00:16:11,159\nIt will tell you the truth.\n\n368\n00:16:11,920 --> 00:16:13,900\nThe first one only tells you that you started.\n\n369\n00:16:15,300 --> 00:16:21,360\n\"Bad is the door, not the room.\" Ben, my fourth idea is the smallest version.\n\n370\n00:16:21,900 --> 00:16:24,800\nBecause some people are afraid even of a bad bowl.\n\n371\n00:16:25,580 --> 00:16:28,260\nSo here is the smallest bad first try.\n\n372\n00:16:29,220 --> 00:16:30,200\nOne minute.\n\n373\n00:16:30,780 --> 00:16:32,620\nSay one bad sentence in English.\n\n374\n00:16:33,240 --> 00:16:34,680\nDraw one bad cat.\n\n375\n00:16:35,280 --> 00:16:36,680\nWrite one bad line.\n\n376\n00:16:37,560 --> 00:16:39,640\nOne minute of bad.\n\n377\n00:16:39,700 --> 00:16:41,200\nAnd then you have started.\n\n378\n00:16:42,520 --> 00:16:43,840\nOne minute of bad, Mia.\n\n379\n00:16:44,490 --> 00:16:45,120\nAnd keep it.\n\n380\n00:16:45,860 --> 00:16:48,020\nThat is my fourth idea.\n\n381\n00:16:48,060 --> 00:16:50,140\nDo not throw the bad one away in shame.\n\n382\n00:16:50,420 --> 00:16:50,820\nKeep it.\n\n383\n00:16:51,530 --> 00:16:53,060\nThe bad one is not garbage.\n\n384\n00:16:53,660 --> 00:16:54,460\nIt is the floor.\n\n385\n00:16:55,420 --> 00:16:58,840\nIf you throw away every floor, you can never build a stair.\n\n386\n00:17:00,170 --> 00:17:07,920\n\"If you throw away every floor, you can never build a stair.\" Ben, that is why my bowl is on the shelf.\n\n387\n00:17:08,460 --> 00:17:09,420\nIt is the floor.\n\n388\n00:17:10,540 --> 00:17:11,900\nDo you have one last idea?\n\n389\n00:17:12,540 --> 00:17:15,940\nFor the people who say they have no time for fifty bad ones?\n\n390\n00:17:17,020 --> 00:17:18,660\nI do, Mia.\n\n391\n00:17:18,700 --> 00:17:20,710\nLook at the time you spend not making.\n\n392\n00:17:21,469 --> 00:17:21,969\nPlanning.\n\n393\n00:17:22,640 --> 00:17:23,400\nReading about it.\n\n394\n00:17:23,960 --> 00:17:24,490\nWorrying.\n\n395\n00:17:25,060 --> 00:17:25,960\nWaiting to be ready.\n\n396\n00:17:27,020 --> 00:17:28,360\nThat time is not small.\n\n397\n00:17:29,240 --> 00:17:31,460\nThree months of notes is not small.\n\n398\n00:17:32,540 --> 00:17:35,500\nFifty bad sentences take one week.\n\n399\n00:17:35,600 --> 00:17:37,760\nThree months of waiting takes three months.\n\n400\n00:17:38,960 --> 00:17:41,920\nBad first is the only way that is not infinite.\n\n401\n00:17:43,100 --> 00:17:50,440\n\"Bad first is the only way that is not infinite.\" Ben, let me tell you about children, because they know this.\n\n402\n00:17:51,220 --> 00:17:53,360\nMy niece draws fifty pictures a week.\n\n403\n00:17:53,920 --> 00:17:55,480\nForty-eight are terrible.\n\n404\n00:17:56,060 --> 00:17:56,920\nShe does not care.\n\n405\n00:17:57,460 --> 00:17:59,000\nShe is the quantity group.\n\n406\n00:17:59,520 --> 00:18:02,220\nShe makes, she looks, she moves on.\n\n407\n00:18:02,940 --> 00:18:05,560\nFifty pictures, floor, next.\n\n408\n00:18:07,960 --> 00:18:12,760\n\"Fifty pictures, floor, next.\" Mia, that is the whole method.\n\n409\n00:18:13,580 --> 00:18:18,200\nAnd I want to be honest about the bowl, because some days, it still hurts a little, doesn't it?\n\n410\n00:18:19,120 --> 00:18:20,469\nThe grey where it should be blue.\n\n411\n00:18:21,280 --> 00:18:22,140\nSome days, Ben.\n\n412\n00:18:22,560 --> 00:18:22,980\nYes.\n\n413\n00:18:23,440 --> 00:18:26,820\nAnd on those days, I make tea, and I drink it from the bowl.\n\n414\n00:18:27,520 --> 00:18:29,500\nA bad bowl still holds tea.\n\n415\n00:18:30,360 --> 00:18:32,500\nThat is the thing about bad first things.\n\n416\n00:18:32,960 --> 00:18:33,540\nThey work.\n\n417\n00:18:34,200 --> 00:18:34,920\nNot perfectly.\n\n418\n00:18:35,420 --> 00:18:36,660\nBut they hold tea.\n\n419\n00:18:37,180 --> 00:18:38,280\nThe tea is hot.\n\n420\n00:18:38,700 --> 00:18:39,940\nThe bowl holds it.\n\n421\n00:18:40,500 --> 00:18:41,900\nThat is a working bowl.\n\n422\n00:18:42,500 --> 00:18:44,300\nAnd the good bowl came out of this one.\n\n423\n00:18:45,810 --> 00:18:50,100\n\"A bad bowl still holds tea.\" Mia, that is our message today.\n\n424\n00:18:50,740 --> 00:19:00,020\nThere is no door from \"never tried\" to \"good.\" The only door is \"bad.\" The pottery class made fifty pounds of pots and found the good one inside.\n\n425\n00:19:00,880 --> 00:19:02,340\nSo make the bad one.\n\n426\n00:19:02,400 --> 00:19:02,730\nToday.\n\n427\n00:19:03,660 --> 00:19:04,020\nKeep it.\n\n428\n00:19:04,920 --> 00:19:06,500\nIt is the floor.\n\n429\n00:19:06,580 --> 00:19:09,480\nEverything good you will ever make is on the other side of it.\n\n430\n00:19:10,940 --> 00:19:12,120\nOn the other side of it.\n\n431\n00:19:12,860 --> 00:19:17,789\nBen, let's repeat today's important words, slowly, because our listeners want to remember them.\n\n432\n00:19:18,860 --> 00:19:19,700\nBad.\n\n433\n00:19:19,760 --> 00:19:20,500\nNot good.\n\n434\n00:19:21,420 --> 00:19:22,400\nA bad bowl.\n\n435\n00:19:23,240 --> 00:19:23,940\nDo it badly.\n\n436\n00:19:24,820 --> 00:19:25,840\nBad first.\n\n437\n00:19:26,880 --> 00:19:27,340\nFirst.\n\n438\n00:19:28,170 --> 00:19:29,320\nThe first try.\n\n439\n00:19:30,020 --> 00:19:30,940\nThe first bowl.\n\n440\n00:19:31,880 --> 00:19:32,360\nBowl.\n\n441\n00:19:33,220 --> 00:19:34,300\nMy bad bowl.\n\n442\n00:19:35,000 --> 00:19:36,340\nA bowl that holds tea.\n\n443\n00:19:37,480 --> 00:19:37,800\nGood.\n\n444\n00:19:38,340 --> 00:19:39,600\nThe opposite of bad.\n\n445\n00:19:40,400 --> 00:19:42,340\nThe good one is inside the bad ones.\n\n446\n00:19:43,020 --> 00:19:45,220\nGood is a wall.\n\n447\n00:19:45,280 --> 00:19:45,660\nMake.\n\n448\n00:19:46,420 --> 00:19:47,640\nTo create with your hands.\n\n449\n00:19:48,260 --> 00:19:49,580\nMake a bad one.\n\n450\n00:19:49,660 --> 00:19:51,060\nMake many.\n\n451\n00:19:51,120 --> 00:19:52,700\nMaking beats thinking.\n\n452\n00:19:54,280 --> 00:19:54,620\nBad.\n\n453\n00:19:55,480 --> 00:19:55,820\nFirst.\n\n454\n00:19:56,880 --> 00:19:57,160\nBowl.\n\n455\n00:19:58,360 --> 00:19:58,620\nGood.\n\n456\n00:19:59,840 --> 00:20:00,080\nMake.\n\n457\n00:20:01,500 --> 00:20:03,000\nFive words, and one bowl.\n\n458\n00:20:03,660 --> 00:20:04,740\nAnd one big idea.\n\n459\n00:20:05,600 --> 00:20:06,700\nDo it badly first.\n\n460\n00:20:07,740 --> 00:20:09,860\nNow, Mia, do you have a question for our listeners?\n\n461\n00:20:11,020 --> 00:20:12,060\nYes, Ben.\n\n462\n00:20:12,120 --> 00:20:14,190\nEveryone listening, here is my question.\n\n463\n00:20:14,940 --> 00:20:16,400\nWhat is your bowl?\n\n464\n00:20:16,460 --> 00:20:20,740\nThe bad first thing you made, or the thing you have not made yet because it will be bad.\n\n465\n00:20:21,590 --> 00:20:23,920\nWrite it in the comments in one short sentence.\n\n466\n00:20:24,500 --> 00:20:31,160\n\"My bowl is a song I have not recorded.\" And then, if you want, add, \"I will make it badly this week.\"\n\n467\n00:20:32,500 --> 00:20:33,660\nBadly, this week.\n\n468\n00:20:34,480 --> 00:20:37,980\nAnd if this episode helped you, please subscribe to Two Cups English.\n\n469\n00:20:38,420 --> 00:20:40,300\nWe make a new episode every few days.\n\n470\n00:20:40,860 --> 00:20:44,880\nSlow English, simple words, and one true story each time.\n\n471\n00:20:45,720 --> 00:20:48,450\nThe full text of this episode is in the description.\n\n472\n00:20:48,500 --> 00:20:50,080\nRead it badly, out loud.\n\n473\n00:20:50,650 --> 00:20:51,200\nThat counts.\n\n474\n00:20:52,780 --> 00:20:54,540\nThank you for spending this time with us.\n\n475\n00:20:55,180 --> 00:20:57,080\nMaybe you have been planning something for months.\n\n476\n00:20:57,300 --> 00:20:58,100\nMaybe years.\n\n477\n00:20:58,680 --> 00:20:59,500\nStop planning.\n\n478\n00:21:00,060 --> 00:21:01,360\nMake the bad one today.\n\n479\n00:21:02,060 --> 00:21:02,500\nKeep it.\n\n480\n00:21:02,920 --> 00:21:03,660\nIt is the door.\n\n481\n00:21:04,220 --> 00:21:07,020\nEverything good you will ever make is on the other side of it.\n\n482\n00:21:07,780 --> 00:21:09,900\nMy bowl is on the shelf, holding tea.\n\n483\n00:21:11,240 --> 00:21:11,820\nThis is Ben.\n\n484\n00:21:12,780 --> 00:21:13,580\nAnd this is Mia.\n\n485\n00:21:14,320 --> 00:21:15,600\nThis was Two Cups English.\n\n486\n00:21:16,260 --> 00:21:17,540\nDo it badly first.\n\n487\n00:21:18,340 --> 00:21:19,330\nWe will see you next time."},{"code":"CUP14","file":"CUP14.srt","srt":"1\n00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,280\nHello everyone, and welcome to Two Cups English.\n\n2\n00:00:03,820 --> 00:00:05,840\nI'm Mia, and Ben is here with me.\n\n3\n00:00:06,720 --> 00:00:09,560\nThis podcast is for people who are learning English.\n\n4\n00:00:10,220 --> 00:00:11,050\nWe speak slowly.\n\n5\n00:00:11,640 --> 00:00:13,440\nWe use simple words.\n\n6\n00:00:13,540 --> 00:00:16,400\nAnd we repeat the important words many times.\n\n7\n00:00:17,340 --> 00:00:19,920\nToday, our topic is asking for help.\n\n8\n00:00:20,540 --> 00:00:22,140\nAnd why we do not.\n\n9\n00:00:23,260 --> 00:00:25,900\nBen, when did you last ask someone for help?\n\n10\n00:00:27,020 --> 00:00:27,600\nHi Mia.\n\n11\n00:00:27,960 --> 00:00:28,480\nHi everyone.\n\n12\n00:00:29,440 --> 00:00:30,200\nYesterday, Mia.\n\n13\n00:00:31,020 --> 00:00:34,120\nI asked my daughter to find a file on my phone.\n\n14\n00:00:34,200 --> 00:00:36,160\nThat is the size of my asks.\n\n15\n00:00:36,880 --> 00:00:39,020\nSmall, and to a seven-year-old.\n\n16\n00:00:40,620 --> 00:00:43,040\nThe big ones, I do not ask.\n\n17\n00:00:43,920 --> 00:00:44,460\nWhat about you?\n\n18\n00:00:45,800 --> 00:00:46,840\nI have a box, Ben.\n\n19\n00:00:47,260 --> 00:00:48,260\nThat is my story.\n\n20\n00:00:49,040 --> 00:00:51,340\nThere was a box on the top shelf of my kitchen.\n\n21\n00:00:51,769 --> 00:00:52,440\nA heavy one.\n\n22\n00:00:53,020 --> 00:00:53,780\nWinter things.\n\n23\n00:00:54,260 --> 00:00:55,500\nCoats, boots.\n\n24\n00:00:56,100 --> 00:00:58,380\nAnd for one year, I could not reach it.\n\n25\n00:00:59,080 --> 00:01:00,060\nI stood on a chair.\n\n26\n00:01:00,600 --> 00:01:01,540\nI used a broom.\n\n27\n00:01:02,020 --> 00:01:03,560\nI almost fell, twice.\n\n28\n00:01:04,239 --> 00:01:05,880\nOnce with the box in my hand.\n\n29\n00:01:06,840 --> 00:01:07,660\nA year, Mia?\n\n30\n00:01:08,360 --> 00:01:11,370\nOn a chair, with a broom, in your own kitchen?\n\n31\n00:01:12,210 --> 00:01:12,840\nAnd your neighbor?\n\n32\n00:01:13,380 --> 00:01:15,110\nThe tall one from our hello episode?\n\n33\n00:01:16,220 --> 00:01:18,300\nOn the other side of the wall, Ben.\n\n34\n00:01:18,340 --> 00:01:19,190\nThe whole year.\n\n35\n00:01:19,800 --> 00:01:21,280\nTwo meters tall.\n\n36\n00:01:21,320 --> 00:01:22,440\nAnd I never asked.\n\n37\n00:01:22,900 --> 00:01:24,580\nBecause I had a picture in my head.\n\n38\n00:01:25,180 --> 00:01:25,660\nI knock.\n\n39\n00:01:26,060 --> 00:01:27,020\nHe opens the door.\n\n40\n00:01:27,520 --> 00:01:29,590\nAnd his face says, \"Really?\n\n41\n00:01:29,980 --> 00:01:30,660\nFor a box?\n\n42\n00:01:31,200 --> 00:01:35,140\nI am busy.\" And I feel small, and I go back to my chair.\n\n43\n00:01:36,430 --> 00:01:42,310\n\"I feel small, and I go back to my chair.\" Mia, that is our topic today.\n\n44\n00:01:43,300 --> 00:01:44,280\nSo here is our plan.\n\n45\n00:01:45,080 --> 00:01:49,400\nIn part one, we talk about the things we do not ask for.\n\n46\n00:01:49,480 --> 00:01:55,560\nIn part two, we ask why asking feels so bad, and what really happens when we ask.\n\n47\n00:01:56,500 --> 00:01:58,320\nAnd I have a true story for that.\n\n48\n00:01:59,540 --> 00:02:03,040\nIn part three, we share simple ways to ask for help this week.\n\n49\n00:02:03,900 --> 00:02:04,660\nDoes that sound good?\n\n50\n00:02:05,640 --> 00:02:06,660\nThat sounds good, Ben.\n\n51\n00:02:07,320 --> 00:02:10,979\nAnd to everyone listening, please do not worry if you miss something.\n\n52\n00:02:11,460 --> 00:02:12,340\nYou will hear it again.\n\n53\n00:02:13,100 --> 00:02:16,040\nAnd if you do not understand a word, ask someone.\n\n54\n00:02:16,620 --> 00:02:17,600\nThat is the topic.\n\n55\n00:02:18,360 --> 00:02:18,940\nOkay, Ben.\n\n56\n00:02:19,220 --> 00:02:19,720\nPart one.\n\n57\n00:02:20,440 --> 00:02:22,730\nDo you want to know what happened when I finally knocked?\n\n58\n00:02:23,600 --> 00:02:24,920\nTell me, Mia.\n\n59\n00:02:24,980 --> 00:02:27,720\nWhat did the face do when you finally knocked?\n\n60\n00:02:27,780 --> 00:02:28,760\nWas it the busy face?\n\n61\n00:02:29,620 --> 00:02:31,300\nI need to know.\n\n62\n00:02:31,360 --> 00:02:32,260\nIt lit up, Ben.\n\n63\n00:02:32,860 --> 00:02:35,000\nHe said yes before I finished the sentence.\n\n64\n00:02:35,549 --> 00:02:36,300\n\"Of course!\n\n65\n00:02:36,780 --> 00:02:41,670\nWhy did you not ask before?\" And he got the box in ten seconds, without a chair.\n\n66\n00:02:42,380 --> 00:02:44,340\nAnd then he asked if I needed anything else.\n\n67\n00:02:45,020 --> 00:02:51,300\nAnd then he told me about his own high shelf, and how his back hurts, and could I help him with something next week.\n\n68\n00:02:51,360 --> 00:02:53,020\nHis shelf, my ladder.\n\n69\n00:02:54,140 --> 00:02:56,240\nHe asked you for help back, Mia.\n\n70\n00:02:56,280 --> 00:02:57,190\nIn the same minute.\n\n71\n00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:02,980\nAnd \"why did you not ask before?\" He was almost hurt, wasn't he?\n\n72\n00:03:03,040 --> 00:03:04,000\nThat you did not ask.\n\n73\n00:03:05,100 --> 00:03:07,140\nHe asked me back in the same minute, Ben.\n\n74\n00:03:07,680 --> 00:03:09,020\nIs that normal?\n\n75\n00:03:09,060 --> 00:03:10,470\nDo people who help want help?\n\n76\n00:03:11,320 --> 00:03:13,620\nI think asking opens a door both ways, Mia.\n\n77\n00:03:14,340 --> 00:03:16,780\nWhen you asked, you told him it was allowed.\n\n78\n00:03:16,880 --> 00:03:17,650\nSo he asked.\n\n79\n00:03:18,660 --> 00:03:22,520\nPeople are waiting for permission, like in our hug episode.\n\n80\n00:03:22,580 --> 00:03:24,460\nYour box gave him permission for his back.\n\n81\n00:03:25,200 --> 00:03:26,980\nOne ask, two people helped.\n\n82\n00:03:27,700 --> 00:03:28,940\nA little, Ben.\n\n83\n00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:31,200\nAnd I did not expect that at all.\n\n84\n00:03:31,940 --> 00:03:34,280\nIn my picture, asking was a bother.\n\n85\n00:03:34,920 --> 00:03:37,740\nIn his face, not asking was the bother.\n\n86\n00:03:38,560 --> 00:03:42,950\nHe had been on the other side of the wall for a year, and I had never needed him.\n\n87\n00:03:43,740 --> 00:03:45,680\nAs far as he knew.\n\n88\n00:03:45,740 --> 00:03:48,040\nThe box was never about the box.\n\n89\n00:03:48,710 --> 00:03:50,640\nThe face did the opposite of the picture, Mia.\n\n90\n00:03:51,490 --> 00:03:53,520\nAnd I have a broom, too.\n\n91\n00:03:53,530 --> 00:03:57,990\nAt work, for two years, I did not know how to use one part of our system.\n\n92\n00:03:58,620 --> 00:03:59,920\nTwo years.\n\n93\n00:04:00,080 --> 00:04:02,100\nI did it the slow way, every day.\n\n94\n00:04:02,740 --> 00:04:04,620\nOne hour instead of five minutes.\n\n95\n00:04:05,420 --> 00:04:09,100\nMy box was a computer, and my broom was a spreadsheet.\n\n96\n00:04:09,760 --> 00:04:11,240\nTwo years, Ben?\n\n97\n00:04:11,300 --> 00:04:13,700\nAn hour a day, for two years?\n\n98\n00:04:14,340 --> 00:04:15,600\nWhy did you not ask?\n\n99\n00:04:15,960 --> 00:04:17,040\nNobody?\n\n100\n00:04:17,580 --> 00:04:20,740\nBecause after month one, asking felt too late, Mia.\n\n101\n00:04:21,690 --> 00:04:25,960\n\"You have been here a year and you do not know this?\" That was the picture.\n\n102\n00:04:26,780 --> 00:04:27,320\nSo I hid it.\n\n103\n00:04:28,220 --> 00:04:30,090\nAnd then a new person started.\n\n104\n00:04:30,140 --> 00:04:32,060\nAnd on day one, she asked.\n\n105\n00:04:32,740 --> 00:04:36,280\n\"How does this work?\" And someone showed her in five minutes.\n\n106\n00:04:37,400 --> 00:04:38,600\nFive minutes, Ben.\n\n107\n00:04:39,180 --> 00:04:41,400\nAnd you did the hour for two years.\n\n108\n00:04:42,020 --> 00:04:44,220\nSo the new person knew something you forgot.\n\n109\n00:04:44,920 --> 00:04:47,020\nShe knew that day one is free, Mia.\n\n110\n00:04:47,800 --> 00:04:50,160\nOn day one, nobody expects you to know.\n\n111\n00:04:50,700 --> 00:04:51,420\nSo you ask.\n\n112\n00:04:52,040 --> 00:04:56,920\nAnd then week two, month two, year two, the asking gets heavier.\n\n113\n00:04:57,600 --> 00:04:58,920\nLike the silence in the hallway.\n\n114\n00:04:59,740 --> 00:05:02,000\nTwo years of hiding a five-minute question.\n\n115\n00:05:02,720 --> 00:05:05,120\nThe best time to ask is day one.\n\n116\n00:05:05,180 --> 00:05:06,760\nThe second best is today.\n\n117\n00:05:07,940 --> 00:05:10,040\nDay one is free, Ben.\n\n118\n00:05:10,080 --> 00:05:13,120\nAnd I am on day one thousand with some questions.\n\n119\n00:05:13,840 --> 00:05:16,220\nIs there a way to get day one back?\n\n120\n00:05:16,800 --> 00:05:17,380\nYes, Mia.\n\n121\n00:05:17,940 --> 00:05:19,279\nYou say the words out loud.\n\n122\n00:05:20,189 --> 00:05:22,200\n\"I should know this, and I do not.\n\n123\n00:05:22,840 --> 00:05:25,380\nCan you show me?\" Honest, and short.\n\n124\n00:05:25,960 --> 00:05:27,380\nPeople do not laugh at that.\n\n125\n00:05:27,880 --> 00:05:30,960\nPeople say, \"Of course.\" The same face as your neighbor.\n\n126\n00:05:31,820 --> 00:05:33,460\nHonesty makes it day one again.\n\n127\n00:05:33,980 --> 00:05:34,980\nIt resets the clock.\n\n128\n00:05:35,800 --> 00:05:37,920\n\"The best time to ask is day one.\n\n129\n00:05:38,260 --> 00:05:43,240\nThe second best is today.\" Ben, that is the hello episode again.\n\n130\n00:05:43,840 --> 00:05:47,300\nAnd I think the word \"help\" is part of the problem.\n\n131\n00:05:47,340 --> 00:05:48,620\nWe make it too big.\n\n132\n00:05:49,300 --> 00:05:50,340\nToo big how, Mia?\n\n133\n00:05:51,040 --> 00:05:51,900\nHelp is help.\n\n134\n00:05:52,460 --> 00:05:54,040\nYou need it, or you do not.\n\n135\n00:05:54,620 --> 00:05:55,140\nWhat do you mean?\n\n136\n00:05:56,000 --> 00:05:58,180\nHelp sounds like rescue, Ben.\n\n137\n00:05:58,220 --> 00:05:59,740\nLike a fire.\n\n138\n00:05:59,840 --> 00:06:02,620\nAnd nobody wants to call the fire truck for a box.\n\n139\n00:06:03,180 --> 00:06:04,980\nA box is not a fire.\n\n140\n00:06:05,780 --> 00:06:07,480\nBut most help is tiny.\n\n141\n00:06:08,080 --> 00:06:08,740\nHold this.\n\n142\n00:06:09,100 --> 00:06:09,720\nLook at this.\n\n143\n00:06:10,320 --> 00:06:11,100\nSay that again.\n\n144\n00:06:11,700 --> 00:06:12,420\nReach that.\n\n145\n00:06:13,360 --> 00:06:18,400\nHelp is a hundred small grey things, and I thought it was only for emergencies.\n\n146\n00:06:19,080 --> 00:06:19,819\nRed things.\n\n147\n00:06:20,560 --> 00:06:27,030\n\"Help is a hundred small grey things.\" Mia, so your daily life had no help in it at all.\n\n148\n00:06:27,100 --> 00:06:29,200\nEverything alone, every day.\n\n149\n00:06:29,220 --> 00:06:30,610\nAnd alone is not strong.\n\n150\n00:06:31,140 --> 00:06:33,560\nAlone is just slow.\n\n151\n00:06:33,620 --> 00:06:35,040\nOkay, so that was part one.\n\n152\n00:06:35,080 --> 00:06:36,140\nOur brooms.\n\n153\n00:06:36,220 --> 00:06:37,820\nNow, part two.\n\n154\n00:06:37,860 --> 00:06:39,980\nWhy does asking feel so bad?\n\n155\n00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:41,100\nAnd what really happens?\n\n156\n00:06:41,880 --> 00:06:42,740\nI have a true story.\n\n157\n00:06:43,340 --> 00:06:45,140\nA true story about asking, Ben?\n\n158\n00:06:45,720 --> 00:06:46,800\nSomebody measured this?\n\n159\n00:06:47,190 --> 00:06:49,700\nWith real strangers, on a real street?\n\n160\n00:06:50,200 --> 00:06:51,180\nTwo people did, Mia.\n\n161\n00:06:51,880 --> 00:06:52,779\nOkay, part two.\n\n162\n00:06:53,340 --> 00:06:54,880\nThis is a true story.\n\n163\n00:06:54,940 --> 00:07:01,010\nIn 2008, Francis Flynn and Vanessa Bohns, at Columbia University in New York, did a simple experiment.\n\n164\n00:07:01,590 --> 00:07:04,900\nThey asked students to go out and ask strangers for small things.\n\n165\n00:07:05,460 --> 00:07:06,240\nFill in a survey.\n\n166\n00:07:06,840 --> 00:07:08,180\nLend me your phone for a call.\n\n167\n00:07:08,900 --> 00:07:09,760\nWalk me to the gym.\n\n168\n00:07:10,200 --> 00:07:12,060\nWalk me to the gym, Ben?\n\n169\n00:07:12,180 --> 00:07:14,110\nTo a stranger, on the street?\n\n170\n00:07:14,780 --> 00:07:15,360\nI would never.\n\n171\n00:07:15,780 --> 00:07:16,069\nNever.\n\n172\n00:07:16,860 --> 00:07:17,660\nNeither would I, Mia.\n\n173\n00:07:18,200 --> 00:07:22,180\nBut first, before the students went out, the researchers asked them to guess.\n\n174\n00:07:22,860 --> 00:07:27,300\n\"How many people will you need to ask, to get ten yeses?\" And the students guessed.\n\n175\n00:07:28,040 --> 00:07:31,060\nAnd then they went out, and they asked people, and they counted.\n\n176\n00:07:31,630 --> 00:07:32,940\nAnd the guess was wrong, Ben?\n\n177\n00:07:33,640 --> 00:07:35,340\nI already know the answer, I think.\n\n178\n00:07:35,700 --> 00:07:36,700\nBut tell me anyway.\n\n179\n00:07:37,320 --> 00:07:37,820\nSlowly.\n\n180\n00:07:38,200 --> 00:07:39,519\nWrong by half, Mia.\n\n181\n00:07:40,080 --> 00:07:44,300\nThe students thought they would need to ask about twenty people to get ten yeses.\n\n182\n00:07:44,380 --> 00:07:46,580\nIn reality, they needed about ten.\n\n183\n00:07:47,240 --> 00:07:50,840\nPeople said yes twice as often as the students expected.\n\n184\n00:07:50,880 --> 00:07:51,860\nFor every task.\n\n185\n00:07:52,360 --> 00:07:54,570\nThe survey, the phone, the walk.\n\n186\n00:07:55,120 --> 00:07:56,540\nTwice as often.\n\n187\n00:07:56,560 --> 00:07:57,880\nTwice as often, Ben.\n\n188\n00:07:58,560 --> 00:08:02,800\nSo the picture in my head, the busy face, was wrong by half.\n\n189\n00:08:02,830 --> 00:08:06,900\nAnd the real face at the door, the one that said yes, was the other half.\n\n190\n00:08:07,380 --> 00:08:08,940\nTwo different worlds, Mia.\n\n191\n00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:12,150\nThe asker's world, where asking is a big frightening thing.\n\n192\n00:08:12,770 --> 00:08:16,760\nAnd the asked person's world, where it is a small normal thing.\n\n193\n00:08:16,840 --> 00:08:21,040\nThe asking feels terrible to the asker, and normal to the asked.\n\n194\n00:08:21,080 --> 00:08:24,300\nA box is heavy for one and light for the other.\n\n195\n00:08:24,310 --> 00:08:32,080\n\"The asking feels terrible to the asker, and normal to the asked.\" Ben, I was living in the asker's world for a year, on a chair.\n\n196\n00:08:32,790 --> 00:08:34,980\nAnd my neighbor was in the other world, waiting.\n\n197\n00:08:35,880 --> 00:08:38,080\nWhy are the two worlds so different?\n\n198\n00:08:38,120 --> 00:08:40,559\nBecause saying no is hard, too, Mia.\n\n199\n00:08:40,600 --> 00:08:42,000\nThat is what the researchers found.\n\n200\n00:08:42,550 --> 00:08:45,029\nThe asker thinks about how hard it is to ask.\n\n201\n00:08:45,580 --> 00:08:49,300\nThe asked person thinks about how hard it is to say no.\n\n202\n00:08:49,360 --> 00:08:51,220\nNobody wants to say no to a face.\n\n203\n00:08:51,960 --> 00:08:52,740\nSo they say yes.\n\n204\n00:08:53,260 --> 00:08:54,760\nAnd they feel good about it.\n\n205\n00:08:54,840 --> 00:08:56,960\nSo the asked person is also afraid, Ben.\n\n206\n00:08:57,500 --> 00:08:58,500\nAfraid of saying no.\n\n207\n00:08:59,240 --> 00:09:01,860\nTwo afraid people, and one box between them.\n\n208\n00:09:02,600 --> 00:09:04,000\nTwo afraid people, Mia.\n\n209\n00:09:04,869 --> 00:09:05,860\nAnd the box is light.\n\n210\n00:09:06,060 --> 00:09:07,260\nThat is the funny part.\n\n211\n00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:09,400\nBoth are afraid of a face.\n\n212\n00:09:10,200 --> 00:09:11,680\nThe box takes ten seconds.\n\n213\n00:09:12,160 --> 00:09:13,620\nThe fear takes a year.\n\n214\n00:09:14,360 --> 00:09:17,780\nAnd when the ask finally happens, both people are relieved.\n\n215\n00:09:18,390 --> 00:09:20,360\nThe asker, because the face was kind.\n\n216\n00:09:20,840 --> 00:09:22,680\nThe asked, because they got to help.\n\n217\n00:09:23,460 --> 00:09:25,920\nEverybody wins, and the box comes down.\n\n218\n00:09:26,860 --> 00:09:27,959\nThey feel good about it.\n\n219\n00:09:28,540 --> 00:09:30,540\nBen, that is the part I never saw.\n\n220\n00:09:31,180 --> 00:09:32,700\nI thought I was sparing my neighbor.\n\n221\n00:09:33,100 --> 00:09:34,440\nSaving him from a bother.\n\n222\n00:09:35,139 --> 00:09:36,720\nBut helping feels good.\n\n223\n00:09:37,420 --> 00:09:40,380\nWhen he got my box, he said yes and he smiled.\n\n224\n00:09:41,020 --> 00:09:44,700\nI was giving him a good minute, and I kept it from him for a year.\n\n225\n00:09:45,560 --> 00:09:46,920\nYou were not sparing him, Mia.\n\n226\n00:09:47,400 --> 00:09:48,400\nYou were starving him.\n\n227\n00:09:49,140 --> 00:09:52,000\nAnd one more number from that study, because it helped me most.\n\n228\n00:09:52,710 --> 00:09:56,880\nThe researchers asked the students, after everyone said yes, to guess again.\n\n229\n00:09:57,620 --> 00:09:58,720\nAnd the guess went up.\n\n230\n00:09:59,580 --> 00:09:59,880\nA little.\n\n231\n00:10:00,540 --> 00:10:01,160\nNot all the way.\n\n232\n00:10:01,660 --> 00:10:02,820\nThey still guessed too low.\n\n233\n00:10:03,860 --> 00:10:04,920\nStill too low, Ben?\n\n234\n00:10:05,580 --> 00:10:07,780\nAfter ten yeses in a row?\n\n235\n00:10:07,840 --> 00:10:08,850\nHow is that possible?\n\n236\n00:10:09,620 --> 00:10:11,300\nTen yeses is a lot.\n\n237\n00:10:12,400 --> 00:10:13,380\nStill too low, Mia.\n\n238\n00:10:14,100 --> 00:10:15,280\nThe picture is strong.\n\n239\n00:10:15,840 --> 00:10:18,260\nIt only changes when the pile of yeses is big.\n\n240\n00:10:18,900 --> 00:10:20,600\nSo one yes is not enough.\n\n241\n00:10:21,180 --> 00:10:21,940\nYou need many.\n\n242\n00:10:22,880 --> 00:10:26,200\nThat is why the box was your first yes, and you need more.\n\n243\n00:10:27,120 --> 00:10:28,840\nAnd I got more, Ben.\n\n244\n00:10:28,940 --> 00:10:32,060\nAfter the box, I asked the woman at the bakery to slice the bread.\n\n245\n00:10:32,480 --> 00:10:32,940\nSmall.\n\n246\n00:10:33,400 --> 00:10:34,140\nShe said yes.\n\n247\n00:10:34,780 --> 00:10:36,500\nThen a colleague, to look at my report.\n\n248\n00:10:37,060 --> 00:10:37,460\nYes.\n\n249\n00:10:38,120 --> 00:10:39,900\nThen my sister, to keep my keys.\n\n250\n00:10:40,520 --> 00:10:40,940\nYes.\n\n251\n00:10:41,480 --> 00:10:42,880\nThe pile is growing.\n\n252\n00:10:43,320 --> 00:10:44,840\nAnd the picture is changing.\n\n253\n00:10:45,620 --> 00:10:46,100\nSlowly.\n\n254\n00:10:47,070 --> 00:10:50,880\n\"The pile is growing.\" Mia, that is exactly how it works.\n\n255\n00:10:51,980 --> 00:10:53,320\nOkay, so that was part two.\n\n256\n00:10:53,840 --> 00:10:55,520\nWhy asking feels so bad.\n\n257\n00:10:56,220 --> 00:10:58,720\nWe talked about Flynn and Bohns and the students.\n\n258\n00:10:59,320 --> 00:11:02,120\nWe said people say yes twice as often as we think.\n\n259\n00:11:02,820 --> 00:11:05,360\nAnd we said the pile of yeses changes the picture.\n\n260\n00:11:06,380 --> 00:11:07,340\nNow, part three.\n\n261\n00:11:08,080 --> 00:11:10,700\nSimple ways to ask for help this week.\n\n262\n00:11:10,760 --> 00:11:11,860\nMia, you go first.\n\n263\n00:11:12,760 --> 00:11:13,670\nOkay, part three.\n\n264\n00:11:14,420 --> 00:11:16,880\nMy first idea is the smallest ask, Ben.\n\n265\n00:11:17,500 --> 00:11:21,700\nBecause some people listening want to ask, and do not know the words.\n\n266\n00:11:21,780 --> 00:11:22,280\nHere it is.\n\n267\n00:11:22,980 --> 00:11:26,240\n\"Could you help me with something small?\" And then wait.\n\n268\n00:11:26,940 --> 00:11:27,460\nThat is all.\n\n269\n00:11:28,280 --> 00:11:29,960\nTwo sentences and a wait.\n\n270\n00:11:30,560 --> 00:11:32,180\nThe wait is the hard part.\n\n271\n00:11:32,920 --> 00:11:41,270\n\"Two sentences and a wait.\" Mia, my first idea is for English, because this is where I see the most people on chairs with brooms.\n\n272\n00:11:42,200 --> 00:11:45,260\nA learner hears a word and does not understand.\n\n273\n00:11:45,320 --> 00:11:45,960\nAnd they nod.\n\n274\n00:11:46,490 --> 00:11:48,480\nThey smile and nod.\n\n275\n00:11:48,520 --> 00:11:51,640\nAnd the conversation goes on, and they understood nothing.\n\n276\n00:11:52,640 --> 00:11:55,020\nI nodded for a whole year in Spanish, Ben.\n\n277\n00:11:55,760 --> 00:11:58,960\nSmiling, nodding, understanding nothing.\n\n278\n00:11:59,640 --> 00:12:01,800\nA year of nodding.\n\n279\n00:12:01,840 --> 00:12:02,520\nWhat is the idea?\n\n280\n00:12:03,540 --> 00:12:04,140\nAsk, Mia.\n\n281\n00:12:04,970 --> 00:12:07,429\n\"What does that mean?\" Four words.\n\n282\n00:12:07,980 --> 00:12:09,480\nAnd here is the study in English.\n\n283\n00:12:10,460 --> 00:12:12,860\nEverybody thinks less of the nod.\n\n284\n00:12:12,960 --> 00:12:14,670\nNobody thinks less of the question.\n\n285\n00:12:15,720 --> 00:12:17,940\nWhen you ask me a Spanish word, I love it.\n\n286\n00:12:18,560 --> 00:12:19,940\nI get to explain.\n\n287\n00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:21,620\nI feel useful.\n\n288\n00:12:21,660 --> 00:12:23,720\nPeople say yes to that question every time.\n\n289\n00:12:24,550 --> 00:12:26,080\nYou are helping me by asking.\n\n290\n00:12:26,730 --> 00:12:30,220\n\"You are helping them by asking.\" Ben, that takes the fear away.\n\n291\n00:12:30,840 --> 00:12:32,380\nSo this week, five people.\n\n292\n00:12:32,990 --> 00:12:34,990\n\"What does that mean?\" Five times.\n\n293\n00:12:35,460 --> 00:12:36,260\nFive yeses.\n\n294\n00:12:36,740 --> 00:12:37,620\nCount the faces.\n\n295\n00:12:38,300 --> 00:12:39,580\nCount the faces, Mia.\n\n296\n00:12:40,360 --> 00:12:42,960\nI promise the faces will be fine.\n\n297\n00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:44,850\nMy second idea is a rule for work.\n\n298\n00:12:45,710 --> 00:12:46,700\nThe small ask.\n\n299\n00:12:47,729 --> 00:12:53,110\n\"Can you look at this for two minutes?\" \"Can you show me where this is?\" Here is the rule.\n\n300\n00:12:53,960 --> 00:12:57,380\nIf it takes you an hour and them two minutes, ask.\n\n301\n00:12:58,140 --> 00:12:59,420\nThe math is not close.\n\n302\n00:13:00,120 --> 00:13:02,540\nAn hour for me, two minutes for them, Ben.\n\n303\n00:13:03,180 --> 00:13:04,960\nI think I have ten of those every week.\n\n304\n00:13:05,700 --> 00:13:07,420\nWhat happens to the people I ask?\n\n305\n00:13:07,780 --> 00:13:09,420\nDo they get tired of me?\n\n306\n00:13:09,480 --> 00:13:11,600\nThey get closer to you, Mia.\n\n307\n00:13:11,660 --> 00:13:14,640\nThat is what happened at my work, after the new person asked.\n\n308\n00:13:15,480 --> 00:13:17,270\nPeople started asking her things back.\n\n309\n00:13:18,100 --> 00:13:20,070\nShe became the person everyone talked to.\n\n310\n00:13:20,780 --> 00:13:21,490\nIn three months.\n\n311\n00:13:22,360 --> 00:13:23,090\nBecause she asked.\n\n312\n00:13:23,970 --> 00:13:25,200\nAsking is not a cost.\n\n313\n00:13:25,880 --> 00:13:26,580\nIt is a thread.\n\n314\n00:13:27,500 --> 00:13:30,740\nEvery ask ties two people together a little more.\n\n315\n00:13:30,790 --> 00:13:37,080\n\"If it takes you an hour and them two minutes, ask.\" Ben, that is your two years in one sentence.\n\n316\n00:13:38,080 --> 00:13:39,820\nMy second idea is about receiving.\n\n317\n00:13:40,280 --> 00:13:42,120\nBecause there is another side of the box.\n\n318\n00:13:42,780 --> 00:13:46,560\nWhen someone says, \"Let me know if you need anything,\" what do you say?\n\n319\n00:13:47,300 --> 00:13:49,240\n\"I am fine,\" Mia.\n\n320\n00:13:49,960 --> 00:13:50,460\nAlways.\n\n321\n00:13:51,380 --> 00:13:54,040\nWhen my father was sick, I did everything alone.\n\n322\n00:13:54,700 --> 00:13:56,360\nHospital, work, home.\n\n323\n00:13:57,080 --> 00:14:03,900\nAnd people said, \"Let me know if you need anything.\" And I said, \"I am fine.\" Thirty times.\n\n324\n00:14:04,820 --> 00:14:05,800\nI said it to my sister.\n\n325\n00:14:06,420 --> 00:14:07,360\nI said it to my boss.\n\n326\n00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:09,220\nAnd I was not fine.\n\n327\n00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:12,300\nAnd \"I am fine\" was pushing them away, Ben.\n\n328\n00:14:12,940 --> 00:14:13,859\nThey wanted to help.\n\n329\n00:14:14,220 --> 00:14:15,320\nThey were waiting in the door.\n\n330\n00:14:16,040 --> 00:14:17,560\nWhat would you do now?\n\n331\n00:14:17,640 --> 00:14:18,900\nNow I say yes, Mia.\n\n332\n00:14:19,720 --> 00:14:20,540\nNot to everything.\n\n333\n00:14:21,240 --> 00:14:21,960\nTo one thing.\n\n334\n00:14:22,950 --> 00:14:23,980\n\"Actually, yes.\n\n335\n00:14:24,680 --> 00:14:29,690\nCould you bring dinner on Thursday?\" One real thing, with a day and a time.\n\n336\n00:14:30,420 --> 00:14:31,470\nAnd they are relieved.\n\n337\n00:14:32,280 --> 00:14:32,880\nRelieved.\n\n338\n00:14:33,720 --> 00:14:39,730\nBecause \"let me know\" is a door, and they are standing in it, waiting for someone to walk through.\n\n339\n00:14:39,800 --> 00:14:40,190\nAnyone.\n\n340\n00:14:41,049 --> 00:14:46,120\n\"'Let me know' is a door, and they are waiting in it.\" Ben, that turns the whole thing around.\n\n341\n00:14:46,800 --> 00:14:51,200\nMy year on the chair was a year of not giving my neighbor his best afternoons.\n\n342\n00:14:51,420 --> 00:14:53,020\nI thought I was sparing him.\n\n343\n00:14:53,080 --> 00:14:54,020\nI was starving him.\n\n344\n00:14:54,760 --> 00:14:57,560\nAnd I became the helper this year, Mia, and I learned something.\n\n345\n00:14:58,340 --> 00:15:00,659\nMy neighbor asked me to move a sofa.\n\n346\n00:15:00,720 --> 00:15:02,540\nI was busy, honestly.\n\n347\n00:15:02,560 --> 00:15:03,280\nAnd I said yes.\n\n348\n00:15:04,180 --> 00:15:05,120\nAnd I moved the sofa.\n\n349\n00:15:05,810 --> 00:15:07,910\nAnd I felt good for the whole afternoon.\n\n350\n00:15:07,920 --> 00:15:10,040\nBetter than the work I was doing.\n\n351\n00:15:10,120 --> 00:15:13,180\nYou might be giving people the best part of their day.\n\n352\n00:15:13,190 --> 00:15:18,700\n\"You might be giving people the best part of their day.\" Ben, my third idea is for home.\n\n353\n00:15:19,340 --> 00:15:21,980\nBecause there is an ask at home that I always skip.\n\n354\n00:15:22,680 --> 00:15:23,720\nI come home tired.\n\n355\n00:15:24,010 --> 00:15:25,050\nThere is dinner to make.\n\n356\n00:15:25,540 --> 00:15:29,580\nAnd I make it alone, angry, while my roommate sits on the sofa.\n\n357\n00:15:29,620 --> 00:15:30,780\nAnd she does not know.\n\n358\n00:15:31,660 --> 00:15:33,420\nShe does not know, Mia.\n\n359\n00:15:33,500 --> 00:15:34,420\nI have been the sofa.\n\n360\n00:15:34,980 --> 00:15:35,720\nMany times.\n\n361\n00:15:36,540 --> 00:15:38,800\nI did not know my wife was drowning in the kitchen.\n\n362\n00:15:39,580 --> 00:15:40,580\nShe thought I should see.\n\n363\n00:15:41,220 --> 00:15:42,100\nI thought she was fine.\n\n364\n00:15:42,980 --> 00:15:45,820\nTwo people, two pictures, one kitchen.\n\n365\n00:15:46,800 --> 00:15:48,800\nTwo pictures, one kitchen, Ben.\n\n366\n00:15:49,320 --> 00:15:50,540\nAnd nobody says a word.\n\n367\n00:15:51,140 --> 00:15:53,580\nWhy is it so hard to ask the people we live with?\n\n368\n00:15:54,600 --> 00:15:56,080\nBecause we think they should know, Mia.\n\n369\n00:15:56,830 --> 00:16:00,090\n\"If he loved me, he would see.\" But nobody sees.\n\n370\n00:16:00,880 --> 00:16:02,960\nPeople see their own picture.\n\n371\n00:16:02,970 --> 00:16:07,690\nThe sofa looks like rest to the person on it, and like laziness to the person at the stove.\n\n372\n00:16:08,570 --> 00:16:10,610\nThe only way across is five words.\n\n373\n00:16:11,400 --> 00:16:11,680\nAsk.\n\n374\n00:16:12,500 --> 00:16:14,760\nThe picture cannot cross the kitchen by itself.\n\n375\n00:16:15,690 --> 00:16:21,380\n\"Two people, two pictures, one kitchen.\" Ben, so now I say five words.\n\n376\n00:16:21,980 --> 00:16:24,840\n\"Can you cut the onions?\" Five words.\n\n377\n00:16:25,460 --> 00:16:27,420\nAnd she says yes, and she jumps up.\n\n378\n00:16:27,820 --> 00:16:28,180\nHappy.\n\n379\n00:16:28,860 --> 00:16:34,079\nBecause the ask says, \"Come and be in this with me.\" That is what asking really says.\n\n380\n00:16:35,090 --> 00:16:38,330\n\"Come and be in this with me.\" Mia, that is beautiful.\n\n381\n00:16:39,300 --> 00:16:44,360\nMy third idea is about the word \"alone.\" Because I think it is the real topic under this one.\n\n382\n00:16:45,240 --> 00:16:48,700\nWe ask for help so rarely because we believe we should not need it.\n\n383\n00:16:49,400 --> 00:16:51,920\nStrong people do it alone.\n\n384\n00:16:51,960 --> 00:16:54,460\nBut strong people are just people with a broom, Ben.\n\n385\n00:16:54,980 --> 00:16:55,600\nOn a chair.\n\n386\n00:16:56,180 --> 00:16:56,820\nFor a year.\n\n387\n00:16:57,570 --> 00:16:58,650\nAnd they are not alone.\n\n388\n00:16:59,160 --> 00:17:01,920\nThey only feel alone, because they are not asking.\n\n389\n00:17:02,680 --> 00:17:07,080\nPeople are all around, on the other side of the wall, saying \"of course.\"\n\n390\n00:17:08,169 --> 00:17:09,880\n\"People are not alone.\n\n391\n00:17:09,940 --> 00:17:16,840\nThey only feel alone.\" Mia, and one last idea, for the people who think their ask is different.\n\n392\n00:17:16,940 --> 00:17:17,169\nBigger.\n\n393\n00:17:18,169 --> 00:17:21,110\n\"My problem is not a box.\" Money.\n\n394\n00:17:21,620 --> 00:17:21,970\nHealth.\n\n395\n00:17:22,600 --> 00:17:23,580\nA hard family thing.\n\n396\n00:17:24,380 --> 00:17:25,020\nI understand.\n\n397\n00:17:25,729 --> 00:17:27,100\nAnd I want to say two things.\n\n398\n00:17:28,060 --> 00:17:28,800\nPlease, Ben.\n\n399\n00:17:29,020 --> 00:17:29,820\nThe two things.\n\n400\n00:17:30,440 --> 00:17:32,160\nFor the people with the big boxes.\n\n401\n00:17:32,600 --> 00:17:33,440\nThe heavy ones.\n\n402\n00:17:34,480 --> 00:17:35,240\nFirst, Mia.\n\n403\n00:17:35,780 --> 00:17:38,100\nThe study was about strangers and small things.\n\n404\n00:17:38,760 --> 00:17:40,820\nBut the same picture works for big things.\n\n405\n00:17:41,680 --> 00:17:43,240\nYou are guessing people at half.\n\n406\n00:17:43,980 --> 00:17:44,740\nThey are at double.\n\n407\n00:17:45,760 --> 00:17:48,140\nSecond, a big ask can start small.\n\n408\n00:17:48,890 --> 00:17:52,600\n\"Can I talk to you about something for ten minutes?\" That is a box.\n\n409\n00:17:53,400 --> 00:17:54,780\nEveryone can lift that box.\n\n410\n00:17:55,620 --> 00:17:57,360\n\"You are guessing them at half.\n\n411\n00:17:57,660 --> 00:18:01,640\nThey are at double.\" Ben, that is our message today.\n\n412\n00:18:01,700 --> 00:18:04,320\nPeople say yes twice as often as we think.\n\n413\n00:18:04,790 --> 00:18:08,600\nThe asking feels terrible to us, and normal to them.\n\n414\n00:18:08,640 --> 00:18:09,730\nHelping feels good.\n\n415\n00:18:10,100 --> 00:18:13,160\nAnd \"let me know\" is a door with a person in it.\n\n416\n00:18:13,280 --> 00:18:14,330\nSo find your box.\n\n417\n00:18:14,620 --> 00:18:15,440\nKnock on the door.\n\n418\n00:18:15,780 --> 00:18:16,640\nSeven words.\n\n419\n00:18:17,080 --> 00:18:20,240\n\"Can you help me with a box?\" And watch the face.\n\n420\n00:18:20,560 --> 00:18:21,640\nIt will light up.\n\n421\n00:18:22,520 --> 00:18:23,540\nIt will light up.\n\n422\n00:18:24,520 --> 00:18:29,420\nMia, let's repeat today's important words, slowly, because our listeners want to remember them.\n\n423\n00:18:30,740 --> 00:18:31,020\nAsk.\n\n424\n00:18:31,760 --> 00:18:32,620\nTo say what you need.\n\n425\n00:18:33,500 --> 00:18:34,120\nAsk for help.\n\n426\n00:18:35,100 --> 00:18:35,860\nAsk a question.\n\n427\n00:18:36,600 --> 00:18:37,220\nJust ask.\n\n428\n00:18:38,500 --> 00:18:38,770\nHelp.\n\n429\n00:18:39,520 --> 00:18:41,639\nTo do something for someone.\n\n430\n00:18:41,700 --> 00:18:42,620\nHelp me with a box.\n\n431\n00:18:43,900 --> 00:18:44,960\nHelping feels good.\n\n432\n00:18:46,320 --> 00:18:46,700\nPeople.\n\n433\n00:18:47,480 --> 00:18:48,500\nPeople say yes.\n\n434\n00:18:49,460 --> 00:18:50,880\nPeople are waiting in the door.\n\n435\n00:18:52,220 --> 00:18:52,560\nYes.\n\n436\n00:18:53,400 --> 00:18:54,880\nThe answer we do not expect.\n\n437\n00:18:55,900 --> 00:18:56,640\nThey said yes.\n\n438\n00:18:57,600 --> 00:18:58,960\nTwice as many yeses.\n\n439\n00:19:00,280 --> 00:19:00,700\nBox.\n\n440\n00:19:01,460 --> 00:19:02,040\nMy box.\n\n441\n00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:03,840\nFind your box.\n\n442\n00:19:05,320 --> 00:19:05,760\nAsk.\n\n443\n00:19:06,400 --> 00:19:06,760\nHelp.\n\n444\n00:19:07,480 --> 00:19:07,980\nPeople.\n\n445\n00:19:08,800 --> 00:19:09,280\nYes.\n\n446\n00:19:10,120 --> 00:19:10,660\nBox.\n\n447\n00:19:11,420 --> 00:19:13,280\nFive words, and one box.\n\n448\n00:19:13,640 --> 00:19:15,120\nAnd one big idea.\n\n449\n00:19:15,720 --> 00:19:16,580\nAsk for help.\n\n450\n00:19:17,340 --> 00:19:19,680\nNow, Ben, do you have a question for our listeners?\n\n451\n00:19:20,320 --> 00:19:20,780\nYes, Mia.\n\n452\n00:19:21,520 --> 00:19:24,340\nEveryone listening, here is my question.\n\n453\n00:19:24,420 --> 00:19:25,400\nWhat is your box?\n\n454\n00:19:26,380 --> 00:19:29,400\nThe thing you have been reaching for alone, with a broom.\n\n455\n00:19:30,400 --> 00:19:32,860\nWrite it in the comments in one short sentence.\n\n456\n00:19:33,820 --> 00:19:43,020\n\"My box is asking my boss for a day off.\" And then, if you want, add, \"I will ask this week.\" We are on chairs, too.\n\n457\n00:19:43,960 --> 00:19:45,040\nWe read every one.\n\n458\n00:19:45,880 --> 00:19:47,180\nWe are on chairs, too.\n\n459\n00:19:47,690 --> 00:19:52,000\nAnd if this episode helped you, please subscribe to Two Cups English.\n\n460\n00:19:52,060 --> 00:19:54,390\nWe make a new episode every few days.\n\n461\n00:19:54,840 --> 00:19:58,840\nSlow English, simple words, and one true story each time.\n\n462\n00:19:59,360 --> 00:20:02,020\nThe full text of this episode is in the description.\n\n463\n00:20:02,540 --> 00:20:06,260\nRead it, and then ask someone what one word means.\n\n464\n00:20:06,320 --> 00:20:09,000\nThank you for spending this time with us.\n\n465\n00:20:09,060 --> 00:20:12,450\nMaybe you are on a chair right now, with a broom, reaching.\n\n466\n00:20:13,560 --> 00:20:14,080\nGet down.\n\n467\n00:20:15,020 --> 00:20:15,780\nKnock on the door.\n\n468\n00:20:16,940 --> 00:20:17,720\nSeven words.\n\n469\n00:20:18,709 --> 00:20:22,100\n\"Can you help me with a box?\" And watch the face.\n\n470\n00:20:23,140 --> 00:20:24,520\nIt will not do what you think.\n\n471\n00:20:25,480 --> 00:20:26,820\nIt will do the opposite.\n\n472\n00:20:27,620 --> 00:20:28,300\nThis is Mia.\n\n473\n00:20:28,800 --> 00:20:29,540\nAnd this is Ben.\n\n474\n00:20:30,220 --> 00:20:32,000\nThis was Two Cups English.\n\n475\n00:20:32,520 --> 00:20:33,400\nAsk for help.\n\n476\n00:20:33,900 --> 00:20:35,120\nWe will see you next time."},{"code":"CUP15","file":"CUP15.srt","srt":"1\n00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,280\nHello everyone, and welcome to Two Cups English.\n\n2\n00:00:03,840 --> 00:00:06,720\nI'm Mia, and Ben is here with me.\n\n3\n00:00:06,780 --> 00:00:09,610\nThis podcast is for people who are learning English.\n\n4\n00:00:10,220 --> 00:00:11,060\nWe speak slowly.\n\n5\n00:00:11,640 --> 00:00:12,970\nWe use simple words.\n\n6\n00:00:13,560 --> 00:00:16,400\nAnd we repeat the important words many times.\n\n7\n00:00:17,360 --> 00:00:19,300\nToday, our topic is the window.\n\n8\n00:00:20,440 --> 00:00:22,580\nSitting by a window, for one minute.\n\n9\n00:00:23,860 --> 00:00:26,310\nBen, what is outside your window right now?\n\n10\n00:00:27,480 --> 00:00:28,180\nHi Mia.\n\n11\n00:00:28,260 --> 00:00:28,820\nHi everyone.\n\n12\n00:00:29,620 --> 00:00:35,420\nRight now, a roof, a grey sky, and one tree, Mia.\n\n13\n00:00:35,500 --> 00:00:37,000\nHalf of a tree.\n\n14\n00:00:37,100 --> 00:00:39,720\nThe other half is behind a wall.\n\n15\n00:00:39,830 --> 00:00:42,700\nAnd I look at it every hour, for one minute.\n\n16\n00:00:43,620 --> 00:00:44,180\nWhat about you?\n\n17\n00:00:44,900 --> 00:00:45,840\nWhat is outside yours?\n\n18\n00:00:46,780 --> 00:00:50,100\nA tree, a roof, and a cat on the roof, Ben.\n\n19\n00:00:50,860 --> 00:00:52,640\nBut I only know that since last month.\n\n20\n00:00:53,240 --> 00:00:55,200\nBecause last month, I moved a chair.\n\n21\n00:00:56,060 --> 00:00:58,040\nThat is the whole story, almost.\n\n22\n00:00:58,860 --> 00:01:03,000\nMy chair was in the corner of my room, facing the wall, next to the desk.\n\n23\n00:01:03,740 --> 00:01:05,120\nFor four years.\n\n24\n00:01:05,900 --> 00:01:07,960\nFour years of paint.\n\n25\n00:01:08,180 --> 00:01:09,960\nFour years facing a wall, Mia?\n\n26\n00:01:10,980 --> 00:01:14,320\nI did not know your room had a window at all.\n\n27\n00:01:14,360 --> 00:01:14,730\nAnd then?\n\n28\n00:01:16,400 --> 00:01:20,640\nAnd then one Sunday, for no reason, I pulled it to the window, Ben.\n\n29\n00:01:21,180 --> 00:01:22,030\nAnd I sat down.\n\n30\n00:01:22,780 --> 00:01:23,800\nAnd I looked outside.\n\n31\n00:01:24,540 --> 00:01:27,000\nAnd I sat there for twenty minutes.\n\n32\n00:01:27,860 --> 00:01:28,260\nTwenty.\n\n33\n00:01:29,300 --> 00:01:30,020\nI did not read.\n\n34\n00:01:30,580 --> 00:01:31,880\nI did not look at my phone.\n\n35\n00:01:32,560 --> 00:01:33,620\nI looked at a tree.\n\n36\n00:01:34,440 --> 00:01:35,080\nJust a tree.\n\n37\n00:01:36,020 --> 00:01:38,420\nAnd there was a tree behind me the whole time.\n\n38\n00:01:39,280 --> 00:01:40,460\nFour years.\n\n39\n00:01:41,230 --> 00:01:46,039\n\"There was a tree behind you the whole time.\" Mia, that is our topic today.\n\n40\n00:01:46,820 --> 00:01:47,960\nSo here is our plan.\n\n41\n00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:52,330\nIn part one, we talk about our own windows and walls.\n\n42\n00:01:53,280 --> 00:01:57,840\nIn part two, we ask why one minute at a window does something to the body.\n\n43\n00:01:58,640 --> 00:02:01,360\nAnd I have a true story for that, from a hospital.\n\n44\n00:02:02,520 --> 00:02:06,520\nIn part three, we share simple ways to use a window every day.\n\n45\n00:02:07,460 --> 00:02:08,180\nDoes that sound good?\n\n46\n00:02:09,009 --> 00:02:10,039\nThat sounds good, Ben.\n\n47\n00:02:10,640 --> 00:02:14,280\nAnd to everyone listening, please do not worry if you miss something.\n\n48\n00:02:14,820 --> 00:02:16,640\nYou will hear it again.\n\n49\n00:02:16,680 --> 00:02:19,800\nAnd if there is a window near you, turn a little toward it.\n\n50\n00:02:20,540 --> 00:02:22,120\nThat is the whole homework.\n\n51\n00:02:22,960 --> 00:02:23,480\nOkay, Ben.\n\n52\n00:02:23,820 --> 00:02:24,359\nPart one.\n\n53\n00:02:25,360 --> 00:02:27,840\nYou asked me why I faced the wall for four years.\n\n54\n00:02:28,680 --> 00:02:29,300\nI did, Mia.\n\n55\n00:02:30,060 --> 00:02:33,540\nFour years is a long time to look at paint.\n\n56\n00:02:33,600 --> 00:02:35,300\nDid you choose the wall?\n\n57\n00:02:35,360 --> 00:02:36,740\nI never chose the wall, Ben.\n\n58\n00:02:37,340 --> 00:02:39,960\nI chose the desk, and the wall came with it.\n\n59\n00:02:40,829 --> 00:02:42,519\nThe desk was where the work was.\n\n60\n00:02:43,200 --> 00:02:45,240\nAnd I think that is how most of us live.\n\n61\n00:02:46,060 --> 00:02:47,780\nWe do not choose to face the wall.\n\n62\n00:02:48,360 --> 00:02:52,940\nWe choose the desk, the screen, the sofa, and the wall comes with it.\n\n63\n00:02:53,820 --> 00:02:55,820\nThe wall comes with the desk, Mia.\n\n64\n00:02:56,700 --> 00:02:58,840\nAnd let me ask about those twenty minutes.\n\n65\n00:02:59,740 --> 00:03:01,160\nWhat did you actually look at?\n\n66\n00:03:01,980 --> 00:03:03,520\nA tree, a roof.\n\n67\n00:03:04,360 --> 00:03:05,880\nBut what did the eyes do?\n\n68\n00:03:06,660 --> 00:03:08,000\nThey wandered, Ben.\n\n69\n00:03:08,440 --> 00:03:09,360\nThat is the word.\n\n70\n00:03:10,160 --> 00:03:12,040\nAt the desk, my eyes are pinned.\n\n71\n00:03:12,860 --> 00:03:15,140\nOne point, thirty centimeters away.\n\n72\n00:03:16,020 --> 00:03:18,120\nAt the window, they wandered.\n\n73\n00:03:18,960 --> 00:03:24,540\nTree, then roof, then sky, then the cat, then back to the tree.\n\n74\n00:03:25,420 --> 00:03:28,040\nNothing held them, and nothing asked.\n\n75\n00:03:28,740 --> 00:03:32,820\nAnd after twenty minutes, something behind my eyes stopped pushing.\n\n76\n00:03:33,600 --> 00:03:38,170\n\"Something behind the eyes stopped pushing.\" Mia, I know that feeling.\n\n77\n00:03:38,740 --> 00:03:40,789\nAnd I have a window, and I earned it.\n\n78\n00:03:41,580 --> 00:03:44,320\nAt my old job, the desks by the window were for the managers.\n\n79\n00:03:45,020 --> 00:03:47,660\nI sat inside, by a wall, for three years.\n\n80\n00:03:48,380 --> 00:03:51,320\nThen I changed jobs, and my new desk was by a window.\n\n81\n00:03:52,120 --> 00:03:53,620\nManagers get the windows, Ben.\n\n82\n00:03:54,120 --> 00:03:55,080\nThat says something.\n\n83\n00:03:55,900 --> 00:03:58,890\nWhy do the people with the most power take the window?\n\n84\n00:03:59,600 --> 00:04:01,240\nBecause they know, Mia.\n\n85\n00:04:01,320 --> 00:04:02,370\nOr their bodies know.\n\n86\n00:04:03,280 --> 00:04:13,300\nNobody says, \"I want the window because my eyes need a far thing.\" They say, \"I want the window because I am the manager.\" But the reason underneath is the same.\n\n87\n00:04:13,900 --> 00:04:15,160\nThe eyes want out.\n\n88\n00:04:16,060 --> 00:04:16,980\nEverybody's eyes.\n\n89\n00:04:17,740 --> 00:04:19,940\nWe just give the window to the person who can take it.\n\n90\n00:04:20,900 --> 00:04:22,220\nA window desk, Ben.\n\n91\n00:04:22,920 --> 00:04:24,080\nAnd did it change something?\n\n92\n00:04:24,600 --> 00:04:27,620\nOr is a desk a desk, wall or window?\n\n93\n00:04:28,440 --> 00:04:30,140\nIt changed my afternoons, Mia.\n\n94\n00:04:30,970 --> 00:04:33,670\nAt the wall desk, at three o'clock, I was finished.\n\n95\n00:04:34,300 --> 00:04:34,750\nTired.\n\n96\n00:04:35,340 --> 00:04:36,480\nHeavy.\n\n97\n00:04:36,520 --> 00:04:41,570\nAt the window desk, at three o'clock, I looked out for one minute, and the tiredness went somewhere.\n\n98\n00:04:42,480 --> 00:04:43,100\nNot all of it.\n\n99\n00:04:43,620 --> 00:04:46,700\nBut some of it went out of the window, and I did not go with it.\n\n100\n00:04:47,770 --> 00:04:52,060\n\"The tiredness went out of the window.\" Ben, where did it go?\n\n101\n00:04:52,660 --> 00:04:53,800\nWhat is the window doing?\n\n102\n00:04:54,780 --> 00:04:56,160\nTwo things, I think, Mia.\n\n103\n00:04:57,020 --> 00:04:58,230\nFirst, distance.\n\n104\n00:04:59,120 --> 00:05:03,780\nAt a desk, the eyes look at something thirty centimeters away, all day.\n\n105\n00:05:03,860 --> 00:05:04,740\nA screen.\n\n106\n00:05:04,860 --> 00:05:05,280\nA page.\n\n107\n00:05:06,140 --> 00:05:09,920\nThe eye muscles hold that distance, hour after hour, tight.\n\n108\n00:05:10,820 --> 00:05:12,620\nAnd the window is far.\n\n109\n00:05:12,660 --> 00:05:14,400\nThe eyes let go.\n\n110\n00:05:14,460 --> 00:05:16,840\nThey rest on something far.\n\n111\n00:05:16,900 --> 00:05:19,740\nThe eyes were tight, and now they are not, Ben.\n\n112\n00:05:20,460 --> 00:05:21,640\nI felt that at the window.\n\n113\n00:05:22,300 --> 00:05:23,680\nSomething let go.\n\n114\n00:05:24,440 --> 00:05:25,220\nAnd the second thing?\n\n115\n00:05:26,080 --> 00:05:26,879\nMovement, Mia.\n\n116\n00:05:27,640 --> 00:05:29,750\nThe window moves, and the wall does not.\n\n117\n00:05:30,520 --> 00:05:31,620\nA cloud goes past.\n\n118\n00:05:32,160 --> 00:05:33,580\nA bird lands.\n\n119\n00:05:33,660 --> 00:05:36,039\nThe light changes from white to gold.\n\n120\n00:05:36,080 --> 00:05:37,420\nNothing important.\n\n121\n00:05:37,440 --> 00:05:39,000\nBut the eyes like it.\n\n122\n00:05:39,009 --> 00:05:40,620\nThey follow it, for one minute.\n\n123\n00:05:41,320 --> 00:05:46,000\nAnd following something slow and unimportant is rest for the eyes and for the head.\n\n124\n00:05:46,910 --> 00:05:49,020\nThe eyes like slow things, Ben.\n\n125\n00:05:49,600 --> 00:05:50,980\nBut the phone moves too.\n\n126\n00:05:51,620 --> 00:05:52,160\nFast.\n\n127\n00:05:52,760 --> 00:05:53,740\nIs that not the same?\n\n128\n00:05:54,620 --> 00:05:56,960\nThe phone moves, Mia, but it asks.\n\n129\n00:05:57,760 --> 00:06:00,080\nEvery movement on the phone asks for something.\n\n130\n00:06:00,740 --> 00:06:01,200\nLook here.\n\n131\n00:06:01,680 --> 00:06:02,220\nTap this.\n\n132\n00:06:02,820 --> 00:06:03,360\nRead that.\n\n133\n00:06:04,060 --> 00:06:05,560\nThe cloud asks for nothing.\n\n134\n00:06:06,180 --> 00:06:07,020\nIt goes past.\n\n135\n00:06:07,660 --> 00:06:08,980\nYou look, or you do not.\n\n136\n00:06:09,680 --> 00:06:10,680\nThat is the difference.\n\n137\n00:06:11,600 --> 00:06:13,220\nMovement that asks is work.\n\n138\n00:06:14,100 --> 00:06:16,160\nMovement that asks nothing is rest.\n\n139\n00:06:16,930 --> 00:06:18,650\nThe window only has the second kind.\n\n140\n00:06:19,520 --> 00:06:27,360\n\"The tree is the only thing in the room that asks for nothing.\" Ben, that is why I stayed twenty minutes.\n\n141\n00:06:28,020 --> 00:06:29,440\nNothing was asking.\n\n142\n00:06:30,200 --> 00:06:31,440\nThe desk asks.\n\n143\n00:06:31,900 --> 00:06:33,220\nThe phone asks.\n\n144\n00:06:33,840 --> 00:06:35,780\nThe tree does not ask.\n\n145\n00:06:37,360 --> 00:06:38,900\nOkay, so that was part one.\n\n146\n00:06:39,380 --> 00:06:40,219\nOur windows.\n\n147\n00:06:40,750 --> 00:06:42,080\nNow, part two.\n\n148\n00:06:43,160 --> 00:06:46,300\nWhy does one minute at a window do something to the body?\n\n149\n00:06:47,120 --> 00:06:49,170\nBen, your story is from a hospital.\n\n150\n00:06:50,240 --> 00:06:50,780\nIt is, Mia.\n\n151\n00:06:51,160 --> 00:06:52,220\nOkay, part two.\n\n152\n00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:54,260\nThis is a true story.\n\n153\n00:06:55,040 --> 00:07:00,640\nIn 1984, a scientist named Roger Ulrich published a small study in the journal Science.\n\n154\n00:07:01,360 --> 00:07:03,740\nHe looked at patients in a hospital in Pennsylvania.\n\n155\n00:07:04,340 --> 00:07:07,500\nForty-six people, all after the same operation.\n\n156\n00:07:08,140 --> 00:07:09,140\nGallbladder surgery.\n\n157\n00:07:09,920 --> 00:07:10,680\nSame doctors.\n\n158\n00:07:11,080 --> 00:07:11,740\nSame nurses.\n\n159\n00:07:12,130 --> 00:07:13,180\nSame rooms.\n\n160\n00:07:13,240 --> 00:07:14,720\nSame food, even.\n\n161\n00:07:14,880 --> 00:07:15,980\nSame everything, Ben?\n\n162\n00:07:16,720 --> 00:07:20,470\nSame doctors, same rooms, same operation?\n\n163\n00:07:20,500 --> 00:07:22,260\nThen what was different in the rooms?\n\n164\n00:07:22,800 --> 00:07:23,720\nThe window, Mia.\n\n165\n00:07:24,500 --> 00:07:26,650\nHalf of the rooms looked at a group of trees.\n\n166\n00:07:27,130 --> 00:07:29,600\nThe other half looked at a brick wall.\n\n167\n00:07:29,640 --> 00:07:30,710\nThat was the only difference.\n\n168\n00:07:31,260 --> 00:07:32,840\nTrees, or a brick wall.\n\n169\n00:07:33,660 --> 00:07:36,590\nAnd Ulrich looked at the records for nine years of patients.\n\n170\n00:07:37,480 --> 00:07:39,400\nNine years of records, Ben.\n\n171\n00:07:40,060 --> 00:07:42,200\nSo it was not one week of patients.\n\n172\n00:07:42,460 --> 00:07:43,960\nIt was many.\n\n173\n00:07:43,990 --> 00:07:46,330\nAnd the doctors did not know about the trees?\n\n174\n00:07:46,940 --> 00:07:49,480\nNobody was thinking about the trees, Mia.\n\n175\n00:07:49,520 --> 00:07:51,620\nThe rooms were given by chance.\n\n176\n00:07:51,640 --> 00:07:53,660\nWhoever came in got the next empty bed.\n\n177\n00:07:54,420 --> 00:07:57,280\nSome got trees, some got the wall.\n\n178\n00:07:57,340 --> 00:07:59,120\nThat is why the study is good.\n\n179\n00:07:59,160 --> 00:07:59,980\nNobody chose.\n\n180\n00:08:00,460 --> 00:08:02,380\nAnd the trees still made a difference.\n\n181\n00:08:03,200 --> 00:08:05,160\nTrees or a brick wall, Ben.\n\n182\n00:08:05,760 --> 00:08:06,140\nAnd?\n\n183\n00:08:06,860 --> 00:08:08,840\nWhat did nine years of records say?\n\n184\n00:08:09,660 --> 00:08:12,060\nThe patients with the trees went home sooner, Mia.\n\n185\n00:08:12,630 --> 00:08:14,380\nAbout a day sooner.\n\n186\n00:08:14,440 --> 00:08:16,540\nThat is one less night in a hospital bed.\n\n187\n00:08:17,310 --> 00:08:20,490\nThey asked for less pain medicine, and weaker pain medicine.\n\n188\n00:08:21,130 --> 00:08:23,460\nAnd the nurses wrote fewer bad notes about them.\n\n189\n00:08:24,240 --> 00:08:28,820\n\"Patient is upset.\" \"Patient needs encouragement.\" Fewer of those.\n\n190\n00:08:29,500 --> 00:08:31,540\nOnly from what the eyes saw from the bed.\n\n191\n00:08:32,320 --> 00:08:33,600\nA day sooner, Ben?\n\n192\n00:08:34,180 --> 00:08:34,960\nFrom a window?\n\n193\n00:08:35,920 --> 00:08:37,520\nOnly from what the eyes saw?\n\n194\n00:08:38,300 --> 00:08:40,580\nThat is a big thing from a small thing.\n\n195\n00:08:41,360 --> 00:08:42,380\nOnly from that, Mia.\n\n196\n00:08:43,010 --> 00:08:44,120\nThe body was the same.\n\n197\n00:08:44,680 --> 00:08:45,840\nThe medicine was the same.\n\n198\n00:08:46,420 --> 00:08:47,800\nThe trees were the difference.\n\n199\n00:08:48,560 --> 00:08:50,420\nTrees do not give medicine.\n\n200\n00:08:50,460 --> 00:08:52,090\nThey give the eyes a place to go.\n\n201\n00:08:52,950 --> 00:08:55,540\nAnd I think the study means something simple.\n\n202\n00:08:55,570 --> 00:08:58,080\nThe eyes are not only for reading.\n\n203\n00:08:58,120 --> 00:09:02,360\nThe eyes are a door, and what comes through the door goes into the body.\n\n204\n00:09:03,340 --> 00:09:11,300\n\"The eyes are a door.\" Ben, my body was not sick, but it was tired, and the wall was not helping.\n\n205\n00:09:11,989 --> 00:09:15,620\nI was feeding my eyes a wall for four years.\n\n206\n00:09:15,660 --> 00:09:18,370\nAnd the patients with the wall needed more medicine.\n\n207\n00:09:19,220 --> 00:09:20,820\nSame thing, smaller.\n\n208\n00:09:21,700 --> 00:09:23,180\nSame thing, smaller, Mia.\n\n209\n00:09:23,770 --> 00:09:26,440\nAnd I think we stopped believing in the outside.\n\n210\n00:09:26,480 --> 00:09:28,140\nWe live inside now.\n\n211\n00:09:28,200 --> 00:09:33,440\nInside the house, inside the office, inside the car, inside the phone.\n\n212\n00:09:34,120 --> 00:09:38,100\nThe outside is a place we cross to get to another inside.\n\n213\n00:09:38,120 --> 00:09:40,900\nAnd the window is the outside, delivered.\n\n214\n00:09:40,940 --> 00:09:41,850\nNo shoes needed.\n\n215\n00:09:42,660 --> 00:09:46,960\n\"The outside, delivered, no shoes.\" Ben, I like that.\n\n216\n00:09:47,500 --> 00:09:49,800\nBecause I am lazy about the outside, honestly.\n\n217\n00:09:50,320 --> 00:09:54,120\nThe walk from our walking episode is good, and I do it, but not every day.\n\n218\n00:09:54,660 --> 00:09:56,400\nThe window is every day.\n\n219\n00:09:56,880 --> 00:09:57,900\nOne minute a day.\n\n220\n00:09:58,480 --> 00:10:00,320\nIt is the outside for tired people.\n\n221\n00:10:00,840 --> 00:10:02,600\nThe outside for tired people, Mia.\n\n222\n00:10:03,240 --> 00:10:08,800\nAnd for busy people, and for people who cannot leave the bed, like Ulrich's patients.\n\n223\n00:10:08,810 --> 00:10:10,580\nThey could not walk to the trees.\n\n224\n00:10:10,620 --> 00:10:12,660\nThe trees came to the window.\n\n225\n00:10:12,760 --> 00:10:14,240\nThat is the whole gift of a window.\n\n226\n00:10:14,720 --> 00:10:16,640\nThe trees came to the window, Ben.\n\n227\n00:10:17,520 --> 00:10:21,420\nSo a window is a small trip outside, without leaving.\n\n228\n00:10:21,480 --> 00:10:23,780\nIs that why it works, even for a minute?\n\n229\n00:10:23,800 --> 00:10:25,300\nI think so, Mia.\n\n230\n00:10:25,360 --> 00:10:27,300\nThe body knows it is inside.\n\n231\n00:10:27,320 --> 00:10:28,960\nBut the eyes do not.\n\n232\n00:10:29,020 --> 00:10:31,320\nThe eyes only know what they see.\n\n233\n00:10:31,340 --> 00:10:33,640\nShow them a tree, and they think they are in a park.\n\n234\n00:10:34,420 --> 00:10:39,160\nFor one minute, the eyes are in the park, and the rest of you comes along.\n\n235\n00:10:39,240 --> 00:10:40,980\nThat is the trick of the window.\n\n236\n00:10:41,040 --> 00:10:43,500\nIt lies to the eyes, in a good way.\n\n237\n00:10:43,540 --> 00:10:45,240\nCan I ask about the minute, Ben?\n\n238\n00:10:45,740 --> 00:10:48,020\nBecause you keep saying one minute.\n\n239\n00:10:48,050 --> 00:10:49,360\nAnd I want to be honest.\n\n240\n00:10:49,640 --> 00:10:51,640\nA minute at a window is long.\n\n241\n00:10:52,700 --> 00:10:53,620\nIt is long, Mia.\n\n242\n00:10:54,260 --> 00:10:57,700\nThe first time, I looked at my watch after twenty seconds.\n\n243\n00:10:57,710 --> 00:11:00,060\nThe eyes wanted to go back to the screen.\n\n244\n00:11:00,100 --> 00:11:01,380\nThey were pulling.\n\n245\n00:11:01,440 --> 00:11:04,360\nBecause the eyes are used to being fed every second.\n\n246\n00:11:04,440 --> 00:11:05,960\nThe window feeds slowly.\n\n247\n00:11:06,600 --> 00:11:08,480\nSo the first minutes are a small fight.\n\n248\n00:11:09,240 --> 00:11:11,720\nTwenty seconds and you looked at your watch, Ben.\n\n249\n00:11:12,280 --> 00:11:14,200\nThat is the eyes begging for the screen.\n\n250\n00:11:14,820 --> 00:11:16,600\nWhat did you do at second twenty-one?\n\n251\n00:11:17,640 --> 00:11:19,140\nI looked at the tree again, Mia.\n\n252\n00:11:19,200 --> 00:11:20,260\nThat is all.\n\n253\n00:11:20,380 --> 00:11:23,690\nI told my eyes, \"Not yet.\" Like a child at a shop.\n\n254\n00:11:24,340 --> 00:11:26,390\nAnd at second forty, they stopped pulling.\n\n255\n00:11:27,040 --> 00:11:30,239\nAnd at the end of the minute, I did not want to go back.\n\n256\n00:11:30,249 --> 00:11:33,160\nThe eyes only fight for the first half of the first minute.\n\n257\n00:11:33,800 --> 00:11:36,000\nAfter that, they remember what they are for.\n\n258\n00:11:36,490 --> 00:11:43,420\n\"The first minutes are a small fight, and then the minute is not enough.\" Ben, that was exactly my week.\n\n259\n00:11:43,880 --> 00:11:47,580\nDay one, twenty minutes felt like a gift, because I was so tired.\n\n260\n00:11:48,080 --> 00:11:50,460\nDay two, five minutes felt like forever.\n\n261\n00:11:51,040 --> 00:11:53,520\nDay five, one minute was not enough again.\n\n262\n00:11:53,940 --> 00:11:54,830\nThe eyes learned.\n\n263\n00:11:55,600 --> 00:11:57,840\nThe eyes learned, Mia.\n\n264\n00:11:57,900 --> 00:11:59,140\nThat is the whole thing.\n\n265\n00:12:00,100 --> 00:12:01,760\nOkay, so that was part two.\n\n266\n00:12:01,800 --> 00:12:02,830\nWhy the window works.\n\n267\n00:12:03,140 --> 00:12:05,780\nWe talked about Ulrich and the hospital with the trees.\n\n268\n00:12:06,120 --> 00:12:07,780\nWe said the eyes are a door.\n\n269\n00:12:08,180 --> 00:12:10,800\nAnd we said the first minutes are a small fight.\n\n270\n00:12:11,260 --> 00:12:12,680\nNow, part three.\n\n271\n00:12:12,740 --> 00:12:15,060\nSimple ways to use a window every day.\n\n272\n00:12:15,540 --> 00:12:16,760\nMia, you go first.\n\n273\n00:12:17,560 --> 00:12:19,040\nOkay, part three.\n\n274\n00:12:19,100 --> 00:12:21,200\nMy first idea is the morning window, Ben.\n\n275\n00:12:21,760 --> 00:12:24,560\nBefore the phone, from our small steps episode.\n\n276\n00:12:25,260 --> 00:12:26,680\nI sit at the window with my tea.\n\n277\n00:12:27,160 --> 00:12:27,900\nThree minutes.\n\n278\n00:12:28,480 --> 00:12:30,280\nI look at the tree and the roof.\n\n279\n00:12:30,860 --> 00:12:33,320\nAnd the day is wider after that.\n\n280\n00:12:33,360 --> 00:12:34,070\nNot longer.\n\n281\n00:12:34,789 --> 00:12:35,200\nWider.\n\n282\n00:12:35,710 --> 00:12:40,680\n\"The day is wider after that.\" Mia, my first idea is the hourly minute.\n\n283\n00:12:41,080 --> 00:12:43,220\nAt my window desk, I made a small rule.\n\n284\n00:12:43,640 --> 00:12:45,780\nEvery hour, one minute at the window.\n\n285\n00:12:46,150 --> 00:12:48,350\nStand up, look far, sit down.\n\n286\n00:12:48,920 --> 00:12:53,330\nAnd I noticed that one minute an hour is worth more than the coffee at three o'clock.\n\n287\n00:12:54,780 --> 00:12:56,300\nWorth more than the coffee, Ben.\n\n288\n00:12:56,660 --> 00:12:57,880\nThat is a big claim.\n\n289\n00:12:58,560 --> 00:13:00,119\nMy second idea is for English.\n\n290\n00:13:00,500 --> 00:13:02,880\nBecause there is a window in language learning, too.\n\n291\n00:13:03,560 --> 00:13:04,060\nListening.\n\n292\n00:13:04,680 --> 00:13:09,360\nWhen I listen to Spanish for a long time, my head gets tight, like my eyes at the desk.\n\n293\n00:13:09,960 --> 00:13:11,600\nSo now I listen at the window.\n\n294\n00:13:12,180 --> 00:13:13,380\nSpanish at the window, Mia?\n\n295\n00:13:13,950 --> 00:13:15,370\nDoes the window help the words?\n\n296\n00:13:15,700 --> 00:13:16,580\nOr only the eyes?\n\n297\n00:13:17,200 --> 00:13:17,960\nIt does, Ben.\n\n298\n00:13:18,450 --> 00:13:21,360\nSpanish at the window is easier than Spanish at the desk.\n\n299\n00:13:21,920 --> 00:13:24,200\nThe tight head is a bad head for language.\n\n300\n00:13:24,840 --> 00:13:26,440\nWords need room to land.\n\n301\n00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:29,420\nAnd the eyes on a far tree give the head room.\n\n302\n00:13:29,980 --> 00:13:31,840\nSo listen to this podcast at a window.\n\n303\n00:13:32,340 --> 00:13:32,880\nOr a door.\n\n304\n00:13:33,520 --> 00:13:34,760\nLet the words land.\n\n305\n00:13:35,230 --> 00:13:40,800\n\"Let the words land.\" Mia, my second idea is for the people who have no window.\n\n306\n00:13:41,160 --> 00:13:42,280\nBecause some are listening.\n\n307\n00:13:42,800 --> 00:13:43,760\nA basement office.\n\n308\n00:13:44,220 --> 00:13:45,780\nA room with a wall.\n\n309\n00:13:45,800 --> 00:13:50,100\nAnd I want to say what Ulrich's patients could not do, and we can.\n\n310\n00:13:50,140 --> 00:13:50,840\nWe can move.\n\n311\n00:13:51,480 --> 00:13:52,940\nBorrow a window.\n\n312\n00:13:52,950 --> 00:13:53,890\nThe kitchen at work.\n\n313\n00:13:54,420 --> 00:13:55,720\nThe corridor with a window.\n\n314\n00:13:56,360 --> 00:13:57,090\nSixty seconds.\n\n315\n00:13:57,680 --> 00:13:58,940\nBorrow a window, Ben.\n\n316\n00:13:59,580 --> 00:14:02,460\nAnd if there is no window at all, there is the door.\n\n317\n00:14:03,120 --> 00:14:05,120\nA door to the street is a big window.\n\n318\n00:14:05,760 --> 00:14:08,039\nSixty seconds outside the door.\n\n319\n00:14:08,100 --> 00:14:10,000\nThe far thing is the point.\n\n320\n00:14:10,060 --> 00:14:11,500\nNot the glass.\n\n321\n00:14:11,560 --> 00:14:13,110\nA gap between buildings counts.\n\n322\n00:14:13,760 --> 00:14:15,660\nEven a window in a door counts.\n\n323\n00:14:15,670 --> 00:14:24,800\n\"The far thing is the point.\" Mia, and one thing about the tree, because Ulrich's study was about trees, and I think trees are special, even in a window.\n\n324\n00:14:25,230 --> 00:14:27,300\nA tree moves without going anywhere.\n\n325\n00:14:27,820 --> 00:14:29,960\nLeaves, branches, light through them.\n\n326\n00:14:30,600 --> 00:14:33,120\nIt is the slowest alive thing you can look at.\n\n327\n00:14:33,540 --> 00:14:38,100\n\"The slowest alive thing.\" Ben, my window tree is not beautiful.\n\n328\n00:14:38,620 --> 00:14:42,160\nIt is a city tree, half dead on one side, next to a bin.\n\n329\n00:14:42,760 --> 00:14:43,560\nAnd it is enough.\n\n330\n00:14:44,240 --> 00:14:48,480\nI watch it move in the wind for one minute, and something in me moves with it.\n\n331\n00:14:49,080 --> 00:14:50,610\nA tree is the right speed.\n\n332\n00:14:52,440 --> 00:14:53,920\nA tree is the right speed, Mia.\n\n333\n00:14:54,500 --> 00:14:55,980\nCan I tell you about my worst month?\n\n334\n00:14:56,400 --> 00:14:59,600\nBecause it shows what happens when we close the window without deciding to.\n\n335\n00:15:00,040 --> 00:15:00,780\nPlease, Ben.\n\n336\n00:15:01,340 --> 00:15:03,200\nHow do you close a window without deciding?\n\n337\n00:15:03,680 --> 00:15:04,260\nCurtains?\n\n338\n00:15:04,660 --> 00:15:05,120\nA screen?\n\n339\n00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:06,680\nCurtains, Mia.\n\n340\n00:15:07,260 --> 00:15:09,040\nLast winter, I closed the curtains.\n\n341\n00:15:09,440 --> 00:15:15,480\nIt was cold, and dark, and I thought, \"Nothing to see.\" And I left them closed for six weeks.\n\n342\n00:15:15,520 --> 00:15:17,520\nAnd my afternoons went back to the wall desk.\n\n343\n00:15:18,080 --> 00:15:18,490\nTired.\n\n344\n00:15:18,980 --> 00:15:19,290\nHeavy.\n\n345\n00:15:20,040 --> 00:15:21,160\nI did not connect it.\n\n346\n00:15:21,700 --> 00:15:26,900\nThen one morning, I opened them to let the cat out, and there was a bird on the bare tree.\n\n347\n00:15:26,980 --> 00:15:29,380\nA bird on the bare tree, Ben.\n\n348\n00:15:29,420 --> 00:15:31,650\nAnd you had closed the door and called it winter.\n\n349\n00:15:32,240 --> 00:15:32,880\nI had, Mia.\n\n350\n00:15:33,560 --> 00:15:35,920\nSo my third idea is very small.\n\n351\n00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:37,700\nLeave the curtains open.\n\n352\n00:15:37,780 --> 00:15:38,500\nEven in the dark.\n\n353\n00:15:39,220 --> 00:15:40,740\nEven when there is nothing to see.\n\n354\n00:15:41,440 --> 00:15:44,680\nA bare tree in winter is still a tree, for the eyes.\n\n355\n00:15:45,460 --> 00:15:47,280\nUlrich's trees were not beautiful either.\n\n356\n00:15:47,880 --> 00:15:48,840\nThey were just trees.\n\n357\n00:15:49,500 --> 00:15:51,080\nThe window is always on.\n\n358\n00:15:51,700 --> 00:15:52,800\nDo not turn it off.\n\n359\n00:15:53,380 --> 00:15:55,220\nThe window is always on, Ben.\n\n360\n00:15:55,779 --> 00:15:56,499\nEven at night?\n\n361\n00:15:57,100 --> 00:15:58,900\nThere is nothing to look at at night.\n\n362\n00:15:59,260 --> 00:16:00,840\nThere is the dark, Mia.\n\n363\n00:16:00,870 --> 00:16:01,960\nAnd the dark is far.\n\n364\n00:16:02,550 --> 00:16:05,940\nThe eyes rest on the dark the way they rest on a tree.\n\n365\n00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:07,160\nA street light.\n\n366\n00:16:07,320 --> 00:16:09,260\nOne window across the road.\n\n367\n00:16:09,270 --> 00:16:10,800\nThe moon, if you are lucky.\n\n368\n00:16:11,460 --> 00:16:14,800\nLook at the far dark for one minute, and the day gets smaller.\n\n369\n00:16:15,520 --> 00:16:16,720\nNight is a window too.\n\n370\n00:16:17,240 --> 00:16:22,340\n\"Night is a window too.\" Ben, my third idea is the evening window.\n\n371\n00:16:23,100 --> 00:16:27,660\nAfter dinner, when the light goes, I sit at the window for the last five minutes of light.\n\n372\n00:16:28,270 --> 00:16:29,500\nThe sky does its thing.\n\n373\n00:16:30,100 --> 00:16:32,260\nGold, then grey, then dark.\n\n374\n00:16:32,820 --> 00:16:35,040\nAnd the day finishes in front of me.\n\n375\n00:16:35,100 --> 00:16:35,880\nNot stopped.\n\n376\n00:16:36,500 --> 00:16:37,040\nFinished.\n\n377\n00:16:37,980 --> 00:16:40,040\nEvery night, the same five minutes.\n\n378\n00:16:40,440 --> 00:16:47,440\n\"The day finished in front of you, not stopped.\" Mia, and my fourth idea is looking with someone.\n\n379\n00:16:47,480 --> 00:16:50,020\nBecause the window is also a place for two people.\n\n380\n00:16:50,670 --> 00:16:53,310\nMy daughter and I sit at the window on Sunday mornings.\n\n381\n00:16:53,940 --> 00:16:54,520\nTen minutes.\n\n382\n00:16:55,160 --> 00:16:57,640\nWe look outside and say what we see.\n\n383\n00:16:57,650 --> 00:17:00,980\n\"A bird.\" \"A man with a dog.\" \"The cat again.\"\n\n384\n00:17:01,480 --> 00:17:03,840\nThe window is the only screen we share, Ben.\n\n385\n00:17:04,560 --> 00:17:07,200\nI do that with my mother, on the phone.\n\n386\n00:17:07,280 --> 00:17:13,780\nI sit at my window, she sits at hers, four hundred kilometers away, and we tell each other what is outside.\n\n387\n00:17:14,340 --> 00:17:14,940\nHer tree.\n\n388\n00:17:15,380 --> 00:17:16,020\nMy tree.\n\n389\n00:17:16,760 --> 00:17:18,680\nTwo windows, one call.\n\n390\n00:17:19,100 --> 00:17:20,540\nTwo windows, one call.\n\n391\n00:17:21,210 --> 00:17:23,340\nMia, that is beautiful.\n\n392\n00:17:23,380 --> 00:17:28,090\nAnd one last idea, about the word \"look.\" Not \"watch.\" Look.\n\n393\n00:17:28,760 --> 00:17:30,140\nWatching is for a screen.\n\n394\n00:17:30,680 --> 00:17:31,540\nIt has a story.\n\n395\n00:17:32,140 --> 00:17:33,440\nLooking has no story.\n\n396\n00:17:34,080 --> 00:17:35,000\nYou look at a roof.\n\n397\n00:17:35,540 --> 00:17:36,860\nNothing happens.\n\n398\n00:17:36,920 --> 00:17:38,300\nThe roof never speeds up.\n\n399\n00:17:39,040 --> 00:17:39,850\nThat is the rest.\n\n400\n00:17:40,640 --> 00:17:46,200\n\"The roof never speeds up.\" Ben, I want to put that on the wall, next to the window.\n\n401\n00:17:47,300 --> 00:17:50,160\nAnd something strange happened at my window, in week two.\n\n402\n00:17:50,910 --> 00:17:52,600\nI started to know the outside.\n\n403\n00:17:53,440 --> 00:17:54,520\nThe cat comes at eight.\n\n404\n00:17:55,060 --> 00:17:56,840\nThe man with the bag at eight fifteen.\n\n405\n00:17:57,560 --> 00:17:59,440\nThe light hits the roof at nine.\n\n406\n00:18:00,160 --> 00:18:02,659\nThe outside has a schedule, and I know it now.\n\n407\n00:18:03,620 --> 00:18:08,140\nA small world, always there, behind the chair, Mia.\n\n408\n00:18:08,920 --> 00:18:10,400\nThat is the tree behind you again.\n\n409\n00:18:11,090 --> 00:18:12,900\nAnd that is our message today.\n\n410\n00:18:12,940 --> 00:18:14,700\nWe face walls without choosing to.\n\n411\n00:18:15,480 --> 00:18:19,040\nThe desk, the screen, the curtains.\n\n412\n00:18:19,060 --> 00:18:23,800\nAnd the eyes were made for far things, slow things, alive things.\n\n413\n00:18:24,600 --> 00:18:25,020\nA tree.\n\n414\n00:18:25,900 --> 00:18:26,360\nA roof.\n\n415\n00:18:27,200 --> 00:18:27,720\nA sky.\n\n416\n00:18:28,840 --> 00:18:29,360\nOne minute.\n\n417\n00:18:30,140 --> 00:18:31,120\nThe eyes are a door.\n\n418\n00:18:32,100 --> 00:18:32,520\nOpen it.\n\n419\n00:18:33,700 --> 00:18:35,040\n\"The eyes are a door.\n\n420\n00:18:35,640 --> 00:18:42,280\nOpen it.\" Ben, let's repeat today's important words, slowly, because our listeners want to remember them.\n\n421\n00:18:43,380 --> 00:18:43,820\nWindow.\n\n422\n00:18:44,720 --> 00:18:45,640\nSit by a window.\n\n423\n00:18:46,500 --> 00:18:47,500\nBorrow a window.\n\n424\n00:18:48,620 --> 00:18:48,940\nLook.\n\n425\n00:18:49,840 --> 00:18:51,900\nTo use the eyes, with no story.\n\n426\n00:18:52,900 --> 00:18:53,460\nLook out.\n\n427\n00:18:54,120 --> 00:18:54,740\nLook far.\n\n428\n00:18:55,920 --> 00:18:56,460\nMinute.\n\n429\n00:18:57,380 --> 00:18:58,300\nSixty seconds.\n\n430\n00:18:59,040 --> 00:19:00,280\nOne minute at the window.\n\n431\n00:19:01,000 --> 00:19:02,020\nOne minute an hour.\n\n432\n00:19:03,150 --> 00:19:03,540\nTree.\n\n433\n00:19:04,410 --> 00:19:06,020\nThe slowest alive thing.\n\n434\n00:19:06,800 --> 00:19:08,620\nA tree behind you.\n\n435\n00:19:08,680 --> 00:19:10,220\nA tree is the right speed.\n\n436\n00:19:11,420 --> 00:19:12,480\nEye.\n\n437\n00:19:12,520 --> 00:19:13,440\nThe eyes wander.\n\n438\n00:19:14,080 --> 00:19:15,260\nThe eyes are a door.\n\n439\n00:19:16,420 --> 00:19:16,820\nWindow.\n\n440\n00:19:17,840 --> 00:19:18,080\nLook.\n\n441\n00:19:19,180 --> 00:19:19,640\nMinute.\n\n442\n00:19:20,920 --> 00:19:21,240\nTree.\n\n443\n00:19:22,520 --> 00:19:22,780\nEye.\n\n444\n00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:25,460\nFive words, and one chair.\n\n445\n00:19:26,220 --> 00:19:27,240\nAnd one big idea.\n\n446\n00:19:28,140 --> 00:19:28,980\nSit by a window.\n\n447\n00:19:30,100 --> 00:19:32,480\nNow, Mia, do you have a question for our listeners?\n\n448\n00:19:33,380 --> 00:19:33,960\nYes, Ben.\n\n449\n00:19:34,340 --> 00:19:36,390\nEveryone listening, here is my question.\n\n450\n00:19:37,260 --> 00:19:39,050\nWhat is outside your window right now?\n\n451\n00:19:39,860 --> 00:19:40,930\nOr the nearest window.\n\n452\n00:19:41,750 --> 00:19:44,150\nWrite it in the comments in one short sentence.\n\n453\n00:19:44,850 --> 00:19:54,000\n\"A roof and a grey sky.\" \"A tree and a bin.\" And then, if you want, add, \"I will look at it for one minute today.\"\n\n454\n00:19:54,100 --> 00:19:55,000\nOne minute today.\n\n455\n00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:59,940\nAnd if this episode helped you, please subscribe to Two Cups English.\n\n456\n00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:02,520\nWe make a new episode every few days.\n\n457\n00:20:02,600 --> 00:20:06,800\nSlow English, simple words, and one true story each time.\n\n458\n00:20:07,680 --> 00:20:10,010\nThe full text of this episode is in the description.\n\n459\n00:20:10,800 --> 00:20:12,520\nRead it at a window, if you can.\n\n460\n00:20:13,500 --> 00:20:15,250\nThank you for spending this time with us.\n\n461\n00:20:15,740 --> 00:20:18,290\nNow, before you do anything else, look up.\n\n462\n00:20:19,040 --> 00:20:20,180\nFind the far thing.\n\n463\n00:20:20,820 --> 00:20:24,100\nA window, a door, a gap between buildings.\n\n464\n00:20:24,820 --> 00:20:25,380\nOne minute.\n\n465\n00:20:25,980 --> 00:20:26,980\nLet the eyes go.\n\n466\n00:20:27,880 --> 00:20:29,160\nThere is a tree behind you.\n\n467\n00:20:29,500 --> 00:20:30,540\nThere always was.\n\n468\n00:20:31,320 --> 00:20:32,040\nTurn the chair.\n\n469\n00:20:32,480 --> 00:20:33,520\nLook for one minute.\n\n470\n00:20:34,100 --> 00:20:35,140\nLook again tomorrow.\n\n471\n00:20:36,160 --> 00:20:36,740\nThis is Ben.\n\n472\n00:20:37,560 --> 00:20:38,380\nAnd this is Mia.\n\n473\n00:20:39,100 --> 00:20:40,500\nThis was Two Cups English.\n\n474\n00:20:41,220 --> 00:20:42,100\nSit by a window.\n\n475\n00:20:42,940 --> 00:20:43,950\nWe will see you next time."}];
